local P = LoreForeverPacks.Begin("LoreForever_Voice_Female_MorePlaces")
P.transportVersion = 1
P.transport = {}
P.transport["subzone:agmond-s-end"] = {
  hash = "6b8d16",
  fullHash = "6b8d16",
  text = "Agmond's End. Agmond's End is an abandoned dwarven excavation site tucked into the southern hills of the Badlands. Once a hopeful exploratory dig led by the prospector Agmond, the camp turned into a slaughterhouse when burrowing troggs overran the operation and killed its leader. Like many sites in the arid Badlands, Agmond's End began as a dwarven venture to uncover ancient relics and delve into the earth's buried history. That dream ended abruptly when savage troggs surged from the surrounding crevices and overwhelmed the miners. Agmond himself was cut down during the attack, giving the site its grim name. Today, the ruined dig remains utterly infested by Stonevault troggs, with shattered crates and abandoned tools left exactly where they fell. Near the ruined excavation sits a solitary camp inhabited by Theldurin the Lost, a dwarf whose sanity has long since frayed. He remains one of the few souls willing to brave the southern desert near the doomed site.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_agmond-s-end-fe4172e3a485d082cf3a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 60.2122449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:aku-mai-s-lair"] = {
  hash = "e3fcf1",
  fullHash = "e3fcf1",
  text = "Aku'mai's Lair. Beyond the Moonshrine Sanctum, Aku'mai's Lair lies beneath Blackfathom Deeps. In its flooded depths, cultists and naga gather around Aku'mai, a hydra they honor as a servant of the Old Gods. Four braziers flank a naga statue behind the Moonshrine Sanctum's sealed double doors. Lighting them awakens guardians sworn to defend the threshold. Beyond the doors, a sunless grotto lies beneath the water. Twilight's Hammer cultists and invading naga gather there in reverence of Aku'mai, an ancient, three-headed hydra they consider a living servant of the Old Gods. Twilight's Hammer cultists see Aku'mai as an omen of a promised age of darkness. They believe the beast's emergence and continued sustenance will herald the breaking of subterranean prisons holding their ancient deities. Sacrificial offerings feed Aku'mai, while dark magic guards the pool where it dwells. The cult's plans center on this beast as ancient evil claws its way back into Kalimdor.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_aku-mai-s-lair-cc18eabedb8deff29286/1-0000.mp3", duration = 61.5967347, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:altar-of-storms-burning-steppes"] = {
  hash = "aa1f5d",
  fullHash = "aa1f5d",
  text = "Altar of Storms (Burning Steppes). Perched in the jagged crags of the Burning Steppes, the Altar of Storms is a dark monument dedicated to fel rituals and demonic power. Blackrock clan warlocks occupy the sinister site, performing foul magic beneath the shadow of Blackrock Spire. Its dark aura draws ambitious practitioners of the shadow from across Azeroth. Constructed during the earlier wars to channel corrupting fel magic, the Altar of Storms stands as a grim monument to dark pacts. In the Burning Steppes, hostile warlocks of the Blackrock clan control the grounds, conducting occult rituals and drawing upon demonic forces. Nearby, the encampment of the hermit Gorzeeki Wildeyes and his master Mor'zul Bloodbringer overlooks the site, where renegade practitioners study its violent energies and concoct demanding rituals for ambitious spellcasters seeking greater dark power.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_altar-of-storms-burning-steppes-5db8c182a95c4774e29d/1-0000.mp3", duration = 53.6816327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:alterac-mountains-classic"] = {
  hash = "575587",
  fullHash = "575587",
  text = "Alterac Mountains (Classic). The Alterac Mountains are a rugged, overcast highland rising sharply north of Hillsbrad Foothills, crowned with permanent snow and jagged peaks. Once the home of the human kingdom of Alterac and the scholarly redoubts of Dalaran, the region now lies largely in ruins, fought over by brutal ogres, noble-born bandits, and the opposing armies of Alterac Valley. Rising high above the Hillsbrad lowlands, the Alterac Mountains are a harsh realm of rain, pine, and high-altitude snow. Once anchored by three major human powers—the mountain kingdom of Alterac, the magocracy of Dalaran, and the fortified town of Strahnbrad—the region collapsed under betrayal and war. Today, the ruined capital of Alterac is squatted by the Crushridge ogres, Strahnbrad is occupied by outlaws, and among the shattered remnants of Dalaran its surviving wizards work tirelessly to restore their ruined sanctuary. Beyond the indigenous mountain lions and yeti that stalk the frostbitten ridges, the uplands are contested by desperate mortal factions. The Syndicate—dispossessed human nobles turned brigands—wield blade and ambush from old estates and foothill encampments, clashing incessantly with the encroaching ogres. Meanwhile, deep passes leading into the high crags serve as staging camps for the bloody war between the Stormpike Expedition and the Frostwolf Clan over the hidden reaches of Alterac Valley.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_alterac-mountains-classic-4a011a71fa7145138d91/1-0000.mp3", duration = 85.8383673, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:andorhal"] = {
  hash = "8731d3",
  fullHash = "8731d3",
  text = "Andorhal. Once the thriving agricultural heart of northern Lordaeron, Andorhal became ground zero for the Kingdom's destruction during the Third War. It was here that Kel'Thuzad tainted the grain with the Plague of Undeath, unleashing horror across the region. Today, the ruined town is held firmly in the iron grip of the Scourge. Before the Third War, Andorhal was the prosperous granary of the Eastweald, gathering grain from across Lordaeron and distributing it eastward to Hearthglen and Stratholme. Because of this vast distribution network, Kel'Thuzad and the Cult of the Damned chose Andorhal as the staging ground for their horrific plot. By tainting the grain crates with the Plague of Undeath, the cult turned the breadbasket of the kingdom into an engine of necrosis, infecting countless citizens before the Crown even understood the threat. Prince Arthas Menethil and Jaina Proudmoore initially confronted and slew Kel'Thuzad here, but the necromancer's death was not the end. When Arthas returned from Northrend as a champion of the Scourge, he marched back to Andorhal to reclaim Kel'Thuzad's remains. Uther the Lightbringer and the remnants of the Silver Hand made a desperate last stand in defense of King Terenas's ashes. The paladins were overwhelmed, Uther was slain by his former pupil, and the grand settlement was reduced to a blight-choked Scourge stronghold.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_andorhal-988b8a8de5d511767b2c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 80.7183673, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:ariden-s-camp"] = {
  hash = "771b28",
  fullHash = "771b28",
  text = "Ariden's Camp. Ariden's Camp is an abandoned encampment nestled just north of the path threading through Deadwind Pass. Left decaying in the shadow of Karazhan, it stands as an eerie, quiet anomaly amidst the desolate canyon winds. Travelers between Duskwood and the Swamp of Sorrows often pass it with unease, wondering who once pitched a tent here. Tucked against the crags north of the gorge road connecting Duskwood to the Swamp of Sorrows, this dilapidated campsite looks like an ordinary transient stop long forsaken by living men. Yet in the oppressive gloom of Deadwind Pass, nothing remains unmarred by dark sorcery. Whoever named the camp Ariden's and whatever fortune its owner sought in Karazhan's orbit, the pass's lingering horrors swallowed it, leaving only weathered cloth, cold ashes, and an enduring mystery.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_ariden-s-camp-7e453d7739643cc8d928/1-0000.mp3", duration = 53.6816327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:bal-lal-ruins"] = {
  hash = "721572",
  fullHash = "721572",
  text = "Bal'lal Ruins. Bal'lal Ruins are ancient Gurubashi troll ruins set along the northwestern shore of Stranglethorn Vale, just north of Grom'gol Base Camp. Overlooking the murky waters of the Vile Reef, the crumbling stone foundations are currently occupied by hostile Bloodscalp trolls. Perched on the coastline north of Grom'gol Base Camp, Bal'lal Ruins stand as another remnant of the shattered Gurubashi Empire. Once part of the grand troll civilization that dominated southern Azeroth, the site has decayed into moss-covered stone walls and cracked masonry. The ruins sit immediately adjacent to the Vile Reef, offering an elevated vantage over the treacherous coastal shallows. Today, aggressive Bloodscalp trolls hold the grounds, fiercely defending the weathered relics of their ancestors against intruders.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_bal-lal-ruins-8c8ec4bb1558e221c6ed/1-0000.mp3", duration = 49.4236735, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:balia-mah-ruins"] = {
  hash = "c798d3",
  fullHash = "c798d3",
  text = "Balia'mah Ruins. The Balia'mah Ruins are the moss-covered remnants of an ancient Gurubashi settlement in northern Stranglethorn Vale. Located east of Grom'gol Base Camp across the river, the crumbling stone tiers are currently claimed and guarded by warriors of the hostile Skullsplitter troll tribe. Like many of the stone edifices dotting the jungle, Balia'mah stands as a remnant of the sprawling Gurubashi Empire that once ruled much of the southern Eastern Kingdoms. Following the fall of their ancient capital, Gurubashi territory splintered into warring factions. Today, the ruins serve as a stronghold for the Skullsplitter tribe, who fiercely defend the stone terraces against wandering beasts, neighboring ogres from the Mosh'Ogg mound, and any outsiders foolish enough to cross the river.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_balia-mah-ruins-5a1df34d5084c5e78258/1-0000.mp3", duration = 47.9085714, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:bay-of-storms"] = {
  hash = "15636f",
  fullHash = "15636f",
  text = "Bay of Storms. The Bay of Storms is an expansive, tempestuous gulf carving into the rugged eastern cliffs of Azshara. Its churning waters harbor ancient ruins, submerged caverns, and perilous marine beasts that make seafaring along this coast hazardous. Opening out toward the Great Sea, the Bay of Storms defines much of Azshara's dramatic coastal geography. Far from a tranquil harbor, its turbulent waters and rocky outcrops are home to dangerous wildlife and ancient aquatic threats. Within and along its waters lie landmarks like the sunken Temple of Arkkoran, the coral reefs of Hetaera's Clutch, and Scalebeard's Cave, leaving the bay largely avoided by casual sailors and claimed instead by coastal monsters.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_bay-of-storms-2f08dba6143733a8eb4e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 44.6955102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:bear-s-head"] = {
  hash = "6e72d4",
  fullHash = "6e72d4",
  text = "Bear's Head. Bear's Head is a secluded mountainous stretch along the southeastern cliffs of Azshara. Named for the resident Timbermaw furbolgs whose settlements dot the rugged high ground, it serves as an outpost linked to their larger homes farther north while lying directly beneath the cliffside sanctum of Archmage Xylem. Tucked away in southeastern Azshara, Bear's Head takes its name from the encampments of uncorrupted Timbermaw furbolgs established upon its heights. These rustic settlements act as outlying camps tied to the major furbolg dens of Ursolan and Timbermaw Hold to the north. Perched atop the looming cliffs directly above the area sits Xylem's Tower, an eccentric sanctuary where Archmage Xylem and his small retinue of apprentices conduct arcane studies in quiet isolation from the rest of the troubled coastline.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_bear-s-head-6f61d2d9828c4ade99f6/1-0000.mp3", duration = 47.2293878, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:beezil-s-wreck"] = {
  hash = "36b74f",
  fullHash = "36b74f",
  text = "Beezil's Wreck. Beezil's Wreck marks the crash site of an unfortunate goblin zeppelin deep within the quagmires of Dustwallow Marsh. Resting east of Tabetha's Farm, the shattered vessel's cargo holds scattered supplies and volatile engineering components that attract scavengers. Hidden away in the humid muck east of Tabetha's Farm lies the splintered skeleton of a goblin zeppelin known as Beezil's Wreck. The craft was hauling cargo southward across Kalimdor before suffering a disastrous plunge into the swamp. While the wreckage still smolders amid tangled reeds, its eccentric captain, Beezil, has seemingly vanished from the marsh, leaving behind scattered crates and ticking machinery to slowly rot in the brine. Despite the harsh environment, the crash site remains a prime target for opportunistic explorers. The airship carried a payload of volatile goods, notably Seaforium boosters and heavy crates that survived the impact. Scavengers daring enough to wade through the surrounding beasts often risk life and limb to secure what is left of the lost shipment before corrosive marsh water ruins the haul entirely.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_beezil-s-wreck-b718f26a82ebd63d347f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 68.9371429, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:bitter-reaches"] = {
  hash = "abe1ee",
  fullHash = "abe1ee",
  text = "Bitter Reaches. The Bitter Reaches is a rugged, windswept wilderness in northeastern Azshara. Largely untouched by civilization, the rugged cliffs are prowled by predatory beasts and massive mountain giants. Occupying the northeastern cliffs of Azshara, the Bitter Reaches remains a stark and untamed stretch of rocky terrain overlooking the Great Sea. Flocks of feral hippogryphs and two-headed chimaeras nest amidst the heights, while acidic oozes seep through the cold ground. Along the high northern ridges, colossal mountain giants have claimed the slopes as their own, driving away lesser creatures and turning the frozen approaches into a perilous hazard for any solitary traveler. Despite the harsh winds and aggressive predators, the craggy landscape sustains rare mountain flora. Sparse clusters of Mountain Silversage cling to the high rock faces and windy perches, making the dangerous terrain occasionally worth traversing for intrepid herbalists daring enough to avoid the giants and territorial chimaeras.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_bitter-reaches-f6efef912479a756f479/1-0000.mp3", duration = 66.1681633, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:black-channel-marsh"] = {
  hash = "d91bd8",
  fullHash = "d91bd8",
  text = "Black Channel Marsh. Raptors prowl Black Channel Marsh beside the road to Menethil Harbor. The mire lies in the Wetlands’ southwest, where travelers and couriers face danger along the coast. Black Channel Marsh spreads across the low ground southwest of the main road to Menethil Harbor. Caravan drivers and couriers know the danger there. The raised road may seem safe, but territorial raptors live among the stagnant channels and thick reeds. Hungry beasts often stray into the road, ambushing recruits and weary travelers who wander even a little from the path.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_black-channel-marsh-4ae62a74171c4acba182/1-0000.mp3", duration = 35.1346939, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:blackchar-cave"] = {
  hash = "f60128",
  fullHash = "f60128",
  text = "Blackchar Cave. Blackchar Cave is a mysterious, sealed-off cavern tucked into the southwestern crags of Searing Gorge, close to the base of Blackrock Mountain. Its entrance is choked with impassable rubble and rock. Tucked away against the scorching crags near the approaches to Blackrock Mountain, Blackchar Cave presents a solid wall of stone where a deep cavern entrance might once have been. Heavy rubble and scorched slag choke the opening, preventing anyone from exploring whatever tunnels lie deeper within the mountain roots. The desolate terrain around it is scorched by ash and magma, offering little comfort to wanderers.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_blackchar-cave-e7880313c3bdb573e324/1-0000.mp3", duration = 37.5118367, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:blackfathom-deeps"] = {
  hash = "65295f",
  fullHash = "65295f",
  text = "Blackfathom Deeps. Blackfathom Deeps lies flooded beneath the Zoram Strand on Ashenvale's northwestern coast. Its halls once honored Elune, but naga, satyr, and the Twilight's Hammer now hold the ruins. Beneath Ashenvale's northwestern cliffs, flooded halls mark the remains of an ancient temple. Before the Great Sundering shattered Kalimdor ten thousand years ago, the temple honored Elune and held its own moonwell. When the Well of Eternity collapsed, the shrine sank beneath the waves and was presumed lost forever. Over millennia, shorelines receded and the ground shifted below, leaving the temple partly flooded beneath the cliffs.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_blackfathom-deeps-7d5b35df9228e505f6c1/1-0000.mp3", duration = 38.7395918, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:blackrock-mountain"] = {
  hash = "8227a4",
  fullHash = "8227a4",
  text = "Blackrock Mountain. Blackrock Mountain is a massive, active volcano that towers between the Burning Steppes and the Searing Gorge. Borne from the cataclysmic summoning of Ragnaros the Firelord, its hollowed depths house ancient dwarven masonry, warring armies, and smoldering horrors. Blackrock Mountain did not exist until the climax of the War of the Three Hammers two and a half centuries ago. Facing annihilation by the united armies of Ironforge and the Wildhammer clan, Sorcerer-Thane Thaurissan reached into the depths of the earth for a weapon. His reckless spell shattered the dimensional barriers and summoned Ragnaros the Firelord into Azeroth. The resulting apocalypse tore the Redridge range apart, gouging out the Searing Gorge and Burning Steppes while thrusting a colossal volcano into the sky. Thaurissan perished in the blast, and the surviving Dark Iron clan was bound into eternal servitude to the Firelord, carving their capital, Shadowforge City, deep within the mountain's roots. During the First and Second Wars, the mountain took on a grim new significance. Following the sack of Stormwind, Orgrim Doomhammer purged the Shadow Council and made Blackrock Spire the fortress capital of the Horde. Today, the mountain remains a vicious theater of war: the Dark Iron dwarves and their elemental master hold the fiery depths, while the rogue Blackrock orcs, dragonkin, and their dark masters occupy the spires above.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_blackrock-mountain-a89aa7d33058214ddda3/1-0000.mp3", duration = 86.804898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:blackrock-pass"] = {
  hash = "c30310",
  fullHash = "c30310",
  text = "Blackrock Pass. Blackrock Pass is a mountain corridor connecting the northern cliffs of Redridge Mountains to the arid wastes of the Burning Steppes. Passing beneath a heavy iron gate, it marks the sudden threshold between the tranquil human heartlands and the dangerous war grounds of the Dark Iron dwarves and the Blackrock orcs. For any weary traveler journeying north on foot, it represents a harsh and perilous frontier. Carved into the jagged spine of the mountains, Blackrock Pass serves as the overland route between Redridge Mountains and the Burning Steppes. A stout iron gate stands along the rocky boundary, demarcating the idyllic forests and lakes of the southern valleys from the volcanic ruin beyond. Crossing this line leaves behind the pastoral lands protected by Lakeshire's beleaguered militia, thrusting travelers into an open wasteland populated by fire elementals, dragonkin, and hostile orc warbands. Few make the crossing on foot unless bound for the shadow of Blackrock Mountain itself.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_blackrock-pass-d7dc255fd57a47e80595/1-0000.mp3", duration = 62.3281633, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:blackrock-stronghold"] = {
  hash = "9af21a",
  fullHash = "9af21a",
  text = "Blackrock Stronghold. Blackrock Stronghold is a heavily fortified encampment carved into the crags of the northern Burning Steppes, positioned right outside the entrance to Blackrock Mountain. It serves as a major field garrison for the Blackrock orcs and their allies under the Dark Horde banner. Sprawling across the scorched foothills below Blackrock Mountain, the stronghold functions as the primary ground base for the Blackrock clan in the Burning Steppes. Unlike Thrall's Horde, these orcs cling to their warlike legacy, serving Rend Blackhand and the dragonkin who lurk above in the spire. The stronghold serves as both an outer defense and a staging ground for attacks into the Redridge Mountains and neighboring human lands. The fortress maintains a constant watch over the southern approaches to the mountain, challenging anyone who dares traverse the blasted wastes.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_blackrock-stronghold-2fe7981e5cb624313938/1-0000.mp3", duration = 50.1028571, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:blackwood-lake"] = {
  hash = "4842fc",
  fullHash = "4842fc",
  text = "Blackwood Lake. Blackwood Lake is a blighted body of water situated in the southern Eastern Plaguelands, teeming with hostile, corrupted elementals. The area is haunted by memories of tragedy and war, watched over by a small frontline camp of the Argent Dawn. Like so much of the Eastweald, Blackwood Lake has been poisoned by the rampant plague of undeath. Diseased and foul water elementals churn beneath the murky surface, attacking anyone who ventures near the shoreline.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_blackwood-lake-ca99c85805227b8edce5/1-0000.mp3", duration = 28.2644898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:bloodfen-burrow"] = {
  hash = "ce64dc",
  fullHash = "ce64dc",
  text = "Bloodfen Burrow. Bloodfen Burrow is a cavern in southwestern Dustwallow Marsh swarming with vicious raptors. The beasts have claimed the cave and the surrounding clearing, completely overrunning the ruins of a settlement that once tried to survive the wetlands. Tucked away in the southwestern reaches of Dustwallow Marsh, Bloodfen Burrow serves as the primary den for the region's aggressive Bloodfen raptors. The cave mouth and surrounding hollow crawl with the pack, drawn to the sheltered crevice and the bountiful hunting ground the marsh provides. Just outside the entrance lie scattered debris and splintered remains of an unidentified settlement, serving as a grim reminder of who truly rules the swamp's interior. Travelers also whisper that Cortello, an adventurous buccaneer, left one of his hidden riddle clues somewhere within this deadly den.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_bloodfen-burrow-a7b55998ea16017aab33/1-0000.mp3", duration = 46.7853061, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:bloodvenom-falls"] = {
  hash = "b655ba",
  fullHash = "b655ba",
  text = "Bloodvenom Falls. Bloodvenom Falls is a towering, foul waterfall plunging down the corrupted cliffs of northern Felwood. Its toxic flow feeds the Bloodvenom River, spreading demon-spawned rot down the mountains and into downstream lands. Pouring over the bluffs in Felwood, Bloodvenom Falls represents one of the most vivid wounds inflicted upon the ancient forests of Kalimdor. Once a pristine mountain cascade, the waters were warped into a virulent sludge during the Third War by demonic magics and the remnants of the Burning Legion. The falls feed directly into the Bloodvenom River, which weaves through the heart of Felwood. From there, the tainted torrent rushes down the mountain slopes to become the foul Wildbend River in Darkshore, carrying blight far beyond Felwood's blighted borders. The virulent runoff from Bloodvenom Falls does not merely discolor the riverbed; it warps everything it touches. The swampy banks surrounding its spray breed twisted treants, sickening beasts, and aggressive slimes. For druids and wardens attempting to contain Felwood's rot, the falls are a chilling reminder that northern Kalimdor's waters remain deeply sickened at their source.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_bloodvenom-falls-9b2b35e8479c8a9c054d/1-0000.mp3", duration = 69.642449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:bloodvenom-post"] = {
  hash = "0141f3",
  fullHash = "0141f3",
  text = "Bloodvenom Post. Bloodvenom Post is a remote Horde settlement perched on the rocky bluffs of western Felwood overlooking Darkshore. Its tauren-style structures and tauren guards provide a foothold in a region infested with demonic taint and corrupted wildlife. Tucked away along the high cliffs above Darkshore, Bloodvenom Post is reached by following the corrupted southern bank of the Bloodvenom River away from Felwood's main thoroughfare. Built with traditional tauren tents, totems, and watch posts, this encampment provides Horde wanderers with a vital base of operations amidst a dying forest. From here, scouts track the encroaching demonic forces, study the spreading corruption of the waters, and attempt to monitor the hostile wildlife mutated by the Burning Legion's lingering power. Beyond observing the tainted wilds of Felwood, the camp sits right upon the precipice peering down over northern Darkshore. Its isolated position serves a double duty: keeping Horde forces informed of night elf movements along the western coastline while remaining well out of easy reach of Alliance patrols traversing the forest's central road.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_bloodvenom-post-c334ea38208a9445402d/1-0000.mp3", duration = 69.0155102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:bloodvenom-river"] = {
  hash = "0c88ab",
  fullHash = "0c88ab",
  text = "Bloodvenom River. The Bloodvenom River is a foul waterway cutting through central Felwood, saturated with the demonic corruption that blighted the ancient forest. Its putrid waters seep downstream across the mountains into Darkshore, transforming into the tainted Wildbend River. Running through the corrupted heart of Felwood, the Bloodvenom River serves as an open conduit of the land's spiritual and physical decay. Its banks are thick with vile oozes, mutated beasts, and foul run-off left in the wake of the Burning Legion's invasion. The corruption within the channel is potent enough to taint lands far beyond Felwood itself, flowing westward down into the coastal forests of Darkshore as the Wildbend River.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_bloodvenom-river-e6c56978bc44bf6af9bd/1-0000.mp3", duration = 40.8293878, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:bluefen"] = {
  hash = "8bcf9b",
  fullHash = "8bcf9b",
  text = "Bluefen. Bluefen is a soggy marshland nestled in northern Dustwallow Marsh just east of Brackenwall Village. Crawling with aggressive wetland predators, the mire has also become a staging ground for covert Theramore Infiltrators keeping watch on local Horde movements. Situated just east of the Stonemaul ogres of Brackenwall Village, Bluefen embodies the untamed hazards of Dustwallow Marsh. The humid swampland teems with predatory beasts, including raptors, giant spiders, carrion-feeding birds, and lurking crocolisks. Beyond natural predators, travelers must contend with Theramore Infiltrators who skulk through the murky terrain to spy on the Horde and project human influence deeper into the swamp.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_bluefen-bec6eafff2744346cb81/1-0000.mp3", duration = 44.9828571, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:bluegill-marsh"] = {
  hash = "f726bb",
  fullHash = "f726bb",
  text = "Bluegill Marsh. In western Wetlands, Bluegill Marsh is claimed by the hostile Bluegill tribe. Their huts and waterways are guarded by Gobbler, and travelers risk being surrounded in the shallows. Bluegill murlocs crowd the muddy flats and reed beds of the western Wetlands. Their tribe has built crude camps in the stagnant water and claims the marsh as its own. They live and hunt in close-knit packs. Travelers and scouts from Menethil Harbor risk ambush from every direction when they wade into Bluegill territory, where the chieftain Gobbler prowls the shallows.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_bluegill-marsh-67d7d8d18bb745b0a913/1-0000.mp3", duration = 34.9779592, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:bolgan-s-hole"] = {
  hash = "db8697",
  fullHash = "db8697",
  text = "Bolgan's Hole. Bolgan's Hole is a subterranean cavern nestled in the southern cliffs of Gelkis Village in Desolace. It serves as the inner sanctum of the Gelkis centaur clan, housing their highest chieftains and sacred earth relics. Carved deep into the craggy bluffs overlooking the Gelkis encampment, Bolgan's Hole functions as the command center for the tribe. It is here that the ruling khan holds court, surrounded by veteran guards and the tribe's most influential shamans. The cavern offers natural fortification against rival clans, most notably the bloodthirsty Magram centaur to the east. Beyond its military importance, the cave possesses profound religious significance to the Gelkis. Luminous blue mineral formations known as the tears of Theradras sprout from the damp stone walls. Gelkis shamans obsessively harvest and guard these crystals, revering them as divine blessings that confer the subterranean protection of Princess Theradras, the primal earth elemental mother of the centaur race.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_bolgan-s-hole-eeaa12865ad29c68ed32/1-0000.mp3", duration = 60.3167347, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:bones-of-grakkarond"] = {
  hash = "1c8889",
  fullHash = "1c8889",
  text = "Bones of Grakkarond. The Bones of Grakkarond are the skeletal remains of a massive bronze dragon lying bleached under the sun of central Silithus. Slain during the ancient War of the Shifting Sands, this fallen wyrm serves as a grim monument held sacred by the qiraji's Anubisath sentinels. Sprawled across the windswept dunes of central Silithus lies the colossal ribcage and skull of Grakkarond, a bronze dragon who met his demise during the ancient War of the Shifting Sands. In that apocalyptic clash a millennium ago, the great dragon was struck down by Ossirian the Unscarred, an Anubisath warrior elevated by the dark powers of the city-kingdom. To the Anubisath, this battlefield trophy is a sacred testament to their might over the dragonflights, left untouched where he fell.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_bones-of-grakkarond-99fb88c97d75e85b9577/1-0000.mp3", duration = 45.9493878, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:booty-bay"] = {
  hash = "832d7d",
  fullHash = "832d7d",
  text = "Booty Bay. Booty Bay is a sprawling pirate haven built into the sheer sea cliffs of southern Stranglethorn Vale, operated by the Steamwheedle Cartel's Blackwater Raiders. Entered by land through the gaping jaws of a giant shark carcass, it serves as a fiercely enforced neutral port where Alliance and Horde mingle under the watchful eyes of heavily armed goblin bruisers. Originally known as Blackwater Cove when it was a human settlement later overrun by jungle trolls, the bay was eventually captured by the goblins of the Steamwheedle Cartel. Today, it is an international crossroads and a haven for scoundrels, outcasts, and merchants alike. Overseen by Baron Revilgaz alongside Fleet Master Seahorn from the balconies of the Salty Sailor Tavern, the port is ostensibly lawless yet strictly managed: anyone may drink, trade, or hire mercenaries here, provided they obey the cartel's peace and pay their harbor fees. Booty Bay's bustling docks connect the Eastern Kingdoms directly to Ratchet across the Great Sea, making it a critical hub for trade and cross-continental travel. However, its immense wealth attracts relentless hostility. The town's primary nemesis is the Bloodsail Buccaneers, an aggressive pirate fleet that constantly raids Blackwater shipping, roams the Stranglethorn coastline, and actively plots the total destruction and plunder of the goblin port.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_booty-bay-9651fb16e75e82c9264f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 84.9240816, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:boulder-gor"] = {
  hash = "244b74",
  fullHash = "244b74",
  text = "Boulder'gor. Boulder'gor is a rugged hill in the western Arathi Highlands, situated directly north of the ruined human kingdom of Stromgarde. It serves as an active encampment for the Boulderfist ogres and their unusual allies among the Witherbark forest trolls. Rising just north of the crumbling ramparts of Stromgarde Keep, Boulder'gor is a steep bluff claimed by the Boulderfist clan. Within its shadows sits the Boulderfist Outpost mound, making it a central bastion of ogre power across the highlands. Uniquely, the ogres here share their fires and fortifications with forest trolls of the Witherbark tribe, including shadowy spellcasters who lend their dark magic to the clan's brute force. This mutual cooperation poses an ongoing danger to any travelers passing along the central roads of Arathi. The slopes around Boulder'gor look down over the open highlands of western Arathi. Hemmed between the Syndicate-infiltrated ruins of Stromgarde and the wild elemental sites of the plains, Boulder'gor remains a heavily guarded and hostile stronghold of tribal solidarity against both Alliance and Horde wanderers.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_boulder-gor-c6d02dd0f8a2eb9119d8/1-0000.mp3", duration = 68.0489796, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:boulderfist-hall"] = {
  hash = "1a8fb6",
  fullHash = "1a8fb6",
  text = "Boulderfist Hall. Boulderfist Hall is a heavily fortified ogre mound nestled in the southern hills of the Arathi Highlands, just north of Thandol Span. It serves as the local stronghold for the Boulderfist ogres, who exert their brute influence across the neighboring trade routes and valleys under the command of Kor'gresh Coldrage. Carved into the rugged southern bluffs of the Arathi Highlands, Boulderfist Hall is the principal redoubt of the Boulderfist clan in the region. Sheltered within its earthen ramparts and deep caverns, ogre brutes and magi maintain a fortified settlement mere steps from Witherbark Village and the strategic bridge at Thandol Span. Deep within the mound lurks their regional chieftain, Kor'gresh Coldrage, alongside enforcers like Phalanax. From this stronghold, the ogres project violent control over the surrounding roads, making any peaceful transit between Arathi and the Wetlands perilous for travelers and supply caravans alike.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_boulderfist-hall-59e3e4f921cb7f2bfee1/1-0000.mp3", duration = 56.4767347, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:boulderfist-outpost"] = {
  hash = "b5f109",
  fullHash = "b5f109",
  text = "Boulderfist Outpost. Boulderfist Outpost is a fortified mound nestled deep within the ogre settlement of Boulder'gor in the Arathi Highlands. Serving as an interior den and holding area, it houses aggressive Boulderfist ogres alongside a few Witherbark trolls. Carved into the earth and stone of western Arathi, Boulderfist Outpost functions as the heart of Boulder'gor. While brute enforcers dominate the settlement above, the outpost's interior concentrates the clan's core strength and provides a dark subterranean shelter. A handful of Witherbark troll witch doctors occasionally mingle among the ogres here, hinting at tenuous tribal cooperation.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_boulderfist-outpost-aaac14c387b4543370ec/1-0000.mp3", duration = 39.915102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:brackenwall-village"] = {
  hash = "0b8951",
  fullHash = "0b8951",
  text = "Brackenwall Village. Brackenwall Village is the primary Horde outpost in Dustwallow Marsh, nestled among the bogs in the northwest corner of the zone. Founded by surviving Stonemaul ogres after dragons devastated their original settlement to the south, the village serves as a tenuous stronghold surrounded by Alliance spies, deserters, and the black dragonflight. When black dragonkin drove the Stonemaul ogres out of their ancient home at Stonemaul Village in the southern marsh, their leader, Overlord Mok'Morokk—successor to Rexxar—led the survivors north into the swamp to build Brackenwall. Formed around traditional earthen mounds, huts, and an old watchtower, the settlement gave the battered clan a defensible haven. Though aligned with the Horde, the village suffers from internal rot: Mok'Morokk sits idle, hoarding supplies and refusing to confront the draconic threat, leaving the clan's spiritual and tactical guidance to figures like the ogre mage Draz'Zilb and Horde emissaries.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_brackenwall-village-5daad32221bcd7b74192/1-0000.mp3", duration = 61.1265306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:broken-hall"] = {
  hash = "ba5f7e",
  fullHash = "ba5f7e",
  text = "Broken Hall. The Broken Hall serves as the flooded outer threshold of the sunken Temple of Atal'Hakkar in the Swamp of Sorrows. Shattered when the Great Dragon Aspect Ysera and her flight dragged the grand ziggurat beneath the waters, this ruin now stands deserted as the Atal'ai zealots retreat into deeper sanctums. When the green dragons brought down their wrath upon the Atal'ai priesthood and submerged their unholy ziggurat beneath the Pool of Tears, the outermost architecture bore the brunt of the cataclysm. The floor of the Broken Hall collapsed completely, allowing murky swamp waters to flood the chamber. Because of the damage and submersion, the troll cultists abandoned this entrance hall, withdrawing further into the complex. Adventurers entering the sunken temple must wade through these sodden ruins before mounting the stairs to reach the Hall of Masks.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_broken-hall-c1700f5f872d2c20165a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 52.8195918, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:broken-pillar"] = {
  hash = "f2ef54",
  fullHash = "f2ef54",
  text = "Broken Pillar. The Broken Pillar is a weathered cluster of ancient troll ruins jutting from the dunes south of Gadgetzan. It serves as an isolated research post for the goblin prospector and scientist Marvon Rivetseeker, who studies the enigmatic artifacts buried beneath the surrounding sands. Rising out of the sands south of Gadgetzan and east of the treacherous Abyssal Sands, the Broken Pillar marks the remnants of ancient troll architecture long swallowed by Tanaris. Despite the desolate surroundings and encroaching desert beasts, the site offers rare clues to the continent's prehistoric civilisations. Marvon Rivetseeker, a goblin researcher originally based out of Ratchet, has established a lonely camp beside the shattered stonework to examine curious relics known as Gahz'ridian scattered across the waste.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_broken-pillar-7bfde02fcd1a991503ca/1-0000.mp3", duration = 48.404898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:bronzebeard-encampment"] = {
  hash = "fa36fc",
  fullHash = "fa36fc",
  text = "Bronzebeard Encampment. Bronzebeard Encampment is a rugged dwarven outpost perched in southern Silithus, just east of the massive Scarab Wall. Staffed by hardy explorers and researchers from the Explorer's League, the camp serves as an operational base for investigating ancient silithid glyphs and the lingering mysteries buried beneath the desert sands. Positioned against the desolate southern bluffs of Silithus, Bronzebeard Encampment offers a rare refuge of dwarven grit in an otherwise hostile wasteland. Founded under the auspices of Ironforge's archaeological pursuits, the camp sits within sight of the towering Scarab Wall. Here, researchers like Rutgar Glyphshaper and Frankal Stonebridge braved scorching heat, scorpids, and encroaching silithid swarms to study the peculiar inscriptions and monuments left behind by forgotten civilizations. Life in the encampment is far from a quiet dig. With the silithid menace spreading out of subterranean hives across the wastes and dangerous horrors like the monstrous scorpid Deathclasp prowling nearby sands, the outpost balances rigorous scholarship with sheer survival. The researchers rely heavily on visiting adventurers to retrieve rubbings of obscure glyphs, decipher ancient insectoid tongues, and search for lost companions who ventured too deep into the madness of the desert.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_bronzebeard-encampment-84eb2f05bde016367f70/1-0000.mp3", duration = 81.7632653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:browman-mill"] = {
  hash = "3966e9",
  fullHash = "3966e9",
  text = "Browman Mill. Browman Mill is a blighted lumber mill situated just north of Light's Hope Chapel in the Eastern Plaguelands. Once a thriving piece of Lordaeron's timber trade, it now swarms with undead minions under the iron grip of the Scourge. Its former proprietor, Stephen Browman, remains trapped in death within his ruined workshop. Before the Third War, Browman Mill served the surrounding towns and homesteads of Lordaeron, processing lumber harvested from the rich forests of the Eastweald. When the Scourge plague rolled across the kingdom, the mill fell alongside the rest of the province. Today, rotting timbers and shattered wheels sit idle while abominations, banshees, and skeletal warriors patrol the perimeter. Its close proximity to Light's Hope Chapel makes it a constant and menacing reminder of the undead horde pressing against the Argent Dawn's doorstep. The mill's original owner, Stephen Browman, met the same grim fate as countless commoners of Lordaeron. Murdered when the Scourge overran the estate, he was raised into undeath as a ravenous ghoul. Stripped of his humanity and enslaved to the Lich King's will, Browman lingers within the dark confines of his former mill, guarding the splintered ruins of his life's work.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_browman-mill-8c69146413ad1f503992/1-0000.mp3", duration = 73.5346939, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:bulwark"] = {
  hash = "ce9e4c",
  fullHash = "ce9e4c",
  text = "Bulwark. The Bulwark is a fortified outpost straddling the border between Tirisfal Glades and the Western Plaguelands. Maintained jointly by the Forsaken and the neutral Argent Dawn, it serves as the Horde's front line against the Scourge and the primary launching point for expeditions into the tainted east. Positioned squarely on the eastern border of Tirisfal Glades, the Bulwark is the final barrier protecting the Forsaken heartland from the blighted horrors of the Western Plaguelands. Today, rough wooden palisades and heavily armed guards defend the pass against roaming undead. It represents an uneasy but necessary collaboration, where Forsaken forces coordinate alongside the Argent Dawn to recruit seasoned champions of the Horde, turning back the minions of the Lich King before they spill into Tirisfal.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_bulwark-04e207bb82a91c13ce1c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 47.6995918, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:caer-darrow"] = {
  hash = "4ba08b",
  fullHash = "4ba08b",
  text = "Caer Darrow. Caer Darrow is a fortified island situated in Darrowmere Lake in the Western Plaguelands. Formerly the aristocratic seat of the noble House Barov, the island's keep was secretly sold to Kel'Thuzad and transformed into the necromantic school of Scholomance, leaving the above-ground town plagued by wandering phantoms. Long before the Scourge, Caer Darrow was a strategic stronghold of Lordaeron that housed an ancient elven runestone. During the Second War, the Horde sacked the fortress and carved up the runestone to craft Gul'dan's Altars of Storms. When Lord Uther the Lightbringer sailed to offer relief to the survivors, his fleet was ambushed by Alterac privateers—an act of treachery that exposed Alterac's betrayal to the Alliance.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_caer-darrow-84b129e7bbfb7a41cc65/1-0000.mp3", duration = 45.1134694, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:camp-e-thok"] = {
  hash = "c5ca5d",
  fullHash = "c5ca5d",
  text = "Camp E'thok. Camp E'thok is a Galak centaur settlement located in northwestern Thousand Needles. The aggressive centaur tribe uses this canyon encampment as a base to stage ambushes against passing caravans traveling through the riverbed below. Tucked into the rugged northwestern reaches of Thousand Needles, Camp E'thok serves as a key settlement for the hostile Galak centaur. Like their kin across Kalimdor, the Galak are relentlessly predatory, preying upon any travelers wandering into the canyon shadows. The camp is notorious for orchestrating raids against merchant caravans, notably ambushing the goblin Wizlo Bearingshiner and seizing his goods—though his prized gadget managed to slip beyond their grasp, guarded by his fierce pet panther.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_camp-e-thok-06711bb5aed2f1cbf02b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 44.6432653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:camp-kosh"] = {
  hash = "381a6c",
  fullHash = "381a6c",
  text = "Camp Kosh. Camp Kosh is an ogre encampment in the northern Badlands, inhabited by the aggressive Dustbelcher ogres. Perched near vital travel routes, its brutes prey on passing caravans and scavenge whatever equipment travelers leave behind in the harsh desert. Situated in the northern wastes of the Badlands, Camp Kosh serves as the northernmost settlement of the Dustbelcher ogres, who also occupy neighboring camps like Boff, Cagg, and Wurg. The ogres stationed here take advantage of their proximity to the roads, raiding vulnerable supply lines journeying toward Kargath or westward into the Searing Gorge. When dwarven prospectors led by Sigrun Ironhew were forced to flee the excavation at Uldaman, the brutes of Camp Kosh swiftly raided their abandoned wagons, hauling away precious food rations and armaments into the dusty encampment.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_camp-kosh-c351fd9d0d97935e4e8e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 51.0693878, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:camp-mojache"] = {
  hash = "d1e48d",
  fullHash = "d1e48d",
  text = "Camp Mojache. Camp Mojache is the primary Horde outpost in Feralas, established as a fortified tauren settlement along the waters feeding Wildwind Lake. It provides shelter, provisions, and flight connections to travelers contending with the untamed wilderness, aggressive wildlife, and ancient ruins of the deep forest. Situated in eastern Feralas along a swift-flowing stream that feeds Wildwind Lake, Camp Mojache is a tauren settlement built to secure a foothold in the wild southern forest. While the dense jungle holds perilous threats—from savage wildlife to Gordunni ogres and aggressive Woodpaw gnolls—the camp stands as an island of safety. Equipped with lodging, trade masters, and a wind rider roost, it serves as the vital logistical hub for Horde champions operating in the region. The camp's hunters and braves are renowned for their courage and thick hides, maintaining constant vigilance against raids from the surrounding wilderness. For the Horde, holding Mojache is essential for projecting influence across Feralas, checking the presence of both the Night Elves from the coast and the dark forces lurking within Dire Maul.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_camp-mojache-49ec20a9c2b40e3e36fe/1-0000.mp3", duration = 68.9632653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:camp-wurg"] = {
  hash = "b1304e",
  fullHash = "b1304e",
  text = "Camp Wurg. Camp Wurg is an ogre encampment situated in the arid expanse of the Badlands. It serves as one of several outposts claimed by the territorial Dustbelcher ogres who dominate much of the Badlands. Tucked among the crags of the Badlands, Camp Wurg is one of four primary camps settled by the Dustbelcher ogres, alongside Camp Cagg, Camp Boff, and Camp Kosh. The ogres stationed here guard their rugged territory fiercely, scavenging what they can from the unforgiving desert wastes. While rank-and-file brutes and enforcers dwell around the crude fires and rough hides of Wurg, their chieftain, Boss Tho'grun, maintains no permanent seat here, instead roaming the Badlands trails to keep watch over all his encampments.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_camp-wurg-de36840a109ecaaa9245/1-0000.mp3", duration = 46.1061224, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:cape-of-stranglethorn"] = {
  hash = "755593",
  fullHash = "755593",
  text = "Cape of Stranglethorn. The Cape of Stranglethorn forms the sun-baked southern tip of Stranglethorn Vale, stretching south from the Gurubashi Arena down to the ocean cliffs. Anchored by the sprawling pirate port of Booty Bay, the region is a battleground contested by goblins, Bloodsail Buccaneers, ancient jungle trolls, and encroaching wildlife. South of the ancient colosseum of Gurubashi Arena, the dense jungle gives way to the coastline known as the Cape of Stranglethorn. Dotted with crumbling troll settlements like the Ruins of Jubuwal and the Ruins of Aboraz, the cape has long since slipped from imperial Gurubashi control. Today, its beaches, coves, and jungle clearings host a chaotic mix of dangerous predators, murloc encampments, and rival pirate crews warring over trade routes and buried plunder. The dominant power along the cape is the Blackwater Raiders, led by Baron Revilgaz and Fleet Master Seahorn from the pirate city of Booty Bay. Carved directly into the cliffs of the southern bay, this neutral harbor connects the Eastern Kingdoms to Kalimdor via steamships to Ratchet. However, goblin control remains precarious. The Bloodsail Buccaneers operate along the southern shoals and outer islands, raiding supply lines and openly scheming to overthrow Booty Bay and seize total dominion over the South Seas.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_cape-of-stranglethorn-f391cf99f3d80aa341a5/1-0000.mp3", duration = 78.315102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:capital-gardens"] = {
  hash = "a71940",
  fullHash = "a71940",
  text = "Capital Gardens. The Capital Gardens form the western wing of the ancient elven ruins of Dire Maul in Feralas. Once a serene, manicured park for the Kaldorei nobility, it has withered into a blighted woodland stalked by corrupted ancients, ghosts, and remnants of the Shen'dralar. Ten millennia ago, Eldre'Thalas stood as one of the shining jewels of the Kaldorei empire. The Capital Gardens served as its outdoor district, lush with enchanted flora and grand marble terraces where Highborne sorcerers debated and relaxed. Following the Great Sundering, the enclave fell on desperate times. Deprived of the Well of Eternity, the resident Shen'dralar turned to dark magic to sustain their immortality, letting the gardens wither away. Today, gray, twisted boughs and corrupted treants fill the broken courtyards, while residual magical corruption prevents the forest from ever truly healing.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_capital-gardens-b31f490186a9d085d790/1-0000.mp3", duration = 54.0473469, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:cauldron"] = {
  hash = "4daf17",
  fullHash = "4daf17",
  text = "Cauldron. The Cauldron is a massive open-pit excavation site dominating the center of the Searing Gorge. Operated by the Dark Iron clan, this fiery quarry is worked day and night by broken slaves under the watch of cruel overseers and roving elementals. Carved deep into the scorched earth of the Searing Gorge, the Cauldron was fortified following the catastrophic summoning of Ragnaros by Emperor Thaurissan. Massive, smoke-belching Dark Iron excavation machinery rims the canyon walls, scraping rock and ore from the smoldering stone. Down within its burning crevices, living fire and magma elementals prowl alongside Dark Iron taskmasters, while subterranean heat rises continuously from the depths of the mountain. Deep within the pit, captured slave workers endure brutal, endless shifts under the lash of Dark Iron overseers. Broken by relentless labor, sweltering heat, and torment, many of these laborers have been driven completely mad. They no longer recognize friend from foe, lashing out violently against any outsiders who disturb their toil—even those who might otherwise offer them a chance at liberation.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_cauldron-bf90018d00c8741bedc1/1-0000.mp3", duration = 69.9297959, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:caverns-of-time"] = {
  hash = "a26aed",
  fullHash = "a26aed",
  text = "Caverns of Time. Tucked into the eastern cliffs of Tanaris, the Caverns of Time serve as the mystical lair of Nozdormu and the bronze dragonflight. Within its winding depths, the dragons monitor and safeguard the fragile timeways of Azeroth against any mortal intrusion or temporal tampering. Carved deep beneath the desert cliffs, the Caverns of Time are a nexus where the currents of history converge. Charged by the titans at the dawn of the world, Nozdormu the Timeless One and his bronze dragonflight act as eternal sentinels over these corridors. Inside, time behaves erratically; glimpses of past and future slip through hidden rifts, and relics plucked from distant eras lie scattered across the sands. While the great wyrm Anachronos keeps watch near the entrance, lesser bronze drakes patrol the winding descent, fiercely protective of the one true timeline. Beyond being a junction of past and future, the caverns serve as an eccentric vault. Over millennia, the bronze flight has gathered historical fragments from every corner of Azeroth: ancient dwarven structures, a watchtower from Lordaeron, chieftain quarters from the orcish clans, and armor dating back to the first kings of Stromgarde. Yet despite the occasional traveler daring to slip past the sentinels to glimpse these wonders, the inner gateways remain tightly shut, awaiting the day the flight deems mortal intervention necessary.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_caverns-of-time-f5ef175b5505019d6b0a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 85.237551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:cenarion-hold"] = {
  hash = "dea159",
  fullHash = "dea159",
  text = "Cenarion Hold. Cenarion Hold is the fortified stronghold of the Cenarion Circle situated in the harsh desert of Silithus. Established to keep watch over ancient dangers buried beneath the sands, it stands as one of the largest druidic settlements on Kalimdor and a rare safe haven in an unforgiving wasteland. Perched among the desolate sands of Silithus, Cenarion Hold serves as the southern command hub for the Cenarion Circle. Reached by winding past Un'Goro Crater, the fortress was raised to monitor the dormant threats sealed beneath the desert after the historic War of the Shifting Sands. In an inhospitable expanse teeming with insectoid silithid and arid storms, the stronghold provides vital shelter, trade, and logistical support for night elf and tauren druids alike who stand sentinel over the imprisoned horrors of the past. Because the duty of safeguarding the world from ancient evils transcends mortal squabbles, Cenarion Hold operates under strict Cenarion Circle neutrality. Both Alliance and Horde travelers are admitted inside its walls, welcomed by local quartermasters and wardens whose supply lines remain precarious. With flight routes connecting it back to outposts in Feralas, Tanaris, and Un'Goro Crater, the hold acts as the lone staging ground for mortal adventurers brave enough to investigate the rising unrest in the desert.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_cenarion-hold-79ef82a631863e143ade/1-0000.mp3", duration = 80.2742857, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:chamber-of-atonement"] = {
  hash = "2fe9fe",
  fullHash = "2fe9fe",
  text = "Chamber of Atonement. The Chamber of Atonement is the grim entrance hall to the Graveyard wing within the Scarlet Monastery. Here, beneath stone arches hung with iron chains, the Scarlet Crusade subjects captured undead and other perceived heretics to brutal interrogation and torture before casting their remains into the cloister beyond. Serving as the threshold to the Forlorn Cloister, the Chamber of Atonement is far from a sanctuary of peaceful prayer. Instead, the Scarlet Crusade has converted the grand entryway into a house of pain, outfitted with racks, shackles, and burning braziers. Zealous inquisitors use these instruments to extract confessions and torment captives, viewing excruciating pain as the only fitting penance for unholy beings. The chamber echoes constantly with the cries of the damned, presided over by cruel taskmasters who ensure that those who enter rarely walk out whole. The chamber is overseen by Interrogator Vishas, one of the Crusade's most sadistic practitioners of coercion. Vishas takes grotesque pride in breaking the resolve of anyone caught crossing the monastery's boundaries. Among his prized victims is the Forsaken captive Vorrel Sengutz, who has endured endless weeks of torment at Vishas's hands. The cruelty practiced within these walls proves to outsiders that the Scarlet Crusade's crusade for purity has twisted into wanton sadism.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_chamber-of-atonement-94e7b1ea21d908585d62/1-0000.mp3", duration = 79.5428571, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:chillwind-camp"] = {
  hash = "f57bf4",
  fullHash = "f57bf4",
  text = "Chillwind Camp. Chillwind Camp sits at the southern pass leading into the Western Plaguelands from the Alterac Mountains. Manned by an Alliance contingent working alongside the Argent Dawn, it serves as the final safe staging ground before the blighted ruins of Lordaeron. From this precarious outpost, commanders direct strikes against the nearby Scourge strongholds and plague cauldrons. Tucked into the rocky pass descending from Chillwind Point, Chillwind Camp is the first line of defense and primary base of operations for Alliance forces pushing into the Western Plaguelands. Though physically separated from the worst of the corruption by the high mountain ridges, the dead chill of the north hangs heavily over the tents and palisades. Under Commander Ashlam Valorfist, a sparse garrison of soldiers, scouts, and Argent Dawn specialists attempts to contain the horrors lingering along the King's Road and gather the intelligence necessary to reclaim Lordaeron's fallen fields. Lacking the standing armies required for a full-scale assault on Andorhal or the necromantic fortress of Scholomance, Chillwind Camp relies heavily on independent champions and mercenaries. Operations here focus on surgical guerilla strikes: gathering field intelligence on the Cult of the Damned, neutralizing the alchemical plague cauldrons poisoning the soil, and keeping supply corridors open to eastern outposts like Light's Hope Chapel. With the Scourge occupying the ruins just to the north and the Forsaken fortifying the western border, the camp's defenders fight simply to keep the pass from being overwhelmed.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_chillwind-camp-5988d707848b29d2c454/1-0000.mp3", duration = 91.9771429, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:chillwind-point"] = {
  hash = "92d9c1",
  fullHash = "92d9c1",
  text = "Chillwind Point. Cold drafts sweep through Chillwind Point, a rocky rise above the mountain pass. The ancient troll ruin sits where Hillsbrad Foothills, the Alterac Mountains, and the Western Plaguelands meet, and Bath'rah the Windwatcher lives among its stones. Chillwind Point overlooks the ravine from Hillsbrad's edge. The pass leads into the Western Plaguelands, while the Alterac Mountains rise nearby. Long before Lordaeron claimed these routes, ancient trolls built stone structures on the heights. Their weather-beaten remains still face the elements. Cold winds now blow south through the ravine, carrying the chill of the fallen north into the foothills below. Bath'rah the Windwatcher lives among the old stones at Chillwind Point. The jungle troll hermit communes with the violent gales that course through the gap. Despite the bleak exposure and the nearby Scourge-ravaged lands, his camp is not entirely deserted. Travelers and shamans heading north often seek him out to learn the secrets of the mountain currents.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_chillwind-point-fde2fe9f476b05653970/1-0000.mp3", duration = 63.0334694, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:circle-of-aquementas"] = {
  hash = "9e4e08",
  fullHash = "9e4e08",
  text = "Circle of Aquementas. The Circle of Aquementas is a mysterious ring of upright standing stones positioned along the eastern shoreline of Tanaris, just south of Lost Rigger Cove. It serves as a ritual site where travelers can call forth the elemental spirit Aquementas. Tucked away along the windswept eastern coast of Tanaris, the Circle of Aquementas sits quietly south of the pirate stronghold at Lost Rigger Cove. Composed of weathered, upright stones arranged in a circular formation, this unusual site hums with elemental energy against the desert shore. It acts as a focus for summoning Aquementas, an elemental entity tied to an ancient artifact. While the surrounding beaches crawl with Southsea brigands and roaming sea creatures, the circle itself remains a solemn pocket of ritual power amidst the dunes.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_circle-of-aquementas-86fbbf4340a6424ced1f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 48.8228571, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:circle-of-east-binding"] = {
  hash = "d6acbb",
  fullHash = "d6acbb",
  text = "Circle of East Binding. The Circle of East Binding is a mysterious ancient ritual site situated in the eastern reaches of the Arathi Highlands, just southwest of Hammerfall. Anchored by the Stone of East Binding, the site is violently contested and guarded by roaming water elementals known as cresting exiles. Positioned amidst the rugged highlands east of Stromgarde, the Circle of East Binding is one of several ancient sites of power scattered across the Arathi basin. At its focal point stands the Stone of East Binding, an enigmatic monument radiating potent elemental magic. Swirling water elementals known as cresting exiles roam the surrounding earth and fiercely defend the stone against any mortals who dare approach. The stones throughout Arathi are not mere natural anomalies; they function as ancient binding wards intended to suppress and imprison volatile elemental forces. The restless water spirits surrounding the eastern circle betray the strain on these wards, as the entities drawn to or trapped by the stone's magic lash out at travelers and adventurers navigating the nearby road to Hammerfall.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_circle-of-east-binding-72c9f829d61273b1c95e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 67.7093878, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:circle-of-inner-binding"] = {
  hash = "40351e",
  fullHash = "40351e",
  text = "Circle of Inner Binding. The Circle of Inner Binding is an ancient ring of ritual stones situated in the southern hills of the Arathi Highlands, just east of Stromgarde Keep. Guarded by hostile earth elementals, it holds the Stone of Inner Binding, the most potent seal used to restrain the imprisoned elemental princess Myzrael deep beneath the earth. Perched upon a plateau south of the main road, the Circle of Inner Binding anchors the strongest of the ancient wards sealing Myzrael beneath Arathi. Towering slabs surround the central Stone of Inner Binding, which thrums with dense geo-magical power. Enormous rumbling earth elementals patrol the stones relentlessly, drawn to the site's resonance or bound as guardians. Despite these potent wards, cracks in this inner seal permit the trapped entity beneath the Highlands to reach out telepathically, whispering pleas for freedom to unsuspecting wanderers who handle shards of its stone.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_circle-of-inner-binding-001aa112367ca90571d9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 55.1183673, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:circle-of-west-binding"] = {
  hash = "87eded",
  fullHash = "87eded",
  text = "Circle of West Binding. The Circle of West Binding is one of several ancient ritual sites scattered across the Arathi Highlands, situated near Thoradin's Wall. Centered around the Stone of West Binding, this arcane ring is overrun by fiery elemental entities bound to the site. Tucked against the rugged slopes near Thoradin's Wall, the Circle of West Binding forms part of a wider network of ritual sites dispersed throughout the Arathi Highlands. At its heart sits the Stone of West Binding, an ancient relic tied to elemental containment. The grounds are swarming with Burning Exiles—volatile fire elementals that relentlessly guard the perimeter, making any approach to the stone a perilous endeavor for travelers traversing the western plains.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_circle-of-west-binding-2d66ae1fae1a0c2189b1/1-0000.mp3", duration = 43.1542857, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:clockwerk-run"] = {
  hash = "f2ca20",
  fullHash = "f2ca20",
  text = "Clockwerk Run. In Gnomeregan, Clockwerk Run lies between the Train Depot and the Hall of Gears. Toxic fallout and irradiated troggs fill the route into the fallen city. Clockwerk Run runs from the outer Train Depot into Gnomeregan. Once, gnomish traffic and supplies moved through this corridor. High Tinker Mekkatorque ordered radioactive waste vented to stop the trogg invasion, turning the passage into a deadly trap. The blast and the fighting that followed brought parts of the hallway down. Toxic fumes and irradiated troggs now block the way to the upper Hall of Gears.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_clockwerk-run-025efd224df8e48517ca/1-0000.mp3", duration = 35.1346939, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:corin-s-crossing"] = {
  hash = "975dd3",
  fullHash = "975dd3",
  text = "Corin's Crossing. Corin's Crossing is a ruined human settlement situated at a major crossroads in south-central Eastern Plaguelands. Once a thriving waystation between Stratholme, Tyr's Hand, Darrowshire, and Andorhal, the devastated town is now utterly overrun by the Scourge. Its crumbling town hall and forge harbor lingering horrors, including invisible spectral watchers. Before the Third War, Corin's Crossing served as a central junction for travel across eastern Lordaeron. Positioned between the shimmering waters of Lake Mereldar and what has now become the blighted chasm of the Infectis Scar, its thoroughfares once bore merchants, couriers, and pilgrims traveling between the great bastion of Stratholme and the southern strongholds. When the plague swept through the province, the settlement stood directly in the path of destruction, collapsing swiftly beneath the sheer weight of the undead vanguard. Today, Corin's Crossing is thoroughly lost to the Scourge. Wandering undead pack the muddy streets, while rotting hulks and ghouls linger inside the hollowed-out blacksmith shop and town hall. The town is notably treacherous for scouts: spectral Unseen Servants drift among the ruins completely invisible to ordinary eyes, piercing mundane stealth and ambushing anyone attempting to slip past the main horde undetected.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_corin-s-crossing-5a3646f98d367d7fba37/1-0000.mp3", duration = 81.0057143, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:corrahn-s-dagger"] = {
  hash = "9f3a7c",
  fullHash = "9f3a7c",
  text = "Corrahn's Dagger. Syndicate cutthroats hold Corrahn's Dagger, the second ridge among northern Hillsbrad's four rocky fingers. They use its high ground as a hideout. Corrahn's Dagger rises in northern Hillsbrad Foothills as the second of four rocky ridges called the fingers of Hillsbrad. Syndicate cutthroats overrun the ridge and use its height to watch the valley below.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_corrahn-s-dagger-1a309ea389bd469cc7ee/1-0000.mp3", duration = 23.3273469, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:crown-guard-tower"] = {
  hash = "847c87",
  fullHash = "847c87",
  text = "Crown Guard Tower. Crown Guard Tower is a ruined Lordaeron scout tower in the southern Eastern Plaguelands. The Scourge destroyed it during the invasion of Lordaeron in the Third War, and its broken stones have stood amid the blight ever since. Once part of the Kingdom of Lordaeron's defenses, Crown Guard Tower was a scout tower watching over the lands of eastern Lordaeron. When the Scourge overran the kingdom, the tower was destroyed and left in ruins, one of several fallen towers scattered across what are now the Eastern Plaguelands. Its charred stones stand in a land the undead still hold.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_crown-guard-tower-e824c04b5b11a3bbb30a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 35.1608163, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:crusader-s-armory"] = {
  hash = "8352bd",
  fullHash = "8352bd",
  text = "Crusader's Armory. The Crusader's Armory is a fortified interior hall within the Scarlet Monastery Armory wing. Stockpiled with munitions, gunpowder, and cannonballs, it serves as a staging ground for the zealots guarding the path toward the inner sanctum. Beyond its locked iron door lies the Hall of Champions. Situated between the Footman's Armory and the grand Hall of Champions, the Crusader's Armory displays the full military readiness of the Scarlet Crusade. Unlike the training yards outside, these narrow stone corridors are packed to the ceiling with crates of volatile gunpowder, stacked cannonballs, and heavily armed crusaders ready to repel intruders. The Crusade has turned a former monastic compound into a bristling fortress, dedicating every available hall to the tools of total war. At the far end of the armory stands a heavy locked iron door, barring entry to the inner chambers unless one holds the Scarlet Key.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_crusader-s-armory-5e51429d4260f0d19116/1-0000.mp3", duration = 55.9281633, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:crypt-badlands"] = {
  hash = "ac0f4c",
  fullHash = "ac0f4c",
  text = "Crypt (Badlands). Tucked away high in the dusty hills of the Badlands lies a secluded underground tomb known simply as the Crypt. Inside rests the silent remnant of an ancient warrior: an armored skeleton seated upon a stone throne, flanked by urns, chests, and a massive relic sword. Perched southeast of Hammertoe's Digsite along an unmarked earthen path rising up the hillside, the Badlands Crypt is easily missed by passersby. Down in its dark stone chambers sits an armored skeleton poised atop a throne. Resting beside the remains is the Sword of the Barbarian King, surrounded by scattered bones, urns, and forgotten chests. While miners and adventurers frequent the surrounding gullies in search of dwarven ruins and riches, the true origin and history of this seated sovereign remain a mystery lost to the arid wastes.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_crypt-badlands-c2a9ad9e043f9cbc52bb/1-0000.mp3", duration = 50.4946939, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:crypt-stromgarde-keep"] = {
  hash = "c8399f",
  fullHash = "c8399f",
  text = "Crypt (Stromgarde Keep). Deep within the ruins of Stromgarde Keep lies the royal crypt, situated past the sanctum chapel in the heart of the district held by the surviving Stromgarde defenders. It holds the tomb of the fallen King Thoras Trollbane and the ancestral treasures of his dynasty. Guarded closely by Prince Galen and faithful caretakers, the vault remains a sanctuary of royal honor amid a shattered kingdom. Hidden beneath the Sanctum of Stromgarde Keep, this subterranean burial vault serves as the final resting place of King Thoras Trollbane, the warrior monarch who led Stromgarde through the Second War. Despite the devastation engulfing the city—split among Syndicate rogues and Boulderfist ogres—the remaining Stromgarde militia fiercely maintains their grip on this quarter. Prince Galen Trollbane personally watches over the crypts alongside dedicated royal caretakers, ensuring his father's body and legacy remain undisturbed by the scavengers and enemies outside. Beyond housing the royal dead, the crypt safeguards prized artifacts belonging to the Trollbane lineage. Chief among these relics is Trol'kalar, the ancient runed greatsword forged centuries ago to slay trolls. Entrusted to the sworn protection of caretakers such as Alaric, Nevlin, and Weston, the chamber and its treasures are secured with powerful safeguards. Galen and his loyalists guard the tomb relentlessly, well aware that rival factions and hostile trolls across Arathi would covet the blade's ancient power.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_crypt-stromgarde-keep-ae84b89ff4bfeb7f9f81/1-0000.mp3", duration = 90.7755102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:crystal-shore"] = {
  hash = "9aabef",
  fullHash = "9aabef",
  text = "Crystal Shore. Crystal Shore is a stretch of sandy coastline along the southeastern edge of Stranglethorn Vale, washed by the waves of the South Seas. Tucked beneath steep coastal bluffs, the beach lies just south of the ancient Ruins of Aboraz and across the waters from Jaguero Isle. Running along the southeastern perimeter of Stranglethorn Vale, the Crystal Shore forms a natural boundary between the dense tropical interior and the open waters of the South Seas. The beach itself is walled off from the inland jungles by steep ridges, leaving only a couple of rugged passes for travelers attempting to move between the coast and the deeper wilderness of Mistvale Valley. To the north lie the crumbling troll structures of the Ruins of Aboraz, while the Wild Shore extends along the southern curves of the cape. Its location makes it a quiet, windswept margin far removed from the bustling docks of Booty Bay.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_crystal-shore-66d61c37c1a1b5438029/1-0000.mp3", duration = 52.3493878, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:crystal-vale"] = {
  hash = "8839d8",
  fullHash = "8839d8",
  text = "Crystal Vale. Tucked away in the far northwestern corner of Silithus, the Crystal Vale is a remote canyon dominated by jagged mineral spires and swirling elemental forces. Earth and air elementals roam its rocky flats, guarding rich veins of raw ore. It is also a site of potent summoning magic, deeply tied to the ancient elemental lords. The Crystal Vale stands distinct from the barren desert sands of southern Silithus. Framed by steep cliffs, the basin is filled with shimmering crystalloid formations, roaming earthen elementals, and roaring whirlwinds of living air. Rich deposits of thorium draw occasional daring prospectors, but the vale is far from quiet. The elemental beings here are inherently hostile to intruders, guarding a realm that feels steeped in the primal conflicts of primordial Azeroth.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_crystal-vale-b43e31b02620b15db27c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 53.1330612, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:crystalvein-mine"] = {
  hash = "2b1940",
  fullHash = "2b1940",
  text = "Crystalvein Mine. Crystalvein Mine is a cavern in central Stranglethorn Vale, situated near the ancient ruins of Zul'Mamwe. The cavern is rich in valuable 'Singing Crystals', but aggressive native basilisks have completely overrun the site, turning prospective miners to solid stone. Tucked away in the jungle hills near Zul'Mamwe, Crystalvein Mine boasts rare deposits of Singing Crystals, a unique mineral that fetches a high price in southern trade ports. Enticed by the promise of immense profit, the Venture Company set its sights on extracting the veins. However, the mine's natural inhabitants have proved catastrophic for business. Local basilisks consume the strange crystals, hardening their scales and making them unnaturally lethal. Outside the cave mouth, petrified statues of trolls and humans stand as a silent testament to the Venture Company's failed expeditions.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_crystalvein-mine-4d7bf698c6e9758960aa/1-0000.mp3", duration = 53.5510204, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:dabyrie-s-farmstead"] = {
  hash = "ef1083",
  fullHash = "ef1083",
  text = "Dabyrie's Farmstead. Dabyrie's Farmstead is an Alliance-aligned rural settlement situated in northern Arathi Highlands. In a realm shattered by the Third War and overrun by Syndicate outlaws and Witherbark trolls, this estate remains a stubborn bastion of Stromgarde citizens led by Kenata Dabyrie. Perched in the northern fields of the Arathi Highlands, Dabyrie's Farmstead is one of the very few functioning human homesteads remaining in the fractured kingdom of Stromgarde. Owned by Kenata Dabyrie and worked alongside her sons, Fardel and Marcel, the grounds are fiercely defended by a local family militia. Surrounded by aggressive wildlife, opportunistic bandits, and the nearby Forsaken presence in the north, the farm stands as a symbol of human endurance in a ruined land.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_dabyrie-s-farmstead-5b86ae15b9423b7c0bb4/1-0000.mp3", duration = 47.0465306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:dalaran"] = {
  hash = "3cca85",
  fullHash = "3cca85",
  text = "Dalaran. Dalaran was once the shining magocratic capital of the Kirin Tor, nestled on the southern shore of Lordamere Lake. Shattered by the Scourge and the demon lord Archimonde during the Third War, its surviving magi are rebuilding the city, having removed its protective violet shield early in the reconstruction. Founded after the ancient Troll Wars by sorcerers of the Arathorian Empire, Dalaran stood for centuries as humanity's premier sanctuary for arcane study. Under the leadership of the Council of Six, the Kirin Tor amassed vast magical archives and guarded powerful artifacts within the Violet Citadel. That age of splendor ended abruptly during the Third War. The traitor prince Arthas led the Scourge through the city gates to seize the Book of Medivh, and soon after, the demon lord Archimonde crushed Dalaran's soaring spires into rubble. In the aftermath of the Legion's defeat, the surviving wizards of the Kirin Tor returned to their devastated homeland. Refusing to surrender their ancestral ground to the Scourge or scavengers, they erected an impassable, shimmering violet barrier over the city's heart. The shield has since been removed, though reconstruction continues. The magi continue rebuilding their fallen sanctuary, and Archmage Ansirem Runeweaver can be found in the Violet Citadel.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_dalaran-5efa0fb3d065b9740b7c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 82.5991837, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:dalson-s-tears"] = {
  hash = "87bfcb",
  fullHash = "87bfcb",
  text = "Dalson's Tears. Dalson's Tears is a blighted farmstead located in the heart of the Western Plaguelands. Once known as Dalson's Orchard, the land was overrun during the Third War and converted into a staging ground for one of the Scourge's noxious plague cauldrons. The undead remains of the former owners still haunt the fields and outbuildings. Before the Third War, this estate flourished as Dalson's Orchard, a productive farm in the breadbasket of Lordaeron. Today, the fields are choked with decay and rechristened Dalson's Tears. At the center of the ruined property stands a massive iron cauldron, constantly billowing virulent green plague vapors across the landscape. The device is overseen by Cauldron Lord Malvinious and defended by a pack of blighted zombies, rotting cadavers, and skeletal horrors serving the Lich King.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_dalson-s-tears-5e5c90ae4b84bd026aeb/1-0000.mp3", duration = 50.4163265, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:dandred-s-fold"] = {
  hash = "84f78b",
  fullHash = "84f78b",
  text = "Dandred's Fold. Dandred's Fold is a secluded farmstead and manor tucked along the misty northwestern shores of Lordamere Lake in the Alterac Mountains. Now overrun by the Syndicate, it serves as a secure hideout for Lord Aliden Perenolde and his co-conspirators. Both the Horde and the Alliance keep a watchful eye on this lakefront retreat for very different reasons. Perched on the western edge of the Uplands with private access to the waters of Lordamere Lake, Dandred's Fold was once a tranquil country estate, likely owned by an Alteraci noble named Dandred. In the aftermath of the kingdom's fall, the estate transformed into a fortified enclave for the Syndicate. Syndicate assassins and enforcers now patrol its fences and docks, shielding the fallen nobility from the eyes of Southshore authorities and the Forsaken across the lake.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_dandred-s-fold-c9e3b90b41cbcdbd56f0/1-0000.mp3", duration = 48.3004082, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:dark-portal"] = {
  hash = "917a2c",
  fullHash = "917a2c",
  text = "Dark Portal. The Dark Portal is a monumental stone gateway standing at the southern rim of the Blasted Lands. First torn open through the combined sorcery of Medivh and Gul'dan, it served as the fateful conduit that brought the Old Horde into Azeroth, initiating the First War. Before its opening, this region was part of the Black Morass, a sodden wetland that stretched across the south. That changed when Medivh, possessed by Sargeras, joined his Tirisfal magics with the fel sorcery of Gul'dan on Draenor. The ritual drained untold lives and tore the fabric of reality apart. The Old Horde raised massive stone architecture to anchor the rift, marching armies through its threshold to shatter the Kingdom of Stormwind in the First War. The portal's fel emanations fundamentally corrupted the surrounding terrain, reducing lush marshes to the red, petrified wastes seen today. Following the Alliance of Lordaeron's victory in the Second War, Archmage Khadgar shattered the physical stone frame with powerful magics, hoping to sever Draenor forever. However, the dimensional tear beneath proved functionally indestructible. The Alliance erected Nethergarde Keep nearby to watch the scar, while demons and remnants of the Shadow Council continue to slink through the blasted caldera. Today the gateway stands reassembled yet dormant, a silent scar between worlds that neither side dares abandon.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_dark-portal-5ece336ebc728cf1217b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 85.524898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:darkmist-cavern"] = {
  hash = "9fe71e",
  fullHash = "9fe71e",
  text = "Darkmist Cavern. Darkmist Cavern is an abandoned mine tucked into northwestern Dustwallow Marsh, completely infested with predatory giant spiders. Crumbling ruins outside hint at an unfortunate former settlement swallowed up by the marsh's hazards. Locals remain divided on whether the cavern gave its name to the arachnids or the reverse. Situated in the northwest reaches of Dustwallow Marsh, Darkmist Cavern appears to have once been an active mining venture. Today, any human presence has long vanished, leaving behind rotting, ruined shacks outside the entrance and a network of tunnels choked with thick webs. The cavern is overrun by Darkmist spiders, predatory horrors whose venom and size make the tunnels deadly to unwary wanderers. Marsh dwellers still dispute whether the cavern was named after the foul brood nesting inside or if the spiders took the title from the misty cave itself. Despite the obvious danger, the cavern attracts occasional scavengers and eccentric marshfolk. The hermit 'Swamp Eye' Jarl is notorious for sending travelers deep into the webs to harvest the eyes of these monstrous spiders, insisting they make a fine stock for broth. Aside from such eccentric culinary outings, most sensible travelers steer well clear of the web-strewn entrance.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_darkmist-cavern-36989ed0cdfad290fd55/1-0000.mp3", duration = 77.2179592, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:darkwhisper-gorge"] = {
  hash = "403bdd",
  fullHash = "403bdd",
  text = "Darkwhisper Gorge. Darkwhisper Gorge is a treacherous, demon-infested ravine carved into the southern heights of Winterspring. When Archimonde was destroyed at the climax of the Third War, remnants of the shattered Burning Legion fled down the slopes of Mount Hyjal and entrenched themselves in these jagged chasms. Today, powerful fiends stalk its heights, guarding ancient minerals and dark planar relics. Following the defeat of Archimonde atop Mount Hyjal, surviving demons of the Burning Legion scattered into the surrounding mountain ranges. A disciplined vanguard took refuge in Darkwhisper Gorge, transforming the southern border of Winterspring into a lethal stronghold. Led by Hederine demons—including lethal manastalkers, slayers, and initiates under Lady Hederine—these fiends fiercely defend their subterranean tunnels and fiery camps. Beyond the immediate threat of its elite inhabitants, an ominous, sealed archway stands at the gorge's lowest point, hinting at sealed passages leading higher up the sacred mountain. Despite the overwhelming danger, brave adventurers and opportunists brave the gorge for its exceptional wealth. The ravines are rich in valuable minerals like truesilver and dense deposits of thorium. Deeper within its shadowed grottos, unusual inhabitants eke out an existence, such as the imp vendor Vi'el peddling fel-tainted reagents like felcloth and gromsblood, and the elusive high elf Earthcaller Franzahl, who possesses ancient metallurgical lore regarding the smelting of rare elementium.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_darkwhisper-gorge-976f8c355b2030ace9e1/1-0000.mp3", duration = 92.1338776, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:darnassus"] = {
  hash = "a40435",
  fullHash = "a40435",
  text = "Darnassus. Darnassus rises among Teldrassil’s high boughs as the kaldorei capital and refuge. Its tranquil waters and groves make it the night elves’ spiritual heart and the Alliance’s primary bastion on northern Kalimdor. High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind and Archdruid Fandral Staghelm guide their people through the trials of newfound mortality. Darnassus took shape after the Third War, honoring the night elves’ survival after they lost the immortality granted by their sacred World Tree, Nordrassil. Unlike the stone cities of Ironforge and Stormwind, it is an open, tranquil settlement of wood-crafted lodges, glowing shrines, and arched bridges over crystalline lakes. Vigilant Sentinels, towering ancients of war, and druids who shape trees watch the paths and preserve nature’s quiet within the city. Darnassus speaks of kaldorei unity, though its leadership is divided between different duties. High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind handles civil and military matters from the radiant Temple of the Moon, with the Sisters of Elune around her. Archdruid Fandral Staghelm leads the Cenarion Circle’s affairs and championed planting Teldrassil to restore the elves’ lost immortality. Malfurion Stormrage is lost in a mysterious slumber deep within the Emerald Dream, and Staghelm’s aggressive vision for the future often clashes with the more patient priesthood.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_darnassus-026b245be804b67852b9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 82.7559184, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:darrow-hill"] = {
  hash = "bd85b4",
  fullHash = "bd85b4",
  text = "Darrow Hill. In northern Hillsbrad, Darrow Hill's rugged grassland rises beneath the Headland. Mountain lions and stags roam its open slopes, while feral yetis lurk in the Foothill Caverns. Alterac Granite draws travelers into this dangerous frontier. Darrow Hill stretches below the Headland, joining southern Hillsbrad's rolling pasture to Alterac's jagged, colder heights. Mountain lions and stags move through tall grasses on its rocky slopes, and towering yetis emerge from the Foothill Caverns. The cavern walls hold deposits of dense Alterac Granite, drawing quarry workers, miners, and adventurers into danger. Regional claims remain contentious, and travelers of either faction can be caught unaware on the trails.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_darrow-hill-d085e4751c85b037f83e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 43.7028571, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:darrowmere-lake"] = {
  hash = "2d0414",
  fullHash = "2d0414",
  text = "Darrowmere Lake. Darrowmere Lake is an expansive freshwater lake situated in the southeastern reaches of the Western Plaguelands. At its heart lies the fortress island of Caer Darrow, whose ruined halls conceal the Cult of the Damned's dread necromantic academy, Scholomance. Darrowmere Lake is among the largest inland bodies of water in the Eastern Kingdoms, bordered by the blight-stricken forests of Lordaeron and fed by the Thondroril and Darrowmere rivers. During the Second War, these deep waters saw intense naval skirmishes between the Horde and Grand Admiral Daelin Proudmoore's fleet. The Horde also struck the island fortress of Caer Darrow at the lake's heart, sacking it and carrying off its ancient runestone. Once a thriving waterway and trade artery for the House of Barov, the lake is now a grim, mist-veiled basin in the heart of the Plaguelands. Dominating the center of the lake is Caer Darrow, once the proud ancestral seat of the Barov family. Beneath its broken foundations lies Scholomance, the Scourge's primary academy for necromancy and dark arts. The waters surrounding the island are stagnant and fraught with danger, reflecting the dark legacy of the Cult of the Damned. Adventurers venturing across its surface must brave not only the perils of the blighted countryside but the pervasive dread cast by the fortress at its core.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_darrowmere-lake-ce0a01622b95882a660a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 80.9273469, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:darrowshire"] = {
  hash = "a11d24",
  fullHash = "a11d24",
  text = "Darrowshire. Darrowshire is a ruined village in the southern hills of the Eastern Plaguelands, once a peaceful hamlet of Lordaeron. During the Third War, its residents mounted a desperate, tragic stand against an overwhelming Scourge force. Today, only shattered timbers, restless spirits, and bitter memories linger amid the silence. Before the plague consumed northern Lordaeron, Darrowshire was a humble rural settlement nestled beneath the southern cliffs. When the Scourge marched south during the Third War, a force led by the death knight Marduk Blackpool and the ghoul lord Horgus the Ravager besieged the town. The villagers and local defenders fought valiantly, momentarily holding the line against impossible odds. Ultimately, the defense collapsed, and the town was slaughtered down to the last soul. Rather than finding peace in death, many of Darrowshire's fallen defenders were reanimated as ghouls to bolster the Scourge's ranks, leaving the village an eerie, empty memorial of lost valor. Though long abandoned by the living, Darrowshire is far from completely empty. The psychic weight of the slaughter clings heavily to the valley, trapping spectral remnants of the townspeople within the ruins. Travelers passing through report strange phenomena, including chilling reenactments where the tragic battle unfolds again in ghostly silence. The ground remains saturated with sorrow, serving as a bleak reminder of what the Scourge tore away from the kingdom.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_darrowshire-befbce841012c1e4fd9e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 89.1036735, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:deadman-s-crossing"] = {
  hash = "c6e2b6",
  fullHash = "c6e2b6",
  text = "Deadman's Crossing. Deadman's Crossing is an ominous junction set within the bleak canyon of Deadwind Pass. Branching between Duskwood, the Swamp of Sorrows, and the haunted tower of Karazhan, it serves as a grim threshold marked by gallows and warning signs. Despite its gruesome appearance, the path remains a vital thoroughfare for travelers moving across the southern Eastern Kingdoms. Situated at the eastern crossroads of Deadwind Pass, Deadman's Crossing links the shadows of Duskwood with the murky depths of the Swamp of Sorrows, while a foreboding southern track ascends toward the haunted tower of Karazhan. The crossing takes its name from the twisted hangman's tree planted at the road's fork, where a pair of rotting corpses dangle overhead as a stark warning to all who pass. Beside the tree, battered wooden signs offer blunt advice to travelers, urging them to turn back and abandon hope. The canyon itself is thick with hostile beasts and dark sorceries, yet travelers moving steadfastly along the packed dirt road will find the crossing itself oddly unmolested, so long as they do not wander into the blighted rocks.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_deadman-s-crossing-437aaa5e3d8a1f8175a0/1-0000.mp3", duration = 67.5265306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:deadmines"] = {
  hash = "050b2c",
  fullHash = "050b2c",
  text = "Deadmines. Beneath Moonbrook, the Deadmines lie silent under a ruined town in Westfall. Once the greatest gold producer in human lands, the mines were abandoned during the First War. Now rumors speak of sinister forces in the forgotten shafts, though their danger remains unknown. The Deadmines once led all human mines in gold, with Whitestone oak for timber and supports. When the orcish Horde pushed north, miners fled in haste. Sir Anduin Lothar entered the treacherous depths to recover the sacred Tome of Divinity. Subterranean ogres held him captive for twenty months before rescuers freed him. After the kingdom fell, the shafts went quiet, and people came to say they were cursed.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_deadmines-24ef2b4266fe91b472a0/1-0000.mp3", duration = 44.2514286, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:deadwood-village"] = {
  hash = "942416",
  fullHash = "942416",
  text = "Deadwood Village. Deadwood Village is a furbolg encampment nestled in southern Felwood. Once a peaceful clan attuned to nature, its residents fell victim to the demonic corruption that blighted the surrounding forest. Driven mad by fel taint, the Deadwood furbolgs now attack anyone who approaches their crude wooden settlement. Situated near the southern border of Felwood, Deadwood Village serves as the primary enclave of the Deadwood tribe. When the Burning Legion's demonic energies washed through the ancient woodlands, the furbolgs lacked the power to resist the creeping rot. Their minds warped by fel malice, they abandoned their ancient traditions and spiritual harmony. Today, the village is a squalid gathering of hostile brutes, shamans, and den watchers who violently defend their polluted grounds against intruders, earning the despair of their uncorrupted cousins, the Timbermaw.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_deadwood-village-8b101fc713015d354bcc/1-0000.mp3", duration = 53.7861224, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:den-of-flame"] = {
  hash = "ae3bae",
  fullHash = "ae3bae",
  text = "Den of Flame. The Den of Flame is a fortified cavern in western Dustwallow Marsh, situated just west of the Stonemaul Ruins. Originally constructed by the Stonemaul ogres as their mound, it has since been overrun by the dragonkin of the black dragonflight serving Onyxia. Built by the Stonemaul ogres to serve as their main mound and sanctuary, the Den of Flame bears the unmistakable, rough-hewn stone architecture of ogre masonry. However, when the black dragonflight laid waste to the Stonemaul clan's settlement, the brood did not merely scatter the ogres—they seized the stronghold for themselves. Today, black dragonspawn patrol the burning perimeter and cavern entrance under the shadow of Onyxia's nearby lair.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_den-of-flame-25df39cda581ea63c2aa/1-0000.mp3", duration = 43.3371429, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:dire-maul"] = {
  hash = "bc95e8",
  fullHash = "bc95e8",
  text = "Dire Maul. Dire Maul is the ruined expanse of Eldre'Thalas, an ancient Highborne city hidden deep in the wilderness of Feralas. Once built to safeguard Queen Azshara's arcane secrets, its crumbling plazas and towering coliseums are now divided between occupying Gordok ogres, invading satyrs, and the withered remnants of its original elven caretakers. Twelve thousand years ago, Eldre'Thalas served as a wondrous bastion for Queen Azshara's favored sorcerers, the Shen'dralar. When the Well of Eternity collapsed during the Great Sundering, Prince Tortheldrin wove a magical barrier that shielded the city from ruin. Severed from their source of magic, the surviving Highborne trapped a colossal demon named Immol'thar beneath their sanctum, siphoning its fel essence to sustain their immortality and arcane powers for millennia.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_dire-maul-b491242bfe7cab4f50f3/1-0000.mp3", duration = 51.1216327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:direforge-hill"] = {
  hash = "1db822",
  fullHash = "1db822",
  text = "Direforge Hill. Hostile Dark Iron dwarves hold Direforge Hill in the northeastern Wetlands, southeast of Dun Modr and northeast of the Green Belt. Dark Iron dwarves hold the northeastern Wetlands hill under Balgaras the Foul, a Dark Iron dwarf warlock. Their encampment lies southeast of Dun Modr. Travelers crossing the northeastern Wetlands face the hostile Dark Iron encampment on Direforge Hill. Balgaras the Foul commands the dwarves there.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_direforge-hill-76f1bebb4de9d928ab10/1-0000.mp3", duration = 26.8277551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:dragonmaw-gates"] = {
  hash = "b69e49",
  fullHash = "b69e49",
  text = "Dragonmaw Gates. At the Dragonmaw Gates, heavy fortifications bar the road climbing from the eastern Wetlands. They guard the ascent to Grim Batol, a mountain fortress in the cursed dwarven ruins. Carved into rugged peaks along the eastern Wetlands, the Dragonmaw Gates seal the winding road to Grim Batol. The barriers take their name from the Dragonmaw orcs, who held the dwarven mountain city during the Second War. The road remains a fortified checkpoint, and the ascent still brings danger. Powerful drakes haunt the pass, while grim reminders of the clan's dark history linger in the mountains. The Dragonmaw orcs were broken and scattered after the Demon Soul was destroyed, but the mountain path remains dangerous. Powerful red dragons have been seen watching the winding slopes and perches around the gates. Monstrous drakes and Dragonmaw remnants still lurk in the nearby hills, and the gates remain a firm barrier against travelers bold enough to venture into the heights.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_dragonmaw-gates-9869266fce619fc4023a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 58.3314286, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:dragonmurk"] = {
  hash = "6b53c0",
  fullHash = "6b53c0",
  text = "Dragonmurk. Dragonmurk is a sprawling, hazardous tract of southern Dustwallow Marsh swarming with black dragonspawn and hatchlings. Flanked by ancient ogre ruins and scorched caverns, it serves as the hostile outer perimeter for the brood of Onyxia. Spanning the southern interior of Dustwallow Marsh, the Dragonmurk marks the threshold where damp wetlands give way to the dominion of the black dragonflight. The waterways and thick mud here crawl with black wyrmkins, dragonspawn, and ravenous whelps. The surrounding landscape has suffered deeply from their presence; adjacent territories such as the ruined village of Stonemaul and the scorched cavern known as the Den of Flame have both fallen completely under the flight's territorial grip.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_dragonmurk-f551528a4467a1a2b8f9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 45.7665306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:dreadmaul-post"] = {
  hash = "7a560f",
  fullHash = "7a560f",
  text = "Dreadmaul Post. Dreadmaul Post is an entrenched ogre settlement situated in the scorched wastes of the Blasted Lands. Established by the Dreadmaul clan, this mound serves as a fortified redoubt near the Dark Portal where ogres have lingered since their arrival from Draenor. Travelers passing through the region must contend with these fierce brutes and the treacherous terrain surrounding their encampment. When the Horde surged through the Dark Portal in decades past, clans of ogres marched alongside them into Azeroth. The Dreadmaul clan chose to dig in near the gateway itself, raising crude stone mounds and earthworks across the red wastes of the Blasted Lands. Dreadmaul Post stands as one of their primary outposts, maintaining a firm grip over the badlands and clashing with wandering beasts and any travelers foolish enough to draw near their territory.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_dreadmaul-post-08571312a29bd8e0ed6e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 52.6628571, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:dreadmaul-rock"] = {
  hash = "e7f301",
  fullHash = "e7f301",
  text = "Dreadmaul Rock. Dreadmaul Rock is a towering crag and cave complex rising out of the fiery wastes of the Burning Steppes. It serves as the primary base of operations for the Firegut ogres, who make their home within its winding, subterranean mounds. Towering above the scorched plains of the Burning Steppes, Dreadmaul Rock is a massive stone formation carved out by a labyrinthine cave system. Though its name hints at a possible connection to the Dreadmaul ogres of the Blasted Lands, the rock is held firmly by the Firegut clan. These brutish ogres use the elevated paths, crags, and interior earthen mounds as a defended encampment, dominating the surrounding wasteland and menacing anyone attempting to traverse the northern slopes toward Blackrock Spire.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_dreadmaul-rock-e67eb87201657dae0570/1-0000.mp3", duration = 46.3934694, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:dreadmurk-shore"] = {
  hash = "82c2c9",
  fullHash = "82c2c9",
  text = "Dreadmurk Shore. Dreadmurk Shore encompasses the foggy coastline and scattered islands of Dustwallow Marsh running north toward the Barrens. Dominated by aggressive Mirefin murlocs and sea beasts, these treacherous waters have recently become the hiding ground for shipwrecked Defias Brotherhood outlaws. Stretching along the northeastern edge of Dustwallow Marsh between Theramore Isle and Northwatch Hold, Dreadmurk Shore is a dismal expanse of muddy shoals, tide pools, and misty islands. The coast is overrun by the Mirefin tribe, a territorial clan of murlocs that aggressively attacks any travelers nearing their coastal villages. Sea turtles and aquatic predators prowl the shallows, making the shoreline dangerous to navigate even without factoring in the surrounding swamplands.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_dreadmurk-shore-185b2e9add5cfb00c818/1-0000.mp3", duration = 45.9755102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:dream-bough"] = {
  hash = "dbae5b",
  fullHash = "dbae5b",
  text = "Dream Bough. Dream Bough is a colossal Great Tree nestled on an island in the center of Jademir Lake in northern Feralas. The ancient tree shelters a mysterious gateway connecting Azeroth directly to the Emerald Dream. Green dragons loyally watch over the sanctuary, keeping unwanted intruders away from its verdant boughs. Rising high above the canopy of northern Feralas, the Dream Bough stands as the largest tree in the region. Situated in the tranquil waters of Jademir Lake, the towering sentinel houses a natural portal carved into its trunk, providing a direct threshold into the untamed Emerald Dream. Because of its connection to the Emerald Dream, the tree is under the vigilant protection of the Green Dragonflight. Dragonkin and drakes patrol the banks of Jademir Lake and the approaches to the great trunk. While primarily a place of contemplation and slumber for druids wandering between realms, travelers should approach with care, as the green dragons tolerate few uninvited visitors near such an important threshold.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_dream-bough-6549b49df7778fb04ef0/1-0000.mp3", duration = 61.675102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:drowned-reef"] = {
  hash = "26fe25",
  fullHash = "26fe25",
  text = "Drowned Reef. The Drowned Reef is a treacherous stretch of coastal waters off the southern cliffs of the Arathi Highlands. Beneath the waves lie ancient sunken ruins, claimed by hostile Daggerspine naga who appear able to control the bay's tides, endangering passing vessels. Just outside the hidden entrance to Faldir's Cove, the Drowned Reef forms a watery graveyard for navigators. Shattered ships, including the Maiden's Folly and the Spirit of Silverpine, lie submerged beneath the waves. Daggerspine naga led by Prince Nazjak inhabit the reef and appear able to control the bay's tides, dooming crews seeking refuge in the cove. Those brave or foolish enough to dive into the deep water will discover the submerged architecture of an ancient elven city. Calcified elven gems remain among its drowned ruins.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_drowned-reef-56280c2aa82364650e48/1-0000.mp3", duration = 49.6587755, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:drowned-sacellum"] = {
  hash = "ef0095",
  fullHash = "ef0095",
  text = "Drowned Sacellum. At Blackfathom Deeps' entrance, the Drowned Sacellum lies submerged beneath the coast. Brackish saltwater and cavern roots surround its broken stones, now occupied by murlocs and makrura. You enter the Drowned Sacellum just beyond Blackfathom Deeps' threshold. The chapel once offered quiet worship to Elune, but it has long since collapsed into the sea-swept abyss beneath Ashenvale's coast. Brackish pools swallow its ancient marble flagstones, where starfish cling beneath the cavern ceiling's massive roots. Dark wisps flicker dimly through the gloom, lighting a broken stone path. Careful steps along it lead deeper into the temple complex, toward the Pool of Ask'ar. Night elf caretakers left the Drowned Sacellum long ago, and shoreline scavengers now hold the ruins. Packs of aggressive murlocs and heavily armored makrura share the murky waters and attack anyone who disturbs their nesting grounds. Darker cultic threats lie deeper in the temple, but these predators guard the first stretch of the underground ruins.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_drowned-sacellum-66a07ff9ded99b48e921/1-0000.mp3", duration = 66.2465306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:drywhisker-gorge"] = {
  hash = "909f0e",
  fullHash = "909f0e",
  text = "Drywhisker Gorge. Drywhisker Gorge is a rugged cleft in the eastern mountains of the Arathi Highlands, situated northeast of the Horde outpost of Hammerfall. The gorge and its inner cavern are held by a hostile clan of kobolds, who fiercely guard rich mineral deposits and ancient elemental relics buried deep underground. Tucked away in the mountainous folds east of Hammerfall, Drywhisker Gorge serves as the primary redoubt of the Drywhisker clan of kobolds. While kobolds are often dismissed as simple mine pests, the Drywhiskers in Arathi are heavily entrenched. They guard their subterranean passages with fierce territorial tenacity, working veins of rare Lesser Bloodstone Ore and defending the deep recesses of the earth. Their proximity makes them a persistent nuisance to the nearby orcs of Hammerfall.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_drywhisker-gorge-4daf94014c7f35e82cf4/1-0000.mp3", duration = 47.2293878, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:dun-mandarr"] = {
  hash = "74ee19",
  fullHash = "74ee19",
  text = "Dun Mandarr. Dun Mandarr is an abandoned dwarven camp situated before an icy cavern in south-eastern Winterspring. Established by the dwarf Jaron Stoneshaper, the site now sits in ruins after its occupants fled an onslaught of enraged local moonkin. Bearing the traditional dwarven prefix 'Dun', Dun Mandarr was established by the explorer Jaron Stoneshaper as a base of operations in the frozen reaches of southeastern Winterspring. Stoneshaper's curiosity proved his undoing when he uncovered a relic known as the Blue-Feathered Amulet. The artifact's disturbance provoked the fury of the native wildkin, who descended upon the encampment in an uncontrollable rage. Overwhelmed and driven out, the dwarves abandoned the camp entirely. Today, no friendly souls remain among the snowbanks; ferocious, territorial moonkin guard the cavern and prowl the broken shelters.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_dun-mandarr-522c66d463a357c0db4c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 51.1216327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:dunemaul-compound"] = {
  hash = "087214",
  fullHash = "087214",
  text = "Dunemaul Compound. Dunemaul Compound serves as the central settlement and ogre mound for the Dunemaul clan in Tanaris. Tucked into the rocky ridges bordering the desert wastes, it houses the clan's brute enforcers and spellcasters. Carved into the rugged desert rock of western Tanaris, the Dunemaul Compound is the primary stronghold of the Dunemaul ogres. These hulking desert dwellers have adapted to the harsh arid climate, scratching out an existence through brute force and opportunism. The compound itself centers on an ogre mound and cave network. Though primitive, the clan fields a dangerous mix of warriors, enforcers, and twisted spellcasters practicing dark warlock and mage magics. For travelers journeying across the dunes, the settlement is a treacherous den of aggressive giants who violently reject uninvited guests.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_dunemaul-compound-fd87179a7759aa172827/1-0000.mp3", duration = 50.0767347, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:dustbowl"] = {
  hash = "7c9fec",
  fullHash = "7c9fec",
  text = "Dustbowl. The Dustbowl is a sprawling, arid expanse in the northwestern Badlands, lying between the Horde outpost of Kargath and the central Valley of Fangs. Swept by scouring desert winds, it is a harsh wilderness populated by scavengers, prowling beasts, and wandering elementals. It is also the site of Camp Wurg, making it a frequent crossing point for travelers braving the wasteland. Stretching across the northwest of the Badlands, the Dustbowl is characterized by dry gulches, cracked clay, and relentless winds that stir up choking plumes of grit. Despite the barren landscape, tough desert flora persists in the form of hardy cacti and scattered, gnarled trees. The open flatland presents constant danger to unguided wayfarers, who must contend with predatory coyotes, circling buzzards, and living rock elementals born of the rugged earth. Tucked within this scrubland lies Camp Wurg, providing a recognizable landmark amidst the otherwise desolate expanse.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_dustbowl-47c4880bcbbde636cb26/1-0000.mp3", duration = 61.3877551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:dustfire-valley"] = {
  hash = "4ee8de",
  fullHash = "4ee8de",
  text = "Dustfire Valley. Dustfire Valley is a dry, windswept gulch nestled along the northern edge of the Searing Gorge. It serves as the scorched threshold leading directly to the fortified gate of Stonewrought Pass, which bars the mountain route into Loch Modan. Tucked away in northern Searing Gorge, Dustfire Valley is defined by choking ash, brittle rock, and barren canyon walls. Its most notable feature is the imposing, locked dwarven gateway sealing Stonewrought Pass. Because of these locked iron doors, direct travel north into the Valley of Kings and Loch Modan remains entirely barred to ordinary wanderers, leaving the valley an isolated cul-de-sac amidst the sulfurous wastes.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_dustfire-valley-5281261cc5ae7c483640/1-0000.mp3", duration = 40.6465306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:dustwallow-bay"] = {
  hash = "d61abe",
  fullHash = "d61abe",
  text = "Dustwallow Bay. Dustwallow Bay is an inlet of the Great Sea tucked between the sodden coast of Dustwallow Marsh and the fortified port of Theramore Isle. Its brackish coastal shallows teem with marine life, treacherous shoals, and secluded coves just off the main maritime routes of eastern Kalimdor. Dustwallow Bay defines the coastal boundary where the stagnant, murky wetlands of Dustwallow Marsh meet the open waters of the Great Sea. Flanked to the east by Theramore Isle, the bay serves as an oceanic approach for vessels arriving at Lady Jaina Proudmoore's island haven. Despite heavy maritime traffic running to and from the docks, the surrounding coastline remains wild and hazardous, pocked with rocky outcrops, murloc encampments, and tucked-away inlets like Tidefury Cove. While the marsh itself is infested with crocolisks and hostile beasts, the bay's islands and inlets draw those who seek isolation and rich catches. The small islets dotting the waters near Tidefury Cove provide a secluded retreat for anglers, most notably the legendary fisherman Nat Pagle, who can often be found angling off the rocks away from the bustling city of Theramore.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_dustwallow-bay-7ee02d0e0daec1d421e9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 70.3216327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:eastern-strand"] = {
  hash = "4e21e8",
  fullHash = "4e21e8",
  text = "Eastern Strand. Daggerspine naga hold the Eastern Strand along Hillsbrad's southeastern coast. The beaches and shallows reach toward the Arathi Highlands border. The Daggerspine naga hold the Eastern Strand on Hillsbrad's southeastern edge. Across the bay lies the Western Strand. Southshore and Tarren Mill clash over the region's human farms, while the naga dominate these sandy flats and tidelines. They come from the Great Sea, crossing the sandbars and settling among rocky outcrops. From there, they watch the mainland.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_eastern-strand-f5806e7af6da18da32d2/1-0000.mp3", duration = 30.9028571, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:eastmoon-ruins"] = {
  hash = "ec20ff",
  fullHash = "ec20ff",
  text = "Eastmoon Ruins. The Eastmoon Ruins are an ancient troll archaeological site situated in eastern Tanaris, perched just west of the Gaping Chasm. Once built by the desert empires of old, the crumbling stone masonry is now occupied by Dunemaul ogres using the remnants as a desert encampment. Rising out of the blistering desert floor just west of the insect-infested Gaping Chasm, the Eastmoon Ruins preserve the crumbling architecture of ancient troll civilization. Decaying pillars, weathered stone plazas, and sun-bleached monuments hint at a time long before the sands swallowed this region. Rather than scholars or troll descendants tending the shrines, the site is firmly held by the Dunemaul ogres, who rely on the broken walls for shelter and vantage over nearby trade routes.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_eastmoon-ruins-8624e05e1e9d1573c606/1-0000.mp3", duration = 46.3673469, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:eastwall-gate"] = {
  hash = "f96180",
  fullHash = "f96180",
  text = "Eastwall Gate. Eastwall Gate is a fortified side entrance set into the perimeter wall of the fallen city of Stratholme. Located in the northern reaches of the Eastern Plaguelands, it offers a direct secondary access point into Stratholme's interior ruins, bypassing the chaos of the city's main gates. While the grand main entrance of Stratholme opens directly onto King's Square, the Eastwall Gate pierces the city's eastern fortifications. Barred securely against intruders, this gateway leads straight into Elders' Square, close by the steps of Alonsus Chapel. For those venturing into the burning heart of the Scourge stronghold, bypassing the front barricades can mean the difference between a swift strike into the city's depths and a meatgrinder assault through the forward undead lines. Gaining entry through the Eastwall Gate is no simple matter. It is sealed tightly by heavy ironwork that requires the Key to the City, recovered from inside Stratholme itself, or an exceptionally skilled infiltrator capable of picking masterwork locks. Once past the threshold, a heavy mechanical lever mounted on the inner wall allows travelers to raise the portcullis from the inside, opening a reliable path for allies to follow.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_eastwall-gate-7e2d5b64612adb5996ca/1-0000.mp3", duration = 70.3738776, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:eastwall-tower"] = {
  hash = "c4f8a5",
  fullHash = "c4f8a5",
  text = "Eastwall Tower. Eastwall Tower is a ruined Lordaeron scout tower situated in the Eastern Plaguelands near the eastern road. Sacked during the Third War when the Scourge swept across the kingdom, its remains overlook the Scourge-infested wilderness. Built as a border sentinel for the kingdom of Lordaeron, Eastwall Tower once watched over travelers heading toward the far reaches of the realm. When the Scourge invaded during the Third War, the outpost was crushed along with the surrounding settlements, leaving shattered stone arches and burnt timber behind. Now, with the Lich King's armies still infesting the region, the tower's elevated position makes it an indispensable forward post. Its ruined ramparts overlook roads through the Scourge-infested countryside.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_eastwall-tower-dc98114c9b357fc33a02/1-0000.mp3", duration = 44.6171429, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:eldreth-row"] = {
  hash = "d10a08",
  fullHash = "d10a08",
  text = "Eldreth Row. Eldreth Row comprises the soaring, ruined outer halls forming the western entrance to Dire Maul in Feralas. Once a grand threshold of ancient Eldre'Thalas, the area is now choked with overgrowth and fiercely patrolled by Gordunni ogres. Standing as the outermost perimeter of ancient Eldre'Thalas, Eldreth Row is an imposing avenue of high-arched elven architecture that sits just outside the deeper quarters and the Broken Commons. Centuries of neglect have cracked its stonework, allowing the creeping flora of Feralas to reclaim its colonnades. Today, the pristine majesty of the Highborne has been replaced by the crude dominance of the Gordunni ogres, who use the halls as an outer staging ground, guarding the paths leading east toward the Warpwood Quarter and west past the Dire Pool.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_eldreth-row-43f6711bf92f1c9f294b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 48.6661224, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:emerald-sanctuary"] = {
  hash = "5bbc9e",
  fullHash = "5bbc9e",
  text = "Emerald Sanctuary. The Emerald Sanctuary is an outpost of the Cenarion Circle situated at the southern entrance of Felwood. Here, night elven and tauren druids unite to study the pervasive demonic taint and attempt the arduous task of healing the corrupted woodland. Perched on the southern edge of Felwood near the Ashenvale border, the Emerald Sanctuary serves as the primary base of operations for the Cenarion Circle in this blighted wilderness. Unlike the bitter factional conflicts burning across Kalimdor, this sanctuary offers a neutral foothold where druids look past politics to focus entirely on ecological restoration. From here, researchers track corrupted flora and direct investigations toward Jaedenar, seeking any viable method to cleanse the poisoned soil.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_emerald-sanctuary-89185d3e0f84220c4057/1-0000.mp3", duration = 46.0016327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:ethel-rethor"] = {
  hash = "0bfeb6",
  fullHash = "0bfeb6",
  text = "Ethel Rethor. Ethel Rethor is an ancient night elven ruin situated along the rugged northwestern coast of Desolace. While a solitary, sealed tower still rises above the waves, the majority of the sunken settlement now lies submerged in the sea and overrun by hostile naga. Perched on the desolate coast of western Kalimdor, Ethel Rethor stands as a haunting reminder of the ancient night elven civilization that flourished before the Great Sundering. Much of the grand architecture has collapsed into the ocean depths, leaving only shattered foundations and a lone, weathered tower protruding from the surf. A small bridge extends toward the tower's sealed portal, but the structure remains shut tight against the elements and intruders alike. The submerged chambers and surrounding shallows have become a stronghold for coastal naga, making the waters treacherous for any who wander near. Despite its remote location and serpentine infestation, Ethel Rethor draws travelers interested in Kalimdor's forgotten histories and ongoing threats. An agent of the Argent Dawn, Azore Aldamort, maintains a watchful presence near the ruins, monitoring the naga menace and offering aid to intrepid adventurers willing to dive into the sunken passages. Between the aquatic dangers offshore and the barren wastes inland, the ruins serve as a lonely coastal crossroads.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_ethel-rethor-80720ecd008578cfa76f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 80.64, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:everlook"] = {
  hash = "c81b4e",
  fullHash = "c81b4e",
  text = "Everlook. Everlook is a snow-swept trading outpost in central Winterspring established by the Steamwheedle Cartel. Situated at the crossroads of the snowy valley's routes, it serves as a neutral haven for merchants, prospectors, and researchers braving the high-altitude cold. Everlook represents the Steamwheedle Cartel's northernmost endeavor, mirroring outposts like Gadgetzan and Booty Bay under a blanket of permafrost. The walled settlement perches at the intersection of Winterspring's snowdrifts, operating under strictly enforced neutrality. It hosts the only operational bank and shared auction house services in the region, drawing both Alliance and Horde travelers in search of warmth and shelter. Guarded by hired bruisers, the town offers refuge from ferocious frostsabers and yetis. Beneath its icy wooden roofs and whimsical penguin wallpaper, Everlook is a hotbed of technological curiosity. It houses specialized engineering workshops and advanced alchemical laboratories where goblin tinkerers push the boundaries of their craft away from prying eyes. Outside diplomacy has also begun knocking on the frost-rimmed gates; the Argent Dawn recently dispatched Gregor Greystone here to negotiate directly with town leadership, keeping a wary watch on the region's ancient ruins and gathering northern threats.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_everlook-4840083a7fdca6e6d063/1-0000.mp3", duration = 80.875102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:faldir-s-cove"] = {
  hash = "318500",
  fullHash = "318500",
  text = "Faldir's Cove. Faldir's Cove is a secluded natural harbor nestled along the southern coastline of the Arathi Highlands. Steeped in human history, it is traditionally remembered as the departure point from which early settlers sailed south to found Stormwind. Today, it serves as an emergency shelter for stranded Blackwater Raiders whose fleet ran afoul of local hazards. Tucked away behind the rugged bluffs south of Stromgarde Keep, Faldir's Cove is linked to the mainland by a concealed cave tunnel. The cove honors the memory of Faldir, the ancient Arathor explorer whose expedition set sail from these very shores to cross the Great Sea and establish the southern kingdom of Stormwind. In the present day, however, its quiet waters house a very different crowd: an expedition of Blackwater Raiders from Booty Bay, led by Captains Steelgut and O'Breen, who sailed north pursuing ancient elven treasures only to end up trapped in the bay. The Blackwater venture turned catastrophic upon reaching the Arathi coast. Strange and unnatural tides conjured by the territorial naga dwelling on the offshore Drowned Reef shattered two of their three vessels. Only the Harbinger survived intact, limping into Faldir's Cove. With the naga prowling the waters and their navigation charts resting beneath the waves, the surviving pirates and their gnomish researchers find themselves marooned, reliant on neutral travelers to battle the aquatic horrors and recover sunken salvage.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_faldir-s-cove-bea34c248ecad574f940/1-0000.mp3", duration = 89.4171429, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:fallow-sanctuary"] = {
  hash = "085a2c",
  fullHash = "085a2c",
  text = "Fallow Sanctuary. The Fallow Sanctuary is a secluded village hidden deep within the bogs of the Swamp of Sorrows. It serves as home to an isolated tribe of mutated draenei known as the Lost Ones, whose severe grief and homesickness have driven them feral. Tucked away among the sunken trees of the Swamp of Sorrows, the Fallow Sanctuary was established by stranded Lost Ones seeking a place of their own. Severed from their ancestral world and deformed by dark energies, these wretched outcasts found no peace in the marshland. Over time, relentless sorrow and isolation shattered their sanity, reducing the tribe to a hostile, feral state where they fiercely attack any outsiders who trespass into their village. The settlement was once guided by figures like Ongeku and Noboru the Cudgel, though the village's leadership crumbled following their absence and demise.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_fallow-sanctuary-034bcaf337aa71923164/1-0000.mp3", duration = 51.722449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:feathermoon-stronghold"] = {
  hash = "6392cb",
  fullHash = "6392cb",
  text = "Feathermoon Stronghold. Feathermoon Stronghold is the premier night elf military base in southern Kalimdor, situated securely on Sardor Isle off the Forgotten Coast of Feralas. Commanded by General Shandris Feathermoon, this fortified outpost projects Sentinel strength across the wild forest and keeps watch over regional threats. Perched on Sardor Isle across the channel from Feralas's mainland, Feathermoon Stronghold serves as the primary strategic base for the Sentinels in southern Kalimdor. Built among ancient elven groves and supplied with a sacred moonwell, the fortress houses archers, huntresses, and support staff under the direct supervision of General Shandris Feathermoon. From this insular sanctuary, the night elves project their influence over the verdant interior of Feralas, guarding against demonic corruption, hostile wildlife, and encroaching Horde expeditions. Because the mainland of Feralas is thick with savage beasts, Gordunni ogres, and the tainted shadows of Dire Maul, Sardor Isle provides an essential defensive buffer. Alliance travelers rely on the ferry crossing from the Forgotten Coast to reach its fortified docks, repair their gear, and gather intelligence. The presence of Shandris Feathermoon—General of the Sentinels and High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind's trusted right hand—ensures that the stronghold remains an unassailable redoubt against all enemies of the kaldorei.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_feathermoon-stronghold-10bf21bdeed0d5e67377/1-0000.mp3", duration = 83.8269388, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:feathermoon-stronghold-classic"] = {
  hash = "2542ca",
  fullHash = "2542ca",
  text = "Feathermoon Stronghold (Classic). Feathermoon Stronghold is a fortified night elf bastion situated on Sardor Isle off the western shores of Feralas. Established by General Shandris Feathermoon, it serves as the Alliance's primary staging ground and military anchor in southern Kalimdor. Perched on Sardor Isle just off the Forgotten Coast, Feathermoon Stronghold stands as a crucial bulwark for the Night Elves and the broader Alliance. Built under the direction of General Shandris Feathermoon—Tyrande Whisperwind's trusted lieutenant for ten millennia—the fortress projects Sentinel power deep into southern Kalimdor. Its isolated island geography shields it from the beasts, ogres, and Horde interlopers prowling the primeval jungles of mainland Feralas. Given its insular position, Feathermoon relies heavily on sea and air links to maintain logistics. A small passenger boat regularly shuttles travellers across the channel between Sardor Isle and the Forgotten Coast dock. For distant travel, a dedicated hippogryph roost ties the stronghold directly to major Alliance hubs, including Thalanaar on the Thousand Needles border, Nijel's Point in the Desolace highlands, and Auberdine in the north.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_feathermoon-stronghold-classic-ae6ed4a45ac026c0c87a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 71.7583673, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:felpaw-village"] = {
  hash = "2188b4",
  fullHash = "2188b4",
  text = "Felpaw Village. Felpaw Village is a corrupted furbolg settlement nestled in the far northern reaches of Felwood, guarding the mountain path leading directly to Timbermaw Hold. Inhabited by the fallen Deadwood tribe, its huts and gathering grounds are tainted by demonic influence and hostile to all outsiders. Situated at the northern edge of Felwood, Felpaw Village serves as the primary northern redoubt for the fallen Deadwood furbolgs. Once gentle forest wardens, the tribe fell victim to the demonic blight unleashed during the Third War, driving them into a state of feral madness. Now, the village stands as a blighted enclave filled with crude huts, ritual totems, and hostile warriors who attack anyone wandering near the tunnel entrance to Timbermaw Hold. The proximity of Felpaw Village to the sacred entrance of Timbermaw Hold makes it a critical threat to the few uncorrupted furbolgs remaining in the region. The Timbermaw guards posted at the northern mouth of Felwood view their fallen cousins with a mixture of mourning and vigilance, frequently calling upon travelers to cull the Deadwood ranks, seize their ritual fetishes, and keep the corruption from spilling through the gates into Moonglade and Winterspring.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_felpaw-village-10df186599e991dc1f09/1-0000.mp3", duration = 70.8963265, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:felstone-field"] = {
  hash = "b60b9a",
  fullHash = "b60b9a",
  text = "Felstone Field. Felstone Field is a blighted farmstead located in the northwestern Western Plaguelands. Once a thriving piece of Lordaeron agricultural land, it has been claimed by the Scourge and anchored by a plague cauldron spewing toxic fumes across the ruined soil. Before the Third War, Felstone Field was a peaceful, productive family farm feeding the citizens of Lordaeron. When the plague swept through the north, the homestead fell swiftly to the Cult of the Damned and the Scourge. Today, the fields sprout dead weeds and creeping rot instead of grain. At the heart of the farmland sits an enormous plague cauldron, continuously churning foul vapors into the sky under the watch of Cauldron Lord Bilemaw and legions of skeletal flayers, sorcerers, and ravenous ghouls.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_felstone-field-343357c08cef5b29619b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 47.3861224, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:feral-scar-vale"] = {
  hash = "42919f",
  fullHash = "42919f",
  text = "Feral Scar Vale. Feral Scar Vale is a rocky gorge tucked within the High Wilderness of western Feralas. Overrun by territorial Feral Scar yetis, its cavernous tunnels are notoriously perilous to travelers who venture off the overgrown forest trails. Situated amid the thick foliage and sheer cliffs of the High Wilderness, Feral Scar Vale serves as the primary den for the savage Feral Scar yetis. While yetis are common in the snowy peaks of Alterac and Dun Morogh, these primal brutes have adapted completely to the wild, humid climate of Feralas. They fiercely defend the vale's cave network, hoarding bone trophies and aggressively driving off any intruders who wander into their hunting grounds. Beyond the primal danger of the yetis, the deeper recesses of Feral Scar Vale hide unexpected oddities. Gnomish engineering distress signals have occasionally been tracked into these dark caverns, where lost exploratory devices—such as stranded robotic survey chickens—have ended up trapped by the ferocious beasts, awaiting activation and extraction.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_feral-scar-vale-e699831b366fc4094977/1-0000.mp3", duration = 62.955102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:fire-plume-ridge"] = {
  hash = "75417b",
  fullHash = "75417b",
  text = "Fire Plume Ridge. Fire Plume Ridge is the active volcano rising directly from the center of Un'Goro Crater. Its searing slopes and subterranean magma channels fuel the crater's geysers and steam pools while playing host to fierce fire elementals. Towering over the primeval basin of Un'Goro, Fire Plume Ridge stands in sharp contrast to the surrounding lush jungle. Lava steadily cascades down its scorched rock faces, carving a landscape of ash and basalt. Subterranean magma arteries spiderweb outward beneath the soil from the ridge, fueling the bubbling geysers and hot springs scattered across the basin. The oppressive heat here is legendary—far exceeding ordinary volcanic temperatures according to goblin measuring devices—making it perilous for ill-prepared explorers to wander without ample water and shade. The volcano is an elemental furnace teeming with living flame. Living flares and roving fire elementals patrol the glowing flows, drawn to the concentration of pure subterranean heat. Deep within a cavern near the volcanic summit resides Blazerunner, the undisputed master among Un'Goro's elemental denizens. Venturing high up the ridge means navigating both lethal heat and volatile elemental spirits that aggressively defend their fiery domain.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_fire-plume-ridge-6919d2e5971fb284ef64/1-0000.mp3", duration = 77.7404082, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:firewatch-ridge"] = {
  hash = "ded010",
  fullHash = "ded010",
  text = "Firewatch Ridge. Firewatch Ridge is a high volcanic bluff in the northern reaches of Searing Gorge, serving as a primary encampment for the Dark Iron dwarves. Looming directly above the vast excavation known as the Cauldron, the ridge is heavily fortified by dwarven sentries, mechanical golems, and nesting fire spiders. To the northwest, an ominous cave system reveals an entrenched presence of the Twilight's Hammer cult. Perched above the industrial devastation of the Cauldron, Firewatch Ridge functions as a critical staging ground for the Dark Iron clan's operations in Searing Gorge. Dwarven guards, engineers, and molten golem creations patrol the crags, fiercely defending their foothold alongside venomous flame spiders native to the smoldering rocks. Its elevation provides the Dark Irons an unobstructed view of the surrounding ash plains, ensuring early warning against rival incursions from Ironforge or the Thorium Brotherhood. Tucked away in the northwestern reaches of the ridge lies an underground cave network claimed by the Twilight's Hammer. These cultists operate in the shadows of the Dark Iron encampment, conducting dark rituals and harboring powerful elite zealots beneath the rock. Their proximity to both the Dark Iron infrastructure and Blackrock Mountain reflects the pervasive, insidious reach of elemental and apocalyptic cults within the fiery heart of the Searing Gorge.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_firewatch-ridge-e16ba262bba338f4c6f7/1-0000.mp3", duration = 85.7077551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:flame-crest"] = {
  hash = "521b67",
  fullHash = "521b67",
  text = "Flame Crest. Flame Crest is a rugged neutral outpost perched atop a rocky ledge overlooking the volcanic wastes of the Burning Steppes. Accessed only across a crude span, this precarious encampment serves as a forward staging ground against the Dark Horde entrenched in Blackrock Mountain. It provides essential aerial connections and a haven for scouts, beast collectors, and strategists. Situated high on an isolated stone outcropping, Flame Crest commands a watchful view over the scorched ash and lava plains of the Burning Steppes. Reached only by crossing a narrow, rickety wooden bridge, the outpost is naturally defensible against the prowling beasts and roaming orc warbands of the wastes. Wind riders roost along the heights, connecting the camp back across the Redridge range to Kargath, Stonard, Thorium Point, and Rog'mar in the Riverglades. Flame Crest is more than just a wind rider roost; it is a critical forward watchpoint over the Blackrock clan entrenched within Blackrock Spire. Here, deep in hostile territory, scouts gather intelligence, lay plans to break the renegade Dark Horde, and trade with eccentric loners like the pet master Kibler in his nearby cave.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_flame-crest-d3ab411d9da3a4c1e03f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 71.3665306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:foothold-citadel"] = {
  hash = "b780d7",
  fullHash = "b780d7",
  text = "Foothold Citadel. Foothold Citadel serves as the central military barracks and command post within Theramore Isle. Overlooking the fortified settlement, it houses the officers and guards who protect the Alliance's primary bastion on southern Kalimdor. Rising within the fortified stone walls of Theramore Isle, Foothold Citadel is the nerve center of local Alliance defense. The citadel houses the high command of the Theramore Guard, including Captain Garran Vimes and Commander Samaul, along with field medics, logistics officers, and quartermasters. Its utilitarian stone halls bustle with regular patrols and drills, keeping Lady Jaina Proudmoore's forces prepared against threats creeping out of the surrounding swamplands. The citadel bears a grim history tied to the founding days of Theramore. When Grand Admiral Daelin Proudmoore seized control of the city to wage his personal crusade against the Horde, this very fortress became the battleground of his final stand. Thrall's allied strike force, spearheaded by the half-ogre warrior Rexxar, breached the citadel and struck the Admiral down. Today, the fortress stands repaired and steadfast, though the memory of that bloody conflict still hangs over its stone ramparts.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_foothold-citadel-7919aabc9843b7c846b3/1-0000.mp3", duration = 73.4040816, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:forgotten-coast"] = {
  hash = "c1c8a6",
  fullHash = "c1c8a6",
  text = "Forgotten Coast. The Forgotten Coast forms the wild western shore of Feralas, where the ancient rainforest meets the Veiled Sea. Lumbering sea giants and water elementals roam this battered strand. Stretching along the western edge of Feralas, the Forgotten Coast is a windswept shoreline confronting the expanse of the Veiled Sea. Unlike the dense canopy inland, this frontier is exposed to the elements and dominated by towering sea giants and roving water elementals pacing the surf. Travelers emerging from the forest canopy must contend with these primeval coastal guardians while navigating access to the sea, where ferry connections link to the night elven bastion of Sardor Isle.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_forgotten-coast-e42d58ba440d80078966/1-0000.mp3", duration = 41.9265306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:forgotten-pool"] = {
  hash = "53b928",
  fullHash = "53b928",
  text = "Forgotten Pool. Past the Moonshrine Ruins, Forgotten Pool lies beneath the flooded halls of Blackfathom Deeps. Its dark waters hide monstrous deep-sea predators. You reach Forgotten Pool by diving through the sunken waterways beyond the Moonshrine Ruins. Dark water fills this isolated cavern, cut off from dry ground. Twilight's Hammer cultists chant nearby, and naga patrol the flooded halls, but the pool lies apart from them. Old Serra'kis, an ancient aquatic terror, lairs in its depths, hidden by the water.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_forgotten-pool-0fb7dac8b07ec242fdd3/1-0000.mp3", duration = 33.1232653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:forlorn-cloister"] = {
  hash = "028745",
  fullHash = "028745",
  text = "Forlorn Cloister. The Forlorn Cloister is the haunted graveyard wing of the Scarlet Monastery. Here, members of the Scarlet Crusade desperately torch the bodies of fallen brothers to prevent them from rising into undeath, while restless spirits and shambling corpses roam among the tombs. Once a peaceful sanctuary where the priests and paladins of Lordaeron laid their honoured dead to rest, the Forlorn Cloister has become a grim charnel ground. Despite the Scarlet Crusade's fanatical vigilance, the taint of the plague has seeped deep into the earth. crusaders patrol the fog-shrouded cemetery with torches, frantically burning remains to stave off the rot. Even so, spectres glide between crypts and the dead pull free from their graves, ruled over by dark magic deep within the grounds. Deep within the cloister lurks Bloodmage Thalnos, a skeletal sorcerer whose undeath embodies the tragedy of the Monastery. Hungering for vengeance, Thalnos wields dark magic, binding the souls of the deceased and slaughtering any who dare intrude upon his blighted graveyard.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_forlorn-cloister-c5da17b283d95a02fe72/1-0000.mp3", duration = 63.3730612, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:forlorn-ridge"] = {
  hash = "1802cf",
  fullHash = "1802cf",
  text = "Forlorn Ridge. Forlorn Ridge is a towering mountain ridge looming over southwestern Azshara. Its strategic elevation and commanding views over the coast have drawn the attention of both the Alliance and the Horde, who have each carved out fortifications along its slopes. Rising sharply above the autumnal forests of western Azshara, Forlorn Ridge dominates the surrounding terrain. Resembling an ancient, long-dormant volcano, its high vantage point offers a sweeping view over the coastal approaches and the ruined elven estates below. Because control over these heights dictates military dominance in the sector, both Alliance and Horde forces have established encampments and outposts along its mountain flanks, watching each other warily across the rugged bluffs.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_forlorn-ridge-ca2bb83d11bc5dabb73b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 43.1020408, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:frayfeather-highlands"] = {
  hash = "18a59b",
  fullHash = "18a59b",
  text = "Frayfeather Highlands. The Frayfeather Highlands form an elevated wilderness in the dense forests of Feralas. This remote plateau is the ancestral roost and nesting ground of the wild Frayfeather hippogryphs. Intrepid scavengers and naturalists often brave its territorial flocks in search of intact hippogryph eggs. Rising above the surrounding forest floor of Feralas, the Frayfeather Highlands are dominated by steep slopes and crags where wild hippogryphs raise their broods. The local flocks are notoriously aggressive toward intruders, fiercely defending the scattered nests tucked among the rock shelves. Because these beasts are so well adapted to the rugged cliffs, their nesting grounds have drawn significant interest from outsiders—most notably goblin entrepreneurs in distant Gadgetzan who seek unhatched eggs for study and profit.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_frayfeather-highlands-960b99927c874430ba5f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 49.2930612, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:frostfire-hot-springs"] = {
  hash = "d150de",
  fullHash = "d150de",
  text = "Frostfire Hot Springs. Frostfire Hot Springs are a curious series of steaming pools nestled amidst the western snowdrifts of Winterspring. Despite the bitter sub-zero temperatures all around them, these waters bubble constantly, attracting curious scholars, hardy fishers, and hostile furbolgs alike. Situated near the tunnel leading to Timbermaw Hold, Frostfire Hot Springs represent one of the strangest natural anomalies in northern Kalimdor. The pools remain hot amid the surrounding snow, although the source of their heat is uncertain. The local Winterfall furbolgs maintain a presence near the water, though their aggressive territorial nature makes approaching the warm banks hazardous for unwary travelers. For those able to navigate the hostile wildlife and winter chill, the unfrozen waters provide a rare haven for fish. The uncanny existence of such intense heat in an otherwise frozen wasteland has drawn the attention of curious observers. Nearby, an inquisitive dwarf named Donova Snowden keeps a lonely vigil over the springs, studying their origin and composition. Snowden suspects that these geothermal anomalies might not be entirely natural, hypothesizing that dark magical corruption seeping northward from the demonic stronghold of Darkwhisper Gorge could be responsible for roiling the subterranean waters.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_frostfire-hot-springs-6efd59e82a200b7079d3/1-0000.mp3", duration = 78.7330612, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:frostsaber-rock"] = {
  hash = "896282",
  fullHash = "896282",
  text = "Frostsaber Rock. Frostsaber Rock is a towering stone promontory rising above the snowy northern reaches of Winterspring. It serves as the primary sanctuary and training grounds for the noble frostsaber cats of the region. Rising high above the frozen wastes of northern Winterspring, Frostsaber Rock commands a sweeping view of the snowbound wild. The crag is named for the fierce, majestic frostsaber predators that prowl the slopes and valleys below. At its highest apex resides Rivern Frostwind, a solitary night elf dedicated to raising and tending these beasts. For champions loyal to the Alliance willing to brave the harsh conditions and prove their devotion, Rivern offers the rare privilege of learning to raise, tame, and ride one of these legendary snowy mounts.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_frostsaber-rock-23a2e2ac809e3bff3442/1-0000.mp3", duration = 46.4979592, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:frostwhisper-gorge"] = {
  hash = "b617b6",
  fullHash = "b617b6",
  text = "Frostwhisper Gorge. Frostwhisper Gorge is a frigid canyon carved into the southern highlands of Winterspring, lying directly north of the sinister Darkwhisper Gorge. Ancient night elven ruins litter its icy floor and surrounding cliffs, while towering Frostmaul giants dominate the chasm. High above the eastern rim, a solitary dwarf camp looks out over the precipice toward the ocean below. Carved into the southern mountains of Winterspring, Frostwhisper Gorge serves as a threshold just before the demon-infested depths of Darkwhisper Gorge. Its snowy floor and cliffside ridges are strewn with the forgotten stonework of ancient night elven settlements. In the present day, the gorge is claimed by immense Frostmaul giants and their preservers. Travel down into the ravine is perilous, as the giants fiercely defend the frozen crags against any wandering intruders. Hidden along a narrow, quiet track at the top of the gorge's eastern cliffs lies a curious outpost. Flying travelers on wyverns or gryphons passing between Azshara, Orgrimmar, and Winterspring often glimpse it from above: a solitary dwarven encampment perched at the very edge of the world. Planted with the banner of Ironforge, the camp features crates of provisions, barrels of ale, a comfortable chair, and a rifle leaning against the rest, providing an unmatched view across the drop to the Great Sea below.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_frostwhisper-gorge-5c0900cc3c5fd174bcfb/1-0000.mp3", duration = 84.7934694, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:fungal-rock"] = {
  hash = "2d2d46",
  fullHash = "2d2d46",
  text = "Fungal Rock. Fungal Rock is a damp, cavernous hollow carved into the northeastern cliff walls of Un'Goro Crater. Dominated by aggressive primates and thick growths of Ghost Mushrooms, it is one of the crater's most dangerous ape dens. Tucked behind a small waterfall and pool along Un'Goro Crater's northeastern edge, Fungal Rock serves as the primary den for the region's massive gorillas. Inside the damp, subterranean corridors, towering Un'Goro Stompers and Thunderers patrol among clusters of rare Ghost Mushrooms. The deepest recesses house territorial behemoths, including the mighty silverback U'cha and the elusive albino gorilla Uhk'loc. Beyond its primal inhabitants, curious travelers may stumble upon artificial oddities near the cave mouth, such as A-Me 01, a damaged mechanical ape abandoned by gnome researchers.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_fungal-rock-ccb5b51229470898bbe6/1-0000.mp3", duration = 52.7412245, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:fungal-vale"] = {
  hash = "5d8329",
  fullHash = "5d8329",
  text = "Fungal Vale. The Fungal Vale is a deeply blighted depression located in the south-central Eastern Plaguelands, just north of the Crown Guard Tower and Marris Stead. Choked in a murky haze and blanketed with dark purple earth, the valley showcases the raw, mutating power of the Plague of Undeath. It swarms with roaming Scourge horrors and mortal acolytes serving the Cult of the Damned. Situated north of Marris Stead, the Fungal Vale represents one of the most thoroughly corrupted pockets in Lordaeron. The ground here has turned a sickly purple, swollen with alien fungal growths and drowning under a perpetual, murky haze produced by necrotic spores. Rather than simply killing the ecosystem, the Plague of Undeath has warped the soil to cultivate unholy flora, feeding monstrous carrion beasts and shambling undead. Cult of the Damned members frequent the area, tending to the blighted blooms and conducting foul rites amidst the unnatural decay.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_fungal-vale-e39fedc1418b92f4e35e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 58.357551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:gadgetzan"] = {
  hash = "a66236",
  fullHash = "a66236",
  text = "Gadgetzan. Gadgetzan is the walled desert hub of the Steamwheedle Cartel, standing as an island of commerce in northern Tanaris. Open to both Horde and Alliance travelers, the town operates under strictly enforced goblin neutrality, welcoming anyone with coin to spend or odd jobs to undertake. Surrounded by scorching sands, Gadgetzan serves as the regional heart for the Steamwheedle Cartel's ventures in southern Kalimdor. Built with characteristic goblin ingenuity and haphazard architecture, the town boasts an independent auction house, bank, inn, and specialized crafting facilities for engineers, blacksmiths, and alchemists. It also houses flight masters catering to both major factions, ensuring steady traffic of adventurers heading toward Tanaris, Feralas, and the untamed basin of Un'Goro. Above all, commerce rules supreme: as long as visitors keep their weapons sheathed and their purses open, they are safe within its gates. Peace in Gadgetzan is maintained not by good will, but by heavily armed goblin bruisers who swiftly pummel anyone who threatens trade or instigates faction brawls. Beyond the gates, however, life is precarious. The Gadgetzan Water Company constantly battles the Wastewander bandits, a ruthless gang occupying the vital Waterspring Field. Water is liquid gold in Tanaris, and the cartel frequently hires outside mercenaries to eliminate bandit leadership and reclaim vital water pouches to keep the town's reservoirs functional.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_gadgetzan-0b2bb4469310ac72b845/1-0000.mp3", duration = 92.7346939, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:gahrron-s-withering"] = {
  hash = "0b183f",
  fullHash = "0b183f",
  text = "Gahrron's Withering. Gahrron's Withering is a corrupted farmstead nestled along the eastern border of the Western Plaguelands near the path to the Eastern Plaguelands. Once a thriving piece of Lordaeron farmland, it is now occupied by the Scourge and anchored by a massive cauldron pumping virulent plague vapors into the air. Before the Third War ruined the kingdom of Lordaeron, this estate was a working farm. Its fertile fields yielded grain for the realm until the Cult of the Damned and the Scourge swept across the land. Now called Gahrron's Withering, the homestead stands broken and derelict. Haunting visions, wailing wraiths, and restless spirits wander the ruined outbuildings, overseen by Scourge overseers sworn to the Lich King.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_gahrron-s-withering-4ee57df63a52085fe12f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 43.9640816, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:gallery-of-treasures"] = {
  hash = "0ba609",
  fullHash = "0ba609",
  text = "Gallery of Treasures. The Gallery of Treasures is an ornate, heavily guarded corridor inside the Scarlet Monastery's halls, connecting the Huntsman's Cloister to the Athenaeum. Lined with recovered paintings, relics, and illuminated texts, it showcases the wealth and Lordaeron heritage preserved by the Scarlet Crusade amidst their zealous war against the undead. Situated deep within the Library wing of the Scarlet Monastery, the Gallery of Treasures serves as a grand hall connecting the kennels of the Huntsman's Cloister to the sacred archives of the Athenaeum. Unlike the hound pens of the cloister, this corridor is lined with fine paintings, historical artifacts, and small chambers filled with books salvaged from across the fallen kingdom of Lordaeron. To the Scarlet Crusade, these relics represent the untainted legacy of humanity they have sworn to protect, fiercely guarded against all outsiders who dare intrude upon their sanctuary.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_gallery-of-treasures-b81020b674b854786ea6/1-0000.mp3", duration = 55.797551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:gaping-chasm"] = {
  hash = "39d8cb",
  fullHash = "39d8cb",
  text = "Gaping Chasm. The Gaping Chasm is a sprawling silithid hive carved deep into the sands of southeastern Tanaris. Infested by aggressive insectoid horrors of the Hazzali brood, it serves as a grim warning of the growing silithid menace spreading beyond the western deserts. Breaking the rolling expanses of Tanaris, the Gaping Chasm plunges down into a teeming subterranean warren of tunnels, resinous mounds, and twitching carapaces. This hive belongs to the Hazzali, an aggressive brood of silithid that have burrowed beneath the desert wastes. Between subterranean stingers, swarmers, and hulking sandreavers, any travelers or treasure-hunters foolish enough to descend into the chasm quickly find themselves hunted as prey or preserved in sticky cocoons. The expanding hive caught the attention of Gadgetzan's enterprising minds, prompting Senior Surveyor Fizzledowser to dispatch his junior surveyor, Scrimshank, into the pit to map and measure the geological shifts caused by the insect tunnels. Unsurprisingly, the expedition ended in disaster. Scrimshank met a grim end amidst the chittering broods, leaving behind scattered gear and measuring instruments that caution anyone against underestimating the silithid threat.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_gaping-chasm-3377ba545492e027c30a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 73.6130612, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:garrison-armory"] = {
  hash = "e39256",
  fullHash = "e39256",
  text = "Garrison Armory. The Garrison Armory is an Alliance mining operation carved into the hills southwest of Nethergarde Keep in the Blasted Lands. Miners and engineers work the underground veins to harvest valuable minerals, sustaining the lonely fortress and shipping raw wealth back to Stormwind. It forms a crucial industrial lifeline in an otherwise blasted, barren wilderness. Maintaining a permanent military garrison in the arid wastes of the Blasted Lands requires constant supply, and Nethergarde Keep cannot rely purely on long supply trains from Stormwind. The Garrison Armory serves as the keep's primary industrial excavation. Nethergarde miners, overseen by foremen and assisted by engineers, work these subterranean shafts to pull valuable minerals from the earth. The ore extracted here is utilized directly by Nethergarde's smiths and fortifications or hauled back to the capital in exchange for the foodstuffs, tools, and provisions the fortress needs to maintain its vigil over the Dark Portal. Because the armory sits away from the main walls of Nethergarde Keep, the excavation remains exposed to the harsh perils of the red desert. Dreadmaul ogres and wild beasts prowl the surrounding crags. The workforce within the tunnels operates under armed guard, knowing that any disruption to the armory directly threatens Nethergarde's ability to maintain its watch over the blasted frontier.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_garrison-armory-d9b5a70b30c9679d80b4/1-0000.mp3", duration = 84.48, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:gelkis-village"] = {
  hash = "844e1c",
  fullHash = "844e1c",
  text = "Gelkis Village. Gelkis Village is the primary stronghold of the earth-aligned Gelkis centaur clan in southwestern Desolace. Built around a rugged ravine connected by wooden spans and cliffside paths, the settlement guards the southern route toward Feralas. Its vantage directly overlooks the main north-south road, making it a perilous crossing for any traveler who has not earned the clan's favor. Gelkis Village sits in a deep, craggy gorge framed by precarious bluffs and rope bridges. Unlike the nomadic raiders of the Barrens, the Gelkis have fortified their ancestral grounds into a permanent settlement, complete with outposts spreading toward the border of Feralas. The heart of the clan's leadership lies within Bolgan's Hole, a cavern carved into the southern cliff face where Khan Shaka oversees his warriors. Travelers trekking south through Desolace must either cut a bloody path through the gorge or tread carefully, as the Gelkis view uninvited wanderers as fair game in their eternal war against their rival centaur brethren.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_gelkis-village-fc01b6d8df05c1c99b03/1-0000.mp3", duration = 62.8506122, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:ghost-walker-post"] = {
  hash = "349146",
  fullHash = "349146",
  text = "Ghost Walker Post. Ghost Walker Post is a modest tauren settlement situated in the heart of Desolace right beside the ancient Kodo Graveyard. Far more exposed to regional dangers than Shadowprey Village on the western coast, it serves as a frontline Horde staging ground against centaur clans and the Burning Blade. Perched on the dusty flats of central Desolace, Ghost Walker Post provides the Horde with an austere foothold in a hostile territory. Unlike the sheltered coastal fishing haven of Shadowprey Village, this post directly faces roaming centaur marauders and demon-worshiping cultists of the Burning Blade. Facilities are spartan, offering only the bare essentials for travelers, with no inn or flight master in sight. Despite the harsh conditions, a garrison of tauren and an orcish detachment sent by Warchief Thrall keep vigilant watch over the badlands. Led by the tauren shaman Gurda Wildmane, the spiritual heart of the outpost is closely entwined with the neighboring Kodo Graveyard. Shamans stationed here tend to the dying beasts that wander in to perish, helping ease them into the next life and communing with their lingering spirits. Between spiritual communion and constant military readiness, the defenders of the post honor the ancient bond between the tauren and the mighty kodo amid the ashes of Desolace.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_ghost-walker-post-feb1e9d811b6bba6738d/1-0000.mp3", duration = 80.0391837, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:gnomeregan"] = {
  hash = "f71771",
  fullHash = "f71771",
  text = "Gnomeregan. In the poisoned halls of Gnomeregan, troggs and leper gnomes roam among broken defenses. Once the gnomes’ subterranean capital, it fell after a desperate defense turned to catastrophe. Gnomeregan’s halls once rang with the work of gnomish engineers. Built with aid from the Ironforge dwarves centuries ago, the city was carved into the snowy crags of western Dun Morogh. Steam-driven gears, copper piping, and vast assembly halls made it the heart of gnomish industry and invention. During the Second War, its designs, flying machines, and complex armaments supplied the Alliance. The city held firm even as Orgrim Doomhammer’s Horde laid siege. During the Third War, troggs surged from deep underground and broke into Gnomeregan’s lowest chambers. High Tinker Gelbin Mekkatorque chose to face the invasion alone, unwilling to draw Alliance armies away from the slaughter in Lordaeron. On the advice of chief engineer Mekgineer Thermaplugg, he ordered radioactive gas released through the lower chambers to wipe out the troggs. Instead, the gas empowered many of them and killed nearly eighty percent of the gnomish people. Those exposed who survived became twisted leper gnomes, and Thermaplugg took control of the poisoned ruins.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_gnomeregan-57c917530007cdb23cb9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 76.564898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:go-shek-farm"] = {
  hash = "86bd69",
  fullHash = "86bd69",
  text = "Go'Shek Farm. Go'Shek Farm is an agricultural outpost in the eastern Arathi Highlands, occupied by the Horde and guarded by orcs from Hammerfall. Behind its mundane rural facade, Forsaken apothecaries conduct secretive alchemical experiments far from prying eyes. Go'Shek Farm is held by the Horde. Positioned south of Hammerfall and just north of the hostile Witherbark troll territory, the farmstead nominally serves as a vital supply and staging hub for the orcish settlement. Hammerfall Grunts patrol the fields and stables, securing food and livestock to sustain the Horde's embattled foothold in the Arathi Highlands.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_go-shek-farm-9b0311fed81fab31a400/1-0000.mp3", duration = 36.0228571, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:golakka-hot-springs"] = {
  hash = "185852",
  fullHash = "185852",
  text = "Golakka Hot Springs. Golakka Hot Springs is a geothermal basin in western Un'Goro Crater, nestled just north of Terror Run. Its bubbling pools and erupting geysers are fueled by the intense volcanic activity of nearby Fire Plume Ridge. The mineral-rich waters attract eccentric researchers, alchemists in search of exotic reagents, and wandering water elementals known as Steaming Furies. Situated in the western reaches of Un'Goro Crater, the Golakka Hot Springs are a direct product of the subterranean heat radiating from Fire Plume Ridge. Scalding water bubbles continuously to the surface, shaping tiered mineral terraces and violently erupting geysers. The environment is heavily prowled by Steaming Furies, elemental manifestations born from the convergence of subterranean groundwater and volcanic heat. Despite the danger, the springs frequently draw explorers, herbalists, and researchers fascinated by the crater's unique geology. The unique properties of Golakka's waters have drawn interest beyond simple sightseeing. Alchemists value the geothermally enriched spring water as a potent catalytic reagent, even utilizing it in delicate concotions such as love potions. The geothermal vent also hosts quirky visitors, such as Spark Nilminer perched atop the central geyser vent, and draws the researcher Krakle, who seeks to decipher how the hot springs directly tap into the volcanic heart of the crater.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_golakka-hot-springs-5a5154256b074a8d100b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 86.804898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:gordok-commons"] = {
  hash = "0b9439",
  fullHash = "0b9439",
  text = "Gordok Commons. The Gordok Commons comprise the fortified northern quarter of the ancient Highborne ruins of Dire Maul. Under the rule of King Gordok, this sprawling stone complex serves as the seat of power for the elite warrior caste of the Gordunni ogres. Occupying what was once an aristocratic wing of Eldre'Thalas, the Gordok Commons have been thoroughly desecrated and refitted for ogre tastes. Grand night elven pillars and arched passageways now house armories, crude barricades, and training grounds for the Gordok—the fiercest, most disciplined fighters among the Gordunni clan. At the deepest end of the wing lies Gordok's Seat, where King Gordok presides over his brutes through sheer force and intimidation. Gordok culture operates entirely on brutal precedent: might makes right. According to clan tradition, whoever slays the sitting monarch claims the title of King Gordok, earning immediate obedience from any surviving captains and guards. Astute intruders exploit this custom by sneaking or scheming past the king's lieutenants rather than slaying them outright. If a challenger executes only the king, the surviving guards will recognize the victor's authority and present lavish tributes rather than fight to avenge their former ruler.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_gordok-commons-97166c1fe487d1eba42c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 76.7477551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:gordunni-outpost"] = {
  hash = "5b4d59",
  fullHash = "5b4d59",
  text = "Gordunni Outpost. Tucked into the rugged cliffs north of Lariss Pavilion, the Gordunni Outpost serves as a fortified camp for the brutish Gordunni ogres of Feralas. The clan's corrupting influence has choked the once-vibrant wilderness here, leaving the surrounding earth dry, withered, and brown. Carved into the northern mountains above the Lariss Pavilion, the Gordunni Outpost stands out as a stark scar against the lush canopy of Feralas. Wherever the Gordunni ogres settle, their reckless magical channeling and destructive habits sap the vitality of the wild, leaving the local flora dead and the hillsides barren. The outpost consists of dirt trails, a shallow entrance cave, and a larger ogre mound burrowed deep into the rear cliffs. Patrolled by heavily armed hillguards, cunning channelers, and ogre tamers, the camp also holds dangerous trophies—including a captured sasquatch named Bigfist kept in crude confinement near the deeper den.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_gordunni-outpost-2beaac7cd802913866af/1-0000.mp3", duration = 59.2718367, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:green-belt"] = {
  hash = "99e634",
  fullHash = "99e634",
  text = "Green Belt. In eastern Wetlands, Green Belt lies below the ridges of Grim Batol. Bog beasts, crocolisks, and toxic oozes roam its marshes, while Rethiel the Greenwarden keeps watch alone. Green Belt fills the eastern interior of Wetlands, where mossy waters make travel hazardous. Massive crocolisks, shifting oozes, and shambling bog beasts roam the fen. Deep within it, a solitary watch post is manned by Rethiel the Greenwarden, a night elf who studies the fen's ecology and keeps its hostile creatures in check. Wreckage from a Dragonmaw clan caravan lies scattered in the muck, left behind after the catastrophic battle at nearby Grim Batol. Crimson whelplings linger around the broken carts and crushed cages, reminders of the clan's past enslavement of Alexstrasza and her red dragon brood.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_green-belt-0b02924b60284b78f17f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 53.1330612, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:grimesilt-dig-site"] = {
  hash = "877fc0",
  fullHash = "877fc0",
  text = "Grimesilt Dig Site. Grimesilt Dig Site is an archaeological excavation in the Searing Gorge originally broken ground by Ironforge dwarves. Overrun by hostile Dark Iron dwarves, the pit is now firmly under enemy control while surviving members of the original expedition struggle to endure in hiding. Situated within the soot-choked badlands of the Searing Gorge, the Grimesilt Dig Site began as an expedition sanctioned by Ironforge scholars seeking relics beneath the crags. That venture met a violent end when the native Dark Iron dwarves drove out the excavators and claimed the equipment for themselves. Today, the trench swarms with Dark Iron sentries, leaving the wounded and scattered survivors of the original Ironforge crew trapped in and around the pit.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_grimesilt-dig-site-ad7943733ebc0253ef12/1-0000.mp3", duration = 47.2816327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:grimtotem-compound"] = {
  hash = "eabed7",
  fullHash = "eabed7",
  text = "Grimtotem Compound. The Grimtotem Compound is a fortified tribal settlement nestled in the central wilds of Feralas, just east of the elven ruins of Dire Maul. Occupied by the aggressive and insular Grimtotem clan, the encampment serves as a base for their mysterious and destructive operations in the region. Spanning two encampments east of Dire Maul, the Grimtotem Compound establishes a firm foothold for the Grimtotem clan in Feralas. Unlike the tauren aligned with Cairne Bloodhoof in Thunder Bluff, the Grimtotem here operate independently and with hostility toward outsiders. In the southern half of the settlement, they keep captive sprite darters locked inside a large wooden cage for reasons of their own, while rogue gnolls stalk the fringes of their northern perimeter.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_grimtotem-compound-3e53dc65711e99e7d149/1-0000.mp3", duration = 46.2889796, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:grom-gol-base-camp"] = {
  hash = "096f23",
  fullHash = "096f23",
  text = "Grom'gol Base Camp. Grom'gol Base Camp is the Horde's primary fortified outpost in Stranglethorn Vale, situated along the western coast of northern Stranglethorn. Commanded by Commander Aggro'gosh, the settlement secures a critical foothold and supply chain in the southern Eastern Kingdoms. With direct zeppelin towers linking to Orgrimmar and the Undercity, it stands as an armed staging ground against local jungle troll tribes and hostile Alliance forces. Carved into the dense jungle of northwestern Stranglethorn Vale, Grom'gol Base Camp is a fortified military bastion commanded by Commander Aggro'gosh. Ringed by defensive palisades and heavily armed grunts, the base camp endures non-stop harassment from Bloodscalp headhunters, lurking naga, and prowling beasts. Despite these threats, its vital strategic location secures safe supply routes north toward Stonard in the Swamp of Sorrows while keeping a watchful eye on Alliance traffic descending from Duskwood or traveling up from Booty Bay. Grom'gol's prominent wooden zeppelin towers serve as one of the most vital logistical hubs in the Eastern Kingdoms. Goblin skycraft tethered here maintain regular routes directly to the capital cities of Orgrimmar and the Undercity. This uninterrupted airbridge allows Horde forces to rapidly deploy reinforcements and resources straight into the heart of Stranglethorn without braving Alliance-dominated highways or the deep sea.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_grom-gol-base-camp-c85633477947b9137482/1-0000.mp3", duration = 88.4767347, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:growless-cave"] = {
  hash = "bf4424",
  fullHash = "bf4424",
  text = "Growless Cave. In frigid Growless Cave, mountain yetis roam the cavern in the Alterac Mountains. The cave also holds the Flame of Uzel, a ceremonial pyre tied to Helcular's dark necromancy. Territorial mountain yetis roam Growless Cave in the crags above Alterac's foothills. Travelers on the treacherous slopes avoid its entrance, especially when Skhowl, a notorious yeti, prowls outside. The cave holds more than danger from the beasts. Within it stands the Flame of Uzel, an arcane brazier left by the human mage Helcular. Before his death, Helcular meant to use the pyre in a dark ritual to become a lich. Its foul warmth once drew Frostmaw, a massive predator, from the frozen wastes.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_growless-cave-9c984ca58ec77dcf528a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 43.284898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:gurubashi-arena"] = {
  hash = "f30b0d",
  fullHash = "f30b0d",
  text = "Gurubashi Arena. Gurubashi Arena is an ancient stone colosseum standing in southern Stranglethorn Vale, originally built by the mighty Gurubashi Empire. In the present day, it serves as a chaotic free-for-all proving ground where pirates and wandering brawlers shed blood over locked chests of treasure. Long before human or orc set foot in Stranglethorn Vale, the Gurubashi Arena—known also as the Great Arena—was built as a monument to martial strength by the sprawling Gurubashi troll empire. In ancient days, champions fought to the death upon its sands until the earth was slick with troll blood. Though the empire has long since fractured into warring tribes, the arena's floor never stays clean for long. Today, lawless buccaneers and ruffians oversee a different sort of bloody spectacle. A pirate named Short John Mithril oversees the grounds, where every few hours a chest of plundered booty is hauled into the center of the ring. Anyone who steps onto the floor is fair game—faction allegiances vanish, and combatants must butcher their way to the prize against all comers.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_gurubashi-arena-fa8a154fedc5be9ac8f2/1-0000.mp3", duration = 67.9183673, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:haldarr-encampment"] = {
  hash = "843ff6",
  fullHash = "843ff6",
  text = "Haldarr Encampment. The Haldarr Encampment is a corrupted satyr outpost nestled in western Azshara, situated northeast of the Shadowsong Shrine and southwest of Valormok. Densely inhabited by the vicious Haldarr satyrs, it stands as a dangerous blight among the ancient elven ruins. It is also where an injured Night Elf Sentinel, Keldara Sunblade, can be found. Perched among the broken stones of western Azshara, the Haldarr Encampment serves as a gathering ground for the Haldarr clan of satyrs. These twisted creatures, ancient remnants of the Highborne cursed by demonic corruption during the Sundering, actively hunt and harass any who traverse the autumn forests. The dense settlement poses an ongoing threat to both the Horde forces stationed at nearby Valormok and the dwindling Night Elf sentinels trying to monitor the region.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_haldarr-encampment-8ff0f239aa751da03c10/1-0000.mp3", duration = 49.8416327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:hall-of-arms-tyr-s-hand"] = {
  hash = "520a5d",
  fullHash = "520a5d",
  text = "Hall of Arms (Tyr's Hand). The Hall of Arms is a heavily guarded wing within Tyr's Hand Abbey in southeastern Eastern Plaguelands. It serves as a staging ground and barracks for the martial vanguard of the Scarlet Crusade garrisoned at the stronghold. Positioned across the abbey from the library, it houses some of the crusade's most zealous and well-armored fighters. Situated to the south of the abbey's main hall, the Hall of Arms functions as an armory and quarters for the militant forces of Tyr's Hand. While the opposing wing holds archives and holy texts, this wing is dedicated purely to the discipline of war. Clad in heavy armor and sworn to eradicate the undead and anyone deemed impure, the Scarlet defenders stationed here maintain a tireless watch over their eastern bastion, keeping weapons sharpened and shields polished against the surrounding horrors of the Plaguelands.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_hall-of-arms-tyr-s-hand-15b71b2f2549dae08603/1-0000.mp3", duration = 50.0506122, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:hall-of-champions"] = {
  hash = "533d9f",
  fullHash = "533d9f",
  text = "Hall of Champions. The Hall of Champions is the inner sanctum of the Scarlet Monastery's armory wing. It serves as the seat of Herod, the Scarlet Champion, and a proving ground for the Scarlet Crusade's elite. Situated past the Footman's Armory, the Hall of Champions lies at the heart of the monastery's armory wing. Here Herod, the Scarlet Champion, holds sway over the Crusade's fiercest fighters, and Scarlet trainees harden their resolve to purge Lordaeron of all undead and outsiders.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_hall-of-champions-80e9a264eb2e6d33247e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 27.3240816, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:hall-of-masks"] = {
  hash = "710711",
  fullHash = "710711",
  text = "Hall of Masks. The Hall of Masks is an ominous chamber inside the sunken Temple of Atal'Hakkar in the Swamp of Sorrows. Lined with giant skull masks and marred by a bone-strewn trench, it marks one of the early thresholds deeper into the Atal'ai stronghold. Located just past the staircase descending from the Broken Hall, the Hall of Masks serves as a central junction within the Sunken Temple. The chamber earns its name from the imposing, stylized skull masks mounted along its stone walls. Below them runs a deep trench choked with discarded bones, suggesting a macabre purpose whose exact nature is unknown. Beyond this eerie hall lie passages branching out to the Butchery, the Chamber of Blood, and the Den of the Caller.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_hall-of-masks-ce8ce923849a53b20b15/1-0000.mp3", duration = 43.8595918, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:hammerfall"] = {
  hash = "9edff3",
  fullHash = "9edff3",
  text = "Hammerfall. Hammerfall is the Horde's primary fortified outpost in the Arathi Highlands, built upon the ruins of a notorious Alliance internment camp. It honors the legendary former Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer, who fell nearby while fighting to liberate his captive people. Today, orcs and Forsaken garrison the settlement against hostile Boulderfist ogres, Witherbark trolls, and the League of Arathor. Following the Second War, the human Alliance built vast internment camps across Lordaeron to contain the defeated orcs. The compound that once stood here was among the largest. Years later, Thrall and Orgrim Doomhammer assaulted the camp to liberate the lethargic captives. During the chaos of their retreat, a charging Alliance knight drove a lance through Doomhammer's backplate, mortally wounding the legendary Warchief. Following the Third War, the new Horde reclaimed the ruined ground and established an outpost here, naming it Hammerfall in memory of their fallen leader. Today, Hammerfall serves as the Horde's lifeline in Arathi. Positioned against hostile surroundings, the garrison contends with aggressive Boulderfist ogres from Boulder'gor and Boulderfist Hall, as well as the native Witherbark trolls. Furthermore, Forsaken Defilers have made the outpost their staging ground to claim the contested resources of the Arathi Basin against the Alliance's League of Arathor.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_hammerfall-854382acbb8ba2066de8/1-0000.mp3", duration = 85.7077551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:harborage"] = {
  hash = "889fff",
  fullHash = "889fff",
  text = "Harborage. The Harborage is a quiet settlement of exiled Draenor natives situated in the northern reaches of the Swamp of Sorrows. While many of their afflicted kin wandering the surrounding bogs have succumbed to madness and primal hostility, the inhabitants here have managed to hold onto their sanity and wits. They live cautiously along the waterline, seeking peace amidst a harsh and unforgiving wilderness. Tucked away against the northern mountains of the Swamp of Sorrows, the Harborage stands in stark contrast to the feral tribes wandering the nearby waters. Constructed of rugged leather shelters and adorned with mysterious hanging crystals, this small encampment provides sanctuary to exiled Draenor survivors who have refused to surrender their minds. Under the guidance of elders like Magtoor, they maintain a fragile, civilized enclave while remaining watchful of the hostile wilderness, native predators, and the feral tribes of their fallen brothers lurking just beyond their perimeter.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_harborage-1256a51c7b821e7942b9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 60.6302041, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:hearthglen"] = {
  hash = "c5311f",
  fullHash = "c5311f",
  text = "Hearthglen. Tucked away in the northern hills of the Western Plaguelands, Hearthglen is a fortified settlement that remarkably escaped complete physical destruction during the fall of Lordaeron. Today, its sturdy stone walls, towers, and surrounding farmlands serve as the primary northern stronghold and training ground for the fanatical Scarlet Crusade. While neighboring Andorhal lies shattered in skeletal ruin, Hearthglen still resembles a living town of Lordaeron, complete with a lumber mill, scout towers, stables, and a functioning mine. Historically governed from Mardenholde Keep by Lord Tirion Fordring, Hearthglen was renowned for its prosperity and staunch defense during the Second War. When the Plague of Undeath swept Lordaeron during the Third War, Prince Arthas Menethil intercepted tainted grain here, purging the infected townsfolk and repelling a massive Scourge siege alongside Uther the Lightbringer. Through that desperate intervention, Hearthglen's formidable walls survived intact. After the Silver Hand fell into disarray following the kingdom's collapse, Tirion's son, Taelan Fordring, renounced the order and joined the Scarlet Crusade, and the grief and zealotry that birthed the Crusade soon consumed the fortress. Now, Hearthglen is a fortress of religious extremism. Scarlet crusaders patrol its approaches and man its ramparts, violently executing any outsider, living or undead, who trespasses on their land. Highlord Taelan Fordring rules from Mardenholde Keep, a devout servant to the Crusade's uncompromising war against the Plaguelands.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_hearthglen-881326d9be4f49b5f402/1-0000.mp3", duration = 99.4742857, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:hetaera-s-clutch"] = {
  hash = "1da21d",
  fullHash = "1da21d",
  text = "Hetaera's Clutch. Hetaera's Clutch is a sunken night elven temple dedicated to Elune, nestled deep beneath the churning waters of Azshara's Bay of Storms. Once a sacred site of devotion, the submerged ruins are now overrun by deadly hydras hatched by their broodmother, Hetaera. Adventurers braving the depths will find ancient stonework claimed by vicious aquatic predators. Before the Great Sundering tore ancient Kalimdor apart, this structure served as a grand temple dedicated to the moon goddess Elune. Cast into the depths of the Bay of Storms, its stone pillars and sacred halls have spent ten thousand years beneath the sea. Today, all signs of holy veneration have been swallowed by the tides, leaving behind a solemn monument to the lost Kaldorei empire. The submerged sanctuary has taken on a far deadlier identity as the nesting ground of Hetaera, a massive multi-headed hydra who stalks the nearby coastal waters and rocky isles. Her numerous offspring, the Spawn of Hetaera, prowl through the ruined arches and corridors of the drowned temple, attacking any surface-dweller bold enough to dive into the bay.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_hetaera-s-clutch-1ac9ee8db6345aa8a86b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 66.2987755, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:hidden-grove"] = {
  hash = "56a68e",
  fullHash = "56a68e",
  text = "Hidden Grove. Tucked away behind towering cliffs in northeastern Winterspring, the Hidden Grove is a secluded crater basin. Once an untroubled haven for Arctic hares, the basin has fallen under the domain of fiercely territorial wild moonkin. Walled off from the bitter howling winds of Winterspring, the Hidden Grove forms a sunken, snow-dusted forest crater that is nearly impossible to access from the outside cliffs. Travelers who discover its concealed rim must brave a sheer, dangerous drop or navigate a narrow cavern passage tucked into the southeastern ridge. Though the valley was long the undisturbed home of harmless white hares, aggressive wild moonkin have seized the glade, turning an isolated natural paradise into an ambush for unwary wanderers.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_hidden-grove-6f3557c365c1775c9c6d/1-0000.mp3", duration = 46.7069388, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:high-wilderness"] = {
  hash = "c281ef",
  fullHash = "c281ef",
  text = "High Wilderness. The High Wilderness is an expansive, untamed stretch of primordial forest covering much of western Feralas. Bordered by the Verdantis River to the east and the Forgotten Coast to the west, it shelters a wide array of wildlife, wandering treants, and dangerous beasts beneath its towering emerald canopy. Stretching across the western half of Feralas, the High Wilderness represents the quintessential untamed wilderness of southern Kalimdor. Thick foliage and colossal trees block out much of the sky, creating a lush expanse teeming with wolves, bears, hippogryphs, and yetis. Ancient treants wander among the roots, while aggressive ogre clans establish footholds in the shaded clearings. Navigating this vast green expanse requires vigilance, as the dense brush hides both natural predators and lingering primeval hazards.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_high-wilderness-8fd5c55e4891ab916438/1-0000.mp3", duration = 50.5730612, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:hive-ashi"] = {
  hash = "93c9da",
  fullHash = "93c9da",
  text = "Hive'Ashi. Hive'Ashi is a sprawling silithid colony dominating the northern sands of Silithus. As one of three major insectoid hives in the region, its creeping tunnels and towering mounds churn with countless hostile workers, defenders, and stingers. Situated in northern Silithus, Hive'Ashi serves as a prime hive-complex for the re-emerging silithid swarms. Its chitinous spires carve out a distinct territory among the dunes, sending foraging workers and venomous defenders south and east toward the Swarming Pillar. Anyone seeking to understand the strange, coordinated intelligence stirring within the desert wastes inevitably finds their path leading directly into the dangerous, buzzing tunnels beneath these mounds. Cenarion scouts stationed across Silithus monitor Hive'Ashi closely, commissioning brave combatants to thin its ranks and retrieve reconnaissance reports from deep within the colony. The glyphs and coordinated behavior documented within Ashi suggest that these mindless-looking insects share an eerie, singular will. As their numbers swell, the hive threatens to spill beyond northern Silithus entirely, making the eradication of its brood a top priority for those watching the southern borders.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_hive-ashi-fefc2d2810f155cfda6e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 73.0906122, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:hive-regal"] = {
  hash = "792a31",
  fullHash = "792a31",
  text = "Hive'Regal. Hive'Regal is a massive silithid hive burrowed into the southern reaches of Silithus. As one of the three primary insectoid colonies dominating the arid wastes, it teems with aggressive silithid castes that threaten to overrun the surrounding desert. Sprawling across southern Silithus, Hive'Regal represents one of the greatest concentrations of silithid activity in Kalimdor. Its towering resin spires and cavernous underground tunnels churn with dangerous insectoid broods, from venomous spitfires and burrowers to predatory slavemakers. The hive acts as a coordinated warren, aggressively defending its perimeter and harvesting organic material from any unfortunate travelers who wander too close to its borders. Beneath the red sands, Hive'Regal's labyrinthine passages delve deep into the earth. Strange glyphs etched near its entrance and deep inside the tunnels suggest an organized intelligence directing the swarm rather than mere feral instinct. Scholars and scouts studying the silithid threat believe that Hive'Regal, along with its sister hives, functions as part of a larger emergent power stirring beneath the wastes of Silithus.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_hive-regal-5fbbd2ca7a82ce1e67dc/1-0000.mp3", duration = 69.3812245, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:hive-zora"] = {
  hash = "e4ed19",
  fullHash = "e4ed19",
  text = "Hive'Zora. Hive'Zora is one of the three major silithid colony mounds dominating the arid expanse of Silithus. Located along the western edge of the desert, this teeming subterranean hive crawls with aggressive insectoid castes serving the greater swarm. Rising out of the barren sand dunes of western Silithus, Hive'Zora serves as a bustling hub of insectoid activity. Great, porous stone spires and mounds mark the entrances into deep subterranean tunnels dug out by silithid burrowers. Waywatchers and reavers patrol the perimeter relentlessly, while tunneling castes and hive sisters brood within the depths below. For the Cenarion Circle and other scouts keeping watch over the desert, the hive represents a terrifying sign of the insectoid menace steadily reclaiming the ancient frontier.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_hive-zora-c18d7f5066f945086853/1-0000.mp3", duration = 48.5355102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:horizon-scout"] = {
  hash = "4cc929",
  fullHash = "4cc929",
  text = "Horizon Scout. The Horizon Scout is a sunken vessel resting on the ocean floor in the Ruined Reaches off the southern coast of Azshara. While its captain remains stranded ashore, several crew members remain trapped in the submerged hull, kept alive beneath the waves by an arcane water-breathing enchantment. Resting beneath the waves among the treacherous shoals of the Ruined Reaches, the Horizon Scout met a violent end off Azshara's jagged coast. While Captain Vanessa Beltis managed to reach land on a nearby peninsula, a portion of the crew went down with the hull. Miraculously, the survivors inside have not drowned; a gnome named Roland Geardabbler maintains a sustained magical bubble of water breathing that preserves them within the wreckage. Surrounded by hostile aquatic predators and predatory naga, the remaining sailors wait in the dark forward compartments, unable to surface on their own. Evidence aboard the ship hints at the sinister forces behind its destruction. In the forward stairwell lies a small shard of raw Azsharite, a mysterious mineral unique to these shores. The Spitelash naga prowling the waters around the Ruined Reaches likely struck the Horizon Scout to plunder whatever artifacts or resources the expedition carried, dragging the vessel down to ensure no survivors could escape with secrets or treasures of the coast.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_horizon-scout-1d150445152878cfe68b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 78.3412245, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:ice-thistle-hills"] = {
  hash = "c058fe",
  fullHash = "c058fe",
  text = "Ice Thistle Hills. The Ice Thistle Hills are a snow-covered highland rise lying just southeast of the goblin outpost of Everlook. Dominated by a rugged cavern system, the area is claimed by ferocious Ice Thistle yetis that threaten any travelers venturing off the main road. Rising sharply out of the central drifts of Winterspring, the Ice Thistle Hills serve as the primary hunting grounds and nesting territory for the valley's aggressive Ice Thistle yetis. Centered around a prominent cave dug deep into the rockface, these hulking beasts roam both the rocky slopes and the winding tunnels within. Because of their proximity to Everlook, the yetis present a constant nuisance and danger to goblin commerce and visiting prospectors, who regularly commission intrepid hunters to cull the beasts for their valuable thick pelts and pristine horns.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_ice-thistle-hills-26de5e00cf29454d8bfe/1-0000.mp3", duration = 46.3412245, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:infectis-scar"] = {
  hash = "443c54",
  fullHash = "443c54",
  text = "Infectis Scar. The Infectis Scar is a deep, unnatural chasm carved into the blighted terrain of the Eastern Plaguelands near Corin's Crossing. Unlike natural ravines, this wound in the earth was ripped open by necromantic power when a cursed runeblade shattered against a dwarven shield. The blight festering at the bottom of the fissure remains an enduring testament to the Scourge's dark sorcery. While neighboring gorges like the Pestilent Scar originated as natural terrain features poisoned by the plague, the Infectis Scar is entirely artificial. It was created during the third war's violent clashes near Corin's Crossing. When the notorious death knight Marduk Blackpool clashed with a stalwart dwarf captain, Marduk's cursed runesword shattered against the captain's shield. Discarding the broken weapon, Marduk kept the hilt, but the severed runeblade sank deep into the soil. Its foul enchantment violently tore open the crust of the earth, creating the jagged chasm that sits festering in the Plaguelands today.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_infectis-scar-03b906e7c82880696ef9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 60.16, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:ironforge"] = {
  hash = "ee2d26",
  fullHash = "ee2d26",
  text = "Ironforge. Ironforge's Great Forge burns beneath the mountain, at the heart of dwarven rule and Alliance trade. Deep inside Ironforge Mountain in Dun Morogh, the city is the political and mercantile anchor of Khaz Modan and a bulwark of the Alliance. Ironforge fills the deep halls of Ironforge Mountain, its stone rings circling the Great Forge. The dwarves founded the city millennia ago, after awakening and migrating from Uldaman. It has weathered ages of history, including the ancient War of the Three Hammers, which left the Bronzebeard clan in sole control. Natural magma pours through the mountain to power the city, while massive stone gates guard its halls. Dwarven craftsmen and warriors share them with many exiled gnomes, who came after the recent fall of Gnomeregan. Ironforge is perhaps the Alliance's busiest center of commerce and industry. King Magni Bronzebeard rules Khaz Modan from the High Seat, while dwarven prospectors, miners, and engineers work alongside gnome refugees in Tinker Town. The Deeprun Tram runs beneath the mountains to Stormwind City, carrying trade and troops at high speed and keeping the capitals linked in mutual defense.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_ironforge-52688f3438463e36547d/1-0000.mp3", duration = 74.3444898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:ironstone-camp"] = {
  hash = "080693",
  fullHash = "080693",
  text = "Ironstone Camp. Ironstone Camp is an outpost established by the Horde along the western border of the Shimmering Flats in Thousand Needles. Originally deployed to expel Alliance dwarven excavators from the nearby digsite, the encampment's forces now face a deadly threat after subterranean silithid overran the excavation. Ironstone Camp sits directly on the threshold between the high pillars of Thousand Needles and the blazing salt expanse of the Shimmering Flats. The Horde detachment stationed here was initially dispatched with a clear and singular objective: dislodge the dwarven excavators digging at the Rustmaul Digsite. However, their military confrontation was cut short when a swarm of voracious silithid burst forth from a nearby cavern, violently engulfing the dig and attacking both sides. Led by Moktar Krin, the surviving camp defenders have diverted their efforts to combating the insectoid infestation before the swarm spreads unchecked across the region.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_ironstone-camp-3f574a436f6ef6cce88d/1-0000.mp3", duration = 57.4955102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:irontree-woods"] = {
  hash = "e7b397",
  fullHash = "e7b397",
  text = "Irontree Woods. Irontree Woods is a blighted grove situated in northern Felwood, once cherished by the night elves as a peaceful forest sanctuary. Ravaged by the demonic corruption of the Third War, its ancient protectors have been petrified into stone while twisted treants and elementals prowl among the ruins. Irontree Woods was once a tranquil woodland glade tended by the kaldorei, but the demonic taint of the Burning Legion during the Third War choked the life from its soil. The grove's guardian Ancients—Hastat, Stoma, and Vartrus—succumbed to the dark magic, petrifying into lifeless stone monuments that still stand entombed amid the corrupted brush. Now, maddened treants, vile bog beasts, and tainted water elementals wander the twisted forest floor, striking violently at any travelers who dare approach.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_irontree-woods-8dcf8cc8718600d03ad5/1-0000.mp3", duration = 52.0881633, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:isle-of-dread"] = {
  hash = "11bca3",
  fullHash = "11bca3",
  text = "Isle of Dread. The Isle of Dread is a desolate, cliff-bound island situated in the Great Sea off the southwestern coast of Feralas, south of Sardor Isle. It is notorious for sheltering monstrous two-headed chimaeroks and an entrenched coven of hostile naga hidden within its subterranean sea caves. Rising out of the crashing surf of the South Seas, the Isle of Dread lives up to its ominous name. Ringed by sheer cliffs, the island's interior valley is only accessible through a narrow pathway on its southeastern coast. Within this enclosed highland roams a lethal population of chimaeroks—ravenous, two-headed beasts empowered by ambient arcane energies. These beasts are led by the monstrous Lord Lakmaeran, making the island a deadly deathtrap for unwary explorers who cross the channel from Sardor Isle. While the inland plateau belongs to the chimaeroks, the island's northern shore conceals a cavern system occupied by the Hatecrest naga. Led by Lord Shalzaru, these serpent-folk lurk within deep submerged chambers, plotting against the night elven sentinels stationed at nearby Feathermoon Stronghold. Their subterranean lair includes hidden underwater passages that wind into secluded grottoes, serving as a heavily fortified outpost away from mainland scrutiny.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_isle-of-dread-540352e931647f22d2a0/1-0000.mp3", duration = 77.3485714, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:itharius-s-cave"] = {
  hash = "918520",
  fullHash = "918520",
  text = "Itharius's Cave. Itharius's Cave is a secluded hollow tucked into the far southwestern cliffs of the Swamp of Sorrows. It serves as the refuge of the enigmatic Itharius, who appears to be a high elf and tends to the brood of green dragonkin inhabiting the surrounding marsh. Tucked away in the treacherous, moss-draped crags of the southwestern Swamp of Sorrows, this damp cavern shelters the recluse Itharius. Outside its mouth, unruly green whelps wander the mire. Though he appears to be a high elf, a folk who rarely wander so far south into wild swamps alone, Itharius keeps a watchful eye on these young dragonkin as they grow, living alongside them in quiet isolation.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_itharius-s-cave-0f64a364b26f3f55bd39/1-0000.mp3", duration = 42.5795918, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:jadefire-run"] = {
  hash = "94edf8",
  fullHash = "94edf8",
  text = "Jadefire Run. Jadefire Run is a heavily fortified satyr encampment nestled in northern Felwood. It houses some of the most powerful and cunning satyrs in the region, serving as a bastion of demonic corruption deep within the tainted forest. Situated amid the blighted woods of northern Felwood, Jadefire Run functions as a stronghold for the Jadefire satyrs. Unlike lesser outposts scattered throughout the valley, this camp features substantial demonic construction and shelters formidable spellcasters and tricksters. The satyrs here actively conspire to spread corruption further across Kalimdor, employing dark sorcery and foul trickery against anyone who dares intrude on their territory.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_jadefire-run-ac3bf6d71ffb3a60f7c8/1-0000.mp3", duration = 42.6318367, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:jaedenar"] = {
  hash = "6ef9f4",
  fullHash = "6ef9f4",
  text = "Jaedenar. Jaedenar is a blighted hollow in central Felwood built over ancient night elf ruins and druidic barrow dens. Named in foul homage to the demon lord Kil'jaeden, it serves as the primary base of operations for the Shadow Council in northern Kalimdor. Cultists, warlocks, and summoned demons scour the corrupt hollow, guarding the subterranean descent into the Shadow Hold. Long ago, these hollows sheltered night elf sacred grounds and slumbering druids connected to the Emerald Dream. After the devastation of the Third War, the vile remnant of the Shadow Council laid claim to the valley, christening it Jaedenar to honor the demon lord Kil'jaeden. Warlocks—predominantly renegade orcs and Forsaken—have gathered here alongside satyrs, succubi, and felguards. They occupy both the surface ruins and the deeper barrow dens, twisting what was once holy woodland into a grim center of demonic invocation. Above ground, the cultists gather at profane sites like the Shrine of the Deceiver, burning braziers of fell flame and conducting rituals to further corrupt the timberland. Beneath the surface lies the entrance to the Shadow Hold, the subterranean stronghold where the council's true masters direct their operations across Felwood. The Emerald Sanctuary watches the festering enclave with profound concern, regularly enlisting daring wanderers to disrupt their summoning circles and douse the dark flames that seal their tunnels.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_jaedenar-18e6aa0cb8c8b350d670/1-0000.mp3", duration = 89.1820408, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:jagged-reef"] = {
  hash = "3fce65",
  fullHash = "3fce65",
  text = "Jagged Reef. The Jagged Reef forms the rugged northern coast of Azshara, where sandy beaches meet jagged stone spurs and churning surf. Treacherous rocks lurk beneath the shallow water, making maritime travel impossible. The desolate shore is primarily populated by prowling hydras, scavenging makrura, and territorial murloc tribes. Stretching across Azshara's northern edge, the Jagged Reef is a harsh littoral expanse where broken cliffs and jutting stone outcroppings divide the sandy shores into secluded inlets. Beneath the breaking waves lie submerged crags and shifting shallows, turning the waters into a graveyard for any vessel foolish enough to venture near. On land, the wild coastline offers little comfort; salt-crusted beasts, predatory hydras, and aggressive makrura wander the surf line alongside entrenched murlocs scouring the tides for scraps and unwary prey.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_jagged-reef-82d818412376eb6b7a8a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 55.4057143, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:jaguero-isle"] = {
  hash = "da2934",
  fullHash = "da2934",
  text = "Jaguero Isle. Jaguero Isle is an isolated, jungle-covered island resting off the eastern coast of Stranglethorn Vale's Wild Shore. Dominated by fierce wild panthers and gorillas, it is infamous as the domain of the colossal silverback gorilla known as King Mukla. Situated off the southeastern coast of Stranglethorn Vale, Jaguero Isle stands well apart from the mainland pirate camps and troll ruins. The island is entirely overrun by untamed fauna, mainly panthers and aggressive gorillas. Looming over them all is the gargantuan ape King Mukla, whose rule over the rocky interior is absolute and territorial. Few sailors willingly approach its reefs, though occasionally washed-up bottles along the mainland beaches carry desperate pleas for rescue from those unfortunate enough to be stranded ashore.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_jaguero-isle-43739ba460d7ae85dc61/1-0000.mp3", duration = 47.4644898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:janeiro-s-point"] = {
  hash = "47a743",
  fullHash = "47a743",
  text = "Janeiro's Point. Janeiro's Point is a small, rocky island situated just outside the harbor of Booty Bay in southern Stranglethorn Vale. It is crowned by a colossal stone statue of an unknown goblin welcoming incoming maritime traffic with outstretched arms. The island's waters and shores are dominated by the menacing sea giant known as Mok'rash the Cleaver. Standing as the monumental gateway to Booty Bay, Janeiro's Point greets every vessel charting a course into the southern seas of Stranglethorn Vale. Atop the crag looms an immense stone effigy of a goblin, arms spread wide in a grand gesture of welcome and unbridled mercantile ambition. Though visible to sailors and corsairs for miles around, landing on the island's shores is a perilous prospect due to Mok'rash the Cleaver, a terrifying sea giant who has claimed the outcrop as his territory.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_janeiro-s-point-7cfd088bbb7f4a548fa7/1-0000.mp3", duration = 55.04, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:karazhan"] = {
  hash = "df931e",
  fullHash = "df931e",
  text = "Karazhan. Karazhan is the ominous, secluded tower standing at the southern end of Deadwind Pass. Once the sanctum of Medivh, the Last Guardian of Tirisfal, the citadel sits atop a potent convergence of ley lines that warps time, space, and reality around it. Though Medivh was slain during the First War, the abandoned fortress still hums with volatile arcane energy, restless spirits, and hidden dangers. Long before the First War, the tower of Karazhan was raised upon ground shaped like a human skull, situated at a massive nexus of mystical ley lines. It served as a secluded refuge and repository of untold magical secrets for the Guardian Medivh. Within its curving halls, Medivh lived alongside his loyal castellan Moroes, entertaining noble guests and housing an immense archive of arcane texts and relics that eclipsed even the libraries of the Kirin Tor. Yet the tower was also the stage for Azeroth's greatest betrayal, as Medivh fell under the demonic possession of Sargeras and secretly orchestrated the opening of the Dark Portal. Following Medivh's death at the hands of Anduin Lothar and his apprentice Khadgar, Karazhan sealed its gates and was left to decay. However, the tower has never truly slept. Its intersection of potent ley lines continues to warp the fabric of reality, casting ghostly echoes, illusions, and temporal distortions across Deadwind Pass. Rumors persist that an evil presence has begun stirring inside the quiet fortress once more, and strange entities—including the shadowy Dark Riders who scour the surrounding lands for forgotten relics—are inexorably drawn to its towering silhouette.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_karazhan-87096900344e0a42c944/1-0000.mp3", duration = 104.986122, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:kargath"] = {
  hash = "9a30bb",
  fullHash = "9a30bb",
  text = "Kargath. Kargath is a fortified Horde outpost perched in the western Badlands, serving as their sole foothold in Khaz Modan. Established by the Kargath Expeditionary Force under Warchief Thrall's orders, the settlement anchors crucial transit lines across the Eastern Kingdoms while staging operations toward Blackrock Mountain and the excavation at Uldaman. Named in honor of the Shattered Hand chieftain Kargath Bladefist, the outpost of Kargath stands as the Horde's solitary settlement throughout the mountain realms of Khaz Modan. Surrounded by arid wastes, aggressive wildlife, and hostile dwarven factions, the garrison provides a vital midway sanctuary for travelers crossing the continent. Its flight master bridges the long divide between northern holdings like the Undercity and Hammerfall and southern redoubts like Stonard and Grom'gol Base Camp. Dispatched on direct orders from Warchief Thrall, the Kargath Expeditionary Force maintains a dual objective in the region. Troops make frequent forays into the ancient titan halls of Uldaman to uncover why Ironforge's Prospectors prize the dig site so dearly. Concurrently, scouts labor to carve a path through the Searing Gorge into Blackrock Mountain, seeking to strike at Rend Blackhand's false 'Dark Horde' while contending with the entrenched Dark Iron dwarves.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_kargath-847d37c0969566711214/1-0000.mp3", duration = 80.6922449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:kodo-graveyard"] = {
  hash = "b02147",
  fullHash = "b02147",
  text = "Kodo Graveyard. The Kodo Graveyard is a sprawling expanse in central Desolace littered with the massive, bleached remains of ancient beasts. By instinct or tradition, aging and dying kodos make a final pilgrimage to this desolate hollow to collapse among the ribs of their ancestors. Sprawling across the grey earth of Desolace, the Kodo Graveyard holds the colossal skeletons of ancient beasts, some far larger than any living kodo seen today. Dying kodos wander here to spend their final breaths among centuries of accumulated remains. This sacred peace is fragile: scavengers prowl the perimeter, and those who disturb the calcified ribs and skulls risk stirring furious kodo spirits from the dust. The boundary between the living and the dead wears exceptionally thin across the bone fields. Travelers who peer into the spirit realm find restless, towering kodo apparitions wandering silently among the dust storms. Nearby, the Horde maintains Ghost Walker Post, watching over the sacred grounds and keeping a wary eye on the wandering herds and spectral manifestations that linger around the bones.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_kodo-graveyard-658e8d2ab2746efb9734/1-0000.mp3", duration = 65.802449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:kormek-s-hut"] = {
  hash = "fafb90",
  fullHash = "fafb90",
  text = "Kormek's Hut. Kormek's Hut is a remote, ramshackle dwelling sitting amid the barren flats of north-central Desolace. Despite its desolate surroundings and mysterious namesake, it currently shelters an eccentric pair: an oddly peaceful cultist and a goblin obsessed with harvesting kodo bones. Perched out in the arid wastes of north-central Desolace, this small hovel is named after a figure whose whereabouts and identity remain completely unknown. Instead of Kormek, travelers stumbling upon the hut encounter an unusual pairing living out in the middle of nowhere: Willow and Bibbly F'utzbuckle. Inside the hut sits Willow, a member of the otherwise hostile and nihilistic Twilight's Hammer who curiously treats outsiders with calm hospitality. Outside, a pen littered with sun-bleached kodo bones serves as the personal dance floor and staging ground for Bibbly, an excitable goblin captivated by the commercial potential of bone scavenging.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_kormek-s-hut-09c7d1caf58ab36ba370/1-0000.mp3", duration = 57.7828571, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:kurzen-s-compound"] = {
  hash = "957018",
  fullHash = "957018",
  text = "Kurzen's Compound. Kurzen's Compound is a heavily fortified human settlement tucked into the hills of northern Stranglethorn Vale. Established by an expedition dispatched from Stormwind under Colonel Kurzen, the outpost has devolved into a rogue camp of cutthroats and wild beasts following their commander's descent into madness. What began as an official expedition into the untamed jungles of Stranglethorn turned into a nightmare when Colonel Kurzen lost his mind. Rather than fulfilling Stormwind's mission, the garrison broke away from the kingdom's authority, cutting ties with the crown and descending into tyranny. Today, the compound operates like a mercenary stronghold, complete with a functional lumber mill, stables for training captured Stranglethorn panthers and tigers as weapons of war, and an extensive cave complex known as the Stockpile where the rogue soldiers stash their plundered supplies. Not all of the expedition's original members followed their commander into savagery. When Kurzen's madness took hold, a faction of his men mutinied and fled west to form the Rebel Camp, sparking a bitter and bloody feud within the dense canopy. From their fortified compound, Kurzen and his loyal men ruthlessly hunt down the deserters, treating any outsider who steps into their jungle domain as an enemy of the expedition.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_kurzen-s-compound-2cb2a8d48bd7a8e78783/1-0000.mp3", duration = 76.6432653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:lake-elune-ara"] = {
  hash = "4b6ffb",
  fullHash = "4b6ffb",
  text = "Lake Elune'ara. Lake Elune'ara is the shimmering, sacred body of water that occupies the heart of Moonglade. Revering the Moon Goddess Elune, the lake serves as a place of spiritual reflection for the Cenarion Circle, though deep within its murky floor lies the dormant, corrupted demigod Omen. Framed by the moonlit groves of Moonglade, Lake Elune'ara provides serene waters said to be blessed by the White Lady herself. The lake lies at the spiritual center of the druidic haven, bordered by Nighthaven to the north and the shrine grounds of the Cenarion Circle. Initiates have long plumbed its tranquil depths to retrieve long-lost relics, attuning themselves to the currents of nature as they seek harmony with the land. Despite the tranquility on the surface, ancient peril rests beneath the gentle ripples. Centuried legends speak of the two-headed wolf demigod Omen, a revered ancient corrupted by the Burning Legion during the War of the Ancients. Defeated and cast into the lake bed long ago, Omen slumbers deep below the dark waters, an enduring reminder of nature's wounds.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_lake-elune-ara-982379f3891a0fd71af9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 68.6236735, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:lake-kel-theril"] = {
  hash = "323e5d",
  fullHash = "323e5d",
  text = "Lake Kel'Theril. Lake Kel'Theril is a vast, frozen lake nestled in the heart of Winterspring. Along its ice-locked shores stand ancient kaldorei ruins haunted by restless spirits who have lingered since the Great Sundering. Lake Kel'Theril is shrouded in eternal cold, its waters sealed under thick sheets of ice. Scattered across its surface and snowy banks are the shattered pillars and arches of ancient elven architecture. These are the remnants of Kel'Theril, once an outpost of the ancient Highborne civilization before the Great Sundering shattered Kalimdor. Rather than finding rest in death, the spirits of the fallen aristocracy wander the ice as tormented ghosts and specters, forever tethered to the tragic remnants of their former glory.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_lake-kel-theril-98eb5d01d2f6ca5d0f0e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 45.4008163, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:lake-mennar"] = {
  hash = "3f0ca5",
  fullHash = "3f0ca5",
  text = "Lake Mennar. Lake Mennar is a tranquil body of freshwater nestled amidst the shattered ruins of Azshara. It is remarkably renowned as one of the exceedingly rare havens outside of Northrend where members of the blue dragonflight reside. While the perimeter teems with protective blue dragonspawn, the shores of the lake itself remain strangely serene. Situated in the ancient, broken landscape of Azshara, Lake Mennar serves as a secluded gathering ground for the blue dragonflight. Aside from Mazthoril to the north in Winterspring, it is virtually unheard of to encounter blue dragons in such numbers across Kalimdor. Blue dragonspawn tirelessly patrol the ridges and approaches circling the basin, fiercely guarding the waters. Yet for any wanderer daring enough to slip through this vigilant ring of sentries, the calm waters of the lake offer unexpected quietude, shielded from the arcane turmoil and roaming beast packs common to the rest of the peninsula.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_lake-mennar-79ba7cb65746cf0bff30/1-0000.mp3", duration = 57.2865306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:lake-mereldar"] = {
  hash = "7502eb",
  fullHash = "7502eb",
  text = "Lake Mereldar. Lake Mereldar is a sprawling, contaminated body of water resting south of Corin's Crossing in the Eastern Plaguelands. Once part of the idyllic rural heartland of Lordaeron, its corrupted waters now spawn twisted elementals and rot beneath the lingering taint of the Scourge. Situated just south of the crossroads town of Corin's Crossing, Lake Mereldar was once a peaceful fixture of northern Lordaeron's countryside. Dotting its shores are the decaying ruins of an old homestead, an abandoned shack, and a dilapidated ferry that once carried local farmers and travelers across the gentle waters. With the coming of the Scourge and the plague of undeath, the lake fell into complete ruin. The surrounding soil withered, and the waters turned foul and poisonous. The unnatural blight seeping into Lake Mereldar has thoroughly infected the local ecosystem and the spirits tied to it. Blighted Surges—corrupted water elementals driven mad by necromantic residue—lurk in the shallows and surface without warning. Along the weed-choked banks, vicious beasts such as Plague Ravagers prowl the mud, preying on whatever living creatures stray too close to the dead shoreline.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_lake-mereldar-98070f0e227ad366e08f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 73.195102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:lake-nazferiti"] = {
  hash = "fc4ea4",
  fullHash = "fc4ea4",
  text = "Lake Nazferiti. Lake Nazferiti is a sprawling freshwater lake nestled in the dense jungle of northern Stranglethorn Vale. Fed by a vast river system stretching far north into the mountains, its waters are infested with ferocious crocolisks and snapping frenzies, while its shores host an industrial encampment operated by the goblin Venture Company. Lake Nazferiti sits along the Nazferiti River, one of the longest continuous waterways across the southern Eastern Kingdoms. The river traces its origins deep into the northern heights, tumbling down waterfalls from the Redridge Mountains and possibly originating as far inland as Deadwind Pass. Within Stranglethorn, the water widens into this murky lake, teeming with vicious predators like snapping river frenzies and ambush-hunting crocolisks that make casual swimming deadly. The natural balance of Lake Nazferiti is under siege by the goblins of the Venture Company. Setting up heavy operations along the lake's banks, these ruthless profiteers exploit the jungle's timber and resources with mechanical saws, drills, and clear-cutting crews. Their sprawling base pollutes the waterways and constantly clashes with local wildlife and wandering hunters braving the vale.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_lake-nazferiti-135e41668d01c42787a3/1-0000.mp3", duration = 73.5608163, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:lakkari-tar-pits"] = {
  hash = "d66985",
  fullHash = "d66985",
  text = "Lakkari Tar Pits. The Lakkari Tar Pits are a series of foul-smelling, bubbling pools of thick black sludge in northern Un'Goro Crater. Situated just below the cliffs of Marshal's Refuge, they act as a natural death trap for unwary beasts and an incubator for hostile tar elementals. The Lakkari Tar Pits dominate the low ground of northern Un'Goro Crater, directly south of Marshal's Refuge. Thick, reeking pools of pitch entrap anything careless enough to step into them, quickly bogging down beasts and adventurers alike. The protruding bones of hundreds of prehistoric creatures break the black surface, bearing witness to generations of animals sucked into the mire. Opportunistic scavengers circle the margins waiting for trapped prey to exhaust itself, while living tar beasts, lurkers, and creepers rise directly from the muck to batter interlopers.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_lakkari-tar-pits-8e57986a5cd6acfdf88e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 52.5322449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:land-s-end-beach"] = {
  hash = "85e66d",
  fullHash = "85e66d",
  text = "Land's End Beach. Land's End Beach is a desolate stretch of coastline at the far southern tip of Tanaris, effectively marking the southern edge of Kalimdor. Shielded by towering mountain ridges and vast ocean waters, it remains one of the most secluded and inaccessible shores on the continent. Tucked away behind the southern mountain ranges of Tanaris and the sealed titan gateway to Uldum, Land's End Beach represents the absolute southern edge of Kalimdor's mainland. Cut off from overland routes across the desert dunes, reaching this windswept coast requires swimming along the dangerous, fatigue-inducing open waters of the South Seas. The beach is a barren frontier of crashing surf, shifting sand, and quiet isolation, largely untamed by the cartels or desert explorers who dwell farther north around Gadgetzan. Beyond the breakers of the beach lie lonely, unnamed islets separated from the mainland by vast expanses of exhausting deep water. Navigating to these distant specks of rock is treacherous enough to push even seasoned swimmers to their physical limits. Few mortals have any reason to seek out the lonely waters at Kalimdor's end, leaving the beach and its surrounding shoals undisturbed beneath the blazing southern sun.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_land-s-end-beach-af6e8e04f79e5ae11cae/1-0000.mp3", duration = 75.2587755, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:lariss-pavilion"] = {
  hash = "97e983",
  fullHash = "97e983",
  text = "Lariss Pavilion. Lariss Pavilion is an ancient elven structure tucked into the wilderness of Feralas just north of Camp Mojache. While once a peaceful retreat of classical night elf architecture, the surrounding forest has grown dangerous due to corrupted dryads, and an exiled Highborne mage now bides his time within its shade. Standing quietly on the bluffs north of Camp Mojache, Lariss Pavilion showcases the graceful stonework and towering archways of the ancient kaldorei empire. In its prime, it served the great city of Eldre'Thalas, now known as Dire Maul. Today, it offers a sheltered overlook above the dense canopy of Feralas, but the peace here is frail: the once-gentle dryads wandering the surrounding glades have fallen to madness and corruption, turning fiercely hostile to anyone who draws near. The pavilion's most notable resident is the elusive elf Azj'Tordin. Having broken away from Prince Tortheldrin's decadent rule within Dire Maul, Azj'Tordin escaped the ruins through a secret passage connecting the pavilion directly to the ancient city's underbelly. However, his quiet exile has been disrupted: an erratic imp named Pusillin stole his treasured book of incantations, leaving the exile seeking outside aid to recover it.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_lariss-pavilion-6504c394d56be46b747a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 78.602449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:launch-bay"] = {
  hash = "f64f3d",
  fullHash = "f64f3d",
  text = "Launch Bay. Hostile leper gnomes and corrupted machines hold Launch Bay, deep in Gnomeregan beyond the Hall of Gears. The underground airfield once served gnomish flying machines and bombers; now the city’s former hangar is under enemy control. Launch Bay’s two tiers once housed Gnomeregan’s aerial fleet. Ramps from the upper walkway lead to a central platform where flying machines and heavy bombers remain parked, left behind before the city was evacuated. Crazed leper gnomes now roam the flight decks, maintaining and guarding the idle war machines in place of skilled pilots. Launch Bay also joins several parts of the complex. Ramps from its southeastern corridor descend into the Engineering Labs. On the lower deck, invading Dark Iron dwarves choke the southern passage. That tunnel offers a perilous route toward Tinkers' Court, deep in the city.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_launch-bay-9569bae2753a10b5481b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 52.0881633, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:lethlor-ravine"] = {
  hash = "5389e4",
  fullHash = "5389e4",
  text = "Lethlor Ravine. Lethlor Ravine is a jagged, rocky canyon winding through the eastern reaches of the Badlands. It serves as a perilous roost and breeding ground for the black dragonflight, swarming with aggressive whelps and mature drakes. Carved deep into the scorched plateaus of the eastern Badlands, Lethlor Ravine is one of the most hazardous stretches of the desert. The craggy canyon walls provide a natural aerie for the black dragonflight, housing nests of fierce dragon whelps guarded by larger, fire-breathing drakes. Few travelers dare approach the valley floor, as the shadows overhead constantly betray the flight of winged predators circling among the rocky spires. Beyond the scaly brood that claims it, Lethlor Ravine is marked by curious crystalline obelisks scattered throughout the crags. These crystalline formations are involved in a local quest chain. Their origin is not established.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_lethlor-ravine-8adfcc5677e91f13bf53/1-0000.mp3", duration = 55.275102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:library-wing-tyr-s-hand"] = {
  hash = "a7e310",
  fullHash = "a7e310",
  text = "Library Wing (Tyr's Hand). The Library Wing is a secluded hall inside Tyr's Hand Abbey, nestled within the Scarlet Crusade's heavily fortified redoubt in the Eastern Plaguelands. Once dedicated to holy study and contemplation, its shelves are now consumed by the zealous order's rigid dogma and propaganda. Members of the Crusade congregate here to study scripture and guard the sanctum's religious relics. Situated off the central nave of Tyr's Hand Abbey opposite the martial quarters, the Library Wing reflects the ideological core of the Scarlet Crusade. Within these quiet stone alcoves, priests and crusaders immerse themselves in theological tracts, militant doctrines, and writings twisted by years of relentless warfare against the Scourge. Rather than preserving open scholarship, the texts sheltered here fuel the Crusade's paranoia, reinforcing their conviction that all outsiders carry the taint of corruption. Beyond its collection of partisan scrolls and scriptures, the Library Wing is closely guarded by the Scarlet priesthood. Reaching its back rooms requires fighting through elite Scarlet defenders who treat the library as both a bastion of faith and an impenetrable fortress.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_library-wing-tyr-s-hand-6d2707b0ba504968a386/1-0000.mp3", duration = 71.3142857, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:light-s-hope-chapel"] = {
  hash = "1fd246",
  fullHash = "1fd246",
  text = "Light's Hope Chapel. Light's Hope Chapel is a sacred sanctuary situated in the eastern reaches of the Plaguelands, serving as the primary redoubt and headquarters for the Argent Dawn. Once a secluded shrine of the Church of the Holy Light in the Eastweald, it now stands as an enduring bulwark against the Scourge. Originally a chapel of the Church of the Holy Light within the verdant Eastweald, Light's Hope Chapel endured the fall of Lordaeron to become a vital shelter in a blighted realm. When the zealous extremists among the survivors splintered to form the Scarlet Crusade, more temperate knights and priests banded together to establish the Argent Dawn. Choosing this secluded, hallowed site as their forward base of operations, the Dawn opened its doors to all races and factions—Alliance and Horde alike—united in the common crusade to purge the Scourge and reclaim the northern lands.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_light-s-hope-chapel-bf2ce698a1b9b4474543/1-0000.mp3", duration = 51.0171429, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:loading-room"] = {
  hash = "33a42e",
  fullHash = "33a42e",
  text = "Loading Room. In Gnomeregan's depths, the Loading Room is a dangerous place to pass through. The ruined chamber once staged cargo for the city's transit networks, but radioactive fallout and rogue machinery now threaten anyone who enters. The Loading Room once handled cargo for Gnomeregan's underground transit network. Before the city fell, goods passed through its staging bay on their way across the tinker capital. Now the room is caught in the ruin that has overtaken Gnomeregan. Radioactive fallout, invading troggs, and failing machinery make the area perilous. Techbot, a rogue mechanized construct, guards the vicinity. Gnome survivors and Alliance reclaimers who try to secure the perimeter must face that danger.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_loading-room-fcbb4a4a1ba6ec17a598/1-0000.mp3", duration = 42.7363265, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:lordamere-internment-camp"] = {
  hash = "347762",
  fullHash = "347762",
  text = "Lordamere Internment Camp. Lordamere Internment Camp is a former Second War detention facility perched on the heights overlooking Dalaran. Once built to confine lethargic orcs, the camp is now occupied by surviving Dalaran magi and guards alongside a treacherous band of Forsaken defectors. Following the Second War, the Alliance constructed internment camps across the northern kingdoms to contain the defeated orcish Horde. Lordamere was unique as the sole camp established within the Alterac Mountains, placed under Dalaran's jurisdiction. With the orcs long since liberated or fled, the camp today functions under Warden Belamoore as an outpost for Dalaran remnants studying dangerous magical relics.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_lordamere-internment-camp-d9f6c9316d6b8e32b285/1-0000.mp3", duration = 41.3257143, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:lost-fleet"] = {
  hash = "16b431",
  fullHash = "16b431",
  text = "Lost Fleet. The Lost Fleet lies broken off the Wetlands’ northern coast, along Baradin Bay. The Kul Tiras Third Fleet was destroyed in the Second War by red dragons enslaved by the Dragonmaw clan, and its wrecks are haunted by cursed undead sailors. On its last voyage in the Second War, the Kul Tiras Third Fleet came under attack from red dragons enslaved by the Dragonmaw clan. Dragonflame engulfed its ships, and the Wave Mistress and the Intrepid crashed onto the shallows and reefs of northern Bluegill Marsh. Almost the entire contingent drowned or burned. First Mate Fitzsimmons was the lone living survivor.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_lost-fleet-55c90440c847844713d8/1-0000.mp3", duration = 38.164898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:lost-point"] = {
  hash = "47c3e6",
  fullHash = "47c3e6",
  text = "Lost Point. Lost Point is a dilapidated wooden watchtower standing in western Dustwallow Marsh, originally constructed by Theramore's forces as an advance scout post. It has since fallen into disrepair and slipped from the control of Lady Jaina Proudmoore's guard, now serving as a hideout for rogue deserters. Erected by the expeditionary forces of Theramore to monitor traffic and threats emerging from the Barrens, Lost Point has long outlived its intended military function. The outpost has decayed under the relentless moisture of the marsh, its timber rotting and its defenses abandoned by regular troops. Instead of garrison sentries, the tower and its immediate surroundings are occupied by human deserters who broke away from Theramore's service, finding shelter in the isolated wilderness far from the discipline of the city's commanders.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_lost-point-3aec845531ac407f9623/1-0000.mp3", duration = 49.7632653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:lost-rigger-cove"] = {
  hash = "9b12d4",
  fullHash = "9b12d4",
  text = "Lost Rigger Cove. Tucked away along the eastern cliffs of Tanaris, Lost Rigger Cove serves as the primary base of operations for the Southsea Pirates in the region. Shielded by towering desert mountains on three sides, this hidden port boasts docks, fortified encampments, and an active shipyard constructing new pirate vessels. Naturally fortified by sheer rock walls on almost every side, Lost Rigger Cove provides the Southsea Freebooters an ideal maritime stronghold on Kalimdor's southeastern edge. Here, pirate cutthroats, dockworkers, and brigands haul salvage and assemble fresh hulls right under the noses of Gadgetzan's authorities. The cove allows the Southseas to project power across the Great Sea while raiding trade routes between Ratchet, Booty Bay, and coastal settlements. The pirates do not hold their cove unchallenged. The goblins of Gadgetzan and nearby Steamwheedle Port resent the raiders on their doorstep and pay mercenaries to slaughter the Southseas, thin their ranks, and disrupt their operations.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_lost-rigger-cove-dd4f1e2efcb15556d95e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 65.28, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:lower-wilds"] = {
  hash = "26adae",
  fullHash = "26adae",
  text = "Lower Wilds. The Lower Wilds form the eastern threshold of Feralas, where the humid forests meet the arid canyons of Thousand Needles. The main road cutting through these woods connects Thalanaar at the border to the settlements further west, flanked by ancient greenery and unpredictable woodland creatures. Spanning the eastern reaches of Feralas, the Lower Wilds serve as the natural transition between the sun-baked ravines of Thousand Needles and the deep, primordial jungle to the west. Travelers entering the zone must traverse this stretch of forest along the main highway, past the elven outpost of Thalanaar guarding the mountain pass. The dense canopies here house ancient treants, alongside indigenous wildlife that makes straying from the beaten path perilous. Because it links the southern wilderness to the eastern territories, the Lower Wilds see frequent movement of travelers, scouts, and couriers. While the night elves hold the gateway at Thalanaar, the Horde maintains Camp Mojache further inland. The road winding through the Lower Wilds is therefore a critical lifeline for anyone moving supplies across southern Kalimdor, fraught with both nature's hazards and faction tensions.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_lower-wilds-2bd92cc0f1e189a5708c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 68.4146939, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:magram-village"] = {
  hash = "b8440a",
  fullHash = "b8440a",
  text = "Magram Village. Magram Village is the settlement of the Magram centaur clan, situated among foul yellow pools in southeastern Desolace. It serves as the primary base for the Magram as they wage relentless war against their hated rivals, the Gelkis. Sprawled across islands and shores around stagnant, sulfurous pools in southeast Desolace, Magram Village is a rugged settlement cobbled together with crude wooden bridges and hide tents. The Magram centaur dwell here in constant vigilance against neighboring clans. Known for their sheer brutality and deep-seated animosity toward the southern Gelkis clan, the Magram are willing to tolerate outsiders only if those travelers prove their worth by slaughtering their Gelkis enemies.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_magram-village-8b9d554b32dcc268297a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 42.1877551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:maker-s-terrace"] = {
  hash = "27a1f8",
  fullHash = "27a1f8",
  text = "Maker's Terrace. The Maker's Terrace is a sprawling outdoor excavation site situated in the northern Badlands, directly fronting the entrance to the ancient titan vault of Uldaman. Travelers venturing south from Loch Modan must navigate its rugged perimeter, which is aggressively occupied by Dark Iron dwarves. The dig represents a contested threshold between the surface wastes and the titan mysteries entombed below. Sprawling across the northern bluffs of the Badlands, the Maker's Terrace serves as the exterior staging ground for the subterranean halls of Uldaman. Long before travelers delve into the subterranean ruins, they must contend with the sun-scorched earthworks, scaffolding, and stone trenches of this grand excavation. It sits right along the pass coming down from Loch Modan, making it the first landmark greeting any traveler daring to venture into the arid wastes. The Terrace buzzes with violent industry as rival factions seek a way into the ancient secrets buried beneath the rock. The site is firmly under the boot of the Dark Iron clan, who patrol its ditches and perimeters with hostile vigilance. Drawn by the scent of titan relics and subterranean dominance, these subterranean dwarves dig incessantly into the hillside, seeking whatever armaments, records, or earthen relics the ancient Makers left behind. Anyone trying to reach the deep halls of Uldaman without clearing through these fortified sentries will quickly find themselves overwhelmed.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_maker-s-terrace-a0da3bc76d3d422ff4e3/1-0000.mp3", duration = 90.3836735, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:mannoroc-coven"] = {
  hash = "4faf94",
  fullHash = "4faf94",
  text = "Mannoroc Coven. Mannoroc Coven is a demon-infested ruin in southern Desolace, named after the pit lord Mannoroth. Settled among ancient night elf architecture, it stands as the largest stronghold of the Burning Legion in central Kalimdor. Cultists of the Burning Blade maintain active summoning portals here, filling the ash-choked land with felhounds, succubi, and infernals. Situated south of Ghost Walker Post and east of Shadowprey Village, Mannoroc Coven blankets the ancient night elf ruins in an oppressive, fel-tainted pall. The coven is commanded on the ground by Lord Azrethoc, alongside the Burning Blade warlock Jugkar Grim'rod. Together, they harness active summoning portals to pull demonic reinforcements straight into Kalimdor, including doomguards, infernals, and felhounds. Even as Horde-aligned travelers attempt to sever these links using relics like the Hand of Iruxos, new rifts continue to tear open through the scarred earth.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_mannoroc-coven-14540753bf4d8dd09c75/1-0000.mp3", duration = 59.8726531, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:mardenholde-keep"] = {
  hash = "fc5c6c",
  fullHash = "fc5c6c",
  text = "Mardenholde Keep. Mardenholde Keep is the imposing stone citadel overlooking Hearthglen in the northern reaches of the Western Plaguelands. Once the ancestral seat of Lord Tirion Fordring, it now serves as the regional command center for the fanatical Scarlet Crusade under the rule of his son, Highlord Taelan Fordring. Built as the administrative heart of the Hearthglen principality, Mardenholde Keep was long celebrated by the people of Lordaeron for having never breached during the Second War. From its council chambers, Lord Tirion Fordring governed the surrounding vales alongside his trusted advisors, maintaining peace until his controversial trial and exile for defending the orc Eitrigg. Following Tirion's fall, governance fell to his ambitious apprentice Barthilas and eventually passed to Tirion's son, Taelan, who inherited his father's domain on the eve of Lordaeron's destruction.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_mardenholde-keep-43aeec122d8828a59f37/1-0000.mp3", duration = 52.0097959, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:marris-stead"] = {
  hash = "7de516",
  fullHash = "7de516",
  text = "Marris Stead. Perched on a ridge in the southwestern Eastern Plaguelands, the Marris Stead was once a peaceful Lordaeron homestead. Following the devastation of the Third War, it now serves as the lonely, heavily defended redoubt of Nathanos Blightcaller, Champion of the Forsaken.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_marris-stead-a14bdf4e38e857ccc5d3/1-0000.mp3", duration = 17.7893878, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:marshal-s-refuge"] = {
  hash = "aafcef",
  fullHash = "aafcef",
  text = "Marshal's Refuge. Marshal's Refuge is a precarious, neutral haven tucked into the northern cliffs of Un'Goro Crater. Established by Williden Marshal and his expedition party, it serves as an emergency redoubt after the basin's primeval beasts routed their lower camps. When Williden and Hol'anyee Marshal led their expedition into Un'Goro Crater, they drastically underestimated the ferocity of its prehistoric denizens. After their initial base camps were repeatedly overrun by rampaging beasts, the battered survivors scrambled up into the cavernous northern cliffside to escape slaughter. Re-established as Marshal's Refuge, the camp clings high above the jungle floor, offering the only stable resting ground, basic supplies, and flight masters for weary explorers in the northern crater. Because Un'Goro Crater contains ancient biological wonders and mysterious crystal formations, Marshal's Refuge attracts a motley band of researchers, fortune seekers, and eccentric wanderers. Naturalists from many lands, including dwarven scholars and gnomish tinkers, share the crowded ledge alongside the surviving Marshal crew. From here, scouts strike out toward volcanic summits like Fire Plume Ridge or bubbling landmarks like the Golakka Hot Springs, hoping to catalog the primordial wilderness before it consumes them.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_marshal-s-refuge-d3d15ab9171e868c3680/1-0000.mp3", duration = 78.6285714, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:marshlands"] = {
  hash = "0f7e57",
  fullHash = "0f7e57",
  text = "Marshlands. The Marshlands is a sprawling, sodden marsh occupying the southeastern reach of Un'Goro Crater. Sitting directly at the base of the winding ramp down from Tanaris, it is often the first wilderness newcomers witness upon entering the primeval basin. Descending the steep trail from the arid expanse of Tanaris brings travelers directly into the humid mire of the Marshlands. Navigating anywhere else along the sheer crater rim invites a lethal fall, making this treacherous swath of wetlands the primary threshold into Un'Goro. Dense canopies, standing water, and mud conceal abundant packs of fierce raptors, including venomous varieties that stalk the reeds. Amid the damp clearings, explorers can find Blue Power Crystals scattered across the earth, unique to this quarter of the crater. Despite the constant danger of predators, several solitary wilderness experts maintain outposts across the marsh. Perched upon a prominent earthen mound, the tauren hunter Torwa Pathfinder keeps watch over the primeval fauna. Across the wetlands, the troll Mor'vek studies and trains native ravasaurs.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_marshlands-ce6b309d806cc8fae6a3/1-0000.mp3", duration = 67.5526531, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:master-s-cellar"] = {
  hash = "7f9b05",
  fullHash = "7f9b05",
  text = "Master's Cellar. The Master's Cellar is a sprawling subterranean complex situated beneath the ruined settlement south of Karazhan in Deadwind Pass. Once serving Medivh's estate as wine cellars and storage vaults, it has descended into a haunted ruin teeming with the restless spectres of former servants and caretakers. Just south of the imposing tower of Karazhan lie the ruined remnants of a hamlet that once housed the servants, groundskeepers, and attendants who maintained Medivh's estate. Beneath these crumbled homes sits the Master's Cellar. Accessible through two separate entrances in the surface ruins, the complex expands far underground. One doorway opens into an extensive vaulted wine cellar, while the other descends through collapsed stonework into deep natural caverns. Whatever dark demise befell Karazhan and its master swallowed this basement as well, leaving the unliving remnants of caretakers, wailing spectres, and damned souls trapped in endless servitude. The deepest recesses of the complex, designated the Lower Cellar, reveal where the cellar floor gave way into the raw subterranean rock of Deadwind Pass. The cellar's foundations crumbled into these cavern systems, creating a jagged labyrinth that echoes with ghostly wails. While adventurers exploring the Pass rarely find living souls here, the pervasive magical corruption leaking from the nearby tower has firmly anchored these apparitions to the stone, making the caverns as perilous as the ruins above.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_master-s-cellar-a9a35679fa58f88f62dc/1-0000.mp3", duration = 90.3053061, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:maul-subzone"] = {
  hash = "c843ca",
  fullHash = "c843ca",
  text = "Maul (subzone). The Maul is a sunken fighting arena situated in the Broken Commons outside the ruins of Dire Maul in Feralas. Formerly a grand venue of ancient elven Eldre'Thalas, the pit is now an uncontrolled bloodsport ring where wanderers fight for glory or survival against fierce beasts and rival champions. Carved deep into the stone of the Broken Commons, the Maul once served the Highborne of Eldre'Thalas as a theater or ceremonial arena. Following the Sundering and the subsequent occupation by the Gordok ogres, the pit devolved into a raw colosseum. Like the notorious Gurubashi Arena in distant Stranglethorn Vale, entering the sands of the Maul suspends all allegiances, casting combatants into a lawless free-for-all where faction lines dissolve and survival depends purely on martial strength. Challengers who descend into the arena must mind their approach. While daring fighters often leap directly from the high ledges of the commons, the fall carries brutal impact. A safer western archway leads through tunnels lined with slumbering Gordok hyenas, providing the only foot exit out of the fighting floor. At irregular intervals, dangerous beasts and notorious gladiators emerge into the pit, making every descent an unpredictable trial of steel and instinct.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_maul-subzone-6f4a3d032f7fbe9ab724/1-0000.mp3", duration = 77.844898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:mazthoril"] = {
  hash = "13ef3e",
  fullHash = "13ef3e",
  text = "Mazthoril. Mazthoril is a massive, frostbitten cavern in central Winterspring that serves as the primary redoubt for the blue dragonflight in northern Kalimdor. Heavily guarded by dragonkin, spell-wielding wyrms, and arcane sentries, its winding ice tunnels conceal powerful magic and lead to the aerie of the reclusive blue matriarch Haleh. Carved into the frozen mountains just south of Everlook, Mazthoril houses an extensive brood answering to the Spell-Weaver, Malygos. The cavern and its surrounding crags are patrolled by vigilant dragonkin including Spellmaw, Azurous, and General Colbatann, while elite guardians watch the inner caverns. Intruders who wander into Mazthoril face dragonspawn thoroughly attuned to raw arcane energy, making any venture inside perilous without serious preparation.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_mazthoril-3d623feae909399386b0/1-0000.mp3", duration = 49.5281633, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:menethil-bay"] = {
  hash = "409c20",
  fullHash = "409c20",
  text = "Menethil Bay. Menethil Bay carries Alliance traffic along the western coast of the Wetlands. The sheltered waters open into Baradin Bay, with Menethil Harbor to the north and Dun Morogh's steep cliffs to the south. Crocolisks prowl the shallows, and sunken vessels lie below. Menethil Bay opens from the Wetlands into the Great Sea as part of Baradin Bay. Menethil Harbor lies to the north, Black Channel Marsh to the east, and Dun Morogh's coastal bluffs to the south. Crocolisks of many sizes prowl the shallow margins and marshy shores, making the water dangerous for travelers without a guide. The bay carries Alliance sea traffic, and near its mouth rests the ruined Flying Osprey, a warning of the wreckage beneath the surface.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_menethil-bay-23d38fffff612b1a4a2b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 44.0163265, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:menethil-keep"] = {
  hash = "9aa656",
  fullHash = "9aa656",
  text = "Menethil Keep. Menethil Keep commands the skyline above Menethil Harbor in the Wetlands. Four watchtowers guard the Alliance port, and Captain Stoutfist leads its militia from within the stone walls. Menethil Keep rises over Menethil Harbor’s wooden docks and the Wetlands marshes. Four stone watchtowers overlook the town and bay, watching for seaward raiders, renegades, and swamp beasts pushing into the area. Within the fortress, Captain Stoutfist directs the town militia and organizes patrols to defend the port and protect Alliance interests across the region.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_menethil-keep-fe3ab63d90c7a8b2e699/1-0000.mp3", duration = 35.8661224, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:mirage-flats"] = {
  hash = "d52a26",
  fullHash = "d52a26",
  text = "Mirage Flats. Mirage Flats is a barren, sun-scorched expanse nestled in the central wastes of the Badlands. Lying east of Dustbelch Grotto, this dry plateau is notorious for shimmering heat hazes and hungry predators. Situated east of Dustbelch Grotto, the Mirage Flats present travelers with an unforgiving stretch of cracked red dirt and shimmering heat waves that give the plain its name. Few civilized souls linger here; instead, the open flats are dominated by opportunistic scavengers and lethal beasts. Flocks of giant buzzards circle lazily on hot thermals above, watching for the leavings of ferocious ridge stalkers and pack-hunting crag coyotes that scour the brush for easy prey.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_mirage-flats-2ac7c35c753f66cbbbd7/1-0000.mp3", duration = 45.2963265, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:misty-reed-post"] = {
  hash = "69eb9c",
  fullHash = "69eb9c",
  text = "Misty Reed Post. Misty Reed Post is a rugged Horde encampment situated between Stagalbog and the Misty Reed Strand in the eastern reaches of the Swamp of Sorrows. Established as a frontline watch under the authority of Stonard, it serves to monitor the dangerous wetlands and the treacherous coastline. Tucked away in the humid gloom between the Stagalbog and the eastern shore, Misty Reed Post is little more than a spartan camp under Stonard's banner. It is manned by a handful of Stonard hunters, a shaman, Katar, and Tok'Kar, who oversees the watch. Conditions are rough, offering no fortified shelter from the marsh's hostile wildlife and lingering damp. Tok'Kar harbors quiet ambitions that the Horde command in Stonard will one day reinforce the position with a sturdy orcish burrow or watchtower, though for now, the scouts must hold the marsh with tents, campfire smoke, and sharpened steel.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_misty-reed-post-dcf2ea5ddcc145a7c9db/1-0000.mp3", duration = 54.282449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:misty-reed-strand"] = {
  hash = "84bef0",
  fullHash = "84bef0",
  text = "Misty Reed Strand. The Misty Reed Strand is the fog-shrouded, desolate eastern coastline of the Swamp of Sorrows. Lined with jagged rocks, washed-up wreckage, and creeping swamp mist, it is home to aggressive crabs, crocolisks, and coastal murloc tribes. Travelers venturing down to the surf often find themselves contending with the local wildlife for whatever resources wash ashore. Stretching along the entire eastern border of the Swamp of Sorrows, the Misty Reed Strand forms a damp barrier between the deep mire and the Great Sea. Heavy mists constantly roll in off the water, obscuring vision and muffling the cries of native wildlife. The beaches are littered with sea debris, driftwood, and decaying vegetation washed out from the bog inland. Because of its remote position and harsh climate, no formal permanent settlements hold the shoreline, leaving the sands to aggressive crustaceans, stalking crocolisks, and territorial murlocs who violently defend their foraging grounds. Both Alliance and Horde scouts periodically direct adventurers out to the strands, though neither faction claims the beach as sovereign territory. The Alliance often gathers driftwood and beach salvage here while attempting to thin the skittering crab population. Meanwhile, Horde outposts further inland keep a wary eye on the coastline's murloc encampments, sending seasoned fighters to cull their numbers before the amphibious raiders can push deeper toward Stonard or threaten marsh travel.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_misty-reed-strand-c4ecb11309e017a76268/1-0000.mp3", duration = 87.0661224, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:misty-shore"] = {
  hash = "6a1b86",
  fullHash = "6a1b86",
  text = "Misty Shore. Misty Shore is the quiet lakeshore of the Alterac Mountains, skirting the eastern shore of Lordamere Lake. Stretching between Dandred's Fold and the perimeter of Dalaran, it offers wide, foggy views of the Dawning Isles and the sinister silhouette of Fenris Keep. Running along Lordamere Lake, the Misty Shore stretches between Dandred's Fold and Dalaran. Thick, damp air regularly drifts off the lake, obscuring snapping turtles and the occasional zealous fanatic who wanders down from regional strongholds. From the banks of the Misty Shore, travelers look out across the waters toward the Dawning Isles and the imposing shadow of Fenris Keep. Because of its vantage point across Lordamere Lake and its proximity to both Dalaran and Scarlet-controlled pockets, travelers along the bank often keep a low profile to avoid unwanted attention.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_misty-shore-5b4972d48ea0480c0f90/1-0000.mp3", duration = 51.2783673, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:misty-valley"] = {
  hash = "3a4521",
  fullHash = "3a4521",
  text = "Misty Valley. Tucked away in the northwestern reaches of the Swamp of Sorrows, the Misty Valley is a shadowed, fog-drenched hollow north of the pass into Deadwind. The secluded basin is overrun by primordial swamp beasts, wandering swampwalkers, and tangled horrors. Deep in its rearmost cave dwells the colossal and terrifying Mire Lord. Nestled beneath the high cliffs separating the Swamp of Sorrows from Deadwind Pass, the Misty Valley is perpetually choked with thick marsh vapor. Unlike the open wetlands to the east, this isolated pocket is dense with colossal marsh elementals, including looming swampwalkers, ancient swampwalker elders, and creeping tangled horrors. At the far end of the misty gorge lies a cavern that serves as the lair of the Mire Lord, one of the most formidable natural threats haunting the bog.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_misty-valley-d6ef29ceb8301b898c24/1-0000.mp3", duration = 51.5395918, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:mizjah-ruins"] = {
  hash = "8a0353",
  fullHash = "8a0353",
  text = "Mizjah Ruins. The Mizjah Ruins are ancient stone remains nestled in northern Stranglethorn Vale, situated just southeast of Grom'gol Base Camp. Long abandoned by their original troll architects, the crumbling structures have been seized by the brute-strength Mosh'Ogg ogres. From this vantage, the ogres launch disruptive ambushes against passing supply caravans traveling toward the Swamp of Sorrows. Like many of the ancient stone ruins scattered throughout Stranglethorn Vale, the Mizjah Ruins were built by the Gurubashi Empire long before modern empires rose. Today, the moss-choked arches and cracked flagstones are occupied by the Mosh'Ogg clan, primarily brute warriors and crude witch doctors. Far from keeping to their stolen perimeter, these ogres routinely raid nearby supply convoys bound for the Horde settlement of Stonard, transforming ancient history into a persistent, bloody thorn in the side of Grom'gol's commanders.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_mizjah-ruins-fdaa53017aad154e704d/1-0000.mp3", duration = 57.7567347, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:moon-horror-den"] = {
  hash = "88d51a",
  fullHash = "88d51a",
  text = "Moon Horror Den. Moon Horror Den is a secluded, frost-rimed cave located just northeast of the goblin trading post of Everlook in Winterspring. Tucked against the jagged mountainside, the cavern serves as a nesting hollow for ferocious raging owlbeasts. Travelers must take care when venturing near its icy mouth, as these territorial wildkin fiercely defend their shelter. Situated only a short trek northeast from Everlook's guarded gates, the Moon Horror Den cuts deep into the mountain rock. The cave is home to powerful raging owlbeasts—formidable wildkin known for their brute strength and feral disposition in the harsh snows of Winterspring. Although geographically close to the furbolg settlement of Winterfall Village to the south, the sheer mountain face creates an impassable natural barrier, ensuring the den remains an isolated refuge for the wildkin rather than a corridor of travel.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_moon-horror-den-2456dbbdc19085a86383/1-0000.mp3", duration = 52.7934694, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:moonshrine-sanctum"] = {
  hash = "030c67",
  fullHash = "030c67",
  text = "Moonshrine Sanctum. Within Blackfathom Deeps, Twilight's Hammer gathers beneath Queen Azshara's statue. Twilight Lord Kelris watches over the ancient shrine, which guards the sealed way to Aku'mai's lair. Past the flooded Moonshrine Ruins, cultists gather in the Moonshrine Sanctum, the heart of Twilight's Hammer activity inside Blackfathom Deeps. Night elves built the hall millennia ago to honor the moon and their rulers. Now the cult has defiled it, yet carefully maintains the towering statue of Queen Azshara. Beneath the cavernous stone ceiling, Twilight Lord Kelris watches as the cultists chant dark rituals day and night.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_moonshrine-sanctum-83690410de4910b48ac2/1-0000.mp3", duration = 40.9861224, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:morgan-s-plot"] = {
  hash = "0e3ed9",
  fullHash = "0e3ed9",
  text = "Morgan's Plot. Morgan's Plot is a desolate burial ground and crumbling chapel tucked away just behind the tower of Karazhan in Deadwind Pass. Dominated by restless undead and brooding silence, it holds the forgotten crypts and final resting places of those once tied to Medivh's domain. Nestled against the sheer rocky slopes directly behind Karazhan, Morgan's Plot is a sorrowful, overgrown cemetery centered on a ruined chapel and underground crypts. Long before the land decayed into Deadwind Pass, the estate served the burial needs of the tower's inhabitants and nearby grounds. Following the downfall of the Last Guardian, Medivh, dark energies seeped into the earth, driving away all normalcy. Today, restless unliving wander the chapel grounds, haunting the headstones of servants and historic figures alike.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_morgan-s-plot-196fe61739e4eaaa247c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 50.9126531, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:morgan-s-vigil"] = {
  hash = "b59370",
  fullHash = "b59370",
  text = "Morgan's Vigil. Morgan's Vigil is a ruined Alliance redoubt nestled in the southeastern heights of the Burning Steppes. Once a small village, its charred homes and shattered wizard's tower now serve as an austere forward camp for scouts watching the passes to Blackrock Mountain. It remains the sole Alliance foothold in an ash-choked land dominated by Dark Iron dwarves and Blackrock orcs. Perched upon high stone overlooking the fiery wastes, Morgan's Vigil was once a modest settlement before the horrors of the Burning Steppes overran it. Today, charred timbers and the cracked shell of a ruined mage tower stand as grim reminders of the onslaught. The remaining defenders make do beneath rough canvas tents pitched amid the soot. Despite the ruin, the camp retains a gryphon roost, offering an unbroken aerial link back across the mountains to Stormwind City. It serves as a desperately needed haven where scouts, marshals, and passing adventurers catch their breath away from the marauding warbands below. The outpost draws its name and purpose from the vigilance kept against the looming threat of Blackrock Mountain. With the Dark Iron clan delving deep into dark sorceries and the Blackrock orcs claiming the surrounding wastes, the camp acts as a forward staging ground. Its defenders maintain the camp's defenses, coordinate scouting runs across the ash, and secure the vital pass leading south into Redridge.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_morgan-s-vigil-b9da6e052993ce3bb732/1-0000.mp3", duration = 86.1779592, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:morlos-aran"] = {
  hash = "277729",
  fullHash = "277729",
  text = "Morlos'Aran. Morlos'Aran is an ancient night elf ruin nestled in the southern reaches of Felwood. Once a proud and thriving settlement before the Great Sundering, it now stands as a grim monument to a lost golden age, drowned under the demonic blight that plagues the surrounding forest. Ten thousand years ago, prior to the Sundering, Morlos'Aran was a grand urban center of the ancient kaldorei civilization. Much like sister settlements such as Ameth'Aran, Bashal'Aran, and Ordil'Aran across northern Kalimdor, its marble arches and moonlit plazas bustled with elven life and arcana. When the Well of Eternity collapsed, the city broke, leaving only shattered colonnades behind as monuments to their civilization's zenith. While neighboring ruins in Darkshore and Ashenvale fell to decay and wilderness, Morlos'Aran met a far grimmer fate during the Third War. Positioned directly within the corridor trampled by the Burning Legion, its surviving foundations were choked by demonic corruption and toxic mire. Today, the ruins are haunted by wandering beasts, corrupted elementals, and remnants of the shadow forces lingering in Felwood.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_morlos-aran-bcd86fca1d44181b6e0a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 72.2285714, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:mosh-ogg-ogre-mound"] = {
  hash = "97e9e9",
  fullHash = "97e9e9",
  text = "Mosh'Ogg Ogre Mound. The Mosh'Ogg Ogre Mound is a fortified cavern complex in northern Stranglethorn Vale, situated just south of the ruins of Zul'Gurub. It serves as the primary stronghold for the brute Mosh'Ogg tribe in the jungle, dominated by their ruthless ogre mage chieftain, Mai'Zoth. Carved into the rugged jungle ridges south of Zul'Gurub, the Mosh'Ogg Ogre Mound swarms with brutish Mosh'Ogg maulers, shamans, and warmongers. The ogres seized the cavernous network as an ideal redoubt against hostile jungle trolls and prowling beasts. Deep within the twisting interior of the mound presides Mai'Zoth, the formidable two-headed ogre mage who commands the Mosh'Ogg clan. In his possession is the Mind's Eye, a potent and dangerous arcane relic that enhances his mental and sorcerous grip over his followers. Mai'Zoth's mastery over magic and raw brute force makes entering the deepest reaches of the mound a peril for any traveler braving northern Stranglethorn.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_mosh-ogg-ogre-mound-6bae229acb887ce015f6/1-0000.mp3", duration = 59.9771429, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:mosshide-fen"] = {
  hash = "6db38d",
  fullHash = "6db38d",
  text = "Mosshide Fen. In eastern Wetlands, Mosshide Fen is held by the Mosshide gnolls. Its primitive camps lie far from the main trade roads, where travelers face danger. Mosshide gnolls hold the tangled marsh of eastern Wetlands. Rough lean-tos and bone-strewn camps mark their territory. Gnawbone and other pack leaders command the tribe, which ambushes and devours travelers, scouts, and prospectors who enter its hunting grounds.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_mosshide-fen-d09bea9af28939bea8e6/1-0000.mp3", duration = 26.122449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:nat-s-landing"] = {
  hash = "08f5a6",
  fullHash = "08f5a6",
  text = "Nat's Landing. Nat's Landing is a modest fishing outpost nestled along the shores of Dustwallow Bay in Dustwallow Marsh. It serves as the personal camp and retreat of Nat Pagle, widely regarded as the most famous angler on Azeroth. Adventurers from across the world seek out this humble dock to hone their mastery of the fishing craft. Tucked away southwest of Theramore Isle along the murky waters of Dustwallow Bay, Nat's Landing is little more than a weathered campsite. It consists of a simple canvas tent, drying racks heavy with the day's catch, extra tackle hung from trees, and a rickety wooden pier jutting into the bay. Despite the mists and hazards of the swamp, this quiet patch of shoreline provides the legendary angler Nat Pagle with a solitary place to cast his line and enjoy a quiet drink away from civilization's clamor. Though unassuming at a glance, the landing is an essential stop for aspiring anglers. Fishermen who have exhausted the teachings of standard trainers in Ironforge, Orgrimmar, or elsewhere are directed here to prove their worth directly to Nat. Pagle cares nothing for politics or factional squabbles; any angler who braves the marshland predators to reach his dock must demonstrate patience and skill across the world's most perilous waters to earn his highest instruction.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_nat-s-landing-c6ad8e81769517f5b178/1-0000.mp3", duration = 79.7518367, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:nek-mani-wellspring"] = {
  hash = "79f15e",
  fullHash = "79f15e",
  text = "Nek'mani Wellspring. Perched high in the cliffs above the Southern Savage Coast, the Nek'mani Wellspring holds a fountain of remarkably pure holy water. It is renowned as the only pristine source of fresh water found south of Duskwood. However, this sacred site has recently been overrun by hostile naga explorers who jealously guard its flow. The Nek'mani Wellspring sits tucked away in the jagged coastal heights of western Stranglethorn Vale. Dispensing the only untainted freshwater south of Duskwood, the spring is hailed by various scholars and pilgrims as holy. Because differing cultures and traditions each claim its divine waters were blessed uniquely for their own kind, it remains a subject of intense theological fascination. Despite its spiritual renown, peaceful contemplation here is impossible: a vicious contingent of naga has recently seized the basin and guards the waters against all intruders.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_nek-mani-wellspring-d00851645c86fca78e84/1-0000.mp3", duration = 54.8571429, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:nesingwary-s-expedition"] = {
  hash = "105b5b",
  fullHash = "105b5b",
  text = "Nesingwary's Expedition. Nesingwary's Expedition is a rustic hunting encampment situated in northern Stranglethorn Vale, just north of Lake Nazferiti. Established by the famed dwarven marksman Hemet Nesingwary, the outpost serves as a staging ground for hunters seeking to test their mettle against the jungle's fiercest predators. Tucked alongside the river north of Lake Nazferiti, this remote encampment is a bustling haven for big game hunters, trackers, and marksmen. Hemet Nesingwary established the camp to stalk the legendary beasts of Stranglethorn Vale, surrounded by seasoned peers like Sir S. J. Erlgadin, Ajeck Rouack, and Barnil Stonepot. Guarded by hired marksmen and supplied by neutral vendors, the camp welcomes anyone capable of handling a rifle or bow, regardless of their allegiances, uniting them under the singular pursuit of tracking down the deadliest predators the jungle has to offer. Life in the expedition camp revolves around intense competition and grand tall tales. Hunters are continually challenged to master the tracking and slaying of the jungle's native panthers, raptors, and tigers, culminating in hunts for apex beasts. Aside from the thrill of the chase, the camp contends with its own small logistical disasters, notably the scattering of Nesingwary's sprawling manuscript across the wilds, leaving camp scholars eager to recover every missing chapter.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_nesingwary-s-expedition-6ad51933d5de9a2bed97/1-0000.mp3", duration = 82.9910204, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:nethander-stead"] = {
  hash = "6f58c6",
  fullHash = "6f58c6",
  text = "Nethander Stead. Nethander Stead lies abandoned along the road east of Southshore, overrun by Mudsnout gnolls. Once a human farm, its fields now hold giant white Mudsnout Blossoms that draw Forsaken alchemists. At Nethander Stead, ruined fences and outbuildings stand within sight of Southshore's eastern borders. The farm once formed part of Hillsbrad's working fields, but it was abandoned as security weakened and wild packs moved in. Now the Mudsnout gnolls make their only home in the foothills here. They drive off travelers who come too close to their territory.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_nethander-stead-11c84e28b5a268689d33/1-0000.mp3", duration = 35.2130612, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:nethergarde-keep"] = {
  hash = "62467f",
  fullHash = "62467f",
  text = "Nethergarde Keep. Nethergarde Keep is a fortified Alliance stronghold perched high upon the bluffs along the road entering the Blasted Lands. Raised by the Alliance after the Second War, it stands as a vigilant sentinel over the dormant Dark Portal and the chaotic energies bleeding across the red wastes. Today, mages and garrisons supported by Stormwind continue their lonely vigil against demons and lingering corruption. Following the destruction of the Dark Portal at the end of the Second War, Archmage Khadgar and the leaders of the Alliance of Lordaeron recognized that Azeroth could never again leave the rift unguarded. Builders labored to raise towering bastions, barracks, and mage spires along the canyon pass into the Blasted Lands. Staffed by Kirin Tor sorcerers, its dual purpose was to keep constant watch on the shattered gateway and weave wards preventing the demonic blight of the scorched landscape from creeping northward into the human kingdoms. When the Dark Portal reopened and the Alliance Expedition ventured into Draenor, Khadgar and his companions sealed the gateway behind them, leaving Nethergarde to guard a silent remnant. For two decades, scholars from Dalaran and the Wizard's Sanctum have analyzed the portal's lingering energies, mutated local fauna, and demonic incursions. With Dalaran ravaged during the Third War, Nethergarde relies entirely on vital supply caravans from Stormwind City to sustain its soldiers and mages as they stand alone against the horrors haunting the wasteland.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_nethergarde-keep-b329599771e7d18278c7/1-0000.mp3", duration = 91.1673469, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:nighthaven"] = {
  hash = "b821eb",
  fullHash = "b821eb",
  text = "Nighthaven. Nighthaven is a secluded settlement resting on the northern shores of Lake Elune'ara in Moonglade. Serving as the heart of the Cenarion Circle, it is a haven where night elf and tauren druids study together in peace beyond the factional wars of Azeroth. Built with traditional night elf architecture along the calm shores of Lake Elune'ara, Nighthaven is a tranquil retreat largely secluded from the outside world. Here, moonwells cast a gentle glow over groves and halls dedicated to preserving the natural balance. As the primary enclave of the Cenarion Circle, it provides trainers, herbalists, and specialized suppliers for druidic initiates. The settlement maintains a strict neutrality, welcoming practitioners regardless of whether their home loyalties lie with the Alliance or the Horde. Nighthaven's peace has been tested throughout history. Centuries after the War of the Ancients, champions gathered here to confront the enraged demigod Omen, driving him beneath the depths of Lake Elune'ara. The village also opens its secluded borders during the Lunar Festival, welcoming pilgrims from all over Azeroth to honor the ancestors and celebrate under Elune's watchful gaze.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_nighthaven-7bb56de47af0fa45d473/1-0000.mp3", duration = 71.7844898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:nijel-s-point"] = {
  hash = "e1e1dc",
  fullHash = "e1e1dc",
  text = "Nijel's Point. Nijel's Point is a fortified Alliance and Cenarion Circle redoubt perched high on the northern bluffs of Desolace. Tucked safely away from the blighted valleys below, it serves as the sole safe haven for Alliance travelers, researchers, and soldiers navigating the harsh wasteland. Carved into the sheer cliffs bordering the Stonetalon Mountains, Nijel's Point stands well above the gray dust and marauding centaur packs of Desolace. The settlement is held by a detachment of the Stormwind Army under Captain Pentigast, charged with monitoring the violent clan wars that threaten Alliance interests across southern Kalimdor. Its high elevation and narrow access trails make it readily defensible, providing a secure staging ground for couriers, scouts, and griffin riders travelling between Stonetalon Peak, Theramore, and Feathermoon Stronghold. Beyond the Stormwind garrison, the outpost shelters emissaries with distinct and perilous agendas. Keeper Marandis and his dryads use the camp as an operational base to confront Princess Theradras deep within Maraudon, seeking to recover the fallen demigod Zaetar. Even the Scarlet Crusade maintains an emissary here in Brother Anton, who seeks to scour the undead ravaging the valley floor. Despite the bustling presence of soldiers and druids, the identity of Nijel himself remains an ancient mystery lost even to those garrisoned atop the ridge.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_nijel-s-point-7d404f0d31713bd9c0b8/1-0000.mp3", duration = 86.0734694, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:northdale"] = {
  hash = "c0c06e",
  fullHash = "c0c06e",
  text = "Northdale. Northdale is a ruined settlement nestled north of Light's Hope Chapel in the Eastern Plaguelands, near the border of Quel'Thalas. Once a thriving northern township, it now lies broken and overrun by banshees, shadowy watchers, and corrupted elementals. It is most famous as the bitter crossroads where Taelan Fordring abandoned the Order of the Silver Hand to embrace the fanaticism of the Scarlet Crusade. Situated along the hills north of Light's Hope Chapel, Northdale was once a peaceful community along the northern highway leading toward Quel'Thalas. The Third War left it in shattered ruins, its hearths cold and its population slain or driven out. Today, wailing banshees drift through the crumbling masonry, while invisible Eyeless Watchers lurk in the dark to survey the cursed roads. Even the nearby lake has succumbed to the plague, its waters bubbling with twisted, corrupted water elementals.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_northdale-20838c7dcda0f800ff24/1-0000.mp3", duration = 55.0922449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:northfold-manor"] = {
  hash = "7a23ad",
  fullHash = "7a23ad",
  text = "Northfold Manor. Northfold Manor is a dilapidated agricultural estate situated on the northwestern fringes of the Arathi Highlands. Once a prosperous human homestead before the collapse of Stromgarde, the grounds are now occupied by criminal operatives of the Syndicate engaging in unsettling, dark practices. Perched on the rolling highlands near the Thoradin's Wall passage, Northfold Manor once supported the kingdom of Stromgarde. In the chaos following the realm's fracturing, displaced Alteraci nobility and outlaws of the Syndicate claimed the manor house, barn, and fields as an operational base. Instead of tending crops, Syndicate mercenaries, highwaymen, and pathstalkers use the farmstead to stage ambushes along the roads and coordinate their presence across Arathi.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_northfold-manor-bb9bb5b9655d2c5f87cf/1-0000.mp3", duration = 45.2963265, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:northpass-tower"] = {
  hash = "f8c431",
  fullHash = "f8c431",
  text = "Northpass Tower. Northpass Tower is a ruined Lordaeron scout tower situated in the northern reaches of the Eastern Plaguelands along the road to Quel'Thalas. Shattered during the Third War, it still overlooks the northern road toward the elven lands. Built by the Kingdom of Lordaeron to guard the vital thoroughfare leading north into Quel'Thalas, Northpass Tower was reduced to a broken shell during the Scourge invasion. Despite its shattered stonework, the tower's elevated position retains immense tactical value, overlooking the blight-choked frontier between Stratholme's blighted outskirts and the distant elven lands. No garrison mans the tower today. Its broken walls stand along a road now haunted by the Scourge, a reminder of the watch Lordaeron once kept over the route to Quel'Thalas.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_northpass-tower-439ce818c1511e3d9a8b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 46.7853061, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:northridge-lumber-camp"] = {
  hash = "b1922a",
  fullHash = "b1922a",
  text = "Northridge Lumber Camp. Northridge Lumber Camp is an active lumber mill nestled in the northern hills of the Western Plaguelands near Hearthglen. Currently held and fortified by the Scarlet Crusade, it provides timber vital to sustaining their nearby strongholds. Both the Alliance and the Horde view the facility as a prime target to cripple the zealots' regional infrastructure. Situated just down the road from Hearthglen, the Northridge Lumber Camp serves as a vital logistics node for the Scarlet Crusade. Despite the lingering plague choking the surrounding forests, the camp fells and processes regional timber to construct fortifications, repair watchtowers, and furnish the crusade's war effort in Lordaeron. Heavily garrisoned by fanatical laborers and guards, the mill is fiercely protected against both the Scourge and outside trespassers. Because Hearthglen depends heavily on this lumber to maintain its defenses, outside forces actively conspire to disrupt it. Both Alliance commanders and Horde scouts recognize that an overt assault on fortified crusader positions carries high costs. Instead, agents look to unconventional sabotage—such as unleashing voracious wood-boring termites into the mill's lumber stores—to quietly rot the Crusade's supplies from the inside out.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_northridge-lumber-camp-12f8fb6ae62ad2f195ba/1-0000.mp3", duration = 77.6097959, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:noxious-glade"] = {
  hash = "8bd37c",
  fullHash = "8bd37c",
  text = "Noxious Glade. Tucked away in the hills just north of Light's Hope Chapel, the Noxious Glade is a secluded basin steeped in concentrated plague. Choked with toxic green air and darkened soil, it functions as a nearby redoubt for Scourge operations right under the Argent Dawn's nose. While the Argent Dawn maintains its vigil at Light's Hope Chapel, an encampment of the Scourge lurks directly in the hills above them to the north. The Noxious Glade suffers from an infection far more severe than much of the surrounding terrain: the air hangs thick with a sickly green mist, and the soil has rotted into deep bruised purples. Within the hollow, the undead have erected ziggurats, a slaughterhouse for cobbling together abominations, and a plague cauldron that continuously spews pestilence into the atmosphere. Because of its close proximity to the chapel, the glade poses a persistent threat to travelers, couriers, and holy warriors working out of Light's Hope. The Scourge forces entrenched here construct foul monstrosities and brew toxins undisturbed by the sunlight, kept hidden by the surrounding natural ridge until an unwary traveler climbs the path directly into their midst.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_noxious-glade-507ff30493357fec01a6/1-0000.mp3", duration = 69.6685714, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:noxious-lair"] = {
  hash = "6f46c1",
  fullHash = "6f46c1",
  text = "Noxious Lair. The Noxious Lair is a sunken silithid hive situated in the southwestern deserts of Tanaris, bordering Un'Goro Crater. Once a verdant basin with flowing water, the territory has been infested and drained of its vital resources by the burrowing Centipaar swarm. Now, it stands as an acidic breeding ground filled with swarming insectoids and rich mineral veins. Resting against the western ridges overlooking Un'Goro Crater, the Noxious Lair occupies a depression in the sand that once held a lake or shallow river. When the Centipaar silithid invaded, they consumed the desert's remaining underground moisture and transformed the basin into an encroaching colony. Vast pincers and twitching limbs rise directly from the sand dunes, surrounding subterranean tunnels slick with corrosive insect secretions and clusters of incubating eggs. Subterranean tunnels link the surface entrances to humid, acid-coated breeding chambers beneath the sands. Centipaar workers, tunnelers, wasps, and colossal sandreavers patrol the perimeter, defending their brood from outside threats. Beyond its biological dangers, the upheaval caused by silithid excavation has unearthed deep mineral deposits, creating an unusually diverse hunting and prospecting ground where subterranean ores sit exposed amid the swarming hives.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_noxious-lair-abb769d90e8ed015a733/1-0000.mp3", duration = 81.92, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:old-port-authority"] = {
  hash = "317c58",
  fullHash = "317c58",
  text = "Old Port Authority. The Old Port Authority is a bustling, multi-vendor warehouse nestled along the boardwalks of Booty Bay. Beneath its roof, travelers browse everything from exotic pets and firearms to hearty rations and finely crafted weapons. Perched inside the pirate haven of Booty Bay, the Old Port Authority serves as an indoor marketplace divided into distinct stalls, and its interior buzzes with brisk commerce. Merchants hawk general adventuring supplies, firearms, bows, food, and weapons to scoundrels and privateers alike. It also houses exotic imports, offering colorful birds and nautical oddities straight off the docks to anyone with coin.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_old-port-authority-1eb1eed99f638a6200fd/1-0000.mp3", duration = 40.0979592, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:oneiros"] = {
  hash = "050546",
  fullHash = "050546",
  text = "Oneiros. Oneiros is a sprawling expanse of ancient night elven ruins located south of Jademir Lake in northern Feralas. Tied intimately to the nearby portals leading toward the realm of Ysera, its moss-covered stones are guarded by elite green dragonkin. Stretching across the dense forest floor of northern Feralas, the crumbling stone arches and column fragments of Oneiros hint at an ancient Kaldorei settlement abandoned millennia ago. In the present day, nature has reclaimed the structures, entwining them with massive roots and verdant overgrowth. Elite guardians of the Green Dragonflight relentlessly patrol the shaded paths and broken plazas, warding mortals away from ancient secrets.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_oneiros-773f4cc438785e42488f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 42.8930612, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:onyxia-s-lair"] = {
  hash = "b2991f",
  fullHash = "b2991f",
  text = "Onyxia's Lair. Onyxia's Lair is a subterranean cavern located deep within the Wyrmbog of Dustwallow Marsh. It serves as the nesting sanctuary and stronghold for Onyxia, broodmother of the black dragonflight. Inside its sweltering, magma-filled chambers, the great dragon watches over clutches of black dragon eggs. Carved into the treacherous mud and petrified wood of the Wyrmbog, the cavern entrance resembles the yawning maw of a dragon. Within, the cool rot of the marsh gives way to intense volcanic heat. Magma seeps from cracks in the obsidian stone, while subterranean tremors routinely shake the ceilings. The central chamber opens into a vast incubator where hundreds of black dragon eggs lie nested amid ash and embers, overseen by flightkin, dragonspawn guards, and Onyxia herself.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_onyxia-s-lair-7e5d35475ea150f687c9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 48.6661224, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:orgrimmar"] = {
  hash = "3cea5d",
  fullHash = "3cea5d",
  text = "Orgrimmar. In northern Durotar, Orgrimmar rises as the New Horde’s warrior capital. Warchief Thrall founded the city in memory of Orgrim Doomhammer, and its red canyons shelter the orcs’ renewal and survival on Kalimdor. After the Third War and the victory at Mount Hyjal, Warchief Thrall brought his surviving people south to make a homeland in Durotar. Orgrimmar was built among steep mountain crags, with spiked timber walls, stone watchtowers, and caverns cut deep into the mountains. Darkspear trolls, tauren stonemasons, and goblin engineers helped raise the fortress. Its defenses turned the wilderness into a stronghold built to withstand a siege. Free of the demonic bloodlust that once bound them, the orcs built Orgrimmar as a beacon of honor, community, and survival. Orcs and Darkspear trolls live in the city, while emissaries from the tauren of Mulgore and the newly allied Forsaken are welcomed there. Shamans teach the next generation ancestral balance, and veterans clash in gladiatorial rings to hone the army that guards Kalimdor’s fragile peace.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_orgrimmar-213253d8e3926bd3fd22/1-0000.mp3", duration = 68.4146939, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:ortell-s-hideout"] = {
  hash = "548d81",
  fullHash = "548d81",
  text = "Ortell's Hideout. Ortell's Hideout is a modest, secluded cave nestled against the mountains in southeastern Silithus. It serves as the refuge of Hermit Ortell, one of the few solitary souls hardy or foolish enough to dwell alone in this barren wasteland. Tucked away in the jagged cliffs of southeastern Silithus, Ortell's Hideout offers sparse sanctuary from the raging sandstorms and prowling silithid swarms that dominate the wastes. The cave is occupied solely by Hermit Ortell, an eccentric recluse who ekes out an existence far from civilization and the fortified garrisons of the Cenarion Hold. Travelers who brave the dangerous journey to this remote corner often seek his peculiar knowledge of the desert and its hazards.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_ortell-s-hideout-df448a098a44c51f485d/1-0000.mp3", duration = 42.6579592, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:owl-wing-thicket"] = {
  hash = "fd5fa7",
  fullHash = "fd5fa7",
  text = "Owl Wing Thicket. Owl Wing Thicket is a rugged cluster of caves and stone roosts in southern Winterspring. It is heavily populated by berserk, moontouched, and crazed owlbeasts that prowl the snowy crags. Deep within, ancient night elf altars preserve sacred knowledge about the origin of these creatures and their connection to the moon goddess Elune. Carved into the snowy highlands south of Everlook, Owl Wing Thicket serves as one of the densest nesting grounds for Winterspring's wild owlbeasts. Long ago, night elf caretakers revered these creatures as sacred guardians blessed by Elune. Ancient shrines and moonstone altars are still tucked among the cavern overhangs, watched over by the lingering spirits of the Guardians of Elune. Today, however, many of the beasts have succumbed to madness, lashing out at travelers who trespass upon their holy hollows.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_owl-wing-thicket-aed28c5d2fb78bd61616/1-0000.mp3", duration = 53.76, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:pestilent-scar"] = {
  hash = "a675b1",
  fullHash = "a675b1",
  text = "Pestilent Scar. The Pestilent Scar is a deep, blighted gorge carved into the blighted terrain of the Eastern Plaguelands. Unlike the jagged rents torn open by catastrophic Scourge blight, this chasm appears to be a natural canyon or dried lakebed that was subsequently corrupted and overrun by ravenous oozes. Winding through the eastern reaches of the Eastern Plaguelands, the Pestilent Scar forms a gloomy rift in the corrupted landscape. While nearby rifts like the Infectis Scar were ripped into the earth by sudden catastrophic eruptions of the Plague of Undeath, scholars note that the Pestilent Scar seems to have originated as a natural geological formation. It was likely a deep river canyon or natural lake that dried up when the Scourge swept across Lordaeron, leaving a barren trench that gradually pooled with noxious runoff and foul slime. Deprived of pure water and bathed in persistent unholy decay, the hollow has become a breeding pit for mutated, acidic slimes and carrion vermin. Traveling near its crumbling rim is treacherous; the air above the drop is choked with acrid fumes, and the unstable soil frequently threatens to dump unwary adventurers straight into the churning muck below.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_pestilent-scar-7c5fa68ec1626bb7b305/1-0000.mp3", duration = 70.5828571, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:plaguemist-ravine"] = {
  hash = "b9b38d",
  fullHash = "b9b38d",
  text = "Plaguemist Ravine. Plaguemist Ravine is a narrow mountain passage connecting the verdant wilderness of the Hinterlands to the haunted shores of Darrowmere Lake in the Western Plaguelands. Named for the sickly fog that rolls down from the north, it provides a quiet and desolate route across the mountain range. Tucked away in the mountains northwest of Quel'Danil Lodge, Plaguemist Ravine acts as a geographic threshold between two radically different realms. To the south lies the lush, untamed highland forest of the Hinterlands, while to the north the pass opens directly upon Caer Darrow and the blighted expanse of the Western Plaguelands. The ravine earned its name from the foul, unnatural vapors that creep southward out of the Plaguelands, creating a jarring contrast with the crisp mountain air of the neighboring wilderness. Despite cutting directly into the edge of Scourge-ravaged territory, the ravine itself remains remarkably deserted. Travelers find neither wild beasts nor wandering undead lingering within the gorge itself. Because of this eerie stillness, the winding path serves as a convenient, albeit ominous, back road for daring couriers and adventurers wishing to bypass the heavily fortified highways and border checkpoints leading into Lordaeron.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_plaguemist-ravine-7b2576a7dcae02ec3d03/1-0000.mp3", duration = 74.2138776, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:plaguewood"] = {
  hash = "f05234",
  fullHash = "f05234",
  text = "Plaguewood. Once a peaceful woodland known as Greenwood, the Plaguewood is now a blighted, fungus-choked expanse stretching south of Stratholme. Crawling with Scourge infrastructure and patrolling Scarlet Crusaders, it stands as one of the most corrupt corners of the Eastern Plaguelands. Before the Third War, this region was Greenwood, a vibrant forest that mirrored the lush woods of Elwynn. A modest village sat near its entrance, and its roads were traveled by farmers and soldiers alike. All of that collapsed when tainted grain arrived from Andorhal. Today, spongy, corrupted soil has taken the place of rich earth, and titanic mushrooms dwarf the dead trees, constantly spewing noxious brown vapors across the woodland floor. Now completely claimed by the Scourge, the Plaguewood serves as a grim staging ground for the undead surrounding Stratholme. Ziggurats and slaughterhouses dot the decayed landscape, churning out abominations and cultists. To make matters worse for travelers, fanatic Scarlet Crusaders relentlessly patrol the road toward Stratholme's gates, eager to purge the Scourge and brutally slaughter any living wanderers they encounter.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_plaguewood-f1e6a9bdb7bc32281d46/1-0000.mp3", duration = 69.9036735, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:plaguewood-tower"] = {
  hash = "48c8b7",
  fullHash = "48c8b7",
  text = "Plaguewood Tower. Plaguewood Tower is a ruined Lordaeron scout tower situated in the northern reaches of the Plaguewood near Terrordale. Shattered during the Scourge invasion, it now stands as a broken ruin amid the blighted forest. Standing on the edge of the blight-choked Plaguewood and the blighted town of Terrordale, Plaguewood Tower once served the Kingdom of Lordaeron as a vital northern watchpost. When the Scourge swept across the land, the fortification was battered into crumbling masonry and left in ruins amid corrupted fungal canopies. Today the fractured stones still overlook the northern trails, a silent reminder of the watch Lordaeron once kept over this land.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_plaguewood-tower-b6ec799fbb36e8b66d8c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 39.4710204, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:pool-of-tears"] = {
  hash = "4c49cb",
  fullHash = "4c49cb",
  text = "Pool of Tears. The Pool of Tears is a large, murky lake dominating the eastern reaches of the Swamp of Sorrows. Deep beneath its surface lies the sunken Temple of Atal'Hakkar, brought low by the Green Dragonflight to contain the horrors within. Today, vicious frenzies patrol the turbid waters, and ancient troll ruins litter its drowned banks. Long ago, fanatical Atal'ai trolls raised a monumental ziggurat to their bloodthirsty god, Hakkar the Soulflayer. Horrified by the blasphemies taking place within, the Dragon Aspect Ysera shattered the ground beneath the structure, submerging the massive temple into the depths of the marsh. The existing lake flooded over the temple ruins. While the dragons sought to bury the evil forever, the ziggurat still stands beneath the silt and waves, and green drakes remain vigilant in the area to keep its corruption sealed. Navigating the murky lake is treacherous for any adventurer heading toward the sunken halls. Flesh-eating frenzies infest the waters, while Atal'ai artifacts and drowned stonework remain scattered across the muddy floor. Travelers must brave these hostile shallows to access the upper passages of the submerged temple, where cultists and lingering dragon guardians vie for control of the dark sanctuary.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_pool-of-tears-db1da472ef1a501e7d87/1-0000.mp3", duration = 77.4008163, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:purgation-isle"] = {
  hash = "d2c903",
  fullHash = "d2c903",
  text = "Purgation Isle. Purgation Isle is a spiraling, rocky island lying off the southern coast of the Hillsbrad Foothills. Crowned with a cemetery and a ruined scout tower, the desolate rock is cursed and swarming with the restless, condemned spirits of fallen humans and dwarves. Rising abruptly from the sea south of Hillsbrad, Purgation Isle is a grim sight marked by winding paths that lead past tombstones to an abandoned watchtower. No living nation claims it today. Instead, the isle is plagued by condemned souls—former paladins, priests, mages, and judges who wander among the graves, unable to find peace in death. The lingering souls trapped upon the island have made it a site of sinister interest. Dark spellcasters, such as the ogre necromancer Kormok, are said to have delighted in drawing upon the island's anguished dead, summoning forth its spirits to do their bidding.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_purgation-isle-8a15e3a4ef8ac4574c31/1-0000.mp3", duration = 57.0514286, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:quagmire"] = {
  hash = "0cdc02",
  fullHash = "0cdc02",
  text = "Quagmire. The Quagmire comprises the sprawling, treacherous heart of central Dustwallow Marsh. Bound by Brackenwall Village to the north and the Dragonmurk to the south, its waterlogged flats are notoriously difficult to navigate. Hostile beasts and ravenous oozes make any journey through this central fen perilous. Occupying the wide expanse between Lost Point in the west and Dustwallow Bay in the east, the Quagmire represents the wild core of the marsh. Footing is unreliable at best, with solid earth constantly giving way to murky water and clinging mud. Travelers must contend with predatory crocolisks lurking beneath the scum, aggressive spiders nesting in the dead brush, and strange oozes that seep through the muck. Beyond its natural hazards, the Quagmire serves as an unpredictable crossroads for the sentient powers contesting the swamp. Tucked away within the bog lies Tabetha's Farm, a lonely refuge for practitioners seeking hermit wisdom amid the dangers of the swamp.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_quagmire-c58d2edc38c4a2e997e1/1-0000.mp3", duration = 62.1453061, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:quel-lithien-lodge"] = {
  hash = "598dc1",
  fullHash = "598dc1",
  text = "Quel'Lithien Lodge. Quel'Lithien Lodge is an isolated high elven hunting lodge nestled in the forested northern hills of the Eastern Plaguelands near the entrance to Quel'Thalas. Its Farstrider rangers hold out among the blighted woods, surrounded by the Scourge and hunted by Forsaken agents, while guarding old elven archives. Before the Third War, Quel'Lithien was a Farstrider camp on the road toward Quel'Thalas, and it housed elven records of the ranger order. The lodge fell on hard times after the Scourge swept through Lordaeron and into the elven kingdom. Today its rangers keep their vigil in the corrupted forest, one of only two major concentrations of high elves left in Lordaeron.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_quel-lithien-lodge-eb9d23f4be612a8d2d36/1-0000.mp3", duration = 42.004898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:rage-scar-hold"] = {
  hash = "3dfa8c",
  fullHash = "3dfa8c",
  text = "Rage Scar Hold. Rage Scar Hold is a cavern carved into the wilderness of Feralas, situated on the mainland not far from Feathermoon Stronghold. It is notorious for housing some of the most vicious yetis in the region. Strangely, scattered books, symbols, and traces of night elven architecture deeper within suggest the den once served a far more civil purpose before the beasts claimed it. Carved into the rugged wilds of Feralas, Rage Scar Hold is dominated by savage yetis that attack anyone venturing near their lair. The creatures make the surrounding forest trails hazardous for both Alliance sentinels and passing travelers. While yetis are common to the untamed reaches of Kalimdor, the denizens of Rage Scar Hold stand out for their raw aggression and territorial ferocity. Deeper in the dark corridors of the cave lie subtle clues of an older occupancy. Carved stonework and night elven architecture suggest the cavern was once a barrow den or an arcane sanctuary. Forgotten tomes and mystic markings remain among the dirt and bone, leaving travelers to wonder whether unfortunate scholars were slaughtered by the beasts or if the yetis somehow gravitate toward the lingering magic of the site.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_rage-scar-hold-b0dcd5c4009f0809c69a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 71.8106122, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:ragefire-chasm"] = {
  hash = "8d67ff",
  fullHash = "8d67ff",
  text = "Ragefire Chasm. Beneath Orgrimmar, Ragefire Chasm threatens the Horde from below. Its magma-lit tunnels hold savage troggs, and the Cleft of Shadow gives access to the caverns. Beneath Orgrimmar, dangers threaten the capital from below. While Thrall works to secure Durotar above, Ragefire troggs infest the chasm’s unstable vents and magma rivers. They rose from unknown depths and violently rejected Magatha Grimtotem’s attempts at diplomacy. Left unchecked, their numbers could undermine the city and overrun it.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_ragefire-chasm-48b2dc52c1f4a668776a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 31.3208163, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:raptor-ridge"] = {
  hash = "e86750",
  fullHash = "e86750",
  text = "Raptor Ridge. Raptor Ridge rises above the eastern Wetlands, northwest of the Dragonmaw Gates. Razormaw raptors hunt and nest among its crags, with a vicious matriarch deep in the caverns. Razormaw raptors hunt in packs across Raptor Ridge, where rocky trails climb above the eastern Wetlands. Travelers near the foothills of Khaz Modan risk meeting these predators. In a cavern at the ridge's heart, the Razormaw Matriarch guards her brood and nests from intruders. The Dragonmaw Gates lie close by.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_raptor-ridge-dbce210a16c06a6edfc4/1-0000.mp3", duration = 30.5110204, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:ravaged-twilight-camp"] = {
  hash = "627dda",
  fullHash = "627dda",
  text = "Ravaged Twilight Camp. Tucked away in the secluded Crystal Vale of northwestern Silithus, the Ravaged Twilight Camp sits ruined among towering mineral formations. It serves as an isolated redoubt for the eccentric hermit Highlord Demitrian, a devotee of the Windseeker. While the Twilight's Hammer maintains numerous active outposts across the sands of Silithus, this broken encampment in the Crystal Vale tells a different story. Nestled far from the primary cult fortresses, the camp lies largely ruined and deserted, save for Highlord Demitrian. A servant of Thunderaan, Demitrian lingers amidst the stones, seemingly abandoned by or detached from the wider Twilight hierarchy while brooding over elemental bindings and the lost power of the Windseeker.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_ravaged-twilight-camp-6face297128e7e7b593b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 43.7812245, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:ravencrest-monument"] = {
  hash = "f188f9",
  fullHash = "f188f9",
  text = "Ravencrest Monument. Perched upon the windswept cliffs of southern Azshara, the Ravencrest Monument marks the shattered remnant of an ancient night elven colossus. Once a towering tribute overlooking the Great Sea, the statue has long since broken apart, leaving only towering legs and scattered stone ruins now stalked by hostile Spitelash naga. Standing high above the ocean southeast of the Ruins of Eldarath, the Ravencrest Monument was once an awe-inspiring marvel of Kaldorei stonework. Today, little remains of the majestic colossus beyond its pair of gigantic broken legs rooted to the stone plinth, with the shattered fragments of its torso and head either buried in the cliffside dirt or lost beneath the waves below. Reaching this remote coastal promontory requires trekking across the mainland bridges of Azshara, as sheer, rocky drops prevent any approach from the sea. Scholars often speculate that the monument commemorates Lord Kur'talos Ravencrest, the famed noble who rallied and led the night elven host during the War of the Ancients after the fall of Zin-Azshari. However, its close proximity to the pre-Sundering city of Eldarath and ambiguous surviving stonework leave its true origins debated. Rather than pilgrims or night elven caretakers, the surrounding cliff is currently claimed by the serpentine Spitelash naga, who defend the fallen monument with vicious territorial aggression.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_ravencrest-monument-a1a06175fbea251d0ca6/1-0000.mp3", duration = 83.0955102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:rebel-camp"] = {
  hash = "c7e00b",
  fullHash = "c7e00b",
  text = "Rebel Camp. Tucked just off the road in northern Stranglethorn Vale, the Rebel Camp serves as a fortified refuge for Alliance soldiers who broke away from Colonel Kurzen. Led by Lieutenant Doren, these troops refused to surrender to the madness gripping their commander's expedition and now fight to survive and undo his descent into tyranny. When Colonel Kurzen's expedition pushed deep into the jungles of Stranglethorn Vale on behalf of Stormwind, things rapidly fell apart. Afflicted by the dark jungle magic and the curse of the ogre mage Mai'Zoth, the Colonel and his core loyalists succumbed to madness, turning rogue and terrorizing the wilds. A handful of sound-minded soldiers led by Lieutenant Doren refused to participate in the atrocities. Breaking away from Kurzen's Compound, they established this hidden camp in the north to stage a desperate guerrilla campaign against their former comrades. Perched precariously near the Duskwood border, the Rebel Camp operates under constant threat. Cut off from regular Stormwind relief, Doren relies on covert supplies and defensive armaments—including goblin explosives brokered through Booty Bay—to hold the perimeter against Kurzen's raiding parties. For Alliance travelers venturing south into the savage jungle, the encampment provides a vital first foothold of sanity.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_rebel-camp-c4fff3d49cdc81ea237f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 78.1322449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:refuge-pointe"] = {
  hash = "d2e89d",
  fullHash = "d2e89d",
  text = "Refuge Pointe. Refuge Pointe is the primary redoubt for the Alliance and the surviving remnants of Stromgarde within the Arathi Highlands. Perched centrally along the highlands' trade road, it serves as a staging ground to reclaim the shattered capital of Stromgarde Keep and hold back rival forces across the region. Following the devastation of the Third War and the catastrophic fall of Stromgarde Keep, the bulk of Stromgarde's surviving populace fled south toward friendlier lands. Those who remained formed a resolute vanguard, digging in at Refuge Pointe under Captain Nials. Composed of battle-hardened Stromgarde soldiers, League of Arathor forces, and supporting Alliance specialists, the encampment represents the kingdom's last organized stand in its ancestral homeland. Refuge Pointe is constantly on the brink of being overwhelmed. Hemmed in by the Forsaken and the Horde based out of Hammerfall, Witherbark trolls, Syndicate rogues, and Boulderfist ogres, the garrison has zero reserves to spare. When dwarven commanders at the battered Thandol Span desperately requested reinforcements to fight off the Dark Iron clan, Captain Nials had to refuse. Every sword, shield, and scrap of supply stationed at Refuge Pointe must remain dedicated to defending the highlands and preparing a counteroffensive to retake Stromgarde Keep.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_refuge-pointe-cbe7553bcce30f9c9816/1-0000.mp3", duration = 84.1665306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:roguefeather-den"] = {
  hash = "3779b0",
  fullHash = "3779b0",
  text = "Roguefeather Den. Roguefeather Den is a cavern nestled in the south-central reaches of Thousand Needles. Infested with ferocious screeching harpies, the cave serves as a fortified roost where the flock hoards scavenged provisions deep within its shadowed recesses. Carved into the sheer sandstone bluffs of southern Thousand Needles, Roguefeather Den is the foul nesting ground of local harpies. Known for their feral screeching and aggressive territorialism, the harpies treat the cavern as both a secure haven and a storehouse, vigilantly guarding the gathered food and stolen supplies they pile in the dark recesses at the back of the cave.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_roguefeather-den-5aa4acb2d71435685c34/1-0000.mp3", duration = 35.9967347, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:ruined-reaches"] = {
  hash = "632615",
  fullHash = "632615",
  text = "Ruined Reaches. The Ruined Reaches comprise the treacherous southern coastline of Azshara near the border with Durotar. Marked by crumbling ancient ruins, shattered shipwrecks, and jagged shallows, these beaches are overrun by aggressive shoreline predators. Amid the desolation, a small gnomish outpost perches high on a cliffside above the surf. Stretching along the southern borders of Azshara just north of Durotar, the Ruined Reaches are a maze of rocky inlets, cliff faces, and debris-strewn sand. Like the Jagged Reef further north, hidden shallows make maritime travel extremely hazardous, leaving numerous ruined vessels broken along the shore. The waters and beaches teem with hostile wildlife, including territorial murlocs, vicious hydras, and armored makrura, making overland transit just as dangerous as navigating the surf. Perched upon a western cliff overlooking the beaches sits an unexpected pocket of tinker civilization: a gnomish workshop accessible via a steep trail from Azshara's mainland. Run by Blimo and Jubie Gadgetspring, this outpost offers engineering components and general trade goods to passing adventurers. The interior features an elevator ascending to private quarters with three beds, though who—if anyone—claims the third bunk remains a quiet domestic mystery.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_ruined-reaches-f5a513f2267d66893db3/1-0000.mp3", duration = 81.8155102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:ruins-of-aboraz"] = {
  hash = "d3321e",
  fullHash = "d3321e",
  text = "Ruins of Aboraz. The Ruins of Aboraz are ancient Gurubashi stone ruins nestled along the southeastern cliffs of Stranglethorn Vale. Once part of the sprawling jungle troll empire, the crumbling terraces now serve as the secluded redoubt for the renegade witch doctor Zanzil the Outcast and his mind-addled thralls. Perched on the bluffs southeast of the Ruins of Jubuwal and south of the Crystalvein Mine, the weathered masonry of Aboraz has been transformed into an alchemical testing ground. Zanzil the Outcast was banished by the local tribes and the mainlanders alike, retreating here to brew potent mind-altering concoctions and toxic elixirs. Within the stone courtyards, Zanzil exerts total control over an unsettling menagerie of servitors, including shambling undead ghouls, corrupted troll thralls, and even co-opted coastal naga.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_ruins-of-aboraz-a2d51927d09fd9ada03c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 52.48, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:ruins-of-alterac"] = {
  hash = "d844cf",
  fullHash = "d844cf",
  text = "Ruins of Alterac. The snow-covered Ruins of Alterac are all that remain of Alterac City, once the proud mountain capital of the Kingdom of Alterac. Cast out of the Alliance and put under martial law after King Aiden Perenolde's treason during the Second War, the shattered stronghold is now overrun by the brutish Crushridge ogres. Alterac was once an independent human realm carved out of the old Arathor empire, perched high on the trade routes through the mountains. During the Second War, fearing destruction at the hands of the invading Old Horde, King Aiden Perenolde secretly struck a pact with the orcs. When the betrayal was uncovered following the fighting at Caer Darrow, the Alliance of Lordaeron retaliated swiftly. King Terenas Menethil II put the city under martial law and deposed Perenolde. The grand fortifications, church, and palace fell into disrepair as the populace fled or turned to outlawry. While surviving Alteraci nobles and rogue soldiers formed the Syndicate to wage guerrilla campaigns from towns like Strahnbrad, they lost their ancestral capital entirely. Today, the Crushridge ogres under Mug'thol dwell among the broken ramparts, palace halls, and wrecked ballistae. Neither the Syndicate nor the human defenders of Southshore have managed to reclaim the fallen city, leaving its crumbling towers and abandoned vaults as dangerous territory for treasure hunters and scavengers.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_ruins-of-alterac-fc4ecffef95f41d7c645/1-0000.mp3", duration = 83.9053061, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:ruins-of-andorhal"] = {
  hash = "2a713a",
  fullHash = "2a713a",
  text = "Ruins of Andorhal. Once the agricultural hub and grain distribution center of northern Lordaeron, the Ruins of Andorhal now lie at the heart of the Western Plaguelands as a devastated Necropolis stronghold. Under the dominion of the lich Araj the Summoner, the shattered township crawls with legions of undead abominations, ghouls, and wraiths. Andorhal was the initial staging ground for the plague of undeath in Lordaeron, where grain tainted by the Cult of the Damned was packed and distributed across the human kingdom. Today, the ruined city serves as a sprawling bastion of the Scourge in the Western Plaguelands. Araj the Summoner presides over the center of the ruins, flanked by ghouls, skeletal horrors, and towering flesh golems. Scouting parties of the Scarlet Crusade periodically probe the city's outskirts, but the Scourge maintains iron control over the shattered streets.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_ruins-of-andorhal-ffb5640325eb4f9d4ce9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 52.8979592, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:ruins-of-constellas"] = {
  hash = "79285b",
  fullHash = "79285b",
  text = "Ruins of Constellas. The Ruins of Constellas are the shattered remnants of an ancient night elf settlement in Felwood, nestled near the roots of Mount Hyjal. Today, the once-sacred grounds are overrun by the Jadefire satyrs, who have violently profaned the settlement's moonwell with demonic fel magic. The classical stone arches and crumbling plazas of Constellas reveal that this was once an idyllic night elven community. During the Third War, the Burning Legion's march through Felwood shattered the haven, leaving it exposed to lingering demonic corruption. Now, the foul Jadefire satyrs hold dominion over the stones. Led by Xavathras, they actively tend to a defiled moonwell, transforming pure waters into a font of noxious fel power on behalf of their master, Lord Xavaric. The defilement of Constellas weighs heavily on all who would heal Felwood. To those working to cleanse the forest, Xavathras and his Jadefire are among its foulest corrupters, and the satyrs fiercely guard their tents among the ruins and the tainted moonwell at their heart against any who come to end their work.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_ruins-of-constellas-336d41d3c4ba9c8a44c1/1-0000.mp3", duration = 65.0710204, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:ruins-of-eldarath"] = {
  hash = "926dfa",
  fullHash = "926dfa",
  text = "Ruins of Eldarath. The Ruins of Eldarath are the shattered remains of a pre-Sundering Highborne city perched along the rocky bluffs of Azshara. Once a bastion of Kaldorei arcane scholarship, its fallen plazas and crumbling temples are now overrun by hostile naga. Scholars and adventurers brave the dangerous coastal ruins searching for inscribed tablets and forgotten relics of the ancient past. Before the Great Sundering shattered ancient Kalimdor ten thousand years ago, Eldarath was a flourishing night elven city. Renowned Highborne mages gathered here, carving their deepest arcane insights and fundamental magical rules into grand stone tablets left in public squares for their peers to study. The Sundering dropped much of Eldarath into the Bay of Storms. The portions that survived along the eastern cliffs have sat shunned by modern night elves for millennia, slowly claimed by creeping salt spray and ivy. In recent times, vicious naga have emerged from the sea to seize the shattered streets of Eldarath, commanded by warlords lurking within nearby sanctuaries like the Temple of Zin-Malor. Despite the danger posed by the serpents, the ruins remain an irresistible lure for outsiders. The Explorers' League has dispatched Alliance adventurers into the broken courtyards to take rubbings of Eldarath's ancient arcane tablets. Whispers of lost treasures and lingering magical energy also drew the blue dragon Azuregos to the surrounding wilderness, further confirming that secrets of immense power still sleep beneath the ruins' cracked flagstones.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_ruins-of-eldarath-c320dd84c8c26c7595eb/1-0000.mp3", duration = 93.4138776, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:ruins-of-isildien"] = {
  hash = "226fb9",
  fullHash = "226fb9",
  text = "Ruins of Isildien. The Ruins of Isildien are the sprawling remains of an ancient night elven city in southwestern Feralas, destroyed ten millennia ago during the Great Sundering. Today, the overgrown stonework and cracked temple courtyards are occupied by brutish Gordunni ogres. Ten thousand years ago, before the world shattered, Isildien was a grand night elven settlement nestled in the primordial forests of ancient Kalimdor. Its graceful marble arches and grand terraces stood as monuments to kaldorei civilization and devotion. When the Well of Eternity imploded and tore the continents apart, Isildien fell into ruined desolation, smothered under millennia of untamed jungle growth. Dominating the upper terrace is a colossal domed structure remarkably reminiscent of Darnassus's Temple of the Moon, suggesting the city was once a significant sanctuary dedicated to Elune. In the modern era, the elegant stonework of Isildien serves a far cruder purpose. The Gordunni ogres of Feralas have claimed the sprawling terraces and outcroppings as one of their primary settlements. The ogres have fortified the crumbling walkways, desecrating ancient plazas with crude bonfires, primitive barricades, and war banners, fiercely defending their stolen home against any outsiders who venture too close to the southern hills.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_ruins-of-isildien-df6378dcf10946114280/1-0000.mp3", duration = 78.7853061, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:ruins-of-jubuwal"] = {
  hash = "388195",
  fullHash = "388195",
  text = "Ruins of Jubuwal. The Ruins of Jubuwal are the crumbling remnants of an ancient Gurubashi settlement located just east of the Gurubashi Arena in Stranglethorn Vale. Once a place of empire and ritual, the ruins have fallen under the sway of the rogue witch doctor Zanzil the Outcast and his bizarre retinue of minions. Situated off the primary thoroughfare cutting through Stranglethorn Vale, Jubuwal was once part of the grand Gurubashi civilization. Today, stone carvings and ivy-draped masonry have been overtaken by Zanzil the Outcast, an eccentric witch doctor known for his potent concoctions and foul voodoo. Zanzil has filled the ruins with an unnatural menagerie of followers, including enthralled ghouls, subjugated naga, and renegade trolls. Desperate fugitives also lurk among the stone arches, such as Jon-Jon the Crow and Maury 'Club Foot' Wilkins, both hiding from the ruthless debt collector 'Sea Wolf' MacKinley back in Booty Bay.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_ruins-of-jubuwal-2c5b4ced93d35c921130/1-0000.mp3", duration = 56.8946939, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:ruins-of-kel-theril"] = {
  hash = "b07b46",
  fullHash = "b07b46",
  text = "Ruins of Kel'Theril. The Ruins of Kel'Theril are the shattered remnants of an ancient night elven city bordering the frozen expanse of Lake Kel'Theril in central Winterspring. Destroyed ten thousand years ago during the Great Sundering, its frostbitten stone pillars and temples remain haunted by the tormented spirits of Highborne who perished in the cataclysm. Long before Winterspring became a frozen highland, Kel'Theril stood as a grand settlement of the ancient night elven empire. When the Well of Eternity collapsed during the Great Sundering, the city was blasted into ruin and buried beneath ice and snow. The disaster caught the residing Highborne unaware, binding their souls to the snowbound stones. Today, the spirits of these former spellcasters linger among the frostbitten arches, endlessly reliving their final, catastrophic moments. Travelers braving central Winterspring encounter restless, spectral Highborne haunting the ruined lakeside colonnades. These phantoms are often hostile, driven mad by millenniums of isolation and the violent magic that tore their civilization apart. However, a few sorrowful apparitions, such as the Remorseful Highborne, seek out travelers willing to hear their lingering laments and make peace with their forgotten past.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_ruins-of-kel-theril-8f38b5f1c2f32fd7f9d1/1-0000.mp3", duration = 77.5836735, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:ruins-of-lordaeron"] = {
  hash = "17c1ee",
  fullHash = "17c1ee",
  text = "Ruins of Lordaeron. The Ruins of Lordaeron are the hollowed-out surface remnants of Capital City, the once-glorious heart of humanity's northern empire. Destroyed when Prince Arthas murdered his father and unleashed the Scourge, the blasted courtyards and throne room now serve as the fortified gateway to the Undercity below. Capital City was once the radiant summit of human civilization, uniting the northern realms under King Terenas Menethil II. When Prince Arthas returned from Northrend carrying the cursed runeblade Frostmourne, the celebrations turned into a slaughterhouse. Arthas slew his father on the throne, leaving the metropolis to rot under Scourge rule. Following the civil war that tore the Scourge apart, Lady Sylvanas Windrunner claimed the devastated palace, securing the surface while expanding the crypts and dungeons beneath into the Undercity. The palace ruins remain psychically scarred by their violent fall. Travelers walking through the entryway and courtyards often hear the ghostly chime of celebration bells, hushed murmurs of adoring crowds, and the chilling dialogue between Arthas and his father just before the regicide. Blood still stains the floor of the grand throne room, and those possessing magical detection can glimpse spectral citizens eternally reliving the afternoon their prince doomed them all.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_ruins-of-lordaeron-0fdbe6cc7c3c7a011f79/1-0000.mp3", duration = 79.9085714, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:ruins-of-ravenwind"] = {
  hash = "c5c669",
  fullHash = "c5c669",
  text = "Ruins of Ravenwind. The Ruins of Ravenwind are the shattered remnants of an ancient Highborne city nestled in northern Feralas. Today, the crumbling marble structures are heavily infested by the vicious Northspring harpies and guarded by brooding dragonkin. Much like the great city of Dire Maul to the east, the Ruins of Ravenwind stand as a solemn monument to the height and hubris of ancient Highborne civilization before the Great Sundering. Long abandoned by its original kaldorei architects, the site has succumbed to creeping wild foliage, forgotten arcane altars, and the foul roosts of Northspring harpies who prey on anything wandering beneath the shattered arches. Beyond the screeching harpies, Ravenwind is bordered by diverse and territorial threats. Fierce green dragonkin known as the Jaedemir circle the central groves and waterways, guarding ancient arboreal sites. Meanwhile, stray wanderers—from distracted night elf explorers to peculiar, solitary gnolls lingering nearby—frequently find themselves trapped in the crossfire of the ruins' lingering magics and wild predators.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_ruins-of-ravenwind-1a437b2607612fbc11c7/1-0000.mp3", duration = 66.6122449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:ruins-of-thaurissan"] = {
  hash = "d3e727",
  fullHash = "d3e727",
  text = "Ruins of Thaurissan. The Ruins of Thaurissan are the scorched remnants of the Dark Iron clan's original surface capital in the Burning Steppes. Once a proud city nestled in the lush northern reaches of the Redridge Mountains, it was blasted to cinders during the War of the Three Hammers when Sorcerer-Thane Thaurissan inadvertently summoned the Firelord, Ragnaros. Roughly two centuries ago, following their exile from Ironforge after the death of High King Modimus Anvilmar, the Dark Iron clan journeyed south and established Thaurissan City. At the climax of the War of the Three Hammers, facing total defeat by the combined forces of the Bronzebeard and Wildhammer clans, Sorcerer-Thane Thaurissan sought an ultimate weapon to turn the tide. He and seven trusted sorcerers channeled ancient magic deep beneath the earth. Instead of an obedient servant, their ritual ripped open a conduit to the Elemental Plane and summoned Ragnaros the Firelord. The cataclysmic eruption shattered the surrounding mountains, burned the verdant landscape into the ashen Burning Steppes, raised the volcano of Blackrock Mountain, and incinerated the city.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_ruins-of-thaurissan-293ed36dcf9128740df2/1-0000.mp3", duration = 70.8179592, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:ruins-of-uldum"] = {
  hash = "57d879",
  fullHash = "57d879",
  text = "Ruins of Uldum. Tucked into the jagged cliffs of the Valley of the Watchers in southern Tanaris, the Ruins of Uldum consist of an ancient stone hall guarding a sealed Titan facility. Massive stone doors protect its secrets from grave robbers and curious explorers alike. Carved deep into the bedrock of southern Tanaris, the Ruins of Uldum appear to be an antechamber or grand threshold to a far larger, sealed subterranean complex. Its colossal stone gates stand shut against the outside world, and no mechanism or key known to modern scholars has managed to open them.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_ruins-of-uldum-c98a4f03a8df9243ca3e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 32.0783673, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:ruins-of-zul-mamwe"] = {
  hash = "91306f",
  fullHash = "91306f",
  text = "Ruins of Zul'Mamwe. Tucked away in a mountainous pocket south of the Mosh'Ogg Ogre Mound, the Ruins of Zul'Mamwe serve as a heavily fortified stronghold for the Skullsplitter troll tribe. Enclosed by steep cliffs on almost all sides, the ancient stone platforms now teem with fierce jungle trolls under the rule of their ruthless chieftain, Ana'thek the Cruel. The Ruins of Zul'Mamwe represent one of the primary seats of power for the Skullsplitter tribe in central Stranglethorn Vale. Cradled within a natural mountain amphitheater, the site has only a single open approach from the north, making it an exceptional defensive redoubt. From atop its weathered ziggurats and stone terraces, Chief Ana'thek the Cruel directs his warriors and witch doctors, defending their territory fiercely against both intruding adventurers and rival jungle troll tribes. Like many of the stone settlements dotting the jungle, Zul'Mamwe is a relic of the ancient Gurubashi Empire that once held undisputed dominion over the southern reaches of the continent. Although the grand empire splintered long ago into squabbling, hostile factions, modern Skullsplitters fiercely guard these ancestral stones, continuing age-old tribal rituals amid the overgrown masonry and broken monoliths.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_ruins-of-zul-mamwe-c9bc61e3d3c4f3693348/1-0000.mp3", duration = 77.844898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:rustmaul-dig-site"] = {
  hash = "1ec1da",
  fullHash = "1ec1da",
  text = "Rustmaul Dig Site. The Rustmaul Dig Site is a ruined excavation in the southern reaches of the Shimmering Flats. Originally established by the dwarven Explorers' League to unearth ancient ruins, the camp fell into tragedy when a Horde war party slaughtered the miners, after which a subterranean silithid swarm emerged. Now abandoned by mortals, the site crawls with hostile insectoids emerging from the caverns below. Prospectors from the Ironforge Explorers' League set up the Rustmaul Dig Site hoping to uncover forgotten artifacts buried beneath the salt flats. Their efforts were brutally cut short when an aggressive Horde war party, led by Moktar Krin, fell upon the camp and wiped out the dwarven excavators. However, the conquerors enjoyed no triumph. The violent clash apparently disturbed a dormant cave network beneath the site, releasing an endless horde of silithid insects that quickly overwhelmed and drove off the battered survivors. Today, Rustmaul stands entirely lost to the surface races. What remains of the mining equipment and scaffolds is buried under skittering mandibles and alien carapaces. The surviving members of Moktar Krin's war party retreated north to Ironstone Camp, terrified by the unrelenting tide of insectoids that emerged after their attack.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_rustmaul-dig-site-1f257c99678d24c13660/1-0000.mp3", duration = 79.9608163, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:saltspray-glen"] = {
  hash = "ba14e4",
  fullHash = "ba14e4",
  text = "Saltspray Glen. Along Baradin Bay, salty spray sweeps across Saltspray Glen, where wild raptors roam the lowland. The glen lies between murloc waters and ancient dwarven ruins. In the Wetlands' far northwest, salty spray blows inland across Saltspray Glen from Baradin Bay. Bluegill Marsh and its murloc-haunted waters lie to the south, while the quiet bluffs of Ironbeard's Tomb rise to the east. Cunning wild raptors nest through the marshy grass and hunt across the brackish flats. Their kin range nearby at Raptor Ridge and Black Channel Marsh, leaving little comfort for travelers passing through.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_saltspray-glen-1af41bdf220f557951c0/1-0000.mp3", duration = 38.2432653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:salty-sailor-tavern"] = {
  hash = "51b3a7",
  fullHash = "51b3a7",
  text = "Salty Sailor Tavern. The Salty Sailor Tavern is the beating heart of Booty Bay's social life, serving as the port's primary inn and watering hole. Run by the goblin Innkeeper Skindle, it welcomes scoundrels, mercenaries, and travelers from both factions seeking respite from the perils of the jungle. Perched amidst the wooden tiers of Booty Bay, the Salty Sailor Tavern offers warm beds and strong drinks under the watchful eye of the Blackwater Raiders. Behind the bar stands Innkeeper Skindle, keeping glasses filled while a menagerie of buccaneers, traders, and adventurers mingle on neutral ground. Outside the establishment hangs a massive, curious curio—a Zul'Gurub Protector Stone—serving as a strange trophy in this bustling den of commerce.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_salty-sailor-tavern-3b4d8844e98ece13f434/1-0000.mp3", duration = 44.6171429, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:sanctum-stromgarde-keep"] = {
  hash = "dfce48",
  fullHash = "dfce48",
  text = "Sanctum (Stromgarde Keep). The Sanctum is a sacred redoubt tucked behind the chapel within Stromgarde Keep. Sheltered within the crumbling fortress, it guards the entrance to the ancestral Trollbane family crypt and stands as one of the few remaining bastions of the Holy Light amidst Arathi's ruin. While much of Stromgarde Keep has fallen to Syndicate infiltrators and the Boulderfist ogres, the Sanctum and its adjacent chapel have remained under the fierce protection of the surviving Stromgarde defenders. Located behind the house of worship, the Sanctum borders the citadel's graveyard and marks the threshold to the royal crypt where the kings of Stromgarde rest. For the battered loyalists rallying around Prince Galen Trollbane, holding this hallowed ground represents more than a tactical foothold; it is a desperate fight to keep their kingdom's ancestral heritage and faith from being trampled by invaders. Beneath the stone flags of the Sanctum lies the passage into the royal tomb. The ground here is consecrated, intended as a place of peaceful reflection and worship of the Holy Light. In the wake of King Thoras Trollbane's murder and the kingdom's subsequent collapse, the Sanctum has instead become a defensive retreat. Those seeking to breach the inner sanctum must contend not only with the royal guards garrisoned throughout the halls, but with the haunting reality of an ancient human lineage clinging to survival on its sacred threshold.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_sanctum-stromgarde-keep-7de232616302c38964c0/1-0000.mp3", duration = 83.3567347, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:sandsorrow-watch"] = {
  hash = "d38ab2",
  fullHash = "d38ab2",
  text = "Sandsorrow Watch. Sandsorrow Watch is a sprawling desert outpost located just west of Gadgetzan in Tanaris. The surrounding dunes and ruins are overrun by hostile Sandfury trolls and their reanimated dead, who also hold the eastern watch tower overlooking the waste. Situated directly west of the bustling goblin trading hub of Gadgetzan, Sandsorrow Watch is a grim borderland in the Tanaris desert. The broad expanse is dominated by the Sandfury tribe, who aggressively defend the arid territory alongside legions of shambling troll zombies. An elevated watch tower at the eastern edge anchors the position, providing a fortified vantage point amidst the encroaching desert wilderness and the hostile indigenous tribes. Even the watch tower at the eastern edge, within sight of Gadgetzan's walls, is held by the Sandfury. From its heights the Sandfury keep a wary eye on the goblin town, and travelers leaving through Gadgetzan's western gate walk almost directly into their territory, a constant reminder of the volatile frontier tension between Tanaris's opportunistic settlers and the native sand trolls.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_sandsorrow-watch-64196b57f00987dea386/1-0000.mp3", duration = 68.2318367, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:sar-theris-strand"] = {
  hash = "440d71",
  fullHash = "440d71",
  text = "Sar'theris Strand. Sar'theris Strand forms the western coastline of Desolace, fronting the Veiled Sea. In stark contrast to the barren, grey inland wastes, the shore is sunny and grassy, though it remains perilous due to prowling naga, sea giants, and makrura. Running the length of western Desolace, Sar'theris Strand offers a bizarre respite from the ash and dust of the inland plains. Here, the clifftops and beaches are bright and green. Yet the strand is far from welcoming: serpentine naga patrol the surf alongside towering sea giants and territorial makrura. Shipwrecks litter the shoals, including the ruined Karnitol vessel to the north and small shattered skiffs beneath the towers of Ethel Rethor, proving the sea is as treacherous as the shore. Beyond the constant threat of naga and giants, the waters off the strand are known among seasoned travelers for their peculiar bounties. The jagged coastal shallows conceal salvage from lost expeditions and merchant ventures, while the deep surf is home to rare marine wildlife, notably the prized Sar'theris Striker fish sought out by master fishermen across Kalimdor.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_sar-theris-strand-4c422fc4466005573d55/1-0000.mp3", duration = 70.4522449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:sardor-isle"] = {
  hash = "39c966",
  fullHash = "39c966",
  text = "Sardor Isle. Sardor Isle is a lush island situated off the western coast of Feralas, north of the perilous Isle of Dread. It serves as the home to Feathermoon Stronghold, the primary night elven military bastion in southern Kalimdor. Despite the heavy sentinel presence, parts of the island remain prowled by wild beasts and hostile naga creeping up from the shallows. Resting across the water from mainland Feralas, Sardor Isle is anchored by Feathermoon Stronghold, an impressive elven garrison commanded by General Shandris Feathermoon. While the fortress itself is heavily fortified and linked to the mainland via regular ferry crossings, the broader island is far from tame. Roaming wolf packs claim the timbered slopes, while encroaching naga lurk along the rocky western and northern beaches, constantly testing the resolve of the night elven sentinels.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_sardor-isle-ed0aad52ba3911702097/1-0000.mp3", duration = 52.5583673, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:sargeron"] = {
  hash = "ad0732",
  fullHash = "ad0732",
  text = "Sargeron. Sargeron is the crumbled ruin of an ancient night elf city located in northern Desolace. Shattered during the Great Sundering, its former temples of Elune have been defiled and claimed by the Hatefury satyrs, who renamed the grounds in tribute to their master, Sargeras. Before the War of the Ancients broke the continent, this settlement was a flourishing center of worship dedicated to Elune, housing sacred grounds such as Elune's Temple, Sanctuary, and Cathedral. Its true name is lost to antiquity. When the world shattered, the city collapsed into rubble, leaving behind sanctified relics like the Cup of Elune, Elune's Brazier, and Elune's Handmaiden. In the millennia since, corrupted Hatefury satyrs overran the stones, repurposing the sacred Kaldorei architecture into a stronghold honoring the titan Sargeras.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_sargeron-a9d48f4ee5a2dcba3b26/1-0000.mp3", duration = 51.7746939, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:savage-coast"] = {
  hash = "b1e6a3",
  fullHash = "b1e6a3",
  text = "Savage Coast. The Savage Coast is the rugged western shoreline of northern Stranglethorn Vale, stretching from the southern border of Westfall down toward Grom'gol Base Camp. Prowled by river crocolisks, shore basilisks, and hostile troll tribes, the strand earns its brutal name with every tide. Running from the cliffs bordering Westfall south toward Grom'gol Base Camp, the Savage Coast marks the perilous western edge of northern Stranglethorn Vale. The sandy banks offer little hospitality: snapjaws, crocolisks, and stonestalkers roam freely among the tide pools and drift logs. Just inland lie territorial jungle troll encampments, making any casual walk along the beach a fight for survival. Offshore, the waters dip toward the submerged dangers of the Vile Reef, ensuring that danger lurks both on the sand and under the waves.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_savage-coast-8ff812367a671bc182e6/1-0000.mp3", duration = 51.1216327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:scarab-dais"] = {
  hash = "25bf2a",
  fullHash = "25bf2a",
  text = "Scarab Dais. The Scarab Dais is an ancient raised platform situated northwest of the Scarab Wall in southern Silithus. It holds the legendary Scarab Gong, the ritual instrument tied directly to the magical wards sealing the qiraji empire within Ahn'Qiraj. Overlooking the shifting sands before the colossal barrier of the Scarab Wall, the Scarab Dais serves as the focal point for the seal placed upon Ahn'Qiraj a millennium ago. Upon this stone dais rests the Scarab Gong, a relic forged during the War of the Shifting Sands. According to ancient night elven history and the bronze dragonflight, striking this gong with the reconstructed Scepter of the Shifting Sands is the only means to shatter the magical barrier and breach the gates. Until that fateful moment arrives, the platform remains an ominous vigil over the sealed insectoid horrors slumbering beyond the wall.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_scarab-dais-5c228715eba0143644d1/1-0000.mp3", duration = 53.0546939, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:scarab-wall"] = {
  hash = "a76192",
  fullHash = "a76192",
  text = "Scarab Wall. The Scarab Wall is a colossal barricade at the southern border of Silithus, before the insectoid empire of Ahn'Qiraj. Conjured at the end of the War of the Shifting Sands, the barrier combined stone and bronze dragonflight sorcery to contain the ancient swarms. It stands as an imposing reminder of the terrible cost of that war. Spanning the desert from east to west, the Scarab Wall was raised a thousand years ago at the conclusion of the catastrophic War of the Shifting Sands. Pushed to the brink of annihilation by the qiraji, the night elf armies and their dragon allies forced the swarms back into their ancient kingdom. To contain them permanently, an immense rampart of desert stone was raised, crowned by a colossal magical barrier woven by the bronze dragonflight. The wall successfully severed the insectoid armies from Kalimdor, though the dread presence locked behind its stone remains palpable.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_scarab-wall-fa8fb044a451ee23ec68/1-0000.mp3", duration = 53.6555102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:scarlet-base-camp"] = {
  hash = "19279c",
  fullHash = "19279c",
  text = "Scarlet Base Camp. Scarlet Base Camp is a fortified outpost on the eastern shore of Lake Mereldar in the Eastern Plaguelands, just west of Tyr's Hand. Its barricades mark an outer defensive position of the Scarlet Crusade. Perched on the eastern bank of Lake Mereldar, Scarlet Base Camp is a barricaded fortification just west of Tyr's Hand. It marks an outer position of the Scarlet Crusade in the blighted countryside. Tyr's Hand is one of the Scarlet Crusade's strongholds in Lordaeron. The camp lies along its western approaches, in a region where the Crusade seeks to reclaim the land from the undead and treats outsiders with suspicion.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_scarlet-base-camp-dbcb9bf39781d8616785/1-0000.mp3", duration = 37.6685714, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:scarlet-basilica"] = {
  hash = "e30cf2",
  fullHash = "e30cf2",
  text = "Scarlet Basilica. The Scarlet Basilica is a grand cathedral standing at the heart of Tyr's Hand in the Eastern Plaguelands. It serves as the high seat of spiritual and military authority for the Scarlet Crusade in the east. Overseen by Crusader Lord Valdelmar, its halls represent the zealots' unyielding bastion against the Scourge. Rising prominently over the fortified enclave of Tyr's Hand, the Scarlet Basilica acts as the central cathedral and command hub for the Scarlet Crusade's operations in the Eastern Plaguelands. While the Scarlet Monastery far to the west in Tirisfal Glades serves as the order's spiritual heart, this basilica anchors their surviving territorial stronghold against the blighted horrors of Lordaeron. Guarded fiercely by elite crusaders, the cathedral houses Crusader Lord Valdelmar, who directs the local defense and zealously purges any outsiders who dare approach.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_scarlet-basilica-6c239feb43701221e259/1-0000.mp3", duration = 53.1591837, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:scarlet-monastery"] = {
  hash = "c13340",
  fullHash = "c13340",
  text = "Scarlet Monastery. Scarlet Monastery now shelters the fanatical Scarlet Crusade in Tirisfal Glades. Once a peaceful center of scholarship and worship for the Church of the Holy Light, it has become a fortified redoubt. Anyone approaching without red colors is treated as a carrier of the plague and executed on sight. Before the Third War, the Scarlet Monastery was a proud center of Lordaeron's priesthood. Its halls held theological studies and sacred archives, and paladins trained there. When the Scourge brought down the kingdom, surviving defenders held the site against the undead. Those survivors became the zealous Scarlet Crusade and fortified the cloister. Libraries, gardens, and the cathedral became parts of an armed stronghold. Four wings make up the Scarlet Monastery: a defiled graveyard, an expansive library, an armory, and a grand cathedral. Scarlet Commander Mograine and High Inquisitor Whitemane command its massive garrison. The Crusaders see every outsider as a plague-bearer, living or undead, Alliance or Horde. The Forsaken seek to destroy the garrison that threatens their doorstep, while Alliance leaders increasingly see the Crusade's violent extremism as a dangerous perversion of the Light.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_scarlet-monastery-e9268c2d350abf360160/1-0000.mp3", duration = 71.9934694, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:scarlet-monastery-armory"] = {
  hash = "f3b3d8",
  fullHash = "f3b3d8",
  text = "Scarlet Monastery Armory. The Armory is the fortified martial wing of the Scarlet Monastery, dedicated to manufacturing weapons and training recruits for the Scarlet Crusade. Behind locked iron gates, ranks of zealous footmen and crusaders drill under the fierce command of Herod, the Scarlet Champion. Once part of a peaceful retreat for Lordaeron's priesthood, the monastery was completely overhauled following the Third War. The Armory now serves as the Crusade's primary forge, weapons depot, and combat proving ground. From the open training grounds to the deep weapon halls, ranks of Scarlet footmen, conjurers, and crusaders prepare relentlessly to purge the Scourge and anyone else they deem tainted. The wing remains locked away from the Grand Vestibule, accessible only to those carrying the Scarlet Key found in the neighboring Library. At the heart of the wing lies the Hall of Champions, lined with towering statues dedicated to legendary heroes of the Crusade's past. The hall is overseen by Herod, a brutal warrior whose ferocity and whirlwinds are legendary among the crusaders. Herod trains the next generation of zealots personally; when hard-pressed in battle, his desperate recruits will pour into the chamber to fight and die for their champion.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_scarlet-monastery-armory-0767ab1a65363575d78d/1-0000.mp3", duration = 74.475102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:scarlet-monastery-cathedral"] = {
  hash = "cf3fd0",
  fullHash = "cf3fd0",
  text = "Scarlet Monastery Cathedral. The Cathedral is the spiritual and military heart of the Scarlet Monastery, housing the Crusade's inner sanctum. Once a serene hall of worship for Lordaeron's priesthood, it now serves as the command seat for High Inquisitor Whitemane and Scarlet Commander Mograine, guarded by the Crusade's most devoted zealots. Once a peaceful center of enlightenment and prayer for the priesthood of Lordaeron, the Cathedral fell into madness in the wake of the Third War. Following the fall of the kingdom to the Scourge, surviving zealots transformed the complex into a fortress of unyielding hatred. Beyond the manicured cloister of the Chapel Gardens lies the grand Crusader's Chapel, where priests and warriors gather in devotion to their crusade. They view all outsiders—regardless of race or faction—as carriers of the undead plague who must be cleansed by blade and holy fire.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_scarlet-monastery-cathedral-e09a0e37841383c45e32/1-0000.mp3", duration = 51.9314286, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:scarlet-monastery-graveyard"] = {
  hash = "efc8ef",
  fullHash = "efc8ef",
  text = "Scarlet Monastery Graveyard. The Scarlet Monastery Graveyard is the burial wing of the grand religious complex in northeastern Tirisfal Glades. Once a serene resting place for Lordaeron's clergy and nobility, it has fallen into chilling neglect, overrun by lingering undead and the corrupted magic of the skeletal Bloodmage Thalnos. Before the Third War, this cloistered cemetery served as the peaceful burial ground for the priesthood of Lordaeron. When the Scarlet Crusade fortified the monastery against the undead, their fanatical vigilance failed to cleanse their own crypts. Today, the Graveyard is neglected and deeply haunted. Lingering spirits, restless corpses, and shambling horrors wander the Chamber of Atonement and the mist-covered Forlorn Cloister. Rather than hallowed rest, the dead have found endless torment beneath the crusade's uncaring gaze. At the rear of the grounds stands Honor's Tomb, where the greatest mockery of the Crusade's dogma festers: Bloodmage Thalnos. No record tells who he was in life, but the plague of undeath claimed him, and he rose as a skeletal horror wielding shadow bolts and fire. The crusaders above preach total extermination of the undead plague, yet they harbor a rotting, fallen sorcerer within their own consecrated soil.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_scarlet-monastery-graveyard-acef6b34612445852602/1-0000.mp3", duration = 75.6244898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:scarlet-monastery-library"] = {
  hash = "228f48",
  fullHash = "228f48",
  text = "Scarlet Monastery Library. The Library is the eastern wing of the Scarlet Monastery in Tirisfal Glades, once dedicated to holy scholarship and Lordaeron's priesthood. Now guarded by battle hounds and zealots, it houses ancient archives and rare tomes fiercely coveted by scholars and mages from both factions. Before the Third War, the monastery served as a center for learning and enlightenment for Lordaeron's priesthood. The eastern cloisters housed vast archives of religious thought, historical records, and arcane treatises. When the Scarlet Crusade fortified the monastery against the Scourge, they retained these repositories, turning the quiet halls into an armed redoubt. Today, beastmasters run tracking hounds through the courtyards while battle-mages patrol the shelves, guarding historic volumes like the Compendium of the Fallen and ancient research on the Titans against outsiders. Breaching the Library requires navigating past Houndmaster Loksey in the Huntsman's Cloister, whose trained hounds sniff out infiltrators seeking passage into the inner chambers. Beyond the Gallery of Treasures lies the Athenaeum, overseen by Arcanist Doan. A formidable mage who wields devastating fire and protective sorcery, Doan studies in isolation, fiercely defending the Crusade's hoarded knowledge from any who venture into the heart of the complex.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_scarlet-monastery-library-5f8526c43435c9041986/1-0000.mp3", duration = 80.2220408, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:scholomance"] = {
  hash = "8c8fa7",
  fullHash = "8c8fa7",
  text = "Scholomance. Hidden deep within the crypts beneath Caer Darrow, Scholomance is the Scourge's premier academy of dark arts and necromancy. Here, mortals and undead alike study the Plague of Undeath and fleshcrafting under the sadistic guidance of Darkmaster Gandling and the watchful gaze of the lich Ras Frostwhisper. Before the Third War, Caer Darrow was the proud seat of House Barov, a wealthy aristocratic dynasty holding vast lands across Lordaeron, including Brill, Tarren Mill, and Southshore. Fearing the loss of their wealth and mortality, the Barovs succumbed to Kel'Thuzad's seductive promises of eternal life. They deeded their fortress and ancestral catacombs to the Cult of the Damned, who promptly repaid the nobles by butchering them and turning the sprawling crypts into a hidden necromantic training ground.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_scholomance-db097fb7f3f3a1af71ab/1-0000.mp3", duration = 51.2261224, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:scholomance-classic"] = {
  hash = "25a5fe",
  fullHash = "25a5fe",
  text = "Scholomance (Classic). Scholomance is a subterranean academy of necromancy hidden in the crypts beneath Caer Darrow in the Western Plaguelands. Founded by Kel'Thuzad and the Cult of the Damned, it serves as the Scourge's premier training ground for mortal spellcasters seeking to master death. Before the Third War devastated Lordaeron, the rogue archmage Kel'Thuzad sought recruits for his Cult of the Damned. He found willing benefactors in the wealthy Barov family, lords of Caer Darrow, who traded their lands and crypts in exchange for promised immortality. Instead, the cult slaughtered them and raised them as monstrous undead. Beneath the ruined fortress, the Cult renovated the ancient Arathorian catacombs into an academy for the dark arts. Here, mortal acolytes study plaguecraft, soul binding, and flesh shaping away from watchful eyes, honing the foul magics that shattered Lordaeron. Though Kel'Thuzad has moved on to direct Scourge operations from above, the school remains fully operational. The eternal lich Ras Frostwhisper holds vigil over the complex in the Scourge's name, while the mortal necromancer Darkmaster Gandling serves as its headmaster, tutoring ambitious sorcerers in horrific experiments. Those students who display weakness are discarded into the catacombs and fed to the Ravenian, a voracious wight lurking in the lower vaults.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_scholomance-classic-a0c4fe8986142011c891/1-0000.mp3", duration = 82.3118367, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:sea-of-cinders"] = {
  hash = "3f82b4",
  fullHash = "3f82b4",
  text = "Sea of Cinders. The Sea of Cinders is an expanse of scorched, windblown terrain lying across the southern stretch of the Searing Gorge, directly north of Blackrock Mountain. Ravaged by elemental heat and relentless gusts, the flat ground constantly vents ash and embers, creating a choking wasteland where very little can endure. Stretching between the rocky cliffs of the Searing Gorge and the imposing bulk of Blackrock Mountain, the Sea of Cinders earns its name from the suffocating haze of particulate and burning grit that constantly sweeps its plains. When the Firelord Ragnaros was summoned into the mortal world centuries ago, the violent upheaval fractured the Redridge Mountains and transformed the surrounding landscape into an arid slag heap. The Sea of Cinders is among the most barren remnants of that cataclysm, an uninhabitable plain where the earth itself refuses to cool. Because it borders the north approach to Blackrock Mountain, travelers and scavengers often have no choice but to cross these scorching flats. The winds here kick up dense clouds of blinding ash that hide sudden fissures, elemental horrors, and wandering predators adapted to the searing heat. Between the hostile environment and the looming presence of the Dark Iron dwarves who claim the region, any trek across the cinders demands vigilance and swift passage.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_sea-of-cinders-c171d727172f45c24989/1-0000.mp3", duration = 81.0579592, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:sentry-point"] = {
  hash = "963540",
  fullHash = "963540",
  text = "Sentry Point. Sentry Point is a human watchtower stationed on the mainland edge of Dustwallow Marsh, guarding the causeway bridge that leads directly to Theramore Isle. Maintained by Theramore's forces, it monitors traffic entering and exiting Lady Jaina Proudmoore's island citadel. Positioned at the western end of the long bridge linking Theramore Isle to the treacherous wetlands of Dustwallow Marsh, Sentry Point serves as the stronghold's first line of defense on the mainland. The stone scout tower keeps watch over the swamp road, ensuring that hostile marsh beasts or Horde raiders cannot approach the island city unnoticed.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_sentry-point-fb476ec406182ac85b84/1-0000.mp3", duration = 34.7428571, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:serpent-s-coil"] = {
  hash = "58548f",
  fullHash = "58548f",
  text = "Serpent's Coil. Serpent's Coil is a winding network of caves and burnt-out encampments nestled in the eastern ridges of the Blasted Lands. Littered with scorched orcish architecture left behind from the older wars, it serves as a secluded redoubt for dark cultists. Carved into the jagged eastern cliffs of the Blasted Lands, the winding path and caverns of Serpent's Coil wind past ruined orcish huts that date back to the First and Second Wars. Rather than simple beasts, the tunnels harbor cabals of Shadowsworn cultists and practitioners of dark arts drawn to the residual demonic energies permeating the red wastes. Deep within its twisting hollows, they work far from the watchful eyes of Nethergarde Keep.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_serpent-s-coil-0b76f592d4e766fd32d4/1-0000.mp3", duration = 42.2138776, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:shadow-hold"] = {
  hash = "c6805c",
  fullHash = "c6805c",
  text = "Shadow Hold. Shadow Hold is a sprawling subterranean complex hidden within the blighted canyon of Jaedenar in western Felwood. Once a sacred night elf barrow den, it has been seized and desecrated by the Shadow Council to serve as their primary bastion on Kalimdor. Beneath the corrupted red earth of Jaedenar lies Shadow Hold, a vast underground labyrinth carved out of what was once an ancient night elf barrow den. Where sleeping druids once rested to tend the Emerald Dream, dark warlocks and demonic servitors now stalk twisted corridors illuminated by fel fire. At its deepest tiers, the cult commands a formidable force under the dreadlord Lord Banehollow, Prince Xavalis, and Shadow Lord Fel'dan, making it the supreme seat of demonic influence in the region.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_shadow-hold-25a3660df38855257d74/1-0000.mp3", duration = 48.5877551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:shadowbreak-ravine"] = {
  hash = "647651",
  fullHash = "647651",
  text = "Shadowbreak Ravine. Shadowbreak Ravine is a secluded mountain hollow tucked away in southeastern Desolace. Cloaked among jagged crags near Magram Village, this eerie ravine serves as a hidden redoubt for the demonic forces and mortal zealots of the Burning Blade. Tucked deep into the barren mountain ridges southeast of Magram Village, Shadowbreak Ravine provides the Burning Blade with a secluded staging ground away from the eyes of wandering centaur tribes. Amid strange, oversized mushrooms and grim ritual encampments, cultists labor alongside summoned demons. Here, dark practitioners invoke fel energies and tend heavy ritual chains, using the natural isolation of the ravine to marshal reinforcements and maintain a firm demonic foothold within the badlands of Desolace.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_shadowbreak-ravine-249a11f8271f58e03035/1-0000.mp3", duration = 45.6881633, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:shadowfang-keep"] = {
  hash = "c0187f",
  fullHash = "c0187f",
  text = "Shadowfang Keep. On a rugged bluff above Pyrewood Village, Shadowfang Keep broods over southern Silverpine Forest. Once Silverlaine Keep, it fell to worgen summoned during the Third War and became the lair of the mad Archmage Arugal, haunted by slain nobles and overrun by wolf-beasts. Before the Third War, Baron Silverlaine ruled nearby Pyrewood Village from his ancestral seat, Silverlaine Keep. When the Scourge swept through Lordaeron, desperate wizards sought forbidden means to stem the undead tide. Drawing on Dalaran mage Ur's dimensional research, Archmage Arugal summoned worgen from beyond this world. The savage beasts tore through the Scourge, then escaped control. They rampaged across southern Silverpine and besieged the keep, killing Baron Silverlaine, his family, and the defenders under Commander Springvale. Guilt and grief broke Arugal's sanity. He claimed the fortress, renamed it Shadowfang Keep, and called the monstrous predators his children. Shadowfang Keep is the heart of a growing wolf curse in Silverpine Forest. Arugal dwells in the highest observatory, surrounded by ravenous worgen of the Moonrage and Shadowfang packs. Sinister beasts also prowl the keep, including his massive pet wolf, Fenrus. Below Arugal's sanctum, the restless ghosts of the keep's former lords and household remain trapped in torment. Baron Silverlaine and Commander Springvale are among them, cursed to defend the halls they failed to protect in life. From the walls, Arugal's influence reaches Pyrewood Village and the surrounding woodlands, threatening the neighboring Forsaken who seek to purge the forest of his madness.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_shadowfang-keep-c130f4c5154e616d0487/1-0000.mp3", duration = 101.093878, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:shadowprey-village"] = {
  hash = "2766c5",
  fullHash = "2766c5",
  text = "Shadowprey Village. Shadowprey Village is a remote Horde fishing settlement built along the rocky southern coast of Sar'theris Strand in Desolace. Populated largely by Darkspear troll fishermen and supported by tauren service keepers, it acts as a critical coastal outpost and trade lifeline amidst a barren wasteland. Tucked against the cliffs of southern Desolace, Shadowprey Village offers safe harbor in a region notorious for centaur clans and desolate wastes. The settlement is primarily an encampment of troll fishermen whose hauls sustain not only their own village but also inland outposts like Ghost Walker Post. Despite the rough shoreline, towering wooden piers reach out into the ocean, allowing local anglers to brave deep waters and dive for shellfish among the submerged wreckage dotting Sar'theris Strand. Maintaining a frontier port in Desolace is fraught with danger. Beyond the inland threat of marauding centaur and the demon-summoning Burning Blade, the ocean itself tests the village's survival with rough shoals and hungry sea life. Tauren allies, including Taiga Wisemane and the keepers of the inn and wind rider roost, help shore up defenses and keep trade routes open across Kalimdor.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_shadowprey-village-6beeb5be97235ece77d7/1-0000.mp3", duration = 74.5012245, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:shadowsong-shrine"] = {
  hash = "23cca9",
  fullHash = "23cca9",
  text = "Shadowsong Shrine. Shadowsong Shrine is a ruined hillside sanctuary situated in western Azshara, not far from the Ashenvale border. Haunted by the restless spirits of ancient Highborne, the site bears the illustrious name of the Shadowsong bloodline, though its true historical connection to them remains shrouded in mystery. Perched upon a high hill overlooking the shattered lands of Azshara, the Shadowsong Shrine lies in ancient, overgrown ruin. Like much of the surrounding coast, it is plagued by the lingering apparitions of Highborne ghosts who wander among the fallen stonework, seeking to extinguish any mortal life that trespasses upon their domain. The shrine shares its name with Maiev Shadowsong, the unrelenting warden of Illidan Stormrage. Whether this sanctuary honors her family or dates back to before the Sundering is a secret lost to the ages.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_shadowsong-shrine-ff31bc036b455e6e1096/1-0000.mp3", duration = 52.5844898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:shady-rest-inn"] = {
  hash = "60dd59",
  fullHash = "60dd59",
  text = "Shady Rest Inn. The Shady Rest Inn was once a welcoming human-built waystation sitting just off the road between the Barrens and Theramore Isle. Today, only charred timbers and smoldering ashes remain following a sudden and violent arson attack. The tragedy left the innkeeper shattered and sparked tension across the marsh as investigators sift through the wreckage for clues. Built to serve travelers navigating the treacherous crossing between Dustwallow Marsh and the Barrens, the Shady Rest Inn stood as an outpost of civilized comfort in a harsh wilderness. That ended when the inn was abruptly razed to the ground in a devastating fire. Innkeeper James Hyal managed to survive the inferno, but the blaze claimed the lives of his wife and child. Traumatized and grief-stricken, Hyal was left with nothing as the ruins became an active crime scene, drawing scrutiny from Theramore authorities desperate to identify who ignited the flames.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_shady-rest-inn-23b70ec0af9da6463fbb/1-0000.mp3", duration = 55.6146939, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:shalzaru-s-lair"] = {
  hash = "2b4833",
  fullHash = "2b4833",
  text = "Shalzaru's Lair. Shalzaru's Lair is a sprawling, submerged cavern located at the northern tip of the Isle of Dread in Feralas. It serves as a major stronghold for the Hatecrest naga and shelters their formidable master, Shalzaru. From within these damp tunnels, the serpentine invaders coordinate their aggressive presence just south of Feathermoon Stronghold. Carved into the rugged bluffs at the northern end of the Isle of Dread, Shalzaru's Lair is divided into a massive main cavern and a smaller auxiliary chamber, linked together by submerged subterranean waterways. The interior teems with Hatecrest serpent guards and spell-weaving sorceresses on high alert. Deep within the recesses dwells Lord Shalzaru, the serpentine overlord directing the Hatecrest brood across the island. Its proximity to Sardor Isle poses a direct, constant threat to the night elf Sentinels garrisoned at Feathermoon Stronghold just across the channel.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_shalzaru-s-lair-26acf136bad75eb84837/1-0000.mp3", duration = 58.3836735, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:shaper-s-terrace"] = {
  hash = "2f2a58",
  fullHash = "2f2a58",
  text = "Shaper's Terrace. Perched high on the northern bluffs above Marshal's Refuge, the Shaper's Terrace is a mysterious overlook marked by ancient titan architecture. It is named in honor of Khaz'goroth the Shaper and bears quiet witness to the primordial experiments that shaped Un'Goro Crater. Overlooking the lush, prehistoric basin of Un'Goro Crater, the Shaper's Terrace holds monumental stone edifices left behind by the titans. Believed to be dedicated to Khaz'goroth the Shaper, this high plateau stands as a testament to the celestial architects who cultivated the crater as a living laboratory for early life on Azeroth. From these elevated stones, ancient entities once observed and directed the flora and fauna below. Its cold stone arches and massive metallic plinths stand in stark, enigmatic contrast to the untamed wilderness thriving directly beneath them.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_shaper-s-terrace-c75ad817569fe80279c5/1-0000.mp3", duration = 53.6555102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:shatter-scar-vale"] = {
  hash = "12f370",
  fullHash = "12f370",
  text = "Shatter Scar Vale. Shatter Scar Vale is a scorched basin in central Felwood, located just north of Bloodvenom Falls. It marks the ground zero where demonic infernals crashed into northern Kalimdor during the Third War. Today, the craters still burn with fel fire and host lingering remnants of the Burning Legion. During the Third War, the Burning Legion rained living siege engines down upon northern Kalimdor. Shatter Scar Vale bore the brunt of this celestial assault. Demonic infernals plummeted from the skies, tearing through the ancient canopy and smashing deep craters into the earth. The violent impact shattered the terrain, leaving blackened hollows that smolder with unholy fire to this day. Though the Legion was shattered at Mount Hyjal, the infernals and lingering lesser demons never truly dispersed, maintaining an entrenched foothold in the scarred earth.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_shatter-scar-vale-b8cc7dc7e902eb001628/1-0000.mp3", duration = 48.9795918, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:shattered-strand"] = {
  hash = "882967",
  fullHash = "882967",
  text = "Shattered Strand. The Shattered Strand is a wild, rugged coastline flanking the northern rim of the Bay of Storms in Azshara. Dominated by crashing surf and crumbling ancient ruins, the beach crawls with hazardous marine beasts and tribal aquatic creatures. Stretching along the northern edge of the Bay of Storms, the Shattered Strand is far from a peaceful shoreline. The rocky beaches and tidal shallows are heavily inhabited by aggressive wildlife, including towering sea giants, multi-headed hydras, and snapping makrura, alongside encampments of coastal murlocs. Crumbling elven architecture overlooks the waves, anchored by landmarks such as the ancient Temple of Arkkoran and the solitary Tower of Eldara. For travelers navigating Azshara's treacherous cliffs, descending to this isolated strip of coast means confronting some of the region's deadliest amphibious threats.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_shattered-strand-1eb586c1b99d7b161825/1-0000.mp3", duration = 54.2302041, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:shifting-mire"] = {
  hash = "b527df",
  fullHash = "b527df",
  text = "Shifting Mire. The Shifting Mire forms the vast, muddy heart of the Swamp of Sorrows, lying just west of the Pool of Tears. Its name comes from the strange, restless silt beneath the murky water, which seems to drift and gather under the influence of an unseen force. Spanning the central reaches of the marshland, the Shifting Mire is an unsettling expanse where solid footing is hard to come by. The silt along the swamp floor shifts and collects as though moved by a living will, driven by a strange, ambient energy permeating the peat. Amidst the drifting mud prowl predatory swamp jaguars, massive sorrow spinners, and hungry sawtooth crocolisks, while bands of broken draenei known as the Lost Ones stalk the sunken paths.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_shifting-mire-9232dae31f3cf0e0d7a1/1-0000.mp3", duration = 44.9567347, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:slag-pit"] = {
  hash = "4f55c2",
  fullHash = "4f55c2",
  text = "Slag Pit. The Slag Pit is an extensive subterranean mining excavation nestled inside the Cauldron of Searing Gorge. Overseen by ruthless Dark Iron taskmasters and slavers, these fiery tunnels are driven by forced labor under the shadow of Blackrock Mountain. Trespassers must contend not only with hostile dwarf overseers, but also ravenous incendosaurs prowling the lower levels. Carved into the depths of the Cauldron, the Slag Pit represents the grinding industrial engine of the Dark Iron dwarves in Searing Gorge. Day and night, enslaved workers are driven to extract rich ores and minerals under the lash of brutal overseers and taskmasters. The environment is harsh and suffocating, dominated by scorching heat, choking dust, and the clatter of iron picks. The Dark Irons maintain ruthless discipline over their excavation, violently repelling any outside interference with their supply lines to Blackrock Depths. Beyond the cruelty of the slavers, the physical tunnels themselves pose a deadly hazard to workers and intruders alike. In the lower reaches of the excavation, the earth breaks open into scorching caverns inhabited by incendosaurs—ferocious reptilian beasts native to the fiery underground. Despite the constant danger of being devoured by these subterranean predators, the Dark Iron masters continue to push their labor force deeper into the rock, entirely indifferent to the lives lost in the process.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_slag-pit-d43f81816434a1430102/1-0000.mp3", duration = 91.9771429, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:slaughter-hollow"] = {
  hash = "ae4c3b",
  fullHash = "ae4c3b",
  text = "Slaughter Hollow. In the snowy heights of the Alterac Mountains, Crushridge ogres hold Slaughter Hollow as a secluded camp. Travelers rarely come here except to hunt ogres or cross the frozen passes. Slaughter Hollow shelters the Crushridge ogres high in the peaks between Hillsbrad and Alterac. Makeshift fires, scattered bones, and crude shelters mark the mound. Other ogre dens in the highlands often see fights over territory or stolen supplies, but Slaughter Hollow stays quiet and beyond the reach of major military campaigns. Crushridge ogres sometimes keep prisoners taken along the mountain trails in their secluded mound. Travelers speak of Rizz Loosebolt, an unlucky gnome locked in a crude cage and trying to bargain with anyone who passes. Captives seldom fare well against the clan's appetite.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_slaughter-hollow-582dae705718cf3507ee/1-0000.mp3", duration = 49.4497959, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:slither-rock"] = {
  hash = "8ce7b6",
  fullHash = "8ce7b6",
  text = "Slither Rock. Slither Rock is a secluded cave tucked into the far northeastern cliffs of the Burning Steppes, past the perilous Terror Wing Path. It serves as the solitary refuge of Cyrus Therepentous, a sharp-tongued black dragonspawn with his own hidden agenda. Tucked away in the scorched crags beyond the Terror Wing Path, Slither Rock is easy to overlook amid the fiery wastes of the Burning Steppes. Far from the roaring forges and marching orc warbands of Blackrock Spire, this remote hollow houses the solitary black dragonspawn Cyrus Therepentous. While dragonspawn are typically fiercely loyal soldiers of the Black Dragonflight, Cyrus maintains a strange, cantankerous independence here, willing to converse with mortal travelers rather than slaughtering them on sight—though never without a steady stream of insults.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_slither-rock-f6cea77a2ddf7d8ad91b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 49.7632653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:slithering-scar"] = {
  hash = "2f5b68",
  fullHash = "2f5b68",
  text = "Slithering Scar. The Slithering Scar is a sprawling silithid hive dominating the southern cliffs of Un'Goro Crater. Home to the aggressive Gorishi swarm, its excavated tunnels and organic spires represent a dangerous insectoid encroachment into the primeval basin. Gouged into the red earth and rock of southern Un'Goro, the Slithering Scar stands as an alien intrusion against the lush prehistoric landscape. The hive is controlled by the Gorishi silithid, an organized strain of insectoids that have honeycombed the cliffs with damp burrows and egg chambers. Within these subterranean warrens, workers and tunnelers nurture vast clutches of eggs and grubs alongside towering wasp broods. More alarmingly, the colony cultivates fledgling silithid colossi, monstrous behemoths intended to carve an uncontested path through the crater's ecology. Strange crystalline formations nestled within the hive pulse with unnatural resonance, acting as focal points for the colony's dark, coordinated will. Scholars and explorers who traverse the perimeter of Un'Goro often notice a disturbing anomaly regarding the hive's location. While ancient crystalline pylons stand sentinel across the northern, eastern, and western boundaries of the basin, the southern reaches possess no such pillar. It is precisely in this unprotected gap that the Gorishi have burrowed inward from the neighboring deserts. Whether the silithid purposefully undermined a forgotten southern ward or simply invaded where the crater's primordial wards were weakest remains an unsettling mystery to those watching their rapid expansion.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_slithering-scar-8d993ff593793f3c69a2/1-0000.mp3", duration = 95.7910204, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:sorrow-hill"] = {
  hash = "f2cc50",
  fullHash = "f2cc50",
  text = "Sorrow Hill. Sorrow Hill is an expansive graveyard resting south of Andorhal in the Western Plaguelands. Once the final resting place for commoners, soldiers, and nobility alike, it has been corrupted by nearby plague cauldrons into a sprawling haven of restless undead. Before the Third War, Sorrow Hill served as the primary burial ground for the city of Andorhal and its surrounding townships. When the Scourge unleashed the Plague of Undeath, the gravediggers could not keep pace with the dead. Neatly kept crypts were soon surrounded by shallow, poorly marked earthen pits. Eventually, terrified survivors ceased traditional burials entirely, instead resorting to burning the corpses of their loved ones in a mass pyre to the south out of fear they would rise. Before the town fell completely, the remnants of the populace entombed Lord Uther the Lightbringer atop the cemetery's high ground, bestowing upon the hill its tragic name. The Scourge's foul plague cauldrons across the region have long since corrupted Sorrow Hill. The interred soldiers and citizens now wander the mist-shrouded headstones as skeletal flayers and ravenous ghouls, while skeletal mages and a local Scourge commander nest in the southeastern crypts. Because the cemetery sits directly along the road between Chillwind Camp and Andorhal, both the Alliance and the Argent Dawn frequently order patrols to culled the ranks of undead here to maintain a vital foothold in the region.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_sorrow-hill-74b882a98ae4e080f67d/1-0000.mp3", duration = 85.6816327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:sorrowmurk"] = {
  hash = "371d92",
  fullHash = "371d92",
  text = "Sorrowmurk. The Sorrowmurk is the treacherous wetland expanse lying directly east of the Pool of Tears in the Swamp of Sorrows. Long dominated by patrolling green dragonkin guarding the approach to the Sunken Temple, it remains one of the most perilous regions in the eastern marshes. Stretching out along the eastern edge of the Pool of Tears, the Sorrowmurk is a dense and murky mire that borders the Forbidding Sea. For years, the waters and tangled boughs have crawled with ferocious green dragonspawn and towering drakes, remnants of the brood sent to watch over the submerged temple of Atal'Hakkar. Their presence ensures that only seasoned travelers dare wander through its sodden reaches.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_sorrowmurk-7f20704fca49f98384ac/1-0000.mp3", duration = 40.6465306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:south-seas"] = {
  hash = "019ed5",
  fullHash = "019ed5",
  text = "South Seas. The South Seas encompass the vast, sun-drenched waters separating the southern regions of Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms. Lapping at the shores of Tanaris and Stranglethorn Vale, these tropical expanses are famed for lucrative trade routes, perilous reefs, and brazen pirate crews. Spanning southward beneath the Great Sea and the Maelstrom, the South Seas wash against the deserts of Tanaris and the lush jungles of Stranglethorn Vale. Since the Great Sundering shattered the primeval continent ten thousand years ago, this ocean has nurtured remote tropical islands, unique oceanic beasts, and independent merchant networks. Goblin shipping cartels, operating out of havens like Steamwheedle Port and Booty Bay, maintain tenuous shipping lanes across the waves. However, the waters remain notoriously lawless, crawling with corsairs, Bloodsail buccaneers, and predatory sea life that prey on any vessel daring to venture far from shore. Beyond the coastal trade lanes lies a patchwork of legendary islands and sunken perils. Notable lands scattered across the South Seas include the ancient troll island of Zandalar and the produce-rich shores of Tel'Abim. Yet merchant sailors must contend with far more than cutthroats; the deeper waters harbor colossal giant squids, primeval sea turtles, and lurking naga who guard ancient ruins swallowed by the waves. Navigating these waters demands seasoned captains, reliable charts, and substantial firepower.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_south-seas-ef161f08fc6e7f6a5e69/1-0000.mp3", duration = 95.0073469, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:southern-savage-coast"] = {
  hash = "cdc0cc",
  fullHash = "cdc0cc",
  text = "Southern Savage Coast. The Southern Savage Coast encompasses the long strand of beaches and tidal waters hugging southwestern Stranglethorn Vale. Flanked by the treacherous depths of the South Seas, these shores are home to prowling wildlife, hostile naga, and the ever-present threat of coastal raiders. Stretching down the western edge of the southern jungle, the Southern Savage Coast marks where the mainland gives way to the warm currents of the South Seas. Divided from the northern Savage Coast by the submerged ruins and perils of the Vile Reef, this coastline skirts inlets near the Gurubashi Arena and Nek'mani Wellspring before tapering off past Booty Bay and Janeiro's Point. While the beaches offer open ground compared to the suffocating interior jungle, travelers must contend with aggressive reptilian predators, predatory naga hunting the shallows, and passing corsairs scanning the strand for easy prey.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_southern-savage-coast-99c683416937989147d0/1-0000.mp3", duration = 53.3420408, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:southfury-river"] = {
  hash = "87a60c",
  fullHash = "87a60c",
  text = "Southfury River. The Southfury River is one of the most prominent waterways on central Kalimdor, carving a swift and wide path southward from the heights of Mount Hyjal. It forms the natural border separating Azshara from Ashenvale, as well as Durotar from the Barrens, before plunging into the Great Sea near Ratchet. Beginning high in Mount Hyjal, the Southfury drops over dramatic cascades before coursing through central Kalimdor. Its roaring waters serve as a clear geographic divide, separating the sacred forests of Ashenvale from ancient Azshara, and dividing the arid hills of Durotar from the savanna of the Barrens. Only a few strategic bridges dare cross its swift currents—most notably the heavy span guarding the rear gates of Orgrimmar and the crossing connecting Durotar to the Barrens. The river eventually empties into the Great Sea via a cascading waterfall just northeast of the neutral port of Ratchet. For the Horde, the Southfury is both a shield and a vital natural highway. It protects the western approaches of Durotar and Orgrimmar from immediate ground assault while supplying fresh mountain runoff through an otherwise scorched, sun-baked landscape. Just north of the western crossing into Orgrimmar lies a singular geographic crossroads where four major territories converge: Ashenvale, Azshara, Durotar, and the Barrens. Control over this churning waterway ensures supply lines between Orgrimmar and the outlying Horde settlements remain intact.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_southfury-river-99cf8f8558149e387741/1-0000.mp3", duration = 89.3126531, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:southmoon-ruins"] = {
  hash = "d8f5e0",
  fullHash = "d8f5e0",
  text = "Southmoon Ruins. The Southmoon Ruins are ancient stone remains situated in southern Tanaris, just north of the Valley of the Watchers. Long claimed by the brutish Dunemaul ogres, the crumbling stonework preserves remnants of the ancient troll civilization that dominated the desert before the sands swallowed it. Rising out of the blistering wastes of southern Tanaris, the Southmoon Ruins stand as a weathered testament to the ancient troll empires that once ruled Kalimdor's southern reaches. Like nearby Eastmoon and Noonshade, its stone pillars and altars have weathered thousands of years of wind and abrasive grit. The site has often served as a squalid camp for the Dunemaul ogres, who show little reverence for the craftsmanship of the past, using the ancient structures purely for shelter and territory. The architectural remnants are unmistakably trollish, but little is recorded of the people who raised Southmoon's stones or why they abandoned them. Whatever they left behind lies buried beneath the drifting sand, and the ogres who squat among the pillars care nothing for it.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_southmoon-ruins-5dcf38112fd16f8da535/1-0000.mp3", duration = 64.9926531, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:southpoint-tower"] = {
  hash = "05d321",
  fullHash = "05d321",
  text = "Southpoint Tower. Forsaken scouts watch Alliance movements from Southpoint Tower, near Hillsbrad's western edge. The ruined Lordaeron post overlooks the road into Silverpine Forest. Southpoint Tower overlooks the highway between Silverpine Forest and Hillsbrad Foothills. The Kingdom of Lordaeron built it to guard traffic between the eastern farmlands and the western woods. After the Third War and the Scourge's destruction of the kingdom, the stonework fell into ruin. Now the tower stands roofless and crumbling, a remnant of human rule in fractured lands. Forsaken agents watch Alliance patrols from the ruined tower. Deathstalker Lesh is among those stationed there, keeping an eye on nearby human settlements in Hillsbrad Fields and travelers on the main road. The vantage remains valuable despite the collapsed walls. So close to hostile territory, this lonely post often becomes a flashpoint for skirmishes.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_southpoint-tower-f52b06ad31e64ee8651a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 53.6032653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:southridge-beach"] = {
  hash = "abd9bd",
  fullHash = "abd9bd",
  text = "Southridge Beach. Southridge Beach stretches along the southern shores of the Bay of Storms in central Azshara. Once a grand district of the ancient kaldorei city of Eldarath, its shattered ruins are now overrun by hostile naga, murlocs, and towering sea giants. Southridge Beach forms the southern boundary of the turbulent Bay of Storms. Thousands of years ago, this coast was part of the magnificent Highborne city of Eldarath, but the Great Sundering broke the land and plunged most of its palaces beneath the tides. Today, crumbling marble pillars and weathered stone pavilions dot the sand. Rather than scholars and noble courtiers, the strand is contested by aggressive Spitelash naga, prowling makrura, vicious hydras, and scavenging murloc tribes. Even massive sea giants occasionally wander ashore from the deeps.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_southridge-beach-b8ec7504dce716eb0d6b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 50.8865306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:southshore-town-hall"] = {
  hash = "514df3",
  fullHash = "514df3",
  text = "Southshore Town Hall. At Southshore Town Hall, Magistrate Henry Maleb directs the town’s affairs and defenses. It remains one of Lordaeron’s last centers of civil authority, while the Alliance struggles to hold ground in the plagued north. Southshore Town Hall keeps human rule alive amid Lordaeron’s ruin. Magistrate Henry Maleb governs from his office, working with officials such as Kundric Zanden to secure supplies and preserve order. Undead press from nearby lands, Syndicate rogues threaten the region, and Hillsbrad’s border sees fierce skirmishes. From the hall, town leaders direct Southshore’s survival against these threats.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_southshore-town-hall-3804d418c2919646a818/1-0000.mp3", duration = 39.3665306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:southwind-village"] = {
  hash = "528169",
  fullHash = "528169",
  text = "Southwind Village. Southwind Village is the haunted ruin of a night elf outpost located in the sands of Silithus. Wiped out by the qiraji during the War of the Shifting Sands, its streets are now bound by the restless spirits of fallen defenders and prowling silithid. Southwind Village once served as a primary night elf stronghold during the ancient War of the Shifting Sands a thousand years ago. When the qiraji legions breached the defensive perimeter, Valstann Staghelm—son of Archdruid Fandral Staghelm—led a detachment to hold the village. The defense ended in utter disaster: the night elven defenders were overwhelmed and slaughtered, and Valstann himself was dragged away into captivity by General Rajaxx. The village was completely ruined, leaving its former buildings fractured under the sun. A millennium after its fall, Southwind Village remains an open wound in the desert. The trauma of the assault was so absolute that the spirits of the slain night elf Sentinels and druids could not move on. Driven mad by the horror of their final moments, these restless phantoms—led by the Shade of Ambermoon—endlessly roam the crumbled stone walls, lashing out at any who intrude while predatory silithid swarm the surrounding dunes.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_southwind-village-66664db9474fd3960723/1-0000.mp3", duration = 73.8742857, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:splinterspear-junction"] = {
  hash = "e26ea2",
  fullHash = "e26ea2",
  text = "Splinterspear Junction. Splinterspear Junction is a strategic fork in the main road winding through the heart of the Swamp of Sorrows. Dominated by a rugged orc watchtower and regularly patrolled by Horde sentries, it divides the path between northern retreats and southern outposts. Standing near the center of the Swamp of Sorrows, Splinterspear Junction divides travelers between two paths: the northern trail leading toward the Lost One refuge of the Harborage and the Fallow Sanctuary, and the southern road running directly past the fortress of Stonard toward the Blasted Lands. An ancient orcish watchtower oversees the split, garrisoned and patrolled by active Horde troops. For members of the Horde, it serves as a secure landmark in a treacherous marsh; for Alliance travelers, it represents a deadly ambush point along an already unforgiving trail.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_splinterspear-junction-7f8a1431de246270b80f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 49.0057143, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:stagalbog"] = {
  hash = "321630",
  fullHash = "321630",
  text = "Stagalbog. The Stagalbog is a dense, waterlogged marsh stretch across the southern reaches of the Swamp of Sorrows. Bordering the southern mountain range just below the Pool of Tears, it remains an untamed wilderness. Stretching beneath the shadow of the southern crags, the Stagalbog is typical of the Swamp of Sorrows: stagnant water, drooping reeds, and tangled vegetation. It sits immediately south of the sunken ruins of the Temple of Atal'Hakkar, caught between the tainted lake and the rocky boundary of the region. Apart from rough wildlife, civilization has established no foothold here. Because it borders the Pool of Tears, the Stagalbog frequently catches whatever shadows or corruptions seep from the Sunken Temple.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_stagalbog-3e2fec326b31cfb02fc0/1-0000.mp3", duration = 44.4865306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:stagalbog-cave"] = {
  hash = "162831",
  fullHash = "162831",
  text = "Stagalbog Cave. Stagalbog Cave is a damp subterranean cavern situated in the Stagalbog within the eastern reaches of the Swamp of Sorrows. It serves as a fortified den for the Marsh murlocs and their sinister spiritual leader, the Swamp Talker. Tucked away in the murky eastern wetlands of the Swamp of Sorrows, Stagalbog Cave provides shelter from the relentless swamp rains for the local murlocs. Known as the Marsh tribe, these amphibious creatures aggressively guard the winding cavern chambers alongside their chief mystic, the Swamp Talker. Travelers venturing near the sunken mire of the Stagalbog often hear their gurgling calls echoing out across the mossy banks, warning trespassers away from their nesting grounds.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_stagalbog-cave-085857108640cd4cd0cb/1-0000.mp3", duration = 42.6579592, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:staghelm-point"] = {
  hash = "13aaaa",
  fullHash = "13aaaa",
  text = "Staghelm Point. Staghelm Point is an ancient night elven watchtower perched atop a craggy peak in northeastern Silithus. Overlooking the encroaching dunes and the nearby caverns of Twilight's Run, the tower stands as a vigil against the ancient threats haunting the desert. Rising high above the arid flats of northeastern Silithus, Staghelm Point is crowned by a solitary night elf tower. The outpost serves as a lonely sentinel, keeping watch over the wasteland and the shadowed cultists lurking in nearby caves like Twilight's Run. Watched over by Ralo'shan the Eternal Watcher and accompanied by a bronze drake circling overhead, the tower bears the proud and tragic name of the Staghelm lineage—a reminder of the immense sacrifices made by the Kaldorei during the ancient War of the Shifting Sands.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_staghelm-point-18defb33a89fe13e1898/1-0000.mp3", duration = 47.2816327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:starfall-village"] = {
  hash = "2f3b8d",
  fullHash = "2f3b8d",
  text = "Starfall Village. Starfall Village is a tranquil night elven sanctuary nestled along the snowy road of Winterspring. As the sole Alliance outpost in the frozen valley, it offers shelter and supplies to travelers braving the bitter northern chill. It also stands watch over the nearby barrow den. Situated northwest of the goblin trading post of Everlook and south of Frostsaber Rock, Starfall Village represents the lone bastion of Alliance civilization in Winterspring. Composed of traditional night elven timber lodges, the settlement serves as a haven for wayfarers, providing critical supplies and a warm hearth amid the harsh mountain elements. Despite its peaceful appearance, the village remains ever vigilant, positioned adjacent to a quiet, unoccupied barrow den.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_starfall-village-3ab23f4358f52f7dcadb/1-0000.mp3", duration = 48.1697959, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:steamwheedle-port"] = {
  hash = "595ad8",
  fullHash = "595ad8",
  text = "Steamwheedle Port. Steamwheedle Port is a bustling coastal outpost on the eastern shore of Tanaris, directly connected to Gadgetzan. Operated by the neutral Steamwheedle Cartel, it serves as an essential sea link across the South Seas while constantly warding off encroaching Southsea Pirates. Tucked against the dunes along the South Seas, Steamwheedle Port offers the inland desert city of Gadgetzan a direct lifeline to maritime trade. Run by the Steamwheedle Cartel, the settlement bustles with dockhands, traders, and sea captains braving the treacherous waters. Supply vessels regularly ply the routes between here and distant goblin trading posts, bringing valuable mechanical components, luxuries, and raw materials into southern Kalimdor. Life on the port is far from peaceful. Just south along the coast lies Lost Rigger Cove, home to rampant Southsea Freebooters who regularly raid goblin shipping, steal cargo, and contest control of the coastline. Aside from haggling dockworkers and paranoid merchants trying to recover stolen shipments, the port also shelters unusual travelers, such as the enigmatic troll Yeh'kinya, who studies the ancient and ominous spirits of the world away from prying eyes.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_steamwheedle-port-fdb1f5f350e11ef7ba03/1-0000.mp3", duration = 72.202449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:stonard"] = {
  hash = "29d3fd",
  fullHash = "29d3fd",
  text = "Stonard. Stonard is a fortified Horde outpost situated in the southern marshes of the Swamp of Sorrows. Originally built during the First War, it serves the modern Horde as an essential foothold in the southern Eastern Kingdoms and an observation post watching the Blasted Lands. Stonard stands as one of the oldest orcish settlements on Azeroth. First established when the Old Horde spilled through the Dark Portal into what was once the Black Morass, Stonard and its sister post, Rockard, anchored the invaders' lines. Though abandoned and damaged during the fallout of the Second War, Thrall's New Horde moved to reconstruct and garrison Stonard. Anchored by a traditional orcish Great Hall and fortified burrows, the outpost is now a vital island of Horde authority in an untamed, perilous mire. Modern Stonard functions as both an expeditionary camp and a staging ground. Cartographers and scouts fan out across the surrounding muck to map the uncharted swamp and monitor hostile native beasts, lost draenei wanderers, and the sunken Temple of Atal'Hakkar to the east. Crucially, Stonard also provides the Horde with an organized staging post just north of the shattered Blasted Lands, ensuring a watchful eye remains near the dormant Dark Portal.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_stonard-908d694c0882f3d59446/1-0000.mp3", duration = 76.9828571, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:stonemaul-ruins"] = {
  hash = "4c850f",
  fullHash = "4c850f",
  text = "Stonemaul Ruins. The Stonemaul Ruins are the shattered remnants of Stonemaul Village, once the proud Kalimdor settlement of the Stonemaul ogres. After being driven out by the black dragonflight, the clan fled north, leaving their former home overrun by Firemane dragonspawn. Following their migration to Kalimdor, the Stonemaul ogres founded their settlement amid the murky swamps of Dustwallow Marsh. The village became famous when the beastmaster Rexxar braved the clan's lethal trials, slew their brutal chieftain Kor'gall, and rallied the ogres to defend the nascent Horde against Admiral Proudmoore's forces. After the war, Rexxar left the ogre Mok'Morokk in command. Under Mok'Morokk's selfish rule, the village fell into decline and was ultimately ambushed and shattered by the black dragonflight. The surviving ogres fled north to establish Brackenwall Village, while Onyxia's brood took complete possession of the ruins. Today, the ruins are firmly ensconced within Dragonmurk and crawl with Firemane dragonspawn tending the brood's nearby nesting grounds. While Overlord Mok'Morokk grew cowardly and content to languish in Brackenwall, older and prouder clan members like Tharg and Draz'Zilb refuse to abandon their homeland. Operating out of Brackenwall, they urge Horde champions to slaughter the occupying dragonspawn and smash the dragonflight's eggs in hopes that the Stonemaul may one day march back and reclaim their ancestral village.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_stonemaul-ruins-b9bfe59196622a761680/1-0000.mp3", duration = 88.6595918, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:stonewrought-pass"] = {
  hash = "99027d",
  fullHash = "99027d",
  text = "Stonewrought Pass. Stonewrought Pass is an underground tunnel cut directly through the southern mountains of Loch Modan into the blighted expanse of the Searing Gorge. Masterfully engineered by the famed architect Franclorn Forgewright, this passage connects the Valley of Kings directly to Dustfire Valley. Heavy iron gates secure both ends to protect peaceful dwarven lands from the volcanic terrors to the south. Carved out of the bedrock by the legendary master architect Franclorn Forgewright—famed also for the massive Stonewrought Dam—the Stonewrought Pass serves as a direct corridor between the pastoral beauty of Loch Modan and the scorched wastes of Searing Gorge. Emerging in Loch Modan near the colossal Valley of Kings, it cuts straight through the mountain ridge down into Dustfire Valley. Because Searing Gorge is infested with Dark Iron dwarves and fiery beasts, the Ironforge mountaineers keep the passage locked tight behind heavy gates to prevent danger from spilling into the north.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_stonewrought-pass-aaff64b41cfe1cecb6e7/1-0000.mp3", duration = 59.3502041, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:stormrage-barrow-dens"] = {
  hash = "450b6e",
  fullHash = "450b6e",
  text = "Stormrage Barrow Dens. The Stormrage Barrow Dens are an ancient subterranean sanctuary carved beneath the sacred valley of Moonglade. Named for Malfurion Stormrage, these quiet earth-hewn chambers serve as a dedicated resting place where druids enter long, restorative slumbers to wander the Emerald Dream. Barrow dens serve as sacred havens throughout night elven lands, providing druids a safe subterranean refuge where their physical bodies can lie undisturbed during lengthy immersions in the Emerald Dream. The Stormrage Barrow Dens are particularly esteemed within the sacred realm of Moonglade. Named in honor of Archdruid Malfurion Stormrage, these winding earthen halls hold both ancient reverence and quiet vigil. While some druids like Clintar Dreamwalker still slumber safely within its alcoves, the absence of the great Archdruid himself casts a somber shadow over the hollowed roots and peaceful glowing lanterns. During the desperate days of the Third War, these dens stood on the brink of total ruin. The dreadlord Tichondrius and the Burning Legion's forces sought to eradicate the sleeping druids before they could rally. High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind fought her way through invading demons and hostile corrupted wildlife deep into these earthen tunnels to blow the Horn of Cenarius, waking Malfurion Stormrage in the nick of time. Since Archimonde's defeat at Mount Hyjal, Malfurion's spirit has once more drifted into the Dream, leaving his brethren to watch over these sacred earthen depths.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_stormrage-barrow-dens-8087fd1c28703e7a5af2/1-0000.mp3", duration = 95.8693878, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:stormwind-city"] = {
  hash = "a28f8f",
  fullHash = "a28f8f",
  text = "Stormwind City. Stormwind City leads the Kingdom of Stormwind and the Alliance as their capital and seat of power. Rebuilt after the First War, it is the greatest remaining human stronghold in Azeroth since the northern kingdoms fell. Stormwind City rose over a thousand years ago, founded by Arathi descendants who migrated south. The coastal capital flourished behind stone ramparts, with swift rivermills powering the city. In the First War, the Orcish Horde laid siege, broke through its defenses, and burned Stormwind to the ground. Sir Anduin Lothar led the surviving refugees across the Great Sea to Lordaeron. After the Alliance won the Second War, the Stonemasons' Guild rebuilt the capital with white stone, canals, and towering spires. The fortress now stands north of Elwynn Forest. Stormwind City has become mankind's chief refuge and stronghold after the Scourge shattered Lordaeron and the Third War left Dalaran heavily ruined. The disciplined Stormwind City Guard patrols its broad avenues and defensive walls. The Cathedral of Light's spires guide faith across the southern kingdoms. Beneath the orderly districts and ceremonial gates, political troubles linger. King Varian Wrynn has mysteriously vanished, and his young son Anduin sits on the throne under the regency of Highlord Bolvar Fordragon and the whispered counsel of royal advisor Lady Katrana Prestor.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_stormwind-city-1740ad1e2c701909b7de/1-0000.mp3", duration = 87.4057143, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:stratholme"] = {
  hash = "afd8a5",
  fullHash = "afd8a5",
  text = "Stratholme. Stratholme was once the jewel of the Eastweald and the second greatest city in the Kingdom of Lordaeron. During the Third War, it became the site of Prince Arthas Menethil's infamous culling after its citizens consumed grain tainted by the Plague of Undeath. Today, the ruined metropolis still burns, serving as the Scourge's primary stronghold in northern Lordaeron. Long before its downfall, Stratholme stood as a cornerstone of northern civilization. Emerging centuries ago from the Arathi expansion into the Eastweald, it flourished into Lordaeron's second-largest settlement, renowned for holy shrines and sacred water capable of resisting dark sorcery. Prior to the Second War, Archbishop Alonsus Faol gathered at the city's chapel to establish the Order of the Knights of the Silver Hand, ordaining legends like Uther the Lightbringer. Throughout that war, Stratholme served as a vital port, supply hub, and northern oil refinery for the Alliance of Lordaeron, enduring an assault by the Old Horde before rebuilding in victory. Stratholme's fate was sealed when the Cult of the Damned funneled shipments of infected grain from Andorhal into the city's markets. Unaware, thousands ate the tainted flour. When Prince Arthas arrived alongside Uther and Jaina Proudmoore, he discovered the infection had spread too far to cure. Deeming slaughter the only way to deny Mal'Ganis an entire army, Arthas ordered the purge of every citizen—an atrocity that drove Uther and Jaina away and began the prince's descent into madness. The city was put to the torch, and in the aftermath, the Scourge moved into the smoking ruins to make it their regional capital.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_stratholme-8815fc2df5bbb333fd6c/1-0000.mp3", duration = 102.582857, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:stratholme-classic"] = {
  hash = "f8d1e8",
  fullHash = "f8d1e8",
  text = "Stratholme (Classic). Stratholme, once northern Lordaeron's proudest jewel and the birthplace of the Silver Hand, is now a burning ruin serving as the Scourge's regional capital. Divided between the fanatical Scarlet Crusade and Baron Rivendare's undead legions, the cursed city remains locked in endless, violent war. Before the Third War, Stratholme was the northern powerhouse of Lordaeron and the sacred birthplace of the Knights of the Silver Hand. Its fate was sealed when Prince Arthas purged the city, slaughtering his own subjects after infected grain brought the plague within its walls. Left smoldering and ruined, Stratholme was claimed by the Scourge. Today, under the overarching authority of the lich Kel'Thuzad and the field command of Baron Rivendare, the city serves as the primary base for undead power in the Plaguelands. Stratholme is split between two vicious factions locked in perpetual bloodshed. To the west, the fanatical Scarlet Crusade has fortified Crusaders' Square and the Scarlet Bastion, commanded by Grand Crusader Saidan Dathrohan. To the east lies the Scourge slaughterhouses and necropolises, defended by towering ziggurats and the death knight Baron Rivendare. Anyone entering the city must navigate this grinding crossfire, surrounded by burning tenements and restless spirits.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_stratholme-classic-3eaf90f2929008ad08c2/1-0000.mp3", duration = 81.6587755, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:stratholme-main-gate"] = {
  hash = "4b8a89",
  fullHash = "4b8a89",
  text = "Stratholme Main Gate. The Stratholme Main Gate stands as the grand, burning entrance to Lordaeron's northern metropolis in the northwestern Eastern Plaguelands. It provides direct access through the city's ruined avenues toward King's Square and the crusader fortifications within. Stratholme was once the pride of northern Lordaeron, famed as the birthplace of the Order of the Silver Hand. Today, its colossal arched main gates mark the threshold of an ongoing nightmare. It was here during the Third War that Prince Arthas turned on his mentor, Uther the Lightbringer, and purged his own citizens before the Plague of Undeath could claim them. The city has burned relentlessly ever since, transformed into the primary Scourge stronghold in the region. Approaching through the main gate leads straight into the ruins of King's Square and Market Row, heading toward the fortified district known as the Scarlet Bastion. Zealots of the Scarlet Crusade pushed through this entrance to reclaim the city and purge the dead, carving out an encampment in the ruins. However, the crusaders find themselves surrounded by overwhelming undead monstrosities, fighting an attrition war they cannot win against the dark forces orchestrating Stratholme's defense.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_stratholme-main-gate-df84933908eee7d87781/1-0000.mp3", duration = 72.437551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:stratholme-service-entrance"] = {
  hash = "75090a",
  fullHash = "75090a",
  text = "Stratholme Service Entrance. The Stratholme Service Entrance is a fortified rear gateway located at Eastwall Gate in the northern Eastern Plaguelands. It provides direct, covert access into the Scourge-controlled eastern quarter of the burning city, bypassing the grand front gates. While the monumental Main Gate of Stratholme opens into King's Square and provides a route toward the Scarlet Bastion, the Service Entrance breaches the city's eastern fortification at Eastwall Gate. Today, it leads directly into the corrupted heart of the metropolis: Elders' Square and the blighted pathways leading toward Baron Rivendare's slaughterhouses. Venturing through the service portal offers a shorter approach to the Scourge's eastern districts. The surrounding alleys crawl with gargoyles, crypt fiends, and cultists guarding the dread ziggurats. For the Argent Dawn and daring adventurers seeking to strike at the ruling death knight directly, this hidden iron gate serves as the primary staging point for surgical strikes against the Lich King's commanders.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_stratholme-service-entrance-cd9b002e68dd805651bf/1-0000.mp3", duration = 57.3387755, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:stromgarde-keep"] = {
  hash = "7f4021",
  fullHash = "7f4021",
  text = "Stromgarde Keep. Stromgarde Keep is the battered, divided capital of the fallen human kingdom of Stromgarde in the Arathi Highlands. Once the proud cradle of human civilization known as Strom, the fortress is now partitioned between hostile Syndicate rogues, invading Boulderfist ogres, and a desperate remnant of royal defenders under Prince Galen Trollbane. Long before it bore its current name, this fortress was founded nearly three millennia ago as Strom, the first capital of the unified human empire of Arathor under King Thoradin. It served as the staging ground for humanity's victory against the forest trolls and was the birthplace of human wizardry, where high elven magi tutored the first hundred human spellcasters. Over centuries, as younger sister kingdoms like Dalaran, Lordaeron, and Stormwind rose and prospered, Strom's influence waned. Renamed Stromgarde by the stubborn Trollbane dynasty who stayed behind, it remained a proud warrior bastion throughout the Second War, anchoring the Alliance of Lordaeron against the Orcish Horde. Following the devastation of the Third War, Stromgarde finally buckled under mounting catastrophes and treachery. Today, the majestic stone city sits fractured into three warring sectors. The aristocratic traitors of the Syndicate occupy the grand inner keep, while brutish Boulderfist ogres have overrun the former sanctum of the mage district. Pinned in the remaining quarter, Prince Galen Trollbane and the battered Stromgarde military wage a grueling, bloody war on multiple fronts just to keep their ancestral home from vanishing into memory.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_stromgarde-keep-83afcd01408ee75b7359/1-0000.mp3", duration = 97.2538776, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:sundown-marsh"] = {
  hash = "c7969b",
  fullHash = "c7969b",
  text = "Sundown Marsh. In the Wetlands' far west, Sundown Marsh holds deep water, stagnant pools, and dangerous wildlife. An eccentric pair keeps a solitary home along its muddy borders. Sundown Marsh fills the Wetlands' deepest, westernmost reaches, where deep water and thick mud hide a dense population of predators. Giant crocolisks, shifting oozes, roaming raptors, and territorial Mosshide gnolls move through the mist. Most travelers keep to the main cobblestone road between Menethil Harbor and Ironforge, leaving this corner largely untamed. A lonely stone cottage with a churning windmill stands amid the marsh's hazards. The gnome Fradd Swiftgear and the dwarf Wenna Silkbeard make their living scavenging the bog. They sell curiosities and rare trade recipes recovered from the mire to travelers who brave its aggressive denizens.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_sundown-marsh-f588a0fcecd1e6f57fd3/1-0000.mp3", duration = 53.2897959, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:sunnyglade"] = {
  hash = "13b57a",
  fullHash = "13b57a",
  text = "Sunnyglade. Sunnyglade was once a wealthy, prosperous human settlement belonging to the kingdom of Stormwind during the First War. Devastated and eventually abandoned as the front lines collapsed, the ruined settlement now languishes under the dark influence of the Twilight's Hammer cult. Before the First War tore southern Azeroth apart, Sunnyglade was celebrated for its wealth and comfortable prosperity. That peace was violently shattered when the Old Horde invaded. Orc raiding parties attacked the town and enslaved its surviving residents, leaving only a lone wizard's tower intact to study the arcane arts of human conjurers. Though Stormwind forces mounted an effort to rescue the captive peasants and rebuild the settlement, the surrounding Riverglades were steadily swallowed by the advancing Horde. Deeming the outer province lost, Stormwind's commanders eventually withdrew their resources to defend the homeland, leaving Sunnyglade to ruin. Today, Sunnyglade lies far beyond the safe borders of Stormwind's reestablished realm. While the iconic stone mage tower remarkably weathered the First War and the years of abandonment that followed, the peaceful township around it is gone. Instead, the secluded ruins have been occupied by zealots of the Twilight's Hammer cult, who turn the once-prosperous valley to their own sinister designs.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_sunnyglade-7f0bd19529c77470f5a4/1-0000.mp3", duration = 80.9534694, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:swamplight-manor"] = {
  hash = "0c45d4",
  fullHash = "0c45d4",
  text = "Swamplight Manor. Swamplight Manor is an ironically named, ramshackle wooden shack nestled deep within Witch Hill in Dustwallow Marsh. It serves as the eccentric home and kitchen of 'Swamp Eye' Jarl, an isolated recluse known across the mire for his strange culinary appetites. Despite its humble, eerie isolation, the surrounding garden conceals intrigue that catches the attention of both Theramore and Brackenwall Village. Despite what its grandiose title suggests, Swamplight Manor is far from a lordly estate. It is merely a crooked, weather-beaten shack tucked into the haunted wetlands of Witch Hill. Its lone resident, the odd recluse known as 'Swamp Eye' Jarl, lives a solitary existence surrounded by fog, muck, and restless marsh ghosts. Jarl spends his days concocting unusual regional delicacies out of local wildlife, frequently recruiting wandering adventurers to hunt down bizarre ingredients like marsh frog legs and creature eyes to satisfy his strange craft.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_swamplight-manor-1578c0a0e8ef51ee7bf7/1-0000.mp3", duration = 63.2163265, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:swarming-pillar"] = {
  hash = "6c2a5a",
  fullHash = "6c2a5a",
  text = "Swarming Pillar. The Swarming Pillar is a towering rock formation in the heart of Silithus, thoroughly infested and reshaped by the silithid. Situated just south of Cenarion Hold, the spire takes its name from the relentless clouds of winged insectoids buzzing around its peaks and subterranean entrances. Rising abruptly from the shifting dunes of central Silithus, the Swarming Pillar serves as a grim monument to the unchecked expansion of the silithid. What began as a natural rock formation has been carved and encrusted with the resinous secretions and burrows of the insectoid swarm. Just south of Cenarion Hold, it constantly churns out broods of winged workers and soldiers, whose dark clouds fill the dry desert air and warn travelers to keep their distance. Though massive and intimidating on the surface, the Swarming Pillar is merely the outward protrusion of a vast underground network. Beneath its stony exterior lie deep tunnels and cavernous breeding chambers that anchor one of the largest hives in the region. The Cenarion Circle views the spire with immense concern, as its active broods directly threaten druidic operations and hint at the terrifying coordination behind the insectoid menace.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_swarming-pillar-e758b7a62c8cc8365606/1-0000.mp3", duration = 70.7134694, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:tabetha-s-farm"] = {
  hash = "9fb445",
  fullHash = "9fb445",
  text = "Tabetha's Farm. Deep in the murkiest reaches of the Quagmire lies Tabetha's Farm, an isolated homestead centered around a small wooden hut, a modest garden, and a private dock. Despite the treacherous marsh surroundings, this sanctuary serves as a hidden redoubt for the hermit mage Tabetha. Tucked away in the western wetlands of Dustwallow Marsh, Tabetha's Farm defies its unforgiving environment. Consisting of a weather-worn hut, small vegetable plots, and a dock for navigating the swamp's murky waterways, the homestead offers a quiet refuge far from the political rivalries of Theramore and Brackenwall Village. Here, the reclusive human sorceress Tabetha lives in near-total seclusion, pursuing esoteric studies of arcane and fel power without interference from Dalaran or local authorities. Despite its remote location, the farm attracts practitioners of the magical arts from across Kalimdor and the Eastern Kingdoms. Mages seeking mastery over raw arcane surges and warlocks seeking aid to cleanse volatile artifacts like the Orb of Orahil are directed to Tabetha's doorstep.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_tabetha-s-farm-603355e50fdd3cf34736/1-0000.mp3", duration = 67.6832653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:tahonda-ruins"] = {
  hash = "fc168a",
  fullHash = "fc168a",
  text = "Tahonda Ruins. The Tahonda Ruins are the sun-bleached remnants of an ancient tauren settlement swallowed by the salt and sand of the Shimmering Flats. Little is remembered of the villagers who once lived here, and dangerous Saltstone basilisks now stalk among the decaying tent poles and shredded leather. Situated in the southern reaches of the Shimmering Flats near the pass into Tanaris, the Tahonda Ruins stand as a somber monument to an abandoned tauren past. The village fell long ago, its weathered structures gradually engulfed by shifting white salt crusts. While some suspect the local Saltstone basilisks drove the inhabitants away, odd clues linger on the surviving structures. Two distinct sets of three parallel tears cut through the leather of a ruined lodge, suggesting the village met a violent end at the claws of powerful predators or hostile invaders before the desert claimed what remained.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_tahonda-ruins-b0dae3b508e03a0aa68a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 53.1069388, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:tainted-scar"] = {
  hash = "6da1c2",
  fullHash = "6da1c2",
  text = "Tainted Scar. The Tainted Scar is a demon-infested canyon carved into the southwestern Blasted Lands, saturated with foul fel magic from the nearby Dark Portal. It currently serves as the primary redoubt for the remnants of the Burning Legion on Azeroth, commanded by the doomguard Lord Kazzak. Once lush wetlands belonging to the ancient Black Morass, this desolate trench was utterly drained of life and turned the color of dried blood by chaotic fel energies leaking from the Dark Portal. Following Archimonde's defeat at Mount Hyjal during the Third War, surviving forces of the Burning Legion rallied in the south. The colossal ered'ruin Lord Kazzak claimed the Tainted Scar as his stronghold, stationing elite demons across its blackened rises and dispatching dreadful lieutenants like Razelikh the Defiler to hold dominion over the wasted red soils. While most mortals shun the Scar on pain of agonizing death, practitioners of the dark arts find its saturated fel energies irresistible. An eccentric human warlock named Daio the Decrepit has taken up residence by a ruined demonic altar overlooking the horrors, using the rampant fiends to perform dark rituals and instruct aspiring warlocks in how to summon and bind dread infernals.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_tainted-scar-7a777ec2a7e42ccbc3b3/1-0000.mp3", duration = 76.1208163, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:talonbranch-glade"] = {
  hash = "ed9f45",
  fullHash = "ed9f45",
  text = "Talonbranch Glade. Talonbranch Glade is a quiet night elf redoubt nestled in the blighted hills of northern Felwood. Located just south of Timbermaw Hold, it provides a rare haven of pristine moonlight, sanctuary, and hippogryph transit in an otherwise corrupted wilderness. Perched on high ground near the tunnel to Timbermaw Hold, Talonbranch Glade serves as the northernmost bastion of the Alliance in Felwood. Centered around a sheltered night elf lodge and a functioning moonwell, the outpost offers a crucial refuge from the demonic corruption that has swallowed the surrounding forest. Alliance scouts, Sentinels, and passing adventurers use the glade to rest, recover, and plan their excursions deeper into the tainted woods or onward toward Winterspring and Moonglade. Aside from its natural and spiritual defenses, Talonbranch Glade functions as an essential logistics hub. Hippogryphs stationed here connect travelers to Auberdine in Darkshore and Everlook in the snowy expanse beyond Timbermaw Hold. It remains a lonely, vigilant watchpost holding the line against the encroaching shadows of the Shadow Council and feral beast packs roaming the corrupted vales.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_talonbranch-glade-85fa8de2b98baa500f7f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 70.2693878, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:temple-of-arkkoran"] = {
  hash = "07883f",
  fullHash = "07883f",
  text = "Temple of Arkkoran. The Temple of Arkkoran is an ancient night elven ruin resting on the northeastern cliffs of Azshara above the Shattered Strand. Originally built in honor of Elune, the crumbling structure has since been claimed by the towering sea giant Arkkoroc and his aquatic thralls. Like many of the grand ruins dotting Azshara's coast, the Temple of Arkkoran was once a sanctum dedicated to the moon goddess Elune before the Sundering shattered the ancient night elf empire. Today, its weathered marble steps and broken colonnades are overrun by tidal monstrosities. The sea giant Arkkoroc presides over the temple's highest level, surrounded by loyal makrura and vicious murlocs who violently defend the surrounding coastline from any land-dwelling intruders.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_temple-of-arkkoran-db6c8e324780a9e996aa/1-0000.mp3", duration = 45.1918367, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:temple-of-atal-hakkar"] = {
  hash = "09f9e6",
  fullHash = "09f9e6",
  text = "Temple of Atal'Hakkar. The Temple of Atal'Hakkar, widely known as the Sunken Temple, is a submerged fortress built by exiled troll priests dedicated to the blood god Hakkar. Sunk beneath the marshes by the green dragonflight centuries ago, its drowned halls remain a staging ground for forbidden rituals and dark corruption. Over a millennium ago, a catastrophic civil war shattered the Gurubashi Empire when the Atal'ai priesthood attempted to summon Hakkar the Soulflayer. Exiled to the north, the surviving zealots raised a massive stone temple in the Swamp of Sorrows to finish their dark work. Learning of their scheme, the Dragon Aspect Ysera smashed the ziggurat deep into the mire, inundating its chambers beneath the Pool of Tears. Green dragon wardens were left behind to guard the drowned ruins and ensure Hakkar's return was thwarted forever. Instead of burying the cult, the temple's submersion provided the Atal'ai isolation to continue their grisly sacrifices unhindered. Over the centuries, corruption began to rot the guardians from within; the dragon sentinels succumb to madness while the Atal'ai, guided by Jammal'an the Prophet, prepare the way for Hakkar's avatar. Now, both the Horde and Alliance keep an anxious eye on the submerged ziggurat as shadowy rumors slip out from the swamp's depths.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_temple-of-atal-hakkar-f11b1f178778adb1ebeb/1-0000.mp3", duration = 83.2261224, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:temple-of-zin-malor"] = {
  hash = "d257e1",
  fullHash = "d257e1",
  text = "Temple of Zin-Malor. The Temple of Zin-Malor is an ancient night elven sanctuary located within the Ruins of Eldarath in Azshara. Originally dedicated to the moon goddess Elune, the crumbling structure is now held by the Spitelash naga under the direct command of Warlord Krellian. Before the Great Sundering, the Temple of Zin-Malor stood as a grand house of worship dedicated to Elune. Its architecture closely mirrors both the nearby Temple of Arkkoran and the modern Temple of the Moon in Darnassus, bearing the graceful, sweeping stonework characteristic of pre-Sundering night elven civilization. Today, however, its moonlit marble is weathered and broken, overtaken by the serpent-bodied Spitelash naga. These invaders have claimed the holy grounds for themselves, turning a place of quiet reflection into a militarized redoubt. The temple once housed the renowned Crystal of Zin-Malor, a potent artifact that was carried away long ago to Kel'Theril in Winterspring.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_temple-of-zin-malor-e1283587055c59e7df2b/1-0000.mp3", duration = 58.6971429, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:terrace-of-repose"] = {
  hash = "71cba6",
  fullHash = "71cba6",
  text = "Terrace of Repose. The Terrace of Repose is a pristine courtyard garden located just outside the entrance to the Scarlet Monastery in Tirisfal Glades. Maintained by the Scarlet Crusade, it stands as one of the few places in northern Lordaeron completely untouched by the Plague of Undeath, complete with green hedges and clean water. Situated just north of the grand entrance into the Scarlet Monastery, the Terrace of Repose offers a surreal contrast to the rotting forests of Tirisfal Glades. While the rest of Lordaeron chokes under grey mist, corrupt soil, and stagnant bogs, this terrace boasts verdant, neatly pruned hedges and a functioning fountain fed by fresh, uncontaminated water. For the zealots of the Scarlet Crusade, the garden serves as physical proof of their divine righteousness and purity, a sacred bastion demonstrating that holy devotion can ward off the Scourge's corruption.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_terrace-of-repose-eae05f1b24cc0e314f34/1-0000.mp3", duration = 53.9167347, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:terror-run"] = {
  hash = "c67b9f",
  fullHash = "c67b9f",
  text = "Terror Run. Terror Run is a perilous swath of land situated in the southwestern reaches of Un'Goro Crater, directly west of the insectoid hives of the Slithering Scar. True to its fearsome name, the territory is stalked by predatory beasts, ranging from massive devilsaurs to ravenous diemetradons and swooping pterrordax. Stretching across the southwestern shelf of Un'Goro Crater, Terror Run lives up to its ominous reputation. Fierce pterrordax wheel through the humid air above while massive diemetradons, stegodons, and apex devilsaurs roam the low brush below. The territory sits directly in the shadow of the mountainous rim, wedged uncomfortably close to the chittering alien mounds of the Slithering Scar. Along the eastern edge of the flats lie the ruined remnants of a camp left behind by Williden Marshal's expedition, a sobering reminder of researchers who were caught unprepared when the crater's primeval predators closed in. Beyond the sheer concentration of hungry fauna, Terror Run draws naturalists and adventurers alike for a geological rarity: it is the primary ground in the crater where curious Red Power Crystals emerge from the fertile soil. The crimson formations hum with primeval energy akin to the blue, green, and yellow crystals found elsewhere in the basin. Surviving long enough to gather them, however, means contending with the sovereign hunters of the plains—including nesting apex devilsaurs that brook no trespassers in the deep south.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_terror-run-70ced5fae53772a9fc3d/1-0000.mp3", duration = 90.4620408, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:terror-wing-path"] = {
  hash = "4c3c85",
  fullHash = "4c3c85",
  text = "Terror Wing Path. Terror Wing Path is a rocky canyon winding through the eastern reaches of the Burning Steppes. Infested by whelps and dragonkin of the black dragonflight, it serves as a perilous approach into the remote northeastern corner of the Steppes. Carved into the craggy, ash-covered foothills of the eastern Burning Steppes, Terror Wing Path earns its name from the aerial predators and wyrmkin that claim it. Broods of the black dragonflight prowl these defiles, fiercely guarding clutches and hunting anything foolish enough to wander into their shadow. Ash and soot blanket the stone trails, making any trek beneath the canyon walls a treacherous venture. Black drakes patrol the length of the valley, and the rare magma giant Volchan is sometimes seen wandering through it. Only the most seasoned adventurers dare the journey, and seldom alone, yet it is the only way to reach the hidden cave of Slither Rock in the far northeastern corner of the Steppes.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_terror-wing-path-3747766e56ea6a77b4a6/1-0000.mp3", duration = 55.5363265, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:terrordale"] = {
  hash = "13718c",
  fullHash = "13718c",
  text = "Terrordale. Terrordale is a ruined, ghost-haunted settlement nestled in the northwestern edge of the Eastern Plaguelands. Bordered by the blighted Plaguewood and the arachnid-infested Terrorweb Tunnel, the ruined town swarms with the Scourge and the restless dead. Once a quiet settlement in northern Lordaeron, Terrordale fell along with the rest of the kingdom to the Scourge. Today, the village stands in grim ruin near the mouth of the Terrorweb Tunnel. Its streets are choked with restless shades and wandering undead, while the surrounding trees of the Plaguewood encroach on its rotten timber. Amidst the desolation, only a handful of eccentric residents dare remain—such as the peculiar merchant Augustus the Touched and the ghost hunter Egan, who attempts to free the tortured spirits caught between worlds. The anguish of Terrordale's demise ran so deep that the physical world literally buckles beneath the torment of the spirit realm. Terrordale spirits linger in perpetual agony, bound to the earth by their tragic ends. Because of this concentrated spiritual corruption, seasoned paladins under the guidance of the Silver Hand and Lord Grayson Shadowbreaker are dispatched here to perform rites of judgment and sacrifice, banishing the torment as a sacred trial of holy discipline.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_terrordale-cbf854e79c66c6dbd547/1-0000.mp3", duration = 75.5983673, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:terrorweb-tunnel"] = {
  hash = "85b009",
  fullHash = "85b009",
  text = "Terrorweb Tunnel. Terrorweb Tunnel is a subterranean passage carving through the mountains between Terrordale and the Thondroril River. Infested with the Scourge's undead nerubian crypt fiends, it forms a hazardous subterranean shortcut across the western border of the Eastern Plaguelands. Bored straight through the mountains flanking the Thondroril River, the Terrorweb Tunnel links the contaminated village of Terrordale with the riverbanks to the south. Rather than typical ghouls or skeletal sentries, this dark subterranean bypass is claimed entirely by crypt fiends—mummified nerubian arachnids raised into servitude by the Lich King. Traveling the length of the passage cuts out long circuits around the mountain slopes, but requires navigating thick webs and avoiding towering nerubian overseers that patrol the dark.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_terrorweb-tunnel-9084fd54f7381c18ede7/1-0000.mp3", duration = 48.64, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:thalanaar"] = {
  hash = "647787",
  fullHash = "647787",
  text = "Thalanaar. Thalanaar is a secluded night elf outpost nestled directly on the border between the verdant wilds of Feralas and the arid canyons of Thousand Needles. Serving as the primary eastern gateway into Feralas for the Alliance, it offers tired travelers a vital hippogryph roost and shelter beneath the canopy. Though modest in size, it acts as a critical link between mainland Kalimdor and the remote stronghold of Feathermoon. Perched at the natural choke point where the baked sandstone of Thousand Needles gives way to the dense, misty canopy of Feralas, Thalanaar is the first sign of night elven presence awaiting travelers from the east. It is little more than a fortified grove with a few sturdy pavilions and sentinels on watch, yet its position is vital. The outpost shelters Alliance scouts, couriers, and craftsmen venturing into the untamed forest, providing a secure staging ground against the beasts, ogres, and Horde forces roaming Southern Kalimdor. Because overland travel through southern Kalimdor is fraught with peril, Thalanaar's primary function is logistics. Its hippogryph master links the secluded wilderness of Feralas directly to Theramore Isle in Dustwallow Marsh, Gadgetzan in Tanaris, and the island bastion of Feathermoon Stronghold to the west. Craftsmen also make pilgrimage here to seek out master leatherworkers who teach the ancient art of working wild hides under the woodland boughs.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_thalanaar-01d62da1afc43442c951/1-0000.mp3", duration = 86.3085714, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:thalassian-base-camp"] = {
  hash = "5e8fb1",
  fullHash = "5e8fb1",
  text = "Thalassian Base Camp. Tucked onto a high ridge in northern Azshara, the Thalassian Base Camp serves as the staging ground for an expedition of blood elves unearthing ancient arcane relics. Though initially funded under an agreement with the Horde, the camp's leadership quickly broke faith once digging commenced. Now, amidst their red pavilions and pulverized magical artifacts, darker summoning practices hint at their true objectives. The blood elf expedition nestled east of Ursolan was not supposed to be hostile to travelers from Orgrimmar. Their leader, Magus Rimtori, negotiated a formal pact with the Horde: in exchange for supply lines, funding, and armed escort through Azshara's treacherous wildlands, her researchers would share whatever magical riches they uncovered. Instead, as soon as the base camp was fortified and the first relics were pried from the elven ruins, Rimtori reneged entirely, driving away Horde emissaries. While Rimtori searches deeper reaches with an armed retinue, Magister Hawkhelm keeps watch over the ridge, guarding the encampment against retribution.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_thalassian-base-camp-a4790df1767e9d42c17e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 65.6718367, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:thandol-span"] = {
  hash = "a95b07",
  fullHash = "a95b07",
  text = "Thandol Span. The Thandol Span is a pair of colossal dwarven stone bridges connecting the Wetlands of Khaz Modan with the Arathi Highlands of southern Lordaeron. Long celebrated as a marvel of engineering and the vital artery between the northern and southern kingdoms, it is currently in a state of disaster following a sabotage attack by Dark Iron infiltrators. Constructed following the War of the Three Hammers to bridge Ironforge with the Wildhammer clan of the Aerie Peak, the Thandol Span stands among the grandest stonecraft feats of dwarven history. Capable of supporting entire marching armies, it served as the critical land corridor between Lordaeron and Khaz Modan throughout the Second War, notably carrying Sir Anduin Lothar's counter-offensive southward. Today, the crossing is grievously wounded. Dark Iron saboteurs led by Balgaras the Foul and Kam Deepfury mounted a devastating assault from nearby Dun Modr, shattering one span entirely with explosives and threatening to sever overland travel between the allied realms completely. The surviving bridge remains contested and heavily guarded. Mountaineers from Ironforge fight desperately to preserve the remaining roadway and repel Dark Iron sappers, suffering painful losses in the process. The loss of the bridge would leave northern trade and military movements almost entirely reliant on maritime routes, cutting off Alliance forces stationed across the Arathi Highlands from immediate reinforcement.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_thandol-span-cde081ff2cd78f890a1f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 85.76, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:thelgen-rock"] = {
  hash = "efc233",
  fullHash = "efc233",
  text = "Thelgen Rock. In the southern mountain wall of the Wetlands, Thelgen Rock’s caverns hold volatile Incendicite Ore. Venomous spiders infest the passages. Thelgen Rock cuts deep into the mountains between the Wetlands and Loch Modan. Aggressive spiders fill its web-choked passages, and their venom makes every step dangerous. Miners and adventurers still brave the tunnels for the valuable Incendicite Ore embedded in the rock.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_thelgen-rock-4b1de41b8b7a4ea8af63/1-0000.mp3", duration = 24.5028571, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:theramore-isle"] = {
  hash = "b16215",
  fullHash = "b16215",
  text = "Theramore Isle. Theramore Isle is a heavily fortified human port citadel off the eastern coast of Dustwallow Marsh. Founded by Lady Jaina Proudmoore for refugees escaping the fall of Lordaeron, it stands as the Alliance's primary bastion in southern Kalimdor. Following the devastation of Dalaran and Lordaeron by the Scourge during the Third War, Lady Jaina Proudmoore guided a fleet of survivors across the Great Sea to the shores of Kalimdor. After fighting alongside orcs and night elves to defeat the Burning Legion atop Mount Hyjal, her coalition of humans, dwarves, and high elves retreated to the misty fringes of Dustwallow Marsh. On a jagged island just off the coast, they built Theramore Isle—a thriving harbor city anchored by towering stone ramparts and Jaina's personal citadel, created to safeguard what remained of the northern kingdoms.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_theramore-isle-0276edf1a1463e0f89cb/1-0000.mp3", duration = 50.8342857, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:thistleshrub-valley"] = {
  hash = "88ea54",
  fullHash = "88ea54",
  text = "Thistleshrub Valley. Tucked away in the southwestern reaches of Tanaris, Thistleshrub Valley is a bizarre oasis of prickly plant life amidst endless dunes. The canyon is known for its strange grove of giant cacti and hostile animated thistleshrubs that attack anything stepping through their thorn-choked territory. While most of Tanaris is an arid wasteland stripped bare of vegetation, Thistleshrub Valley is curiously choked with life. Giant blooming cacti tower over the canyon floor, providing a sharp contrast to the surrounding sea of sand. However, this is no peaceful sanctuary. The valley takes its name from the hostile, living plant creatures that lurk among the brush. These thistleshrubs lash out with razor-sharp spines at any traveler who wanders near, making navigation hazardous for those trying to push past the cliffs toward the mysterious southern gates. The presence of animated flora in such a dry climate has long puzzled wanderers and desert survivalists. Whether awakened by forgotten elemental energies or stubborn adaptation to the harsh Tanaris climate, the thistleshrubs remain violently territorial. Travelers journeying through the canyon must contend not only with these thrashing thickets of thorn and root, but also the desert predators and venomous serpents that nest among the towering cacti.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_thistleshrub-valley-d66b26c81e4fdedc2665/1-0000.mp3", duration = 79.2816327, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:thondroril-river"] = {
  hash = "b52b0e",
  fullHash = "b52b0e",
  text = "Thondroril River. The Thondroril River is a vital natural boundary separating the Western and Eastern Plaguelands. Originating in the mountains of Northern Lordaeron near the Terrorweb Tunnel, its blighted waters flow southward into Darrowmere Lake. Once a vibrant trade and travel waterway of the fallen kingdom, it is now flanked by monstrous Scourge beasts and the humble refuge of a legendary hermit. Before the fall of Lordaeron, the Thondroril served as an important waterway connecting northern holdings toward Stratholme and south to Darrowmere Lake. Today, the river marks the hazardous divide between the Western and Eastern Plaguelands. The waters carry the taint of the plague, and its banks are infested with giant maggots, plaguebats, diseased bears, and predatory snapjaws, turning what was once a scenic riverbed into a deadly crossing for travelers.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_thondroril-river-b87db007fc3a7c5f8595/1-0000.mp3", duration = 52.4277551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:thoradin-s-wall"] = {
  hash = "f89dc9",
  fullHash = "f89dc9",
  text = "Thoradin's Wall. Thoradin's Wall is a monumental, crumbling stone rampart dividing the Arathi Highlands from the Hillsbrad Foothills. Commissioned millennia ago by King Thoradin during the dawn of the Empire of Arathor, it was built to shield early humanity from the fierce incursions of the Amani trolls. Erected nearly three thousand years ago, Thoradin's Wall marks the western frontier of ancient Strom, the heart of the first unified human empire. King Thoradin recognized that humanity's fledgeling settlements were acutely vulnerable to the predatory raids of the Amani forest trolls. By raising this vast stone barrier across the valley corridor, Arathor secured its heartland and forged a defensible bastion from which humanity could survive and eventually contest troll supremacy in the north. Today, the ancient barrier stands in severe disrepair. Vast breeches gash the stone ramparts, their battlements weathered down by thousands of years of exposure, warfare, and abandonment. Following the splintering of Arathor into rival kingdoms and the eventual fall of neighboring Lordaeron to the Scourge, no central realm maintains the masonry. Now, travelers and roaming bandits slip freely through its broken archways as they journey between Hillsbrad and the untamed Arathi Highlands.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_thoradin-s-wall-d925b1029f2ddaac34e8/1-0000.mp3", duration = 77.9755102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:thorium-point"] = {
  hash = "34b6fd",
  fullHash = "34b6fd",
  text = "Thorium Point. Perched upon the northern bluffs of the Searing Gorge, Thorium Point serves as the primary staging camp for the Thorium Brotherhood. From this neutral outpost, these rogue Dark Iron dwarves recruit passing adventurers and strike back against their tyrannical kin deeper within the volcanic wastes. Overlooking the scorched slag heaps of the Searing Gorge, Thorium Point is a modest mountain encampment built out of necessity. It is held by the Thorium Brotherhood, a renegade faction of Dark Iron dwarves who have severed ties with their enslaved brethren in Shadowforge City. Unwilling to bow to the Firelord or Emperor Dagran Thaurissan, these outcasts maintain an open-air forge, a busy flight master, and a well-guarded perimeter northeast of Firewatch Ridge. Because of their desperate struggle for survival and master-level smithing aspirations, the Brotherhood keeps the camp open to both the Alliance and the Horde, welcoming anyone willing to dig ores out of the ash or bash Dark Iron skulls. Beyond being a staging ground for the Brotherhood's war against their brethren, Thorium Point is an indispensable crossroads in the heart of the Redridge mountain chain. Perched safely above the ravenous spiders and fire elementals that prowl the volcanic basin, the outpost offers direct flight links to Ironforge, as well as forward camps across the Badlands and the Burning Steppes. For wandering mercenaries, prospectors, and crafters looking to earn the secretive favor of master dwarf smiths, this smoky cliffside is the safest hearth to be found in the Gorge.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_thorium-point-31403a1bf9559651e731/1-0000.mp3", duration = 95.0073469, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:thunder-axe-fortress"] = {
  hash = "086f0b",
  fullHash = "086f0b",
  text = "Thunder Axe Fortress. Thunder Axe Fortress is a fortified compound in northwestern Desolace serving as a stronghold for the demonic Burning Blade cult. Within its stone barracks, watchtowers, and darkened caves, cultists perform dark rituals to summon demonic entities and store stolen artifacts of power. The cult remains an active threat to anyone traversing the desolate wastes. Perched in northwestern Desolace, Thunder Axe Fortress serves as a heavily guarded redoubt for the Burning Blade. Composed of stone barracks, defensive watchtowers, and deep caverns, it allows the cult to maintain a fortified foothold far from the gaze of Kalimdor's major cities. Here, the cultists gather fel energies, summon lesser demons, and concoct schemes to bring far more dangerous entities into the world.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_thunder-axe-fortress-25d5375b5a60e25ce1c5/1-0000.mp3", duration = 48.117551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:thunder-bluff"] = {
  hash = "b8690f",
  fullHash = "b8690f",
  text = "Thunder Bluff. On Mulgore’s high mesas, Thunder Bluff shelters the tauren as their capital. Cairne Bloodhoof founded the city after the Third War, bringing a people long scattered across Kalimdor together in a place of refuge and tradition. Thunder Bluff rises above Mulgore, where rope bridges link its stone mesas and wooden elevators carry travelers up from the plains. For generations, the tauren wandered across central Kalimdor, pursued by centaur raiding parties. During the Third War, Cairne Bloodhoof joined with Warchief Thrall and the New Horde. Together, they drove back the centaurs and claimed Mulgore’s fertile grasslands. Cairne gathered the scattered tribes and founded the city atop the mesas, where the heights offered shelter and a place for the tauren to trade and gather in worship. Cairne Bloodhoof’s followers share Thunder Bluff with tauren who hold to other ways. Magatha Grimtotem and her kin dwell on the Elder Rise, while spirit walkers and shaman seek the ancestors’ will at the Pools of Vision beneath the city. Tribal rivalries persist, yet Thunder Bluff remains a spiritual pillar of Kalimdor’s Horde. Hunters return from the Barrens, artisans travel through, and druids come to commune with the Earth Mother.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_thunder-bluff-2085001f0b16bba1e88f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 72.8555102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:tidefury-cove"] = {
  hash = "84c730",
  fullHash = "84c730",
  text = "Tidefury Cove. Tidefury Cove is a treacherous stretch of water and rocky shallows along the southeastern coast of Dustwallow Marsh, south of Theramore Isle. Renowned for its hazardous reefs and wrecked vessels, it serves as a secluded haven for roaming makrura and solitary anglers. Tucked away at the far end of Dustwallow Bay between Theramore Isle and the Wyrmbog, Tidefury Cove presents a notorious navigational hazard to passing sailors. Jagged reefs and shifting shoals have claimed multiple vessels over the years, leaving rotting wooden hulks half-submerged in the tideline. Today, the ruined ships are crawled over by opportunistic makrura, turning the cove into a dangerous, isolated stretch of coastline visited mostly by hardy scavengers or quiet hermits seeking distance from Theramore's busy docks. During the invasion of Durotar, the shores of Tidefury Cove became a crucial strategic battleground. Admiral Daelin Proudmoore's Kul Tiran expedition fortified the coast with an imposing coastal fortress and guard towers to command the approaches to Theramore. To break Proudmoore's grip, Thrall marshaled the combined strength of the young Horde—uniting orcs, Darkspear trolls, and tauren alongside Rexxar and the Stonemaul ogres—to storm the shoreline and dismantle the Kul Tiran beachhead before pressing directly toward the island fortress.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_tidefury-cove-89ffa1fef59d8e8767e7/1-0000.mp3", duration = 81.9461224, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:timbermaw-hold"] = {
  hash = "f30e07",
  fullHash = "f30e07",
  text = "Timbermaw Hold. Timbermaw Hold is a fortified subterranean network of tunnels that links Felwood, Moonglade, and Winterspring. It serves as the stronghold for the Timbermaw furbolgs, one of the last tribes to resist the demonic corruption plaguing northern Kalimdor. Carved deep into the mountain roots between Felwood, Moonglade, and Winterspring, Timbermaw Hold stands as a desperately guarded bastion. While neighboring furbolg tribes fell to the foul taint of the Burning Legion during the Third War, the Timbermaw clan weathered the nightmare within these stone corridors. Though safe beneath the earth, they were forced to watch their beloved forests warp into corrupted mockeries. Extremely protective and deeply suspicious of outsiders, the furbolgs guard every tunnel entrance fiercely, expecting violence from anyone wandering in from the infected woods. Beyond its role as a tribal sanctuary, Timbermaw Hold is the only direct mountain highway connecting the tainted lowlands of Felwood, the sacred sanctuary of Moonglade, and the frozen expanse of Winterspring. Outsiders who wish to pass through without being torn apart by tribal sentries must prove their goodwill. Those who demonstrate their opposition to the region's corrupted furbolg clans find the Timbermaw to be steadfast allies willing to share their ancient shamanic knowledge and leathercrafting traditions.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_timbermaw-hold-5f81a95de01bd448ef14/1-0000.mp3", duration = 81.6326531, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:timbermaw-post"] = {
  hash = "b7a740",
  fullHash = "b7a740",
  text = "Timbermaw Post. Timbermaw Post is a small furbolg camp situated in the snowy expanse of Winterspring, nestled between Frostfire Hot Springs and Lake Kel'Theril. Despite bearing the name of the uncorrupted Timbermaw tribe, the encampment has fallen under the aggressive grip of the corrupted Winterfall furbolgs. Positioned just southeast across the road from Frostfire Hot Springs and west of the frozen Lake Kel'Theril, Timbermaw Post stands as a bitter reminder of the furbolgs' internal strife in Winterspring. Despite its Timbermaw name, travelers approaching the camp will find no friendly welcomes. The outpost is controlled by the hostile Winterfall tribe, driven mad and violent by demonic corruption. Now crowded with savage Winterfall furbolgs, the camp serves as an aggressive staging ground encroaching upon the central road.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_timbermaw-post-6ceec720492c428279f8/1-0000.mp3", duration = 49.684898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:tkashi-ruins"] = {
  hash = "016605",
  fullHash = "016605",
  text = "Tkashi Ruins. The Tkashi Ruins are ancient stone remnants situated southwest of Nesingwary's Expedition in northern Stranglethorn Vale. Overrun by hostile Bloodscalp trolls, the site remains a stronghold of the tribe. Perched southwest of Hemet Nesingwary's hunting camp, the crumbling stone masonry of the Tkashi Ruins marks the ancient domain of Stranglethorn's jungle trolls. Today, the encampment is heavily occupied by the Bloodscalp tribe, who aggressively defend the territory and practice their shamanistic rituals among the vines and decayed stone. While Hemet Nesingwary noted passing through the ruins without trouble in his hunting memoirs, modern travelers find the trolls far less accommodating, guarding valuable tribal relics buried among the rubble.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_tkashi-ruins-fe081a3d07d61b5cbb80/1-0000.mp3", duration = 47.9346939, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:tower-of-arathor"] = {
  hash = "30ab95",
  fullHash = "30ab95",
  text = "Tower of Arathor. The Tower of Arathor is a ruined wizard's tower situated in the former mage district in the southwestern reaches of Stromgarde Keep. Once the workshop of Archmage Trelane, it was overrun during the fall of the city and is currently occupied by Boulderfist ogres. Erected in the heart of Stromgarde Keep's mage district and named after the ancient Empire of Arathor, this spire long served the realm's sorcerers. Its most notable resident, Archmage Trelane, devised a magical defensive grid keyed to enchanted gemstones to protect the sanctum. However, when the Syndicate and the Boulderfist ogre clan assaulted Stromgarde following the Third War, the kingdom fractured. Despite Trelane's desperate attempts to hold the tower using his arcane wards, the sheer brutish force of the ogres overwhelmed him. Forced to flee the city for his life, the archmage left behind his most prized arcane artifacts, leaving the spire in the hands of the Boulderfist brute Ruul Onestone and his followers.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_tower-of-arathor-ba97512474743ce555b8/1-0000.mp3", duration = 62.0146939, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:tower-of-eldara"] = {
  hash = "780885",
  fullHash = "780885",
  text = "Tower of Eldara. The Tower of Eldara is an ancient, sealed night elven spire situated on the far northeastern promontory overlooking the Bay of Storms in Azshara. Though long abandoned, its sturdy stone architecture has survived the Sundering, standing silent above coasts roamed by aggressive coastal beasts. Perched on a remote cliff edge northeast of the Bay of Storms, the Tower of Eldara stands as a lonely testament to the vast kaldorei empire that once dominated this coastline. Like much of Azshara's ruined architecture, it fell into disuse following the Great Sundering thousands of years ago. Today, the entrance remains firmly closed and barred against the outside world, giving no hint of whatever relics or chambers lie sealed inside. The surrounding shores are fraught with danger, prowled constantly by wandering murlocs, clawing makrura, and predatory hydras that keep all but the most daring travelers away.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_tower-of-eldara-81b0bfe8517fe8daa1a2/1-0000.mp3", duration = 53.2636735, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:twilight-base-camp"] = {
  hash = "fc6dd1",
  fullHash = "fc6dd1",
  text = "Twilight Base Camp. The Twilight Base Camp is a fortified outpost of the Twilight's Hammer cult situated in the arid wastes of Silithus. It serves as an active ritual site where cultists convene to commune with elemental powers and orchestrate their apocalyptic agenda. The camp is characterized by strange wind stones and heavily armed cult zealots. Perched among the desolate sands of Silithus, the Twilight Base Camp is one of several enclaves established by the Twilight's Hammer cult. The outpost brims with zealots, geomancers, and stonecallers dedicated to summoning devastating elemental forces. Surrounded by jagged ritual stones, the camp acts as a staging ground where leaders like Twilight Keeper Havunth and Twilight Lord Everun direct efforts to hasten Azeroth's end, standing in open defiance of the Cenarion Hold forces attempting to restore balance to the region. Scattered throughout the outpost are mysterious monuments known as wind stones, ranging in power from lesser conduits to greater monoliths. Through cryptic incantations and cult regalia, the Twilight's Hammer uses these focal points to pull chaotic elemental spirits directly into the physical realm. Disrupting these rituals remains a top priority for travelers and scouts hoping to prevent the cult's influence from spreading across Kalimdor.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_twilight-base-camp-77a6021e8304883cb467/1-0000.mp3", duration = 81.3453061, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:twilight-outpost"] = {
  hash = "1491fb",
  fullHash = "1491fb",
  text = "Twilight Outpost. The Twilight Outpost is a fortified encampment of the Twilight's Hammer cult nestled in the southwestern sands of Silithus. Located just northwest of the ancient Scarab Wall, it serves as a staging ground where cultists conduct dark rituals to commune with and summon elemental horrors. Tucked away in the southwestern reaches of Silithus, the Twilight Outpost serves as one of several major cultist strongholds dotting the desert wastes. Positioned in the shadow of the colossal Scarab Wall and west of the Bronzebeard Encampment, the outpost teems with fanatical Twilight Avengers, Geolords, and Stonecallers. Under the command of leaders like Twilight Keeper Exeter and the rare Twilight Lord Everun, these zealots toil tirelessly in service of their maddening apocalyptic masters. Scattered throughout the encampment are mysterious Wind Stones, varying in size from Lesser to Greater conduits. Through the use of enchanted cultist regalia and foul incantations, the cult utilizes these monoliths to tear into the elemental planes, dragging forth Abyssal commanders and elemental lords. For adventurers brave enough to infiltrate their ranks, disrupting these summonings remains one of the primary means to strike back against the cult's influence in the desert.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_twilight-outpost-cdd79adab880e13925b5/1-0000.mp3", duration = 74.2922449, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:twilight-post"] = {
  hash = "69cc33",
  fullHash = "69cc33",
  text = "Twilight Post. Twilight Post is a fortified encampment established by the Twilight's Hammer cult in northwestern Silithus. Situated far west of Cenarion Hold and northwest of the Twilight Base Camp, it serves as a critical site for the cultists' elemental summoning rituals. Adventurers venturing here face zealous cultists and the wind stones they use to contact otherworldly forces. Tucked away in the desert sands of northwestern Silithus, Twilight Post serves as an outpost for the Twilight's Hammer cult. The camp is populated by heavily armed defenders and ritualists, including Twilight Avengers, Geolords, and Stonecallers under the leadership of Twilight Keeper Mayna and the rare fanatic Twilight Lord Everun. Like other encampments scattered across the wastes, the post revolves around ancient summoning conduits—Lesser, regular, and Greater Wind Stones. Through dark rites and elemental communion, the cultists utilize these stones to entreat and bind elemental horrors from the realms beyond, threatening the fragile peace maintained by the Cenarion Circle in the region.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_twilight-post-954196a6a79eae8ff1c1/1-0000.mp3", duration = 61.8318367, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:twilight-s-run"] = {
  hash = "9f20c5",
  fullHash = "9f20c5",
  text = "Twilight's Run. Twilight's Run is a secluded subterranean cavern carved into the northern mountains of Silithus beneath Staghelm Point. Occupied by the fanatical Twilight's Hammer cult, it serves as a secluded sanctuary and ritual redoubt away from the harsh desert winds. Tucked away in the northern cliffside below Staghelm Point, Twilight's Run provides the Twilight's Hammer cultists with a natural bastion amidst the windswept hazards of Silithus. Rather than weathering the dust storms or contending directly with roaming silithid hives across the surface dunes, cultists retreat into these shadowy tunnels to commune with dark forces, guard ritual materials, and marshal their forces. Under the direction of overseers such as Vyral the Vile, cult members within the cave maintain a vigilant, hostile presence, ready to strike down any curious travelers or scouts who draw too close to their subterranean encampment.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_twilight-s-run-1e72742504c2da0cd102/1-0000.mp3", duration = 52.3232653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:twin-colossals"] = {
  hash = "8c0cc8",
  fullHash = "8c0cc8",
  text = "Twin Colossals. The Twin Colossals are a pair of towering, sheer-faced mesas that dominate the northwestern skyline of Feralas. Rising high above the canopy, the narrow passage between their sheer cliffs forms the primary overland route connecting Feralas with the arid wastes of Desolace to the north. Towering over the western reaches of Feralas, the Twin Colossals stand as the highest landforms in the region. Their vertical rock faces cut a striking silhouette against the sky, serving as unmistakable landmarks for travelers venturing south out of Desolace. While the lower slopes merge into the dense jungle and the lands stretching toward the Forgotten Coast, the summits are isolated plateaus far above the forest floor. Scaling the vertical cliffs on foot is impossible, yet the summits are not entirely deserted. An intrepid night elf named Marli Wishrunner assists travelers seeking passage to the eastern peak. Those who ascend find a tranquil pond perched atop the stone mesa—though it is inhabited by an unusually large makrura named Diamond Head. To aid adventurers in returning to earth, Kalin Windflight offers sturdy parachutes to descend back into the jungle canopy below.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_twin-colossals-ff93f19d51ba39d3073a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 72.9077551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:tyr-s-hand"] = {
  hash = "1473ec",
  fullHash = "1473ec",
  text = "Tyr's Hand. Tyr's Hand is a heavily fortified human stronghold situated in the southeastern hills of the Eastern Plaguelands. Long revered as an ancient holy site for the Church of the Holy Light, the city now serves as the primary regional bastion and supply hub for the zealous Scarlet Crusade. Perched defensively in what was once Lordaeron's green Eastweald, Tyr's Hand is one of humanity's most ancient sacred sites. The settlement became known as a 'city of churches' as shrines and holy structures were raised across its grounds. Unlike the blight-rotted ruins that litter the rest of the Plaguelands, Tyr's Hand remains remarkably preserved behind formidable masonry, thick walls, and the towering silhouette of the Scarlet Basilica. When the Scourge swept through Lordaeron during the Third War, Lord Valdelmar kept the city standing through sheer military vigilance and faith. After the Knights of the Silver Hand shattered into rival factions, Tyr's Hand aligned firmly with the Crimson dawn of the Scarlet Crusade. Today, it operates as an impenetrable base of operations led by High General Brigitte Abbendis and Valdelmar. Elite crusaders train within its abbey, heavily guarded couriers maintain communication with scarlet forces holding Stratholme, and the city ruthlessly rebuffs all outsiders who approach its gates.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_tyr-s-hand-a0ba263eab2a1a3bb6d6/1-0000.mp3", duration = 79.4906122, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:tyr-s-hand-abbey"] = {
  hash = "467c01",
  fullHash = "467c01",
  text = "Tyr's Hand Abbey. Tyr's Hand Abbey is a well-maintained sanctuary nestled within the fortified bastion of Tyr's Hand in the southeastern Eastern Plaguelands. Controlled by the zealots of the Scarlet Crusade, it serves as an auxiliary house of worship and study alongside the grand Scarlet Basilica. While the imposing Scarlet Basilica dominates the skyline of Tyr's Hand, the abbey provides a quieter, functional center of devotion and martial doctrine for the Scarlet Crusade. Its architecture closely mirrors traditional Holy Light foundations such as Northshire Abbey in Elwynn Forest, featuring a central Main Hall flanked by a Hall of Arms and a dedicated Library Wing. Despite the relentless horrors of the Plaguelands, the Crusade maintains the abbey with meticulous care, fiercely defending its cloisters from all outsiders.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_tyr-s-hand-abbey-5b19c34971fbb78415b4/1-0000.mp3", duration = 47.6473469, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:uldaman"] = {
  hash = "d24951",
  fullHash = "d24951",
  text = "Uldaman. Carved deep beneath the northern mountains of the Badlands, Uldaman is an ancient titan vault dating back to the ordering of the world. Recently unsealed by dwarven excavators, its halls contain the buried origins of the dwarven race alongside savage troggs and vigilant stone sentinels. Uldaman was constructed by titan watchers as a subterranean redoubt and research facility. When early experiments in shaping living stone produced the misshapen troggs, the titans sealed their failed creations within the vault's stasis chambers and began anew, culminating in the stone-skinned earthen. Its deepest chambers came to hold vital archives, including the fabled Discs of Norgannon, guarded by the stonekeeper Archaedas and the giantess Ironaya. Dwarven legend holds that in time the stone-skinned earthen gave rise to the dwarven race, and that their cradle lay buried and forgotten beneath the hills.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_uldaman-ebfdb757379ccd14caa0/1-0000.mp3", duration = 57.835102, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:uldum"] = {
  hash = "6afcda",
  fullHash = "6afcda",
  text = "Uldum. Uldum is a massive, sealed Titan research installation tucked behind colossal stone gates in the southernmost cliffs of Tanaris. Long hidden away behind ancient wards and forgotten machinery, it remains inaccessible to modern explorers. Carved into the rugged southern mountain range bordering the Tanaris desert, the monumental gateway to Uldum stands silent and impassable. Built during the ordering of ancient Kalimdor, the compound served as an advanced Titan facility tied to their mysterious efforts to shape and oversee the planet's biosphere. Titanic wardens and defensive constructs once maintained the perimeter, leaving modern mortals only monumental stone carved arches and buried secrets that have defied all mundane attempts at entry. The site's unmistakable Titan craftsmanship draws the curiosity of dwarven scholars, yet no mechanism or key has been found that might unlock the ancient complex. Legend claims that true insight into the secrets locked within requires the lost Plates of Uldum, leaving the dwarven scholars peering through the keyhole of an empire they cannot yet breach.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_uldum-f1eee3b07554409b4ccc/1-0000.mp3", duration = 68.1273469, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:undercity"] = {
  hash = "fa20a2",
  fullHash = "fa20a2",
  text = "Undercity. Carved beneath the shattered palace of Capital City, the Undercity serves as the sprawling subterranean stronghold of the Forsaken. Originally a tangle of royal crypts, dungeons, and sewers expanded by the Scourge, it was seized by Sylvanas Windrunner and forged into a heavily fortified capital for her free-willed undead. Before the Third War, the depths beneath Capital City held a vast network of sewers, royal catacombs, and dungeons. During the fall of Lordaeron, Prince Arthas Menethil ordered his Scourge to begin expanding these depths into a subterranean seat of power. When the Lich King summoned Arthas away to Northrend, the death knight left the construction unfinished. Amid the civil war in the Plaguelands, Sylvanas Windrunner rallied those undead whose minds had broken free from the Lich King's grip. After overthrowing the dreadlords and driving out rival forces, the newly christened Forsaken claimed the subterranean complex. They completed the twisted maze of catacombs the Scourge had started, fortifying it into an impenetrable stronghold beneath the silent ruins of their former kingdom. Today, the Dark Lady rules her people from within its poisoned halls.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_undercity-31f4e7913fd71c16c247/1-0000.mp3", duration = 73.6130612, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:undercroft"] = {
  hash = "e72958",
  fullHash = "e72958",
  text = "Undercroft. The Undercroft is an ancient burial crypt nestled in the southwestern hills of the Eastern Plaguelands. Once a dignified tomb of Lordaeron, it has been desecrated by a band of undead trolls under the command of Zaeldarr the Outcast. Amid the defiled catacombs lies an ersatz grave dug long ago to feign the death of the exiled paladin Tirion Fordring. Carved into the rugged bluffs southwest of Corin's Crossing, the Undercroft was originally built as a final resting place for the honored dead of Lordaeron. The fall of the kingdom stripped away its sanctity, and the tomb is now infested by a band of savage undead trolls. Led by the hulking monstrosity Zaeldarr the Outcast, these ghoulish marauders use the underground halls as a redoubt from which to stalk the Plaguelands, even daring to raid the grave pits of Light's Hope Chapel to gather raw corpses for their sinister practices.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_undercroft-c107f9cd496c4a8c50e1/1-0000.mp3", duration = 54.3869388, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:uplands"] = {
  hash = "c7f860",
  fullHash = "c7f860",
  text = "Uplands. The Uplands is a rolling, elevated territory in the northeastern Alterac Mountains that once served as the agricultural heartland of the fallen Kingdom of Alterac. Following the kingdom's collapse, the fertile hills fell entirely under the sway of the Syndicate. Today, the criminal organization maintains its strongest base of power here, fortifying multiple encampments across the slopes. Before the Second War broke Alterac apart, the hills of the Uplands were celebrated as the kingdom's breadbasket, providing grain and livestock to feed its mountain fortresses. When the core of the realm fell to ruin and infamy, the farming estates were abandoned by honest folk. In their place rose the Syndicate, led by dispossessed Alterac nobles and cutthroats determined to reclaim their stolen inheritance through force. The Uplands now represents the Syndicate's most heavily concentrated territory in the region, dotted with camps, patrols, and outposts like Dandred's Fold along its western borders. Because the Syndicate commands these hills in force, high-ranking conspirators often oversee operations from the Uplands. Figures such as Baron Vardus, aligned with the shadowy leadership steering the remnants of Alterac's nobility, coordinate brigand movements, raids on neighboring valleys, and resistance against both the Alliance and Horde. Travelers crossing into the northeastern ridges face immediate hostility from entrenched highwaymen who view the entire range as their sovereign soil.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_uplands-45663286f64771563edc/1-0000.mp3", duration = 87.8759184, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:ursolan"] = {
  hash = "567981",
  fullHash = "567981",
  text = "Ursolan. Ursolan is a sprawling Timbermaw furbolg settlement situated in western Azshara, just east of Timbermaw Hold. Bisected by a deep ravine, the encampment honors the bear Ancient Ursol and serves as a vital stronghold for uncorrupted furbolgs defending their territory from outside incursions. Perched on the rugged bluffs of western Azshara, Ursolan constitutes the eastern branch of the Timbermaw tribe's domain, connecting directly through Timbermaw Hold to Bear's Head in the south. Named after the revered bear Ancient Ursol, this camp represents a rare haven of sanity among Kalimdor's furbolg populations, which have largely fallen victim to demonic corruption. The village spans both rims of a dramatic earthen chasm, where the Timbermaw vigilantly guard their borders against encroaching interlopers, including intrusive researchers from the night elven camp of Talrendis.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_ursolan-297987d4f2621aa4cccd/1-0000.mp3", duration = 51.957551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:uther-s-tomb"] = {
  hash = "06005d",
  fullHash = "06005d",
  text = "Uther's Tomb. Uther's Tomb is the sacred resting place of Uther the Lightbringer, situated atop Sorrow Hill in the Western Plaguelands. Constructed by his surviving followers after his defeat at nearby Andorhal, this monument stands as one of the few remaining bastions of the Holy Light amidst a blighted and haunted region. Perched south of the ruins of Andorhal at Sorrow Hill, Uther's Tomb marks the final resting place of the legendary paladin who founded the Knights of the Silver Hand. Following the collapse of Lordaeron, Uther made his last stand in Andorhal, defending the royal urn holding the ashes of King Terenas Menethil II against Prince Arthas Menethil and the Scourge. Arthas struck down his former mentor and desecrated the urn to carry Kel'Thuzad's remains. Surviving soldiers salvaged Uther's body, laying him to rest within a stone sanctuary. Despite the relentless corruption engulfing the surrounding lands, the tomb remains miraculously untouched by decay, held under the steadfast vigil of High Priest Thel'danis. Approached via the Path of Uther, the monument centers on a grand stone effigy depicting the paladin with a tome of the Holy Light in one hand and his warhammer in the other. Pilgrims who travel these treacherous roads frequently seek out the tomb to pay homage, pray, or reflect upon the devotion that defined the first paladins.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_uther-s-tomb-1660097a2f3a86bce6aa/1-0000.mp3", duration = 83.9836735, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:valley-of-bones"] = {
  hash = "07356f",
  fullHash = "07356f",
  text = "Valley of Bones. The Valley of Bones is a desolate canyon along the crags of Shen'dralas, choked with dust, ancient remains, and restless undead. Haunted by the lingering spirits of the Magram centaur clan and patrolled by dark necromancers, it is dominated by the colossal rib cages of mysterious ancient beasts known as the Dead Goliaths. Tucked away in the southern reaches of the region, the Valley of Bones lives up to its grim name. Skeletal horrors walk the dusty ravines, raised and bound by centaur necromancers who draw upon dark sorceries. The spirits of deceased Magram centaur wander the wastes alongside them, bound to this barren earth long after their violent deaths. Adding to the canyon's ominous nature are the Dead Goliaths—two immense skeletons slumped against the rocky cliffs whose true origin remains a total mystery to scholars and wanderers alike.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_valley-of-bones-e54f50c7c07f503b21d9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 53.0546939, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:valley-of-fangs"] = {
  hash = "6671d6",
  fullHash = "6671d6",
  text = "Valley of Fangs. The Valley of Fangs is a rugged, narrow pass carved between towering rock pillars in the central Badlands. Known for the vicious predators that prowl its dusty floor, it also shelters a small, isolated encampment of wandering tinkerers and exiles. Situated between two imposing stone formations in the heart of the Badlands, the Valley of Fangs earns its name from both the sharp silhouettes of its surrounding cliffs and the dangerous beasts roaming its floor. Feral crag coyotes, prowling cougars, and vicious wolves stalk the dusty corridor, making transit hazardous for lone travelers. Despite the hostile wildlife, a handful of wayward figures—including the banished orc Martek the Exiled and his goblin associates Rigglefuzz and Jazzrik—have pitched an informal camp here, tinkering away in seclusion far from the regional outposts.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_valley-of-fangs-6adb8cd0a435dc8b4178/1-0000.mp3", duration = 54.8832653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:valley-of-spears"] = {
  hash = "1aac21",
  fullHash = "1aac21",
  text = "Valley of Spears. The Valley of Spears is a rugged canyon nestled in western Desolace, serving as the stronghold of the Maraudine centaur clan. Enclosed by steep cliffs and guarded passes, the valley leads directly to Maraudon, the sacred ancestral burial ground of all centaurkind. Carved into the rugged heights of western Desolace, the Valley of Spears is a stronghold of the Maraudine clan. Accessible on foot only through narrow, easily defended mountain passes from the south and east, this enclosed valley provides the centaur with a natural bastion. Travelers who venture past the rocky chokepoints face dense encampments of hostile nomads guarding the approach to their most revered holy site. Tucked away at the southwestern end of the valley lies the entrance to Maraudon, the sacred cavern housing the tomb of Zaetar, father of the centaur, where Princess Theradras dwells. Because of this proximity, the Maraudine defend the canyon with zealous fury against rival clans and outside interlopers alike. Anyone foolish enough to stumble into the valley unawares—such as careless gnome cartographers or scouts—frequently ends up captured or slain.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_valley-of-spears-60358c991e6be4f42bef/1-0000.mp3", duration = 68.4146939, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:valley-of-the-watchers"] = {
  hash = "8462e4",
  fullHash = "8462e4",
  text = "Valley of the Watchers. Tucked away in the far southern reaches of the Tanaris desert, the Valley of the Watchers is a sun-scorched canyon defined by monumental stonework. At its end stands a colossal, sealed stone gate leading into the mysterious complex of Uldum, guarded by lumbering giants and ancient stone sentinels. The Valley of the Watchers marks the southern boundary where the shifting sands of Tanaris meet the mountainous edge of the continent. Dominating the canyon is a massive gate crafted from ancient stone, locked tight against the outside world. This sealed portal is believed to guard the entrance to Uldum, a subterranean facility of the enigmatic Titans. Massive Dune Smasher mountain giants roam the dusty approach, ensuring that whatever secrets lie buried beneath the sands remain undisturbed by travelers, tomb robbers, and scholars alike.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_valley-of-the-watchers-f9c0a2030d31d456f30e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 50.964898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:valor-s-rest"] = {
  hash = "e0e884",
  fullHash = "e0e884",
  text = "Valor's Rest. Valor's Rest is a small outpost and cemetery perched at the eastern threshold of Silithus. Guarded by members of the Cenarion Circle, it is the first point of shelter seen by travelers emerging from Un'Goro Crater into the windswept wastes. Perched on the eastern border of Silithus along the ramp leading down from Un'Goro Crater, Valor's Rest serves as the threshold into this desolate frontier. Maintained as a neutral staging ground by the Cenarion Circle, the camp hosts members of both the Alliance and the Horde who dare to brave the arid wastes. Its prominent graveyard stands as a somber reminder of the danger lurking throughout the desert, where insectoid horrors and ancient threats test even the hardiest expeditions.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_valor-s-rest-7647166f3bd373871f4e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 42.6057143, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:valormok"] = {
  hash = "ee037d",
  fullHash = "ee037d",
  text = "Valormok. Valormok is a remote Horde outpost established in the rugged northwest reaches of Azshara. Positioned north of the Haldarr Encampment, this windswept settlement serves as a staging ground for scouts and adventurers braving the cursed, ancient ruins of the coast. Perched among the autumn-colored trees of Azshara, Valormok represents the Horde's tentative interest in a hostile and largely forsaken territory. Life here is bleak and fraught with peril; the camp is lightly fortified and constantly exposed to nearby satyrs, nagas, and prowling beasts. Despite these hardships, its stationed warriors hold the ground, acting as a crucial flight hub and forward bastion connecting Orgrimmar with the wilder stretches of northern Kalimdor.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_valormok-35b750494917498398a4/1-0000.mp3", duration = 49.0579592, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:venture-co-base-camp"] = {
  hash = "e3fe7b",
  fullHash = "e3fe7b",
  text = "Venture Co. Base Camp. Tucked away in the dense jungles of northern Stranglethorn Vale, the Venture Co. Base Camp serves as a forward foothold for the notoriously greedy goblin syndicate. Here, hired overseers and laborers exploit the lush wilderness, focusing their efforts on harvesting timber, hunting local wildlife, and extracting valuable tumbled crystals from the nearby waterways. The Venture Company is infamous across Azeroth for ruthless profiteering and complete disregard for the natural world. At this jungle base camp, steam-powered equipment and makeshift goblin shacks chew into the ancient canopy. Guarded by armed mercenaries, foremen, and field workers, the camp coordinates resource extraction throughout the surrounding valleys, paying particular interest to valuable tumbled crystals found nearby while clear-cutting any jungle that stands in their way.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_venture-co-base-camp-0fc4faa8f7b663a0f042/1-0000.mp3", duration = 52.244898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:vice"] = {
  hash = "1f5c10",
  fullHash = "1f5c10",
  text = "Vice. The Vice is a rugged, desolate expanse of Deadwind Pass situated along the road leading toward Karazhan. It serves as the territory of the Deadwind ogres, who maintain rough mounds and scattered camps in defiance of the land's grim isolation. Travelers passing through must navigate both their crude brutality and their surprisingly adept wielders of dark magic. Stretching across the southern stretches of Deadwind Pass, the Vice is defined by barren earth, jagged rock faces, and crude mounds erected by the Deadwind ogre clan. While the surrounding canyon is largely abandoned to vengeful ghosts and scavenger vultures, these ogres have carved out a stubborn foothold directly beside the main path approaching Karazhan. Why they chose such a bleak and magically scarred waste remains an enduring mystery, but any traveler passing between Duskwood and the Swamp of Sorrows quickly learns they defend their camps with fierce territorial violence. Unlike mere highwaymen, the ogres dwelling in the Vice possess both heavy martial strength and dangerous arcane talent. Alongside hulking bruisers, Deadwind warlocks practice crude yet lethal fel sorceries among the earthen mounds. Their presence so close to the abandoned seat of Medivh suggests a hunger for residual magical power, making the canyon a lethal gauntlet for wandering scholars and solitary scouts alike.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_vice-3f237280517e7e6d4457/1-0000.mp3", duration = 83.4612245, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:vile-reef"] = {
  hash = "3cc250",
  fullHash = "3cc250",
  text = "Vile Reef. The Vile Reef is a submerged, vibrant coral shelf lying off the western coast of Stranglethorn Vale near Grom'gol Base Camp. Beneath its waters, aggressive murlocs make their home among ancient sunken troll ruins, giant clams, and coastal shipwrecks. Stretching beneath the waves of the Great Sea just offshore from Grom'gol Base Camp, the Vile Reef is known for its vivid marine growth and perilous inhabitants. The seabed is dotted with the weathered stonework of submerged troll ruins alongside the enigmatic Altar of the Tides. Flocks of territorial murlocs lurk among the coral shelves and shattered timbers of passing vessels, fiercely defending the shallow waters and preying upon any diver who braves the deep in search of giant clams and rare blue pearls.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_vile-reef-1e4df7fa53439a2d99c3/1-0000.mp3", duration = 45.0873469, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:wailing-caverns"] = {
  hash = "2fc56b",
  fullHash = "2fc56b",
  text = "Wailing Caverns. Steam wails through the bluffs above Lushwater Oasis, where the Wailing Caverns twist beneath the Barrens. Naralex, a night elf druid, entered the springs hoping to restore life to the arid plains, but mutations and agitated wildlife now haunt the caverns.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_wailing-caverns-4bd3ee33a3edf6485345/1-0000.mp3", duration = 16.6661224, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:warpwood-quarter"] = {
  hash = "c6f3a1",
  fullHash = "c6f3a1",
  text = "Warpwood Quarter. The Warpwood Quarter encompasses the eastern district of the ruined Highborne city of Eldre'Thalas, now known as Dire Maul. Once a center of elven nature cultivation and lush gardens, it has devolved into a warped, demon-tainted ruin overrun by frenzied flora and corrupted satyrs. Formerly known for its grand botanical marvels, the Warpwood Quarter has become a choking forest of twisted ancients and lethal lashers. The district fell under the sway of Alzzin the Wildshaper, a satyr who bent his dark magic toward defiling the ancient Fruit of Fertility. Under his malign influence, the plant life grew grotesque and predatory, saturating the halls with vile Felvine shards and drawing demons, rebellious imps, and demonic thralls into the ruined halls. While explorers typically enter the district through the central Broken Commons of Dire Maul, an overgrown subterranean backdoor lies hidden beneath the Lariss Pavilion to the east. Navigating the district requires contending not only with Alzzin's warped guardians, but also with elusive denizens such as the mischievous imp Pusillin, whose thievery leads pursuers on dangerous chases toward the secluded reaches of the ruins.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_warpwood-quarter-b09784467da430f52a32/1-0000.mp3", duration = 74.0832653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:waterspring-field"] = {
  hash = "d86b7b",
  fullHash = "d86b7b",
  text = "Waterspring Field. Waterspring Field is a vital water-pumping operation in northern Tanaris, constructed by the goblins of Gadgetzan to tap underground aquifers. Instead of supplying the boomtown, the field has been overtaken by the ruthless Wastewander bandits, who now hoard the desert's most precious resource. In the searing wastes of Tanaris, nothing carries more value than fresh water. Seeking to support their expanding trade hub, the Steamwheedle Cartel goblins of Gadgetzan erected an array of industrial pumping towers across Waterspring Field to draw from underground reserves. Their plans collapsed when the Wastewanders—a sprawling band of human outlaws and desert marauders—seized the entire field. The bandits now guard the hijacked pumps with their lives, forcing Gadgetzan to look for hired blades willing to reclaim the moisture.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_waterspring-field-b860e9746fb87a5ccdd9/1-0000.mp3", duration = 51.0693878, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:wavestrider-beach"] = {
  hash = "b9b5ba",
  fullHash = "b9b5ba",
  text = "Wavestrider Beach. Wavestrider Beach is the sun-scorched coastal strip lining the eastern edge of Tanaris. Stretching between Steamwheedle Port and the pirate stronghold at Lost Rigger Cove, its sands are inhabited by aggressive crustacean wildlife and overlook distant naval skirmishes out at sea. Running along the ocean border of eastern Tanaris, Wavestrider Beach connects the goblin settlement of Steamwheedle Port in the north to the cliffs guarding Lost Rigger Cove to the south. Rather than a gentle resort, the shoreline is plagued by vicious Steeljaw Snappers and Desert Crawlers scuttling across the baking sand. Out in the deep water just offshore, burning vessels remain locked in a perpetual naval clash, serving as a grim warning of the piratical violence that dominates these coastal waters.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_wavestrider-beach-3d6057fe62932fce15dc/1-0000.mp3", duration = 45.9232653, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:weazel-s-crater"] = {
  hash = "3f2bf4",
  fullHash = "3f2bf4",
  text = "Weazel's Crater. Weazel's Crater is a deep gouge carved into the hard salt of the northern Shimmering Flats. Its origins are mysterious, and Saltstone basilisks inhabit the hollow. Across the blinding white expanse of the Shimmering Flats, experimental goblin and gnome rocket-cars constantly push the boundaries of mechanical sanity. Debris, scorched earth, and impact hollows litter the plains, but Weazel's Crater stands out for its sheer size and depth. The crater's origins are unknown. Today, the mechanical wreckage has largely settled, and the hollow has been claimed as a sunning pit by aggressive Saltstone basilisks.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_weazel-s-crater-278c0f735bb3017a79cc/1-0000.mp3", duration = 36.8065306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:weeping-cave"] = {
  hash = "4064ad",
  fullHash = "4064ad",
  text = "Weeping Cave. The Weeping Cave is a foul, subterranean cavern tucked into the hills of the northeastern Western Plaguelands, just west of the Thondroril River. Once a natural cave system, it now seeps with toxic blight and festers with grotesque fungal beasts and oozes. Carved into the rugged bluffs above the Thondroril River, the Weeping Cave earned its name from the continual trickling of moisture through its rocky ceiling—water that has long since turned rank and corrupt. The Scourge's plague has thoroughly permeated the soil above, draining virulent filth down into the dark. Within, rotting horrors, decaying behemoths, and caustic oozes wander the damp tunnels, accompanied by an enigmatic corrupted elemental entity known simply as The Husk.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_weeping-cave-bab43158cfbfaaec004a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 46.2106122, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:whispering-gardens"] = {
  hash = "211144",
  fullHash = "211144",
  text = "Whispering Gardens. The Whispering Gardens occupy the terraced hillside just south of the Scarlet Monastery in northeastern Tirisfal Glades. Once a place of quiet contemplation, it became the site of a savage clash between early crusade defenders and a massive host of the Scourge. Today, crusaders patrol its overgrown pathways while the faint voices of the fallen linger in the air. Before the Scarlet Crusade fully retreated behind the fortress walls of the Monastery, a fierce battle erupted across these terraced slopes against an encroaching army of undead. The clash claimed countless lives on both sides. In the twilight, travelers through the gardens report hearing strange, soft murmurings on the wind—believed to be the restless echoes of the soldiers and priests who perished defending the holy grounds. Despite the haunting presence, the Scarlet Crusade maintains an active watch over the gardens. Scarlet scouts, magicians, and preservers guard the trails to intercept any intruders approaching from the lower glades. For the zealots within the nearby monastery, these overgrown steps serve as both a defensive bulwark and a solemn reminder of the ceaseless war against the undead.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_whispering-gardens-a427bcd1c5cc01eee00e/1-0000.mp3", duration = 70.8440816, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:wild-shore"] = {
  hash = "f85fca",
  fullHash = "f85fca",
  text = "Wild Shore. The Wild Shore is the southernmost coastline of Stranglethorn Vale, where the jungle meets the waves of the South Seas. It serves as a staging ground and anchorage for the Bloodsail Buccaneers, whose ships lie moored along the shallows. Stretching along the bottom tip of the continent, the Wild Shore marks the border where Stranglethorn Vale plunges into the South Seas. To the northwest lies the fortified inlet of Booty Bay, while a shallow ocean strait separates the mainland beach from Jaguero Isle to the east. The beach continues northeast toward the Crystal Shore. Because of its secluded position away from the Blackwater Raiders' direct cannons, the coast has become a hotbed of piratical activity and wild perils. The shore is heavily occupied by the Bloodsail Buccaneers, who anchor their wooden ships in the surf and pitch camps along the sand. These swashbucklers use the beaches to stage operations, store illicit cargo, and plan strikes against their goblin rivals. Travelers must also contend with natural hazards of the shore, including prowling beasts and Gorlash, a colossal sea giant who stomps through the surf, crushing anyone caught lingering along the tide line.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_wild-shore-e0766628eff56a3b0827/1-0000.mp3", duration = 71.4187755, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:windbreak-canyon"] = {
  hash = "279a25",
  fullHash = "279a25",
  text = "Windbreak Canyon. Windbreak Canyon is a winding gorge along the northern side of Thousand Needles, situated between Freewind Post and the Shimmering Flats. Famous for its harsh drafts and jagged stone, it is heavily infested with nesting wind serpents. Travelers passing along the main road must tread carefully near its mouth to avoid being hunted from above. Carved into the sheer northern walls of the canyon floor, Windbreak Canyon spans a large stretch of territory east of Freewind Post. Rather than offering shelter, the ravine channels whistling winds between the rock spires, creating ideal perches for feral wind serpents. The predators lay their clutches along the stone ledges and strike at unwary travelers drifting off the main road. Those braving the canyon interior will also find the winding trails that climb upward toward the Weathered Nook.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_windbreak-canyon-d26aa138c461b9066439/1-0000.mp3", duration = 51.0171429, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:winterfall-village"] = {
  hash = "4202e1",
  fullHash = "4202e1",
  text = "Winterfall Village. Winterfall Village is a sprawling furbolg encampment nestled in the snowdrifts of eastern Winterspring, just east of Everlook. Once peaceful kin to the other ursine tribes of Kalimdor, the Winterfall furbolgs living here have descended into madness and violent corruption under the rule of High Chief Winterfall. Sprawled across the terraced snowbanks east of the goblin trading post Everlook, Winterfall Village serves as the primary bastion of the Winterfall furbolgs in Winterspring. Like their cousins scattered across northern Kalimdor, these bear-folk were warped and driven mad by the lingering foulness of the Burning Legion's invasion. Led by High Chief Winterfall from a secluded cavern atop the settlement's highest tier, the tribe has abandoned its ancestral ways. Instead of communing peacefully with the wild spirits of the snow, the enraged furbolgs now aggressively assault travelers, patrol the mountain passes, and threaten any who come near their frost-bitten home.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_winterfall-village-3d018e2612bf071e637a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 57.3387755, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:witch-hill"] = {
  hash = "0e8f34",
  fullHash = "0e8f34",
  text = "Witch Hill. Witch Hill is a dreary, mist-shrouded rise in the heart of Dustwallow Marsh along the path toward Theramore. It is best known for the secluded Swamplight Manor, scattered camps of hostile Mirefin murlocs, and sinister rumors of dark hauntings. Rising out of the stagnant waters of Dustwallow Marsh, Witch Hill sits roughly a third of the way along the peninsula leading southeast toward Theramore Isle. The elevated ground provides little refuge from the hazards of the swamp, as aggressive Mirefin murlocs claim the surrounding waters. At its center stands Swamplight Manor, the lonely and unsettling residence of the hermit known as 'Swamp Eye' Jarl. Travelers passing through frequently report eerie phenomena and lingering dark presences across the knoll.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_witch-hill-b2cb122df5069cf2e696/1-0000.mp3", duration = 47.0465306, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:witherbark-village"] = {
  hash = "62a267",
  fullHash = "62a267",
  text = "Witherbark Village. Witherbark Village is a forest troll settlement nestled around a secluded lake in the southeastern hills of the Arathi Highlands. Inhabited by the hostile Witherbark tribe, it serves as a staging ground for troll raiders contesting both the Alliance remnants of Stromgarde and the Horde outpost at Hammerfall. Built along the shores of a quiet mountain lake, Witherbark Village houses the fierce forest trolls of the Witherbark tribe. While fishers, axe throwers, and witch doctors occupy the hut-lined waterfront, tribal leaders such as Zalas Witherbark and deadly shadow hunters oversee operations from a massive cavern cavern tucked behind the settlement. The Witherbark maintain an opportunistic alliance with the Boulderfist ogres of Arathi, working together to carve out territory across the highlands—even establishing a firm foothold amidst the ruins of Stromgarde Keep. Fiercely territorial, they lash out at any outsider venturing into their corner of the basin.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_witherbark-village-6fcb7d4436d832b2176f/1-0000.mp3", duration = 60.7608163, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:woodpaw-den"] = {
  hash = "73a3c8",
  fullHash = "73a3c8",
  text = "Woodpaw Den. Woodpaw Den is a rugged gnoll stronghold nestled in southern Feralas, just north of the ancient Darkmist Ruins. The settlement serves as a major gathering point for the Woodpaw gnolls and their fiercest pack alphas, making it a dangerous pocket of wilderness within the dense canopy. Situated amidst the towering boughs of Feralas, Woodpaw Den stands as the fortified heartland of the local Woodpaw gnolls. Unlike smaller scout camps scattered along the jungle tracks, this encampment is heavily guarded by the pack's seasoned Alphas. The gnolls here defend their territory fiercely against both intruding adventurers and the encroaching wilderness. Located just north of the elven remnants at Darkmist Ruins, the den represents an ever-present predatory threat to travelers attempting to traverse the central wilderness of the region.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_woodpaw-den-91c488870d16ff691765/1-0000.mp3", duration = 49.397551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:woodpaw-hills"] = {
  hash = "6a5bc3",
  fullHash = "6a5bc3",
  text = "Woodpaw Hills. The Woodpaw Hills are a rugged, forested stretch of southeastern Feralas claimed by the Woodpaw gnolls. Once a dominant tribal territory, the hills have become an embattled frontline as insectoid terrors tunnel up from below and ancient stone giants stalk the canopy. The dense forests of the Woodpaw Hills serve as the historical territory of the Woodpaw gnoll clan. While the hyena-folk once roamed unchallenged across these slopes, their dominance has crumbled under pressure from multiple fronts. To their southeast, nightmarish silithid swarmers have breached the earth at the Writhing Deep, forcing the gnolls back and compelling their chieftains to retreat deeper into the Woodpaw Den. Adding to the peril, ancient mountain giants—relics summoned down during the Third War to repel the Burning Legion—now wander the hillside woods, crushing any travelers or Horde interlopers who stray into their path.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_woodpaw-hills-5c9587dccd8150e26ccd/1-0000.mp3", duration = 55.04, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:workshop-entrance"] = {
  hash = "2bfbe8",
  fullHash = "2bfbe8",
  text = "Workshop Entrance. Beyond the Train Depot, Workshop Entrance gives a backdoor into Gnomeregan. The route opens near the Engineering Labs and bypasses the front gates, offering another way into the irradiated city. Past the Train Depot, Workshop Entrance crosses the cavernous workshop floor toward the industrial heart of Gnomeregan. The opening to the Loading Room lies on one side. This passage runs straight to the Engineering Labs, avoiding the fortified entry gates near the surface, where leper gnomes and troggs contest the way. For those trying to reclaim the underground capital, this side door bypasses the first perimeter.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_workshop-entrance-4c8aeaa08990cc925177/1-0000.mp3", duration = 38.9746939, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:writhing-deep"] = {
  hash = "d9d3f6",
  fullHash = "d9d3f6",
  text = "Writhing Deep. The Writhing Deep is a massive, insectoid hive complex carved into southeastern Feralas. Inhabited by the Zukk'ash silithid, this subterranean burrow is a growing danger that threatens nearby wildlife and surrounding encampments alike. The relentless expansion of these subterranean predators has upset the regional balance of the lush forest. Tucked away in the humid lowlands of southeastern Feralas, the Writhing Deep serves as the primary colony for the Zukk'ash silithid. Unlike the typical woodland beasts of the valley, these chittering insects dig labyrinthine tunnels and fiercely guard clutches of resinous eggs. The hive's aggressive encroachment into the nearby territories has destabilized the wild fauna and agitated local gnoll packs, pushing displaced and frenzied predators uncomfortably close to both Horde paths and native sanctuaries.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_writhing-deep-4f4dec8e3f2a660d41c6/1-0000.mp3", duration = 49.92, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:writhing-haunt"] = {
  hash = "5d7915",
  fullHash = "5d7915",
  text = "Writhing Haunt. The Writhing Haunt is a blighted farmstead situated east of the Ruins of Andorhal in the Western Plaguelands. Once fertile land that fed Lordaeron, it has been seized by the Scourge and transformed into a toxic spreading ground for plague vapors. Like many of the old human homesteads surrounding Andorhal, the farmstead now called the Writhing Haunt fell swiftly during the Third War. Today, the fields do not grow crops but instead harvest rot. The Scourge planted an enormous plague cauldron squarely in the center of the tilled earth, continuously venting noxious, diseased vapors into the air. Rotting ghouls, freezing ghouls, and fetid zombies wander the overgrown furrows, guarding the machinery under the command of Cauldron Lord Razarch. Despite the omnipresent danger of the Scourge and the choking miasma from the cauldron, the farm is not entirely abandoned. Inside one of the dilapidated farmhouses lingers Mulgris Deepriver, a tauren druid who came to these lands to heal the damage done to nature. Stricken by the plague himself and unable to cure the suffering beasts, he asks capable travelers to grant the diseased wolves and other wildlife a swift and merciful release.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_writhing-haunt-7d3309571bdd855e7136/1-0000.mp3", duration = 70.164898, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:wyrmbog"] = {
  hash = "4362f9",
  fullHash = "4362f9",
  text = "Wyrmbog. Wyrmbog is the perilous southern expanse of Dustwallow Marsh, lying just south of the Dragonmurk. Dominated by foul mire and jagged spires of stone, it serves as the staging ground and territorial buffer for the black dragonflight. The caves of ancient wyrms, including the broodmother Onyxia and the elder Emberstrife, are carved directly into its surrounding ridges. Wyrmbog represents the most heavily infested black dragon territory in southern Kalimdor. Where the open waters of Dustwallow give way to scorched muck and towering bones, whelps and mature drakes patrol the fetid air. The bog is choked with toxic fumes and predatory beasts driven wild by the draconic presence. Travelers foolish enough to wander into these reaches find themselves directly in the shadow of Onyxia's Lair and Emberstrife's cavernous den.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_wyrmbog-86ec6f4df9d7604c6d75/1-0000.mp3", duration = 52.3493878, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:yojamba-isle"] = {
  hash = "6393ea",
  fullHash = "6393ea",
  text = "Yojamba Isle. Yojamba Isle is a cluster of small tropical islets off the northwestern coast of Stranglethorn Vale, near the waters leading up to Westfall. It serves as an offshore encampment for the scholarly Zandalar Tribe, who have established a foothold here to observe the growing crisis in the jungle. From these shores, the Zandalari seek willing champions from both the Horde and the Alliance to confront the ancient horrors awakening in Zul'Gurub. While most trolls encountered across Stranglethorn Vale are hostile to outsiders, the Zandalar tribe dwelling on Yojamba Isle maintain an uneasy neutrality. As the ancient priests and lorekeepers of troll civilization, the Zandalari have watched with mounting dread as the Gurubashi trolls within Zul'Gurub turned to Hakkar the Soulflayer. Knowing the world-ending danger a fully summoned blood god represents, the Zandalari have ventured far from their ancestral home to establish this staging ground, calling upon any capable adventurer—regardless of faction—to aid in curbing the threat. The island rests quietly along the coastline northwest of the Vile Reef and the ruins that haunt the mainland beaches. To the north lies the massive hidden sea-gate of Ironclad Cove, while the surrounding shallows are frequented by wandering sea beasts. Despite its precarious location, the Zandalari camp functions as a tranquil sanctuary where exotic rituals, sacred altars, and ancient knowledge provide solace and power to those preparing to march deep into the jungle's darkest ruins.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_yojamba-isle-fdad5a2476c290c9eeab/1-0000.mp3", duration = 91.5069388, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:zalashji-s-den"] = {
  hash = "b4901d",
  fullHash = "b4901d",
  text = "Zalashji's Den. Zalashji's Den is a secluded cave tucked along the eastern coast of Tanaris. It serves as the solitary lair of Zalashji, an enigmatic naga who dwells far from the typical gathering grounds of his serpentine kin. Carved into the rugged bluffs along the eastern shoreline of Tanaris, this coastal cavern provides shelter to a single inhabitant: Zalashji. Naga are known across Kalimdor to gather in disciplined war parties or coastal outposts, yet Zalashji resides here entirely alone. Little is known about why he made this isolated cave his domain, far removed from the primary settlements and desert ruins of the region, but any traveler venturing along the surf should approach the cave mouth with caution.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_zalashji-s-den-22588f6495061159640a/1-0000.mp3", duration = 44.277551, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:zul-farrak"] = {
  hash = "7e022f",
  fullHash = "7e022f",
  text = "Zul'Farrak. Zul'Farrak is a sprawling, sun-baked sandstone city tucked into the northwestern cliffs of Tanaris. Long isolated from the rest of their kind, the Sandfury trolls defend this ancient stronghold with fanatic zeal, dark voodoo, and a hoard of undead risen from their sandy graves. Little of Zul'Farrak's distant past survives beyond troll legend, but the Sandfury trolls who hold it have long endured the searing, arid wastes of Tanaris. Known across the desert for their particular ruthlessness, their priests now practice a brutal brand of dark mysticism, exhuming their own dead to build a shambling army within their sun-bleached walls. Whispers throughout Gadgetzan tell of treasures and terrors hidden behind Zul'Farrak's guarded gates. Troll lore speaks of Sul'thraze the Lasher, a legendary broadsword broken into two separate blades that remain hidden somewhere in the city. More alarming are the tales of a band of mercenaries led by Sergeant Bly who wandered into the city and were captured, as well as rumors of Gahz'rilla, an ancient hydra demigod slumbering within a sacred pool at the city's heart.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_zul-farrak-edf7d18d0b1277203b9d/1-0000.mp3", duration = 66.3771429, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:zul-gurub"] = {
  hash = "fc5f00",
  fullHash = "fc5f00",
  text = "Zul'Gurub. Zul'Gurub is an ancient temple city nestled deep in the jungles of northeastern Stranglethorn Vale. Once the glorious heart of the sprawling Gurubashi Empire, it now stands as a perilous stronghold where jungle trolls scheme to summon an ancient, terrible entity into the world. Thousands of years ago, Zul'Gurub reigned supreme as the vibrant capital of the entire Gurubashi Empire, uniting the jungle troll tribes under its grand stone ziggurats. That golden age shattered roughly fifteen hundred years before the opening of the Dark Portal when a vicious civil war tore the empire apart. A radical sect of priests, the Atal'ai, sought to summon Hakkar the Soulflayer—a bloodthirsty ancient god—to seize absolute power. Though loyalist tribes banded together to defeat and banish the Atal'ai northward into the Swamp of Sorrows, the devastation broke the empire's spine, leaving Zul'Gurub a violent shadow of its former grandeur.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_zul-gurub-48d2be4a1479277685fb/1-0000.mp3", duration = 61.8579592, start = 0},
  },
}
P.transport["subzone:zul-mashar"] = {
  hash = "0aae7d",
  fullHash = "0aae7d",
  text = "Zul'Mashar. Zul'Mashar is an ancient forest troll settlement tucked into the northeastern corner of the Eastern Plaguelands. Formerly the proud capital of the Mossflayer tribe, it has deteriorated into a desolate ruin following continuous wars with human kingdoms and the devastation of the Plague of Undeath. Today, its remaining defenders face an insidious collapse as the Scourge works from within to turn them into undead thralls. Long before the Scourge swept across northern Lordaeron, Zul'Mashar served as the seat of power for the Mossflayer forest trolls. Constant border skirmishes against the humans of Lordaeron chipped away at their dominance, leaving their territories fractured. When the Plague of Undeath blanketed the region, it struck the Mossflayers just as mercilessly as it did their mortal enemies. While the great stepped ziggurat of Mazra'Alor still stands, Zul'Mashar is a hollow shell of its former glory. Commanded by Warlord Thresh'jin and Zul'Brin Warpbranch, the remaining trolls cling stubbornly to their ancient home, largely blind to the reality that Scourge influence is already seeping into their ranks and quietly transforming their kin into rotting monstrosities.",
  ["1"] = {
    {file = "Audio/Transport/full_subzone_zul-mashar-ee3616b6eef346fe06ea/1-0000.mp3", duration = 70.7134694, start = 0},
  },
}
